window.LEARN_DATA={"teachers":{"ch":"Chase Hughes","vve":"Vanessa Van Edwards","vg":"Vinh Giang"},"topics":[{"id":"openers","name":"Openers & small talk","emoji":"👋"},{"id":"deeper","name":"Going deeper","emoji":"🌊"},{"id":"listening","name":"Listening","emoji":"👂"},{"id":"warmth","name":"Warmth & liking","emoji":"☀️"},{"id":"groups","name":"Names, groups & friends","emoji":"🫶"},{"id":"presence","name":"Confidence & presence","emoji":"🧭"},{"id":"voice","name":"Voice & delivery","emoji":"🎙️"},{"id":"body","name":"Body & face","emoji":"🙂"},{"id":"stories","name":"Stories & humour","emoji":"📖"},{"id":"tough","name":"Tough moments","emoji":"🛟"},{"id":"needs","name":"The six needs","emoji":"👑"},{"id":"ideas","name":"Big ideas","emoji":"💡"},{"id":"brain","name":"How people work","emoji":"🧠"},{"id":"practice","name":"Practice & habits","emoji":"🔁"},{"id":"teachers","name":"Meet the teachers","emoji":"🎓"}],"cards":[{"id":"v1","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Conversation Blueprint","mv":"Move fact → values → story, one level at a time; stay put when answers get shorter.","tr":"Start light, listen for energy, then go one level deeper than feels comfortable — but only as fast as they go.","w":"You move up only when their answers get longer and warmer.","ev":"Moderate (McAdams; Kardas 2022)","code":"V1","d":{"steps":["Move fact","values","story, one level at a time; stay put when answers get shorter"],"src":["Conversation \"The Conversation Blueprint\"","scienceofpeople.com/conversation","Science of People: Conversation"],"rel":["n-vve-power-questions","n-vve-go-deeper"],"status":"DELIVERY (structures the Moment chips)","how":["Vanessa's map for a good conversation has three levels, taken in order and at the other person's pace: Level 1 puts people at ease (light facts and fun), Level 2 connects more deeply (goals, values, worries), and Level 3 creates meaning (the story they tell about their life).","Each level has three power questions. You move up only when their answers get longer and warmer. If answers shrink, stay where you are or step back a level. Level 3 borrows from psychologist Dan McAdams's work on life stories."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Name the 3 steps of “Conversation Blueprint”.","a":"Move fact → values → story, one level at a time; stay put when answers get shorte"},{"id":"v2","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Power Q1: exciting work","mv":"\"Working on anything exciting these days?\"","tr":"Ask it lightly, then follow whatever lights them up, even if it isn't work.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V2","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Working on anything exciting these days?","how":"","why":"Van Edwards' first power question. 'Exciting' invites a happy answer instead of a job title."},{"say":"Anything exciting going on at work at the moment?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1","Conversation, Level 1 power question 1"],"rel":["n-vve-power-questions"],"status":"USED","how":["Vanessa's swap for \"What do you do?\" It lets people talk about work or anything better, and it doesn't feel like a question about their status or worth."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “Working on anything exciting …”","a":"“Working on anything exciting these days?”"},{"id":"v3","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Power Q2: fun plans","mv":"\"Have any fun plans coming up?\"","tr":"If they say \"not really\", share a small plan of your own and hand the question back.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V3","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Got any fun plans coming up?","how":"","why":"Power question #2. Asking about something to look forward to puts people in a good mood."},{"say":"Anything fun planned for the weekend?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1","Conversation, Level 1 power question 2"],"rel":["n-vve-power-questions","v32"],"status":"USED","how":["The second Level 1 power question. It points the chat at something good in their near future, so it starts on a warm note."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “Have any fun plans …”","a":"“Have any fun plans coming up?”"},{"id":"v4","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Power Q3: passion project","mv":"\"What's your personal passion project?\"","tr":"Follow up on the why: \"What got you into that?\"","w":"It invites a story instead of a fact, and most people light up when asked about the thing they do just because they love it.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V4","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What's your personal passion project at the moment?","how":"","why":"Power question #3. Pet projects reveal what people care about."},{"say":"Is there a side project you love working on?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1","Conversation, Level 1 power question 3"],"rel":["n-vve-power-questions","v44"],"status":"USED","how":["The third Level 1 question. It invites a story instead of a fact, and most people light up when asked about the thing they do just because they love it."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “What's your personal …”","a":"“What's your personal passion project?”"},{"id":"v5","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Power Q4: biggest goal","mv":"\"What's your biggest goal right now?\"","tr":"Only ask once the light chat is flowing, and be ready to be excited for them.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V5","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What's your biggest goal right now?","how":"","why":"Power question #4 opens Level 2: goals and what drives someone."},{"say":"What are you working towards this year?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"Uncovering Secret Dreams\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"Uncovering Secret Dreams\""],"rel":["v37","n-vve-power-questions"],"status":"USED","how":["A Level 2 power question that moves from facts to what someone is working towards. When they share it, get genuinely interested — Vanessa calls that dreambuilding."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “What's your biggest goal …”","a":"“What's your biggest goal right now?”"},{"id":"v6","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Power Q5: always wanted to learn","mv":"\"What have you always wanted to learn?\" (also her \"sound smart\" move)","tr":"Follow up with \"What's stopped you so far?\" if the mood is right.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V6","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What have you always wanted to learn?","how":"","why":"Power question #5. Talking about learning makes both of you sound curious and bright."},{"say":"What's your current YouTube rabbit hole?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"How to Sound Smart\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Sound Smart\""],"rel":["n-vve-power-questions","v67"],"status":"USED","how":["A Level 2 question that shows curiosity and makes you look thoughtful too. It reveals what someone values without asking about values directly."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “What have you always wanted …”","a":"“What have you always wanted to learn?”"},{"id":"v7","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Power Q6: signature question","mv":"\"What do you love to talk about?\" — and build your own signature question.","tr":"Pick a question you'd genuinely love to hear answers to, and use it often enough that it becomes yours.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V7","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What do you love to talk about?","how":"","why":"Power question #6, and her nudge to build your own signature question."},{"say":"What could you talk about for hours?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"Be Known as an Incredible & Unique Conversationalist\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"Be Known as an Incredible & Unique Conversationalist\""],"rel":["n-vve-power-questions","v19"],"status":"USED","how":["\"What do you love to talk about?\" is Vanessa's sixth power question, and she encourages you to build your own signature question — one you're known for, that people enjoy answering."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “What do you love to …”","a":"“What do you love to talk about?”"},{"id":"v8","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Power Q7: character question","mv":"\"What book, movie or TV character are you most like, and why?\" — the back door to self-narrative.","tr":"Answer it yourself too, so it feels like play rather than an interview.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V8","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Which film or TV character are you most like, and why?","how":"","why":"Power question #7: a playful back door into how someone sees their own story."},{"say":"If your life were a book, which character would you be?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3 \"Get People to Reveal Their True Selves\"","Conversation, Level 3 \"Get People to Reveal Their True Selves\""],"rel":["v52"],"status":"USED","how":["\"What book, movie or TV character are you most like, and why?\" is a Level 3 question. It's a gentle back door into how someone sees themselves, without asking anything too direct."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “What book, movie or TV character are you …”","a":"“What book, movie or TV character are you most like, and why?”"},{"id":"v9","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Power Q8: misunderstood","mv":"\"How do you feel most misunderstood?\" — only when the relationship can hold it.","tr":"Never ask it of a stranger. Ask it when there's trust, and listen without fixing.","w":"\"How do you feel most misunderstood?\" — only when the relationship can hold it.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V9","d":{"lines":[{"say":"How do you feel most misunderstood?","how":"","why":"Power question #8. Deep, so save it for someone who trusts you."},{"say":"What do people most often get wrong about you?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3","Conversation, Level 3"],"rel":["v57"],"status":"USED","how":["A Level 3 power question for relationships that can hold it. It shows you want to know the real person, not the polished one."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “How do you feel …”","a":"“How do you feel most misunderstood?”"},{"id":"v10","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Power Q9: if you weren't afraid","mv":"\"What would you do if you weren't afraid?\"","tr":"If they share something big, respond with belief, not a reality check.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V10","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What would you do if you weren't afraid?","how":"","why":"Power question #9: surfaces hidden dreams. Listen more than you advise."},{"say":"If you knew it would work out, what would you go for?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3","Conversation, Level 3"],"rel":["v56","v57"],"status":"USED","how":["The last of the nine power questions. It opens up hopes people rarely say out loud."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “What would you do if you …”","a":"“What would you do if you weren't afraid?”"},{"id":"v11","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Treasure hunt","mv":"Treat small talk as hunting for what lights them up; \"What do you like to do?\" beats \"What do you do?\"","tr":"Watch for the topic that makes them speed up or smile, then dig there.","w":"\"What do you like to do?\" beats \"What do you do?\" because it points at their interests rather than their job title.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V11","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What do you like to do when you're not working?","how":"","why":"Van Edwards: treat small talk as a hunt for what lights someone up. 'What do you like to do?' beats 'What do you do?'"},{"say":"What's usually the best part of your week?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Turn Small Talk into a Treasure Hunt\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"Turn Small Talk into a Treasure Hunt\""],"status":"USED","how":["Vanessa treats small talk as hunting for what lights someone up. \"What do you like to do?\" beats \"What do you do?\" because it points at their interests rather than their job title."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “What do you like …”","a":"“What do you like to do?”"},{"id":"v12","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Stories, not facts","mv":"Ask for the story behind a fact; positive-memory prompts (\"Do anything fun this weekend?\").","tr":"When they give a fact, ask \"How did that happen?\" or \"What was that like?\"","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V12","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Do anything fun at the weekend?","how":"","why":"Facts are dead ends; stories are doorways. Positive-memory prompts get better answers."},{"say":"What's the story behind that?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Ask for Stories, Not Facts\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"Ask for Stories, Not Facts\""],"rel":["v27"],"status":"USED","how":["Ask for the story behind a fact. \"Do anything fun this weekend?\" pulls up a good memory, which feels better to share than a list of facts."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Stories","note":"From preparing your own stories, to asking for theirs, to following the story they tell about their life.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Stories for trigger topics with a hook, struggle and vivid words; then boomerang back to them.","card":"v72"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Ask for the story behind a fact; positive-memory prompts (\"Do anything fun this weekend?\").","card":"v12"},{"label":"Deeper","when":"Conversation · 2026, Level 3","text":"When they reveal how they see their life, follow up: \"What did that teach you?\"","card":"v51"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “Do anything fun …”","a":"“Do anything fun this weekend?”"},{"id":"v13","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Magic phrase: \"I was just thinking of you\"","mv":"When someone crosses your mind, tell them.","tr":"Send it the moment it happens — a text is enough.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V13","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I was just thinking about you. Anything new and exciting?","how":"","why":"Magic phrase #1. Everyone likes being remembered. When someone crosses your mind, tell them."},{"say":"Saw this and thought of you straight away.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Five Magic Phrases\" #1","Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 1"],"rel":["n-vve-magic-phrases","v43"],"status":"USED","how":["The first magic phrase. When someone genuinely crosses your mind, tell them. It's a small, true signal that they matter to you."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “I was just thinking …”","a":"“I was just thinking of you”"},{"id":"v14","t":"vve","tp":"listening","ti":"Magic phrase: \"Tell me more?\"","mv":"Look for moments you can honestly say it.","tr":"Say it with a slight lift and real curiosity, then stay quiet.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Moderate (Huang 2017)","code":"V14","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Tell me more?","how":"","why":"Magic phrase #2. Follow-up prompts raise liking (Huang et al., 2017)."},{"say":"Tell me more about that. I want to understand.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 #2","Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 2","Huang et al. 2017, as cited in the handbook"],"rel":["n-vve-magic-phrases"],"status":"USED","how":["It invites more of what the person cares about, makes them feel heard, and buys you time. Research on follow-up questions backs it: people who ask more follow-ups are liked more."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Magic phrase: \"Tell me more?\"”?","a":"Look for moments you can honestly say it."},{"id":"v15","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Magic phrase: \"Last time you mentioned…\"","mv":"Listen to remember; call back a detail.","tr":"Jot down one detail after a conversation so you can bring it up later.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V15","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Last time we spoke you'd just started the new job. How's it going?","how":"","why":"Magic phrase #3: listen to remember, then call it back. It proves they mattered."},{"say":"You said earlier your mum's been unwell. How is she doing?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 #3","Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 3"],"rel":["n-vve-magic-phrases"],"status":"USED","how":["The third magic phrase. Listen to remember, then call back a detail next time. It proves you were really listening."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Remembering people","note":"From remembering names to remembering what they told you, and calling it back.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Hear it, repeat it, anchor it to someone you know.","card":"v63"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Listen to remember; call back a detail.","card":"v15"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “Last time …”","a":"“Last time you mentioned…”"},{"id":"v16","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Magic phrase: \"Same here!\"","mv":"Spot shared facts and feelings and say so.","tr":"Look for honest moments to use each one this week.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Moderate (similarity-attraction)","code":"V16","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Same here! I thought I was the only one.","how":"","why":"Magic phrase #4: spot shared facts and feelings and say so. Similarity draws people together."},{"say":"No way, me too. How did you get into it?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 #4","Conversation, Level 1 \"Five Magic Phrases\""],"status":"USED","how":["Vanessa's five short phrases that build warmth: \"I was just thinking of you\", \"Tell me more?\", \"Last time you mentioned…\", \"Same here!\" and \"You're so ___!\" Each one only works when it's true."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Finding common ground","note":"Hunting for three things in common became one quick phrase you say out loud the moment it's true.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Find three commonalities in three minutes, ask \"why?\" in different ways, offer help.","card":"v66"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Spot shared facts and feelings and say so.","card":"v16"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Magic phrase: \"Same here!\"”?","a":"Spot shared facts and feelings and say so."},{"id":"v17","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Magic phrase: \"You're so ___!\"","mv":"Be easily impressed and say it — awe out loud.","tr":"Make it specific to something they just did.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V17","d":{"lines":[{"say":"You're so good at explaining things.","how":"","why":"Magic phrase #5: be easily impressed, and say it out loud."},{"say":"Honestly, you're so calm about it. I'd be a wreck.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 #5","Conversation, Level 1 magic phrase 5"],"rel":["n-vve-compliments"],"status":"USED","how":["The fifth magic phrase: be easily impressed and say it out loud. \"You're so organised!\" tells someone what you noticed and admire."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Magic phrase: \"You're so ___!\"”?","a":"Be easily impressed and say it — awe out loud."},{"id":"v18","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Priming words","mv":"Choose feeling words: \"partner\", \"collaborate\"; open with a short positive primer (\"Wonderful to connect\").","tr":"Plan your first sentence before you walk in so it's warm and specific.","w":"Vanessa suggests opening with a short positive primer like \"Wonderful to connect\" instead of the forgettable \"Nice to meet you\", and choosing warm, team words like \"partner\" and \"collaborate\".","ev":"Moderate (priming lit., mixed replication)","code":"V18","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Wonderful to see you. I've been looking forward to this.","how":"","why":"Van Edwards: your first words shape how people treat you. Warm words like 'partner' and 'together' prime trust."},{"say":"Can we work on this together?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Ten Seconds to a Memorable First Impression\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"Ten Seconds to a Memorable First Impression\""],"status":"USED","how":["Vanessa suggests opening with a short positive primer like \"Wonderful to connect\" instead of the forgettable \"Nice to meet you\", and choosing warm, team words like \"partner\" and \"collaborate\"."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"First impressions","note":"From three body basics, to a full kit of charisma cues, to choosing your very first words.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Visible hands, Launch Stance, 60–70% eye contact (illustrative figure). Challenge: handshake audit.","card":"v61"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Cues · 2022","text":"Lean in, nothing blocking you, toes/torso/top facing them, mind personal space, purposeful gaze.","card":"v80"},{"label":"Latest","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Choose feeling words: \"partner\", \"collaborate\"; open with a short positive primer (\"Wonderful to connect\").","card":"v18"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “; open with a short positive …”","a":"“; open with a short positive primer (”"},{"id":"v19","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Break the social script","mv":"Swap autopilot lines for ones that need a real answer.","tr":"Pick one autopilot line you use and replace it this week.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V19","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What's been the highlight of your day so far?","how":"","why":"Autopilot questions get autopilot answers. A slightly unexpected question wakes the conversation up."},{"say":"What's the best thing that's happened to you this week?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"The Weirdest, Best Party\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"The Weirdest, Best Party\""],"rel":["v32"],"status":"USED","how":["Most small talk runs on autopilot (\"How are you?\" \"Good, you?\"). Vanessa says swap in a line that needs a real answer, like \"What was the highlight of your day?\""],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Break the social script”?","a":"Swap autopilot lines for ones that need a real answer."},{"id":"v20","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Affirm, safety, shared mission","mv":"In high-stakes talks, appreciate, create a micro-climate of safety, name the shared mission; ask for advice to look competent.","tr":"Open with one honest appreciation and \"I think we both want…\".","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V20","d":{"lines":[{"say":"We both want this to go well, so let's work it out together.","how":"","why":"For high-stakes talks: appreciate them, make it safe, and name what you both want. Asking advice also makes you look more competent (Brooks et al., 2015)."},{"say":"I value how much you care about this. What would you suggest we do?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"How to Handle High-Stakes Conversations\" (Brooks 2015)","Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Handle High-Stakes Conversations\""],"rel":["v74"],"status":"USED","how":["In a high-stakes talk, Vanessa says appreciate the other person, create a small pocket of safety, and name the mission you share. Asking for advice can also make you look more competent, not less."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Asking for advice","note":"Asking for advice moved from a liking trick into high-stakes talks, alongside appreciation, safety and a shared mission.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Ask for advice; admit a weakness.","card":"v74"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"In high-stakes talks, appreciate, create a micro-climate of safety, name the shared mission; ask for advice to look competent.","card":"v20"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Affirm, safety, shared mission”?","a":"In high-stakes talks, appreciate, create a micro-climate of safety, name the shared mission; ask for advice to look competent."},{"id":"v21","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Context cues","mv":"Use the shared setting (\"How do you know the host?\").","tr":"Scan the room for one shared thing before you start.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V21","d":{"lines":[{"say":"How do you know everyone here?","how":"","why":"Use what you share right now, the place or the host, as an easy opening."},{"say":"What brought you along tonight?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Never Run Out of Things to Say\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"Never Run Out of Things to Say\""],"status":"USED","how":["When you run dry, use what's around you: the setting, the host, the event. \"How do you know the host?\" works almost anywhere."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “How do you know …”","a":"“How do you know the host?”"},{"id":"v22","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Answer \"How are you?\" with a story","mv":"Be funny or story-ready, then turn it back: \"What's been good with you?\"","tr":"Keep one light, true story from your week ready to share.","w":"Instead of \"Good, you?\", have a small story or a fun answer ready, then turn it back with something better than the script, like \"What's been good with you?\"","ev":"Plausible","code":"V22","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Better now I'm talking to you. What's been good with you?","how":"","why":"Turn 'How are you?' into a spark: answer with a little story or warmth, then ask for something good."},{"say":"Honestly? A bit frazzled, but good. What's been good for you lately?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"How to Answer 'How Are You?'\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Answer 'How Are You?'\""],"rel":["v19","v32"],"status":"USED","how":["Instead of \"Good, you?\", have a small story or a fun answer ready, then turn it back with something better than the script, like \"What's been good with you?\""],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Answer \"How are you?\" with a story”?","a":"Be funny or story-ready, then turn it back: \"What's been good with you?\""},{"id":"v23","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Logline for \"What do you do?\"","mv":"A short, hooky one-liner plus a hook back to them.","tr":"Write one sentence that a ten-year-old would find interesting, then add \"How about you?\"","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V23","d":{"src":["Conversation L1 \"How to Answer 'What Do You Do?'\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Answer 'What Do You Do?'\""],"rel":["v2"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Have a short, hooky one-liner about your work, and end with a hook back to them so the conversation continues."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Answering and asking \"What do you do?\"","note":"First she fixed your answer with a logline; in 2026 she also fixes the question itself.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"A short, hooky one-liner plus a hook back to them.","card":"v23"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Learning Leader interview · Oct 2026","text":"Swap \"What do you do?\" for \"What do you like to do?\". Vanessa found it in a veteran TV interviewer's memoir: one word turns a job report into a topic people enjoy.","card":"n-vve-m-like-to-do"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"Finish the line: “What do …”","a":"“What do you do?”"},{"id":"v24","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Signal liking (\"I like you\")","mv":"We like people who like us; your signals are weaker than you think — amplify them.","tr":"Say what you enjoyed — \"I really enjoyed that\" — when it's true.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Strong (liking gap)","code":"V24","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I really like you. Let's do this again.","how":"","why":"We like people who like us, and we underestimate how much others like us (Boothby et al., 2018). Make your liking obvious."},{"say":"I always enjoy talking to you.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"How to Create a Magnetic Presence\" (Boothby 2018)","Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Create a Magnetic Presence\"","Boothby et al. 2018, as cited in the handbook"],"rel":["v77","n-all-liking-gap"],"status":"USED","how":["We like people who like us, and your signals are weaker than you think. Research on the \"liking gap\" shows people usually liked you more than you guessed, so amplify your warmth a little."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Showing you like people","note":"Attunement became a simpler instruction: your liking signals are weaker than you think, so turn them up.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Reciprocal liking, belonging, curiosity. Challenge: tell one person you enjoy their company.","card":"v77"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"We like people who like us; your signals are weaker than you think — amplify them.","card":"v24"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Signal liking (\"I like you\")”?","a":"Say what you enjoyed — \"I really enjoyed that\" — when it's true."},{"id":"v25","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Prepare your topics","mv":"Arrive with a few fresh questions and stories.","tr":"Before an event, note two questions and one recent story.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V25","d":{"src":["Conversation L1 \"How to Be Interesting\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Be Interesting\""],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Interesting people arrive prepared: a few fresh questions and a couple of stories they're ready to tell."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Prepare your topics”?","a":"Arrive with a few fresh questions and stories."},{"id":"v26","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"\"Can I guess?\" (introverts)","mv":"Playful guessing invites quieter people in.","tr":"Keep it light and let them correct you.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Coach's view","code":"V26","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Wait, let me guess. Teacher?","how":"","why":"Playful guessing gives quieter people an easy way in."},{"say":"Can I have a guess where you're going on holiday?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"How to Talk to Introverts\" (Gladwell)","Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Talk to Introverts\""],"rel":["v27"],"status":"USED","how":["Playful guessing (\"Can I guess what you do?\") invites quieter people in without putting them on the spot."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “\"Can I guess?\" (introverts)”?","a":"Playful guessing invites quieter people in."},{"id":"v27","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Numbers, then story","mv":"Start with easy number questions, then build to a story (\"How long have you lived here?\").","tr":"Follow a number answer with \"What brought you here?\"","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Coach's view","code":"V27","d":{"lines":[{"say":"How long have you lived round here?","how":"","why":"Easy number questions are simple to answer and lead naturally to stories."},{"say":"How long have you two been together? Where did you meet?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"How to Encourage People to Open Up\" (Seinfeld)","Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Encourage People to Open Up\""],"status":"USED","how":["Start with easy number questions (\"How long have you lived here?\"), then build towards a story. Small, easy answers warm people up for bigger ones."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “How long have you …”","a":"“How long have you lived here?”"},{"id":"v28","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Don't yuck my yums","mv":"Meet someone's enthusiasm with curiosity, not a wince.","tr":"Ask \"What do you love about it?\"","w":"Meet someone's enthusiasm with curiosity, not a wince.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V28","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I'd never have thought of that. What made you try it?","how":"","why":"Meet someone's enthusiasm with curiosity, not a wince. You get to share their joy."},{"say":"I love that you're into it. How did it start?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Don't Yuck My Yums\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"Don't Yuck My Yums\""],"rel":["v38","v37"],"status":"USED","how":["When someone is excited about something, meet it with curiosity instead of a wince, even if it's not your thing."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Don't yuck my yums”?","a":"Meet someone's enthusiasm with curiosity, not a wince."},{"id":"v29","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Graceful topic transition","mv":"\"Totally shifting gears for a second, can I ask about…?\"","tr":"Use it when a topic has run dry rather than forcing it.","w":"Signal the switch openly: \"Totally shifting gears for a second, can I ask about…?\" It feels smooth instead of abrupt.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V29","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Totally shifting gears for a second, can I ask you about something?","how":"","why":"Transitions rescue a flat conversation. Signal the switch out loud."},{"say":"Before we wrap up, could we go back to what you mentioned earlier?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Talk About Shark Attacks\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"Talk About Shark Attacks\""],"rel":["v21"],"status":"USED","how":["Signal the switch openly: \"Totally shifting gears for a second, can I ask about…?\" It feels smooth instead of abrupt."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “Totally shifting gears for a second, can …”","a":"“Totally shifting gears for a second, can I ask about…?”"},{"id":"v30","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Good-weather only","mv":"Mention weather only when it is good, and build on it.","tr":"\"Isn't this sunshine great? Got anything fun planned for it?\"","w":"Mention weather only when it is good, and build on it.","ev":"Moderate (Rind 1996)","code":"V30","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Isn't this weather glorious? Are you making the most of it?","how":"","why":"Weather talk works only when it's good: positive forecasts lifted tips by about 19% (Rind, 1996)."},{"say":"What a gorgeous day. Got anything nice planned?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Should I Ask About the Weather?\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"Should I Ask About the Weather?\""],"rel":["v32"],"status":"USED","how":["Vanessa says mention the weather only when it's good, and build on it. Bad-weather talk tends to drag the mood down."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Good-weather only”?","a":"\"Isn't this sunshine great? Got anything fun planned for it?\""},{"id":"v31","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Never \"Been busy?\"","mv":"It primes stress; ask for good instead.","tr":"Swap it for \"What's been the best part of your week?\"","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V31","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What have you been enjoying recently?","how":"","why":"'Busy' makes people recall stress. Ask about enjoyment instead."},{"say":"What's been fun lately?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Don't (Ever) Ask This\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"Don't (Ever) Ask This\""],"rel":["v32","v19"],"status":"USED (as the swap)","how":["\"Been busy?\" primes people to think about stress. Ask about something good instead."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Never \"Been busy?\"”?","a":"It primes stress; ask for good instead."},{"id":"v32","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Ask for good","mv":"Open meetings and dinners with \"What's something good?\"","tr":"Go first with a small, real good thing.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Moderate (emotional contagion)","code":"V32","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Before we start, what's one good thing from your week?","how":"","why":"Moods are catching. Starting with something good sets a positive tone for everyone."},{"say":"What's something good that happened today?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Use This Icebreaker…\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"Use This Icebreaker…\""],"status":"USED","how":["Open meetings and dinners with \"What's something good?\" Emotions are contagious, so starting on a good note lifts the whole room."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Ask for good”?","a":"Open meetings and dinners with \"What's something good?\""},{"id":"v33","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Conversational confetti","mv":"Tease something interesting: \"Can I tell you something really weird?\"","tr":"Only tease what you can deliver — it must be genuinely interesting.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V33","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Can I tell you something really weird?","how":"","why":"Tease a fun fact before you share it. A little suspense makes people lean in."},{"say":"Want to hear something interesting I learned this week?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"A Trick from Master Conversationalists\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"A Trick from Master Conversationalists\""],"status":"USED","how":["Tease something interesting before you share it: \"Can I tell you something really weird?\" It sparks curiosity and gives the chat energy."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “Can I tell you something …”","a":"“Can I tell you something really weird?”"},{"id":"v34","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Be a mirror","mv":"Subtly reflect positive gestures and words. Don't mirror negative cues.","tr":"Let it happen naturally; if you notice yourself copying, stop.","w":"Don't mirror negative cues.","ev":"Strong (Chartrand & Bargh 1999)","code":"V34","d":{"src":["Conversation L1 \"Be a Mirror\"","Cues ch. 4","Conversation, Level 1 \"Be a Mirror\"","Chartrand & Bargh 1999, as cited in the handbook"],"rel":["v81"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Mirroring means subtly reflecting someone's positive gestures, posture or words. Vanessa says mirror the positive, never the negative, and keep it subtle — research on the \"chameleon effect\" found light mimicry raises liking, while obvious copying backfires."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Mirroring","note":"Mirroring the positive moved from a list of warmth cues to a stand-alone habit.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Cues · 2022","text":"Time your tilts; nod slowly in threes; brows up for interest; real smiles; light appropriate touch; mirror the positive.","card":"v81"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Subtly reflect positive gestures and words. Don't mirror negative cues.","card":"v34"}]}],"vb":"2022 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Be a mirror”?","a":"Subtly reflect positive gestures and words. Don't mirror negative cues."},{"id":"v35","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"At ease, not impressive","mv":"Drop the clever line; be relaxed and let them shine.","tr":"Make your goal \"help them feel at ease\" instead of \"impress them\".","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V35","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I'll be honest, I came over because you looked friendly.","how":"","why":"Van Edwards: you don't need a clever line. Being relaxed and warm lands better than being impressive."},{"say":"I've no clever opening line. I just wanted to say hello.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"Stop Trying to Impress People\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"Stop Trying to Impress People\""],"status":"USED","how":["Drop the clever line. Vanessa says people connect with someone relaxed who lets them shine, not with someone performing."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “At ease, not impressive”?","a":"Drop the clever line; be relaxed and let them shine."},{"id":"v36","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Graceful exit","mv":"Future cue → compliment + action → wave and go.","tr":"Leave on a high, not when the chat has fizzled.","w":"A big study found conversations almost never end when both people want them to, so ending on purpose is a kindness, not a rejection.","ev":"Moderate (Mastroianni 2021)","code":"V36","d":{"steps":["Future cue","compliment + action","wave and go"],"lines":[{"say":"What's the rest of your day looking like?","how":"","why":"Her exit formula: a cue about what's next, then a warm compliment plus an action. Leave on a high."},{"say":"It's been lovely talking to you. I'm going to grab a drink, but let's catch up soon.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"I'm so glad we caught up. Coffee next week?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L1 \"How to Gracefully End a Conversation\"","Conversation, Level 1 \"How to Gracefully End a Conversation\"","Mastroianni et al. 2021, as cited in the handbook"],"rel":["n-all-end-on-purpose"],"status":"USED","how":["Vanessa's three-step exit: mention something in the future, give a specific compliment plus your next action, then wave and go. \"Good luck with the launch next week. I'm off to grab a drink. Talk soon!\"","A big study found conversations almost never end when both people want them to, so ending on purpose is a kindness, not a rejection."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Graceful exit”?","a":"Leave on a high, not when the chat has fizzled."},{"id":"v37","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Dreambuilding","mv":"When someone shares a goal, get excited, bring ideas, check back later.","tr":"Ask a follow-up and offer one idea or contact that might help.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Strong (capitalisation research)","code":"V37","d":{"lines":[{"say":"That's a brilliant goal. How can I help?","how":"","why":"When someone shares a goal, get excited and help. Enthusiastic responses to good news build closeness (Gable et al., 2006)."},{"say":"I'll check in next month to see how it's going.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"What would the first step be?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"Uncovering Secret Dreams\" (Gable 2006)","Conversation, Level 2 \"Uncovering Secret Dreams\""],"rel":["v43"],"status":"USED","how":["When someone shares a goal or good news, get excited, bring ideas, and check back later. Research on responding to good news shows enthusiastic, active responses build relationships."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Dreambuilding”?","a":"When someone shares a goal, get excited, bring ideas, check back later."},{"id":"v38","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Curious about a dream you don't love","mv":"Ask what got them into it and where it's going instead of doubting.","tr":"Curiosity first; give your view only if they ask.","w":"Ask what got them into it and where it's going instead of doubting.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V38","d":{"lines":[{"say":"That's interesting. What got you into it?","how":"","why":"When you don't love their idea, ask how it started and where it's going instead of picking holes."},{"say":"What's been the most surprising part so far?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"What to Do When You Don't Like Their Dream\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"What to Do When You Don't Like Their Dream\""],"rel":["v28","n-vve-curious-critical"],"status":"USED","how":["Instead of doubting, get curious: ask what got them into it and where they see it going."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Curious about a dream you don't love”?","a":"Ask what got them into it and where it's going instead of doubting."},{"id":"v39","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Assertive curiosity + clear ask","mv":"Ask what/where/who/why; then claim a goal and an action: \"I need X. Can you Y?\"","tr":"Keep the request short and specific, then stop talking.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Moderate (assertiveness training)","code":"V39","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I can't take this on without delaying the other project. Which would you like me to prioritise?","how":"","why":"Van Edwards: assertiveness is a skill. Name what you need and the action you'd like."},{"say":"I need us to share this more evenly. Could you take Tuesdays?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Is there anything else you want to tell me before we finish?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"Be More Assertive\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"Be More Assertive\""],"status":"USED","how":["Ask what, where, who and why to understand, then claim a goal and an action: \"I need X. Can you do Y?\""]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “I need X. Can …”","a":"“I need X. Can you Y?”"},{"id":"v40","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Tough feedback, warmly","mv":"Lead with belief in them, be specific, ask how you can help it not happen again.","tr":"One issue at a time, said calmly, then listen.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V40","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I know how much you care about this work, so I want to be straight with you.","how":"","why":"Lead with belief in them, be specific, and ask how you can help it not happen again."},{"say":"What can I do to help this not happen again?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"How to Deliver Tough Feedback\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Deliver Tough Feedback\""],"status":"USED","how":["Lead with your belief in them, be specific about what happened, then ask how you can help it not happen again."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Tough feedback, warmly”?","a":"Lead with belief in them, be specific, ask how you can help it not happen again."},{"id":"v41","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Calm by naming, not suppressing","mv":"Never \"calm down\"; acknowledge the feeling, ask \"Can you tell me more about that?\"","tr":"Name it as a question (\"Sounds frustrating?\") and slow your own voice.","w":"Never \"calm down\"; acknowledge the feeling, ask \"Can you tell me more about that?\"","ev":"Strong (suppression backfires; Lieberman 2007)","code":"V41","d":{"lines":[{"say":"It sounds like you're really upset. Can you tell me more?","how":"","why":"Never say 'calm down'. Hiding feelings makes them worse; naming them helps (Lieberman et al., 2007)."},{"say":"Of course you're frustrated. Anyone would be.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"How to Calm Someone Down\" (Campbell-Sills 2006)","Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Calm Someone Down\"","Lieberman et al. 2007, as cited in the handbook"],"status":"USED","how":["Never say \"calm down\". Vanessa says acknowledge the feeling, then ask \"Can you tell me more about that?\" Research shows naming a feeling can lower its intensity, while suppressing it backfires."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “; acknowledge the …”","a":"“; acknowledge the feeling, ask ”"},{"id":"v42","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Talk to VIPs at Level 2","mv":"Skip the fan compliment; ask for their take on something real.","tr":"\"What's your read on where this is heading?\" — then listen properly.","w":"Skip the fan compliment; ask for their take on something real.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V42","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Can I get your take on something I'm stuck on?","how":"","why":"With bosses and VIPs, skip the fan compliment. Asking for their view on something real connects better."},{"say":"What's the craziest thing that happened on that project?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"How to Talk to Your Boss (or VIPs)\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Talk to Your Boss (or VIPs)\""],"status":"USED","how":["Skip the fan compliment. Talk to VIPs at Level 2: ask for their take on something real."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Talk to VIPs at Level 2”?","a":"\"What's your read on where this is heading?\" — then listen properly."},{"id":"v43","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Pebbling (be a penguin)","mv":"Send small \"this made me think of you\" pebbles; follow up on what they told you.","tr":"Send one pebble this week to someone you've been meaning to contact.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V43","d":{"lines":[{"say":"You mentioned you had a big presentation. How did it go?","how":"","why":"Like penguins with pebbles: small 'thinking of you' gestures and follow-ups show you remember."},{"say":"I'll send you that podcast I mentioned.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"Be a Penguin\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"Be a Penguin\""],"status":"USED","how":["Penguins give pebbles as small gifts. Vanessa says send small \"this made me think of you\" pebbles — a link, a photo, a quick follow-up on something they told you."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “this made me think …”","a":"“this made me think of you”"},{"id":"v44","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Pet and pest projects","mv":"Ask about the project they love and the hassle they're living with; ask if they want support or just a rant.","tr":"\"Do you want ideas, or do you just want to vent?\"","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V44","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Do you want help with it, or just a good rant?","how":"","why":"Ask about the project they love and the hassle they're living with, then ask what kind of support they want."},{"say":"What are you tinkering with in your spare time?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"Discover Hidden Motivations\", \"Bond Over Pest Projects\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"Discover Hidden Motivations\" and \"Bond Over Pest Projects\""],"status":"USED","how":["Ask about the project they love (pet) and the hassle they're living with (pest). For a pest, ask whether they want support or just a good rant."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Pet and pest projects”?","a":"Ask about the project they love and the hassle they're living with; ask if they want support or just a rant."},{"id":"v45","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Laughter, self-deprecation, inside jokes","mv":"Shared laughs are relationship glue; be your own butt; call back a shared joke.","tr":"Laugh at yourself lightly; never at them.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V45","d":{"steps":["Shared laughs are relationship glue","be your own butt","call back a shared joke"],"lines":[{"say":"Well, that's my one good joke used up.","how":"","why":"Shared laughter is relationship glue. Laughing at yourself is the easiest, safest kind."},{"say":"I'm terrible with names and I've had two coffees. Remind me yours?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"That's going to be our running joke now.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"The Easiest Way to Be Funny\", \"Be Your Own Butt\", \"The Inside Path to Being Cool\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"The Easiest Way to Be Funny\", \"Be Your Own Butt\""],"status":"USED","how":["Shared laughs are relationship glue. Vanessa says the easiest way to be funny is to be your own butt — gently — and to call back a shared joke later."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Name the 3 steps of “Laughter, self-deprecation, inside jokes”.","a":"Shared laughs are relationship glue → be your own butt → call back a shared joke"},{"id":"v46","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Simple, constructive language","mv":"Short words; \"I'm confident that…\"; frame what you want, not what's wrong.","tr":"Cut long words when a short one will do.","w":"Vanessa also suggests \"I'm confident that…\" and framing what you want rather than what's wrong.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V46","d":{"steps":["Short words","\"I'm confident that…\"","frame what you want, not what's wrong"],"lines":[{"say":"I'm confident we can fix this by Friday.","how":"","why":"Short words and 'I'm confident that…' sound smarter than jargon (Oppenheimer, 2006)."},{"say":"Here's what I suggest we do next.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"Speak with Confidence\" (Oppenheimer 2006)","Conversation, Level 2 \"Speak with Confidence\""],"rel":["v85"],"status":"USED","how":["Short, simple words make you sound smarter, not less. Vanessa also suggests \"I'm confident that…\" and framing what you want rather than what's wrong."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Simple, constructive language”?","a":"Short words; \"I'm confident that…\"; frame what you want, not what's wrong."},{"id":"v47","t":"vve","tp":"listening","ti":"Verbal catch (\"How about you?\")","mv":"Take turns; end your bit by handing the ball back.","tr":"If you've talked for over a minute, toss the ball back.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V47","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Anyway, enough about me. How about you?","how":"","why":"Conversation is a game of catch. End your turn by handing the ball back."},{"say":"That's how I ended up in this job. What got you started in yours?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"Make Sure You Don't Talk Too Much\"","Conversation, Level 2 \"Make Sure You Don't Talk Too Much\""],"status":"USED","how":["Treat conversation like a game of catch. After your bit, hand the ball back with \"How about you?\""],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Verbal catch (\"How about you?\")”?","a":"Take turns; end your bit by handing the ball back."},{"id":"v48","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Be a connector","mv":"Introduce people around a shared keyword.","tr":"Give each person one interesting fact about the other.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V48","d":{"lines":[{"say":"You two have to meet. You both love climbing.","how":"","why":"Introducing people over a shared interest makes both of them like you more."},{"say":"I'll introduce you to Jo. I think you'd really get on.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"How to Be a Connector\"","Captivate ch. 4","Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Be a Connector\""],"status":"USED","how":["Introduce people around a shared keyword: \"You both love climbing!\" It makes you memorable and makes them feel looked after."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Introducing people","note":"Raving introductions grew into being the person who connects people around a shared interest.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Introduce people by their best qualities; tell people what impresses you.","card":"v64"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Introduce people around a shared keyword.","card":"v48"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Be a connector”?","a":"Give each person one interesting fact about the other."},{"id":"v49","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Make friends: social hours, shared contexts, friendship tests","mv":"Schedule regular time, build shared contexts, try small invitations.","tr":"Pick one regular thing and show up every week.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V49","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I go every Thursday. Fancy coming along?","how":"","why":"Friendships need time and shared settings (Hall, 2019). Small invitations test the water."},{"say":"We should make this a regular thing.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L2 \"How to Make Friends\" (Hall 2019)","Conversation, Level 2 \"How to Make Friends\""],"rel":["v43"],"status":"USED","how":["Vanessa's approach: schedule regular social hours, build shared contexts (a class, a club, a regular spot), and try small \"friendship tests\" like a low-key invitation."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Making and keeping friends","note":"Making friends was about social hours and shared places; by 2025 she adds a first step: sort who fuels you before you add more.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate and Conversation","text":"Schedule regular time, build shared contexts, try small invitations.","card":"v49"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Dan Buettner Podcast · 2025","text":"Make a private list in three columns: the people who give you energy (the ones you would happily cook for tonight), the people who drain you, and the ambivalent middle you leave feeling \"meh\".","card":"n-vve-m-relationship-audit"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Make friends: social hours, shared contexts, friendship tests”?","a":"Schedule regular time, build shared contexts, try small invitations."},{"id":"v50","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Courage to be disliked","mv":"Not everyone will click; that's fine.","tr":"Treat a flat conversation as information, not a verdict.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","code":"V50","d":{"src":["Conversation L2","Conversation, Level 2"],"rel":["n-all-liking-gap"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Not everyone will click with you, and that's fine. Accepting it frees you to be yourself with the people who do."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Not everyone will like you","note":"An early food picture for the same idea.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"YouTube · Nov 2022","text":"Food doesn't take it personally when someone hates coriander. We all have different flavours, and not clicking with someone isn't a verdict on you.","card":null},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Not everyone will click; that's fine.","card":"v50"}]}],"vb":"2022 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Courage to be disliked”?","a":"Not everyone will click; that's fine."},{"id":"v51","t":"vve","tp":"listening","ti":"Follow narrative hints","mv":"When they reveal how they see their life, follow up: \"What did that teach you?\"","tr":"Listen for \"always\", \"never\" and \"I'm the kind of person who…\".","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Moderate (McAdams)","code":"V51","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What did that experience teach you?","how":"","why":"Level 3: when someone hints at how they see their life, follow it gently."},{"say":"How do you think that shaped who you are now?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3 \"What's Your Story?\", \"How to Listen\"","Conversation, Level 3 \"What's Your Story?\" and \"How to Listen\""],"rel":["v52","v58"],"status":"USED","how":["When someone hints at how they see their life (\"I've always been the responsible one\"), follow it: \"What did that teach you?\""],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Stories","note":"From preparing your own stories, to asking for theirs, to following the story they tell about their life.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Stories for trigger topics with a hook, struggle and vivid words; then boomerang back to them.","card":"v72"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Ask for the story behind a fact; positive-memory prompts (\"Do anything fun this weekend?\").","card":"v12"},{"label":"Deeper","when":"Conversation · 2026, Level 3","text":"When they reveal how they see their life, follow up: \"What did that teach you?\"","card":"v51"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “What did that …”","a":"“What did that teach you?”"},{"id":"v52","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Five self-story archetypes","mv":"Ambitious hero, contented pragmatist, selfless helper, creative disruptor, unlucky struggler — respond to each. Colour, not a label.","tr":"Never name the type out loud; just respond to the story they're telling.","w":"USED (responses only; never name the type)","ev":"Plausible","code":"V52","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What's the next mountain you're climbing?","how":"","why":"Van Edwards' five self-story types each want something different: a next challenge, support, or a moment for themselves. Respond, never label."},{"say":"That sounds really tough. How did you get through it?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"You give so much. What's something you keep just for yourself?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3 \"Know Who You're Dealing With\"","Conversation, Level 3 \"Know Who You're Dealing With\""],"rel":["v51"],"status":"USED (responses only; never name the type)","how":["Vanessa describes five ways people tell their life story: the ambitious hero, contented pragmatist, selfless helper, creative disruptor and unlucky struggler."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Reading personality","note":"From the Big Five to the five stories people tell about themselves. Both are colour, never labels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Notice openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, neuroticism; adapt.","card":"v69"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Ambitious hero, contented pragmatist, selfless helper, creative disruptor, unlucky struggler — respond to each. Colour, not a label.","card":"v52"}]},{"title":"Self-stories","note":"From three story types in 2024, to five in the book, to the idea that stories can be rewritten.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"BigDeal interview · 2024","text":"She described three self-stories from new research: someone who overcomes, someone who gives to everyone, and someone who feels nothing they do changes things.","card":null},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Ambitious hero, contented pragmatist, selfless helper, creative disruptor, unlucky struggler — respond to each. Colour, not a label.","card":"v52"},{"label":"Latest","when":"Learning Leader interview · Oct 2026","text":"She adds that self-stories are editable and tend to come true: people who see themselves as lucky spot more chances. Helping someone rewrite their story is the deepest goal of conversation.","card":null}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Five self-story archetypes”?","a":"Ambitious hero, contented pragmatist, selfless helper, creative disruptor, unlucky struggler — respond to each. Colour, not a label."},{"id":"v53","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"\"Are you lucky?\"","mv":"One question that shows optimism and sense of control.","tr":"Ask it playfully, then follow up on their reason.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Moderate (Wiseman)","code":"V53","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Do you think of yourself as lucky?","how":"","why":"One light question that hints at how in control and optimistic someone feels."},{"say":"What's the luckiest thing that's ever happened to you?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3 \"The One Question…\"","Conversation, Level 3 \"The One Question…\""],"rel":["v54","v52"],"status":"USED","how":["One question that reveals optimism and how much control someone feels over their life."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “\"Are you lucky?\"”?","a":"One question that shows optimism and sense of control."},{"id":"v54","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Money narrative","mv":"\"When do you feel rich?\" — only with people close enough.","tr":"Share your own answer first.","w":"\"When do you feel rich?\" — only with people close enough.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V54","d":{"lines":[{"say":"When do you feel rich?","how":"","why":"Money questions reveal values. Keep them for people you're close to."},{"say":"What's your earliest money memory?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3 \"A Question That Might Lead to Divorce\"","Conversation, Level 3 \"A Question That Might Lead to Divorce\""],"rel":["v53"],"status":"USED","how":["\"When do you feel rich?\" opens up how someone thinks about money. Only for people close enough to hold it."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “When do you …”","a":"“When do you feel rich?”"},{"id":"v55","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Awe","mv":"Be ready to be awed by their story; \"Everything in my past is useful.\"","tr":"Listen as if their story could surprise you — it often will.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V55","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Wow. I'd love to hear the story behind that.","how":"","why":"Be ready to be amazed by people's stories. Awe makes people feel truly seen."},{"say":"That's incredible. How did you even manage that?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3 \"The Single Most Important Connection Ingredient\"","Conversation, Level 3 \"The Single Most Important Connection Ingredient\""],"rel":["v56"],"status":"USED","how":["Vanessa calls awe the most important connection ingredient: be ready to be amazed by someone's story."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “Everything in my past …”","a":"“Everything in my past is useful.”"},{"id":"v56","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"\"I believe in you\"","mv":"Four words that fuel courage, said with conviction.","tr":"Say it slowly and mean it; don't add \"but\".","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V56","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I believe in you.","how":"","why":"Four words that fuel courage, said with conviction."},{"say":"You can absolutely do this. I believe in you.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3 \"Four Words That Fuel Courage\"","Conversation, Level 3 \"Four Words That Fuel Courage\""],"rel":["v57"],"status":"USED","how":["Four words that fuel courage. Said with conviction, they can help someone take a step they're scared of."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “I believe …”","a":"“I believe in you”"},{"id":"v57","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Honour fears and desires","mv":"Make it safe to say what scares them; offer support.","tr":"Thank them for telling you before you say anything else.","w":"Make it safe for people to say what scares them and what they secretly want, then offer support rather than judgment.","ev":"Moderate (Pennebaker)","code":"V57","d":{"lines":[{"say":"That sounds scary. What worries you most about it?","how":"","why":"Talking about fears is freeing (Pennebaker). Make it safe, then offer support."},{"say":"Whatever happens, I'm here if you want to talk it through.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"What did you fail at this week? I'll go first.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3 \"Hidden Fears and Secret Desires\"","Conversation, Level 3 \"Hidden Fears and Secret Desires\""],"rel":["v56"],"status":"USED","how":["Make it safe for people to say what scares them and what they secretly want, then offer support rather than judgment."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Honour fears and desires”?","a":"Thank them for telling you before you say anything else."},{"id":"v58","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Help change the story","mv":"Help them see and reframe their narrative.","tr":"Ask \"Is there another way to read that?\" rather than arguing.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V58","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Maybe it's less 'I'm bad at this' and more 'I'm still learning it'.","how":"","why":"Help someone see their story and choose a kinder version of it."},{"say":"Who do you want to be a year from now?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Conversation L3 \"How to Change the Story\"","Conversation, Level 3 \"How to Change the Story\""],"rel":["v51"],"status":"USED","how":["Help someone see the story they tell about themselves and find a kinder, truer version."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Help change the story”?","a":"Help them see and reframe their narrative."},{"id":"v59","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Allow yourself to be known","mv":"Answer deep questions honestly, not on autopilot.","tr":"Next time someone asks a real question, give a real answer.","w":"Answer deep questions honestly instead of on autopilot.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V59","d":{"src":["Conversation L3 \"Allow Yourself to Be Known\"","Conversation, Level 3 \"Allow Yourself to Be Known\""],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Connection runs both ways. Answer deep questions honestly instead of on autopilot."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Allow yourself to be known”?","a":"Answer deep questions honestly instead of on autopilot."},{"id":"v60","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Social Game Plan","mv":"Go where you thrive (thrive/neutral/survive spots), work the social/side/start zones, bring a Winger, pick a Riser. Challenge: say no to one dreaded event.","tr":"At your next event, find the social zone instead of hovering by the door.","w":"Captivate's first hack: go where you thrive, skip what you dread, and plan your position in the room.","ev":"Coach's view","code":"V60","d":{"src":["Captivate ch. 1"],"rel":["v49"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Captivate's first hack: go where you thrive, skip what you dread, and plan your position in the room. Bring a Winger (a friend on your social adventure) and pick a Riser (someone you want to level up with).","The challenge: say no to one event you dread."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Social Game Plan”?","a":"At your next event, find the social zone instead of hovering by the door."},{"id":"v61","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Triple Threat","mv":"Visible hands, Launch Stance, 60–70% eye contact (illustrative figure). Challenge: handshake audit.","tr":"Before you walk in, check: hands out of pockets, shoulders down, chin up.","w":"The \"60–70% eye contact\" figure she uses is best treated as a rule of thumb, not a lab number.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V61","d":{"src":["Captivate ch. 2"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vanessa's three first-impression cues: visible hands, the Launch Stance (shoulders down and back, chin and chest up, space between arms and body) and good eye contact.","The \"60–70% eye contact\" figure she uses is best treated as a rule of thumb, not a lab number. Her challenge for this chapter is a handshake audit."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"First impressions","note":"From three body basics, to a full kit of charisma cues, to choosing your very first words.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Visible hands, Launch Stance, 60–70% eye contact (illustrative figure). Challenge: handshake audit.","card":"v61"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Cues · 2022","text":"Lean in, nothing blocking you, toes/torso/top facing them, mind personal space, purposeful gaze.","card":"v80"},{"label":"Latest","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Choose feeling words: \"partner\", \"collaborate\"; open with a short positive primer (\"Wonderful to connect\").","card":"v18"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Triple Threat”?","a":"Visible hands, Launch Stance, 60–70% eye contact (illustrative figure). Challenge: handshake audit."},{"id":"v62","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Conversation sparkers & hot buttons","mv":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","tr":"Replace one stale question (\"Where are you from?\") with a sparker (\"Working on anything exciting?\").","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V62","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What's the most interesting thing you've done this month?","how":"","why":"Captivate: novel questions light people up. Notice what makes them lean in and go there."},{"say":"You lit up when you mentioned that. Tell me about it.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 3"],"rel":["n-vve-power-questions","v19"],"status":"USED","how":["Conversation sparkers are fresh questions that get people out of autopilot. Hot buttons are the topics that make someone lean in — when you spot one, dig there."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Conversation sparkers & hot buttons”?","a":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there."},{"id":"v63","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Name Game","mv":"Hear it, repeat it, anchor it to someone you know.","tr":"Say the name back once, clearly, in the first sentence.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V63","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Sorry, could you say your name again? I want to get it right.","how":"","why":"Captivate: hear the name, say it back, link it to something. Names make people feel important."},{"say":"Maya, lovely. My cousin's called Maya.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 3 (pp. 68–69)"],"status":"USED","how":["Vanessa's three steps to remember a name: hear it (really listen), repeat it back (\"Mike, nice to meet you\"), and anchor it to someone you already know with the same name, picturing them together.","She calls it a fun way to gamify names and suggests teaching it to friends so you can help each other find anchors. Practise on a TV show: try the steps every time a new character appears."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Remembering people","note":"From remembering names to remembering what they told you, and calling it back.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Hear it, repeat it, anchor it to someone you know.","card":"v63"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Listen to remember; call back a detail.","card":"v15"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Name Game”?","a":"Say the name back once, clearly, in the first sentence."},{"id":"v64","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Highlighter (raving introductions)","mv":"Introduce people by their best qualities; tell people what impresses you.","tr":"Next time you introduce someone, add one thing that impresses you about them.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V64","d":{"lines":[{"say":"This is Ali. She's the one who turned our whole project round.","how":"","why":"Captivate: introduce people by their best qualities and tell them what impresses you."},{"say":"I've been telling everyone how good your talk was.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 4"],"rel":["v48","v65"],"status":"USED","how":["Introduce people by their best qualities (\"This is Sam — she just ran her first marathon\"). Vanessa says be a highlighter: point out what's great in others."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Introducing people","note":"Raving introductions grew into being the person who connects people around a shared interest.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Introduce people by their best qualities; tell people what impresses you.","card":"v64"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Introduce people around a shared keyword.","card":"v48"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Highlighter (raving introductions)”?","a":"Introduce people by their best qualities; tell people what impresses you."},{"id":"v65","t":"vve","tp":"ideas","ti":"Expect the best (Pygmalion)","mv":"Your expectations shape people; listen for the good. Challenge: a one-day vow of silence.","tr":"Before a meeting, decide one good thing you expect from the other person.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"Moderate (Rosenthal)","code":"V65","d":{"src":["Captivate ch. 4"],"rel":["n-all-assume-liked"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Your expectations of people shape how they behave around you. Listen for the good and you'll tend to bring it out."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Expecting the best","note":"Expecting the best of others became walking in assuming they already like you.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Your expectations shape people; listen for the good. Challenge: a one-day vow of silence.","card":"v65"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Vanessa's \"power of assumption\": walk in assuming people already like you a little more than you fear. The liking gap research suggests that's often true.","card":"n-all-assume-liked"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Expect the best (Pygmalion)”?","a":"Your expectations shape people; listen for the good. Challenge: a one-day vow of silence."},{"id":"v66","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Thread Theory: \"Me too\", Five Whys, ties","mv":"Find three commonalities in three minutes, ask \"why?\" in different ways, offer help.","tr":"When you hit a \"me too\", ask \"What got you into it?\"","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Moderate (similarity)","code":"V66","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Hang on, we've got loads in common.","how":"","why":"Captivate: find a few things in common, ask 'why' in different ways, and offer help where you can."},{"say":"Why did you choose that, out of interest?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"If you ever need a hand with it, give me a shout.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 5"],"rel":["v67"],"status":"USED","how":["Look for three things in common in the first three minutes, ask \"why?\" in different ways to go deeper (the five whys), and offer help where you can. Shared threads tie people together."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Finding common ground","note":"Hunting for three things in common became one quick phrase you say out loud the moment it's true.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Find three commonalities in three minutes, ask \"why?\" in different ways, offer help.","card":"v66"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Spot shared facts and feelings and say so.","card":"v16"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “, Five Whys, ties Find three commonalities in …”","a":"“, Five Whys, ties Find three commonalities in three minutes, ask ”"},{"id":"v67","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"\"Teach me?\"","mv":"When there's no \"me too\", ask them to teach you.","tr":"\"I know nothing about that — teach me?\"","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V67","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Teach me? I've always wanted to understand how that works.","how":"","why":"Captivate: when you've nothing in common, ask them to teach you. People love sharing what they know."},{"say":"I know nothing about wine. What should I be looking for?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 5"],"status":"USED","how":["When there's no \"me too\", Vanessa says ask them to teach you. People enjoy explaining what they know, and you learn something."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “\"Teach me?\"”?","a":"When there's no \"me too\", ask them to teach you."},{"id":"v68","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Decoder (seven microexpressions)","mv":"Notice brief flashes of the seven universal expressions and respond kindly to the feeling you see.","tr":"If you see a flash of a feeling, respond to it gently: \"Did that land oddly?\"","w":"A face only tells you a feeling. Respond to it; never treat it as proof of anything.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V68","d":{"src":["Captivate ch. 6"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Captivate's Decoder teaches the seven universal expressions (anger, contempt, happiness, fear, surprise, disgust, sadness)."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Reading faces","note":"From spotting seven expressions in others, to also checking the signals you send. Both say: respond to the feeling.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Notice brief flashes of the seven universal expressions and respond kindly to the feeling you see.","card":"v68"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Cues · 2022","text":"Distancing, self-comfort, blocking, shame, lip purse, resting face — notice in yourself; incongruence lowers trust.","card":"v84"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2022","q":"What’s the trap with “Decoder (seven microexpressions)”?","a":"A face only tells you a feeling. Respond to it; never treat it as proof of anything."},{"id":"v69","t":"vve","tp":"ideas","ti":"Speed-Read (Big Five matrix)","mv":"Notice openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, neuroticism; adapt.","tr":"Notice, adapt a little, and stay open to being wrong.","w":"It's a guide for adapting, not a way to label people.","ev":"Moderate (Big Five)","code":"V69","d":{"src":["Captivate ch. 7"],"rel":["v52"],"status":"DELIVERY (don't label people)","how":["Vanessa uses the Big Five traits to adapt how you talk: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and neuroticism. It's a guide for adapting, not a way to label people."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Reading personality","note":"From the Big Five to the five stories people tell about themselves. Both are colour, never labels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Notice openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, neuroticism; adapt.","card":"v69"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Ambitious hero, contented pragmatist, selfless helper, creative disruptor, unlucky struggler — respond to each. Colour, not a label.","card":"v52"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Speed-Read (Big Five matrix)”?","a":"Notice, adapt a little, and stay open to being wrong."},{"id":"v70","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Appreciation Matrix","mv":"Five appreciation languages (words, time, acts, gifts, touch); thank people their way.","tr":"Notice how someone thanks others — that's often how they like to be thanked.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V70","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Thank you for staying late. It made a real difference.","how":"","why":"Captivate: people feel thanked in different ways: words, time, help, small gifts. Thank them their way."},{"say":"Can I take you for lunch to say thanks?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"I've cleared my afternoon so we can do this properly.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 8 (after Chapman & White)"],"rel":["v71"],"status":"USED (words, time, acts)","how":["People feel appreciated in different ways: words, time, acts of service, gifts and (where appropriate) touch. Vanessa says thank people their way, not yours."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Appreciation Matrix”?","a":"Five appreciation languages (words, time, acts, gifts, touch); thank people their way."},{"id":"v71","t":"vve","tp":"ideas","ti":"Primary Value","mv":"Resources people trade: love, service, status, money, goods, information.","tr":"Ask yourself what this person seems to value most, then offer that sincerely.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V71","d":{"src":["Captivate ch. 9 (Foa)"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Based on Foa's resource theory, people trade six kinds of value: love, service, status, money, goods and information. Knowing what someone values helps you give in a way that means something to them."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Primary Value”?","a":"Resources people trade: love, service, status, money, goods, information."},{"id":"v72","t":"vve","tp":"stories","ti":"Story Stack + Boomerang","mv":"Stories for trigger topics with a hook, struggle and vivid words; then boomerang back to them.","tr":"Build a stack of three stories for the topics that come up most.","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V72","d":{"lines":[{"say":"That happened to me once too. What would you have done?","how":"","why":"Captivate: share a short story, then throw it back so they can tell theirs."},{"say":"Has anything like that ever happened to you?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 10"],"status":"USED","how":["Prepare stories for common topics, each with a hook, a struggle and vivid words. After you tell one, boomerang the topic back: \"Has that ever happened to you?\""]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Stories","note":"From preparing your own stories, to asking for theirs, to following the story they tell about their life.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Stories for trigger topics with a hook, struggle and vivid words; then boomerang back to them.","card":"v72"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Ask for the story behind a fact; positive-memory prompts (\"Do anything fun this weekend?\").","card":"v12"},{"label":"Deeper","when":"Conversation · 2026, Level 3","text":"When they reveal how they see their life, follow up: \"What did that teach you?\"","card":"v51"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Story Stack + Boomerang”?","a":"Build a stack of three stories for the topics that come up most."},{"id":"v73","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Own It! (buy-in, \"because\")","mv":"Share the mission, hand over ownership, give a reason with requests.","tr":"\"Could you take this on, because you know the client best?\"","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V73","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Would you like to take the lead on this? You know it better than anyone.","how":"","why":"Captivate: people support what they help build, and a reason makes requests easier to agree to (Langer, 1978)."},{"say":"Could we move the meeting, because I want to give this proper time?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 11 (Langer 1978)"],"status":"USED","how":["Share the mission, hand over real ownership, and give a reason when you ask for something. The word \"because\" with a real reason makes requests feel fair."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Own It! (buy-in, \"because\")”?","a":"Share the mission, hand over ownership, give a reason with requests."},{"id":"v74","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Franklin Effect (ask for advice, be anti-perfect)","mv":"Ask for advice; admit a weakness.","tr":"Ask for genuine advice on something you actually want help with.","w":"Showing a small human imperfection early helps people relax, because they stop waiting for the flaw.","ev":"Moderate (Brooks 2015; Franklin effect mixed)","code":"V74","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Can I ask your advice on something?","how":"","why":"Captivate's Franklin Effect: asking for advice and admitting a weakness makes people warm to you (Brooks et al., 2015)."},{"say":"You've done this before. What would you do in my shoes?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Full disclosure, I'm rubbish at this bit.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Have you read or watched anything lately you'd recommend?","how":"Relaxed and curious; follow up on their pick.","why":"Vanessa: with someone you don't click with, asking for their advice is an easy, warm opener."}],"src":["Captivate ch. 12"],"status":"USED","how":["Asking someone for advice tends to make them like you more, and admitting a small weakness makes you easier to warm to. Vanessa calls it being anti-perfect.","Showing a small human imperfection early helps people relax, because they stop waiting for the flaw."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Asking for advice","note":"Asking for advice moved from a liking trick into high-stakes talks, alongside appreciation, safety and a shared mission.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Ask for advice; admit a weakness.","card":"v74"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"In high-stakes talks, appreciate, create a micro-climate of safety, name the shared mission; ask for advice to look competent.","card":"v20"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"What’s the trap with “Franklin Effect (ask for advice, be anti-perfect)”?","a":"Showing a small human imperfection early helps people relax, because they stop waiting for the flaw."},{"id":"v75","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"NUT Job","mv":"Name the fear, Understand it, Transform it into a solution.","tr":"\"Sounds like you're worried about the cost. Tell me more?\" — then work on it together.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V75","d":{"lines":[{"say":"It sounds like you're worried this will land on you. Is that right?","how":"","why":"Captivate's NUT Job: Name the fear, Understand it, Transform it into something you can act on."},{"say":"That makes sense. So what would make this feel safer for you?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 13"],"status":"USED","how":["For someone who's worried or resistant: Name the fear, Understand it, Transform it into a solution together."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “NUT Job”?","a":"\"Sounds like you're worried about the cost. Tell me more?\" — then work on it together."},{"id":"v76","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Counter-offer and saying no","mv":"\"I can't do dinner, but let's grab a coffee.\"","tr":"Offer the version you can genuinely do.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V76","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I can't do dinner, but I'd love to grab a coffee.","how":"","why":"Captivate: say no to the thing, yes to the person. Offer what you can do."},{"say":"I can't take that on right now, but I could help next month.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 13"],"status":"USED","how":["Say no to the thing, yes to the person: \"I can't do dinner, but let's grab a coffee.\""]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “I can't do dinner, but let's …”","a":"“I can't do dinner, but let's grab a coffee.”"},{"id":"v77","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Attunement","mv":"Reciprocal liking, belonging, curiosity. Challenge: tell one person you enjoy their company.","tr":"Say it today to someone it's true about.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V77","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I really enjoy spending time with you.","how":"","why":"Captivate's last hack: tell people you enjoy them, and help them feel they belong."},{"say":"You're one of us now.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Captivate ch. 14"],"rel":["n-all-liking-gap"],"status":"USED","how":["Vanessa's final hack: reciprocal liking, belonging and curiosity. Her challenge: tell one person you enjoy their company."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Showing you like people","note":"Attunement became a simpler instruction: your liking signals are weaker than you think, so turn them up.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Reciprocal liking, belonging, curiosity. Challenge: tell one person you enjoy their company.","card":"v77"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"We like people who like us; your signals are weaker than you think — amplify them.","card":"v24"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Attunement”?","a":"Reciprocal liking, belonging, curiosity. Challenge: tell one person you enjoy their company."},{"id":"v78","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Charisma = warmth + competence (Charisma Dial)","mv":"Turn warmth or competence up to suit the moment; avoid the Danger Zone.","tr":"In most first meetings, lead with warmth, then show one clear point of competence.","w":"Turn warmth or competence up to suit the moment; avoid the Danger Zone.","ev":"Strong (Fiske 2007)","code":"V78","d":{"src":["Cues ch. 1","Fiske, Cuddy & Glick 2007, as cited in the handbook"],"rel":["v81","v82","n-all-warmth-first"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vanessa defines charisma as a balance of warmth (can I trust you?) and competence (can I rely on you?). Her Charisma Dial means turning one up or down to suit the moment. Low on both is her \"Danger Zone\".","This rests on well-supported research on warmth and competence (Fiske, Cuddy and Glick, 2007). Competence without warmth reads as cold; warmth without competence can read as nice but not credible."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Charisma = warmth + competence (Charisma Dial)”?","a":"In most first meetings, lead with warmth, then show one clear point of competence."},{"id":"v79","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Cue Cycle","mv":"Decode → encode → internalise; cues are contagious.","tr":"Change your own cues first — they set the tone.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V79","d":{"steps":["Decode","encode","internalise; cues are contagious"],"src":["Cues ch. 2"],"rel":["v84"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vanessa says cues spread between people: we decode others' signals, encode our own, and internalise them. Your calm or tension can rub off on others."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Name the 3 steps of “Cue Cycle”.","a":"Decode → encode → internalise; cues are contagious"},{"id":"v80","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Charisma cues: lean, anti-block, front, space, gaze","mv":"Lean in, nothing blocking you, toes/torso/top facing them, mind personal space, purposeful gaze.","tr":"Clear the table between you and turn your whole body their way.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V80","d":{"src":["Cues ch. 3"],"rel":["v81"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Show interest with your body: lean in a little, remove barriers between you (laptops, crossed arms, bags), point toes, torso and head towards them, respect personal space and use purposeful eye contact."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"First impressions","note":"From three body basics, to a full kit of charisma cues, to choosing your very first words.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Visible hands, Launch Stance, 60–70% eye contact (illustrative figure). Challenge: handshake audit.","card":"v61"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Cues · 2022","text":"Lean in, nothing blocking you, toes/torso/top facing them, mind personal space, purposeful gaze.","card":"v80"},{"label":"Latest","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Choose feeling words: \"partner\", \"collaborate\"; open with a short positive primer (\"Wonderful to connect\").","card":"v18"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Charisma cues: lean, anti-block, front, space, gaze”?","a":"Lean in, nothing blocking you, toes/torso/top facing them, mind personal space, purposeful gaze."},{"id":"v81","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Warmth cues: tilt, slow triple nod, eyebrow raise, smile, touch, mirroring","mv":"Time your tilts; nod slowly in threes; brows up for interest; real smiles; light appropriate touch; mirror the positive.","tr":"Pick one — the head tilt while listening is easiest — and practise it this week.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V81","d":{"steps":["Time your tilts","nod slowly in threes","brows up for interest","real smiles","light appropriate touch","mirror the positive"],"src":["Cues ch. 4"],"rel":["n-vve-triple-nod","n-vve-head-tilt"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vanessa's warmth cues: a well-timed head tilt, a slow triple nod, a quick eyebrow raise for interest, a real smile, light appropriate touch, and mirroring the positive."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Mirroring","note":"Mirroring the positive moved from a list of warmth cues to a stand-alone habit.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Cues · 2022","text":"Time your tilts; nod slowly in threes; brows up for interest; real smiles; light appropriate touch; mirror the positive.","card":"v81"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Subtly reflect positive gestures and words. Don't mirror negative cues.","card":"v34"}]}],"vb":"2022 → 2026","q":"Name the 6 steps of “Warmth cues: tilt, slow triple nod, eyebrow raise, smile, touch, mirroring”.","a":"Time your tilts → nod slowly in threes → brows up for interest → real smiles → light appropriate touch → mirror the positive"},{"id":"v82","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Power cues: posture, lower-lid flex, steeple, explanatory gestures, palms","mv":"Take up space; focused eyes; gestures that show the point; palm down for a firm point.","tr":"Use gestures that show the size, shape or number you're describing.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V82","d":{"steps":["Take up space","focused eyes","gestures that show the point","palm down for a firm point"],"src":["Cues ch. 5"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vanessa's competence cues: confident posture that takes up space, a focused lower-lid flex when listening hard, the steeple (fingertips touching), explanatory gestures that show your point, and palms down for a firm point."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Power cues: posture, lower-lid flex, steeple, explanatory gestures, palms”?","a":"Take up space; focused eyes; gestures that show the point; palm down for a firm point."},{"id":"v83","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Stop interruptions nicely (the Fish, the Bookmark, the Anchor Touch, the Preview)","mv":"Hand gesture to hold the floor; bookmark their point to come back to; preview how many points you have.","tr":"Start with the Preview before you speak, and the Bookmark if they jump in.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V83","d":{"steps":["Hand gesture to hold the floor","bookmark their point to come back to","preview how many points you have"],"lines":[{"say":"I want to come back to your point, I've made a note. Can I finish this first?","how":"","why":"Cues: stop interruptions kindly. Bookmark their point to come back to, or preview how much you have to say."},{"say":"I've got three quick things, then I want to hear from you.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Cues ch. 5"],"status":"USED (Bookmark, Preview); rest DELIVERY","how":["Vanessa's gentle toolkit for interrupters and talkers: the Fish (open your mouth slightly, as if about to speak), the Bookmark (raise a hand, palm out, to hold your place), the Anchor Touch (a light touch on the arm to bring them back, only where touch is welcome), and the Preview (say \"I've got three points\" up front).","When someone's lost in a monologue, Vanessa suggests the Fish, then the Bookmark, then, if touch is appropriate, a light Anchor Touch on the arm. Use the pause to add your bit — or to say a warm goodbye."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Name the 3 steps of “Stop interruptions nicely (the Fish, the Bookmark, the Anchor Touch, the Preview)”.","a":"Hand gesture to hold the floor → bookmark their point to come back to → preview how many points you have"},{"id":"v84","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Danger-zone cues","mv":"Distancing, self-comfort, blocking, shame, lip purse, resting face — notice in yourself; incongruence lowers trust.","tr":"Check your resting face in a video call — soften it a little.","w":"When your words and body don't match, people trust the body less.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V84","d":{"src":["Cues ch. 6"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Cues that lower trust: distancing, self-comforting (rubbing, fidgeting), blocking, signs of shame, lip purses and a cold resting face. Notice them in yourself first. When your words and body don't match, people trust the body less."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Reading faces","note":"From spotting seven expressions in others, to also checking the signals you send. Both say: respond to the feeling.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Notice brief flashes of the seven universal expressions and respond kindly to the feeling you see.","card":"v68"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Cues · 2022","text":"Distancing, self-comfort, blocking, shame, lip purse, resting face — notice in yourself; incongruence lowers trust.","card":"v84"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2022","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Danger-zone cues”?","a":"Check your resting face in a video call — soften it a little."},{"id":"v85","t":"vve","tp":"voice","ti":"Vocal power: low natural pitch, no uptalk, no fry, volume control, power pause","mv":"Statements end down; pause instead of \"um\".","tr":"Record yourself and check the last word of each sentence goes down.","w":"Statements end down; pause instead of \"um\".","ev":"Moderate","code":"V85","d":{"src":["Cues ch. 7"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vanessa's vocal power basics: speak at your natural low pitch, end statements going down rather than up (no uptalk), avoid vocal fry, control your volume, and use a power pause instead of \"um\"."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"A confident voice","note":"The book gives the vocal power rules; her interviews boil them down to two quick habits you can use today.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Cues · 2022","text":"Statements end down; pause instead of \"um\".","card":"v85"},{"label":"Updated","when":"BigDeal interview · 2024","text":"Vanessa describes a pitch that went wrong in its first line: the founder said his own name with a rising, question-like tone. A name, a number or a price said like a question invites people to doubt you or bargain.","card":"n-vve-m-name-statement"},{"label":"Latest","when":"Interview · Jul 2026","text":"For a warmer, more relaxed voice, breathe in, then say \"hello\" as you breathe out. Vanessa says speaking on the exhale relaxes your vocal cords and keeps you out of a tight, high pitch.","card":"n-vve-m-outbreath-hello"}]}],"vb":"2022 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Vocal power: low natural pitch, no uptalk, no fry, volume control, power pause”?","a":"Statements end down; pause instead of \"um\"."},{"id":"v86","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Permission warmth (vocal hug)","mv":"Open with genuine warmth: \"So good to see you!\"","tr":"Lift your voice a little on the hello.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V86","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Oh, it's so good to hear from you!","how":"","why":"Cues: open with real warmth in your voice. People decide fast whether you're friendly."},{"say":"Anything exciting going on in your neck of the woods?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Cues ch. 8"],"status":"USED","how":["Open with genuine warmth in your voice: \"So good to see you!\" It gives the other person permission to be warm back."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “So good to …”","a":"“So good to see you!”"},{"id":"v87","t":"vve","tp":"listening","ti":"Vocal invitations","mv":"Listening sounds and nudges (\"Mm-hm… and then?\") to invite more.","tr":"Pair them with a slow nod.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V87","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Mm. And then what?","how":"Keep \"Mm\" quiet, give a small nod, then leave a silence for them to fill.","why":"Cues: small listening sounds and nudges invite people to keep going."},{"say":"Go on…","how":"Say \"Go on\" barely above a murmur with a small nod, and wait.","why":""}],"src":["Cues ch. 8"],"rel":["n-vve-triple-nod"],"status":"USED","how":["Little listening sounds and nudges — \"Mm-hm… and then?\" — invite people to keep going."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Vocal invitations”?","a":"Pair them with a slow nod."},{"id":"v88","t":"vve","tp":"voice","ti":"Vocal variety + nonverbal script","mv":"Mark where to smile, pause, emphasise.","tr":"Mark three words to stress and one place to pause in your next talk.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"Moderate","code":"V88","d":{"src":["Cues ch. 8"],"rel":["v85"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vary your voice so people stay with you. For important talks, Vanessa suggests marking your script with where to smile, pause and emphasise."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Vocal variety + nonverbal script”?","a":"Mark where to smile, pause, emphasise."},{"id":"v89","t":"vve","tp":"voice","ti":"Warm vs competent words (verbal chameleon)","mv":"Count your warm and competent words; match the other person's style.","tr":"Before sending an email, check you've got at least one warm word.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V89","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I'm so excited to work on this with you.","how":"","why":"Cues: match the mix of warm and competent words to the person and the moment."},{"say":"Here's the plan, the timeline and who owns what.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["Cues ch. 9"],"status":"USED","how":["Count the warm words (thanks, excited, together) and competent words (results, plan, data) in what you write and say. Then shift the mix to match the other person's style — a verbal chameleon."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Warm vs competent words (verbal chameleon)”?","a":"Count your warm and competent words; match the other person's style."},{"id":"v90","t":"vve","tp":"ideas","ti":"Imagery cues","mv":"Colours, props, backgrounds, photos signal warmth or competence.","tr":"Check your profile photo shows your hands or a real smile.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"Plausible","code":"V90","d":{"src":["Cues ch. 10"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Colours, props, backgrounds and photos also send warmth or competence signals — think about your profile picture or video background."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Imagery cues”?","a":"Check your profile photo shows your hands or a real smile."},{"id":"g1","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Delivery is influence","mv":"Good ideas lose to poor delivery; how you say it decides if it lands.","tr":"Before an important line, decide how you'll say it, not just what you'll say.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"Strong (Schroeder & Epley 2015)","code":"G1","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 1","Schroeder & Epley 2015, as cited in the handbook"],"rel":["n-all-voice-competence"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vinh's starting point: good ideas lose to poor delivery. How you say something decides whether it lands. Research backs the voice part — people who heard a pitch rated the speaker more competent than people who read the same words."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Delivery is influence”?","a":"Good ideas lose to poor delivery; how you say it decides if it lands."},{"id":"g2","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Voice as instrument / communication mirror","mv":"Record, then review audio, muted video and transcript; ask \"does my habitual voice serve me?\"","tr":"Record five minutes of yourself talking, wait a day, then review it three ways.","w":"When something feels strange, he says don't call it fake.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G2","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 2"],"rel":["g6"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vinh treats your voice as an instrument with five foundations: tonality, rate, melody, volume and pause. Most of us play only a few keys. He has you record a \"before\" video and review it three ways — audio only, muted video, and the transcript — then ask whether your habitual voice serves you.","When something feels strange, he says don't call it fake. Your \"natural\" voice is mostly habits picked up from the people around you growing up."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “does my habitual voice …”","a":"“does my habitual voice serve me?”"},{"id":"g3","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Tonality: feel the weird","mv":"Your face drives your tone; practise the seven expressions out loud.","tr":"Before a warm line, let your face warm up first.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G3","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 3"],"rel":["g11"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Your face drives your tone. Put an emotion on your face and you'll hear it in your voice. Vinh's \"Feel the weird\" lesson: hiding emotion makes you harder to connect with, so practise showing the seven core expressions out loud.","He describes people reading his neutral face as unhappy, and fixing it by lifting the corners of his mouth slightly. His exercise has you record yourself making the faces for each emotion while speaking."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Tonality: feel the weird”?","a":"Before a warm line, let your face warm up first."},{"id":"g4","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Rate: find your home base","mv":"Slow down for what matters, speed up for energy.","tr":"Slow down on the one sentence you most want remembered.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G4","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 4"],"rel":["n-vg-calm-pace"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vinh suggests a home base of about 150 words a minute — a 5 on his 1–10 speed scale. From there, play: speed up for energy, slow right down for the line that matters. If you feel you're rambling, you're probably going too fast.","To calm someone who's upset, he says first match their pace, then gradually slow down and they'll tend to slow with you. The real magic is variety; a single speed loses people."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Rate: find your home base”?","a":"Slow down for what matters, speed up for energy."},{"id":"g5","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Melody: siren technique","mv":"Use more of your range; break the monotone.","tr":"Try the siren for one minute, then read the same passage normally and hear the difference.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G5","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 5"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Most people use only about 20% of their pitch range. Vinh's siren technique: read a passage starting at your lowest note and slowly climb to your highest, then back down. It wakes up your range. Also: drop your pitch at the end of a statement so it sounds sure, not like a question."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Melody: siren technique”?","a":"Use more of your range; break the monotone."},{"id":"g6","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Volume: calibrate and match","mv":"Match a quiet person's volume first; add a notch of volume to your hello.","tr":"Ask a friend: \"Where does my voice sit on a 1–10 scale?\"","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G6","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 6","Audible interview"],"rel":["g15","g35"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vinh maps volume on a 1–10 scale: Whisper Zone (1–2), Close Conversation Zone (3–4), Everyday Leader Zone (5–6), Projection Zone (7–8) and Emergency Zone (9–10). Ask three people where your normal volume sits, then practise moving between zones.","With a quiet person, match their volume first so they relax. For a greeting, add a notch more than usual. It's not about being loud — it's about having the whole range available."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Matching people","note":"Match, mirror and lead from his blog became a precise volume habit in the book: match first, then add a notch.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Blog: build rapport with anyone","text":"Match their vocal foundations and body first; then lead gently.","card":"g35"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Match a quiet person's volume first; add a notch of volume to your hello.","card":"g6"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Volume: calibrate and match”?","a":"Ask a friend: \"Where does my voice sit on a 1–10 scale?\""},{"id":"g7","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Pause: replace the filler","mv":"Don't fight \"um\", swap it for a pause; pause before and after key words.","tr":"Next time you're asked a question, pause for one breath before answering.","w":"Don't fight \"um\", swap it for a pause; pause before and after key words.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G7","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 7"],"rel":["g8"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vinh — who calls himself \"the Pause Guy\" — says don't fight your \"um\", replace it. When you feel a filler coming, pause instead: breathe, find your next thought, then carry on. A pause adds suspense, kills fillers, calms your body and lets people take in what you said.","Where to pause: before you answer a question (you sound more considered), before an opinion (more thoughtful), before a punchline (bigger laugh), and before and after a key word. In a disagreement, an extra second of pause can stop you saying something you regret. His exercise: count your pauses and fillers in a recording, then re-record with a pause wherever an \"um\" was."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Pause: replace the filler”?","a":"Don't fight \"um\", swap it for a pause; pause before and after key words."},{"id":"g8","t":"vg","tp":"body","ti":"Presence: stillness","mv":"Stand still and let the body stop leaking nerves.","tr":"Plant your feet before you start speaking.","w":"Stand still and let the body stop leaking nerves.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G8","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 8"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Stillness reads as presence. Vinh says stand or sit still and contained so your body stops leaking nerves — no swaying, tapping or fiddling."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Presence: stillness”?","a":"Stand still and let the body stop leaking nerves."},{"id":"g9","t":"vg","tp":"body","ti":"Posture: doorway reset","mv":"Drop shoulders as you walk through a door; ground before you speak.","tr":"Try it on every doorway today until it becomes automatic.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G9","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 9"],"rel":["g8"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Every time you walk through a doorway, let it trigger a posture reset: drop your shoulders, lift your chest, ground yourself. Doorways are everywhere, and each one is a chance at a first impression. It takes two seconds."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Posture: doorway reset”?","a":"Try it on every doorway today until it becomes automatic."},{"id":"g10","t":"vg","tp":"body","ti":"Gestures: power sphere","mv":"Hands between navel and eyes; palms up to invite; point with all fingers.","tr":"Palms up when you open a conversation, palms down when you make your key point.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Moderate (Hostetter 2011)","code":"G10","d":{"steps":["Hands between navel and eyes","palms up to invite","point with all fingers"],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 10"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Keep your gestures in the Power Sphere — roughly between your navel and your eyes, elbows off your sides. Vinh teaches five gestures: Placater (palms up, to invite and build rapport), Leveler (palms down, firm and certain), Blamer (pointing — use rarely), Thinker (hand to chin, to show you're taking it in) and Distracter (a clap and wave to reset energy).","Palms up versus palms down even changes your voice: say the same firm sentence with palms up and it sounds softer; with palms down it lands. Research suggests gestures help listeners understand."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"The five gestures","note":"Same five gestures, with clearer rules on when to use the strongest ones.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Hands between navel and eyes; palms up to invite; point with all fingers.","card":"g10"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Raj Shamani interview · Nov 2026","text":"He now calls the thinking pose \"the Computer\": use it to show you are processing, then move on. Pointing is for talking to a crowd, never one to one, and the reset clap gets one use per talk.","card":null}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Gestures: power sphere”?","a":"Hands between navel and eyes; palms up to invite; point with all fingers."},{"id":"g11","t":"vg","tp":"body","ti":"Expression: let your face speak","mv":"A blank face reads as \"doesn't care\"; show what you feel.","tr":"Watch a muted recording of yourself — does your face match your words?","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G11","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 11"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["A blank face reads as \"doesn't care\". Vinh says let your face show what you feel, so people can read you and connect."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Expression: let your face speak”?","a":"A blank face reads as \"doesn't care\"; show what you feel."},{"id":"g12","t":"vg","tp":"body","ti":"Movement with purpose","mv":"Only move to mark a new point.","tr":"Stand still while you make a point, then move as you change topics.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G12","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 12"],"rel":["g8"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Only move to mark a new point; otherwise stay still. Vinh's \"three-zone story\" exercise has you tell a story in three parts, moving to a new spot for each one."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Movement with purpose”?","a":"Stand still while you make a point, then move as you change topics."},{"id":"g13","t":"vg","tp":"body","ti":"Eye contact: triangle and one-thought-one-person","mv":"Left eye, right eye, bridge; in groups finish a thought with one person.","tr":"Speaking to a group, say \"you\" to one person at a time while you look at them.","w":"In a group, finish a whole thought with one person before moving to the next — and avoid a robotic left-to-right sweep.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G13","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 13 (p. 122)"],"rel":["g8"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["If eye contact feels intense, Vinh's triangle technique helps: look at the left eye, the right eye, then the bridge of the nose, and repeat. In a group, finish a whole thought with one person before moving to the next — and avoid a robotic left-to-right sweep."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Eye contact: triangle and one-thought-one-person”?","a":"Left eye, right eye, bridge; in groups finish a thought with one person."},{"id":"g14","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Practice: replace, don't just remove","mv":"Swap a habit for a better one; tiny daily reps (\"Good morning!\" with eye contact and a smile).","tr":"Pick one habit and one replacement this week — not five.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G14","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Good morning! How's your day starting?","how":"Give \"Good\" an upward lift so the line starts warm, not flat.","why":"Vinh: replace a habit rather than just removing it. Tiny daily reps: eye contact, a smile, a real 'Good morning'."},{"say":"Morning! I don't think we've met properly. I'm ___.","how":"Let your voice sing a little on \"Morning\", like greeting a friend you've missed.","why":""}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14"],"rel":["g41","n-vg-practical-magic","g42"],"status":"USED (the greeting)","how":["You can't just delete a habit; you replace it with a better one. Vinh suggests tiny daily reps, like a \"Good morning!\" with eye contact and a smile. He also says start with a pause, not an apology."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Greeting with energy","note":"Generosity of energy became a daily practice rep: a real 'Good morning!' with eye contact and a smile.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Audible interview / On Purpose","text":"Give a little more life to a greeting; it lifts the room.","card":"g15"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Swap a habit for a better one; tiny daily reps (\"Good morning!\" with eye contact and a smile).","card":"g14"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Practice: replace, don't just remove”?","a":"Swap a habit for a better one; tiny daily reps (\"Good morning!\" with eye contact and a smile)."},{"id":"g15","t":"vg","tp":"openers","ti":"Generosity of energy","mv":"Give a little more life to a greeting; it lifts the room.","tr":"Add one notch of volume and a real smile to your next greeting.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G15","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Hello! It's so good to see you.","how":"Give \"Hello\" an upward lift so the line starts warm, not flat.","why":"Vinh: give a little more energy to a hello or goodbye. It lifts the other person too."},{"say":"Thanks so much, that really made my day.","how":"Let \"made my day\" drift down in pitch and stop there; don't add anything after it.","why":""}],"src":["Audible interview","On Purpose"],"rel":["g6"],"status":"USED","how":["Give a little more life to your hello than feels necessary. Vinh calls it generosity of energy — it lifts the other person and the room."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Greeting with energy","note":"Generosity of energy became a daily practice rep: a real 'Good morning!' with eye contact and a smile.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Audible interview / On Purpose","text":"Give a little more life to a greeting; it lifts the room.","card":"g15"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Swap a habit for a better one; tiny daily reps (\"Good morning!\" with eye contact and a smile).","card":"g14"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Generosity of energy”?","a":"Add one notch of volume and a real smile to your next greeting."},{"id":"g16","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Stakes","mv":"Say what could be lost so people lean in.","tr":"In your next story, add one sentence about what you stood to lose.","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G16","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I'm raising this because I'd hate us to lose the client over it.","how":"Palms down and steady on \"lose\"; your voice will drop and sound sure without sounding sharp.","why":"Vinh: when people know what could be lost or won, they lean in."},{"say":"If we get this right, it changes a lot for the team.","how":"Slow your last words, \"lot for the team\", and let your pitch settle down like a warm full stop.","why":""}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 16"],"rel":["n-vg-story-bank"],"status":"USED","how":["Say what could be lost. When people know what's on the line, they lean in."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Stakes”?","a":"Say what could be lost so people lean in."},{"id":"g17","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Vulnerability builds trust","mv":"Share a real struggle, not a polished highlight.","tr":"Share something you've learned the hard way — but only what you've processed enough to share calmly.","w":"Share a real struggle, not a polished highlight.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G17","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Honestly, I struggled with this for years.","how":"Keep your body still and your feet planted; stillness makes \"struggled\" sound steady.","why":"Vinh: a real struggle builds more trust than a polished highlight."},{"say":"I'll admit I got defensive earlier. I'm sorry.","how":"Palms up at rib height as you say \"sorry\"; open hands signal you're not a threat.","why":""}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 17","ch. 3 \"Feel the Weird\""],"status":"USED","how":["Share a real struggle, not a polished highlight. Vinh says the emotions you hide to protect people are often the ones that would connect you."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Vulnerability builds trust”?","a":"Share a real struggle, not a polished highlight."},{"id":"g18","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Humour","mv":"Self-aware, true, small stories make people like you.","tr":"Add a \"Humorous Stories\" section to your Story Bank.","w":"You don't need jokes — you need honest moments told with timing (pause before the punchline).","ev":"Moderate","code":"G18","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I'd love to say I handled it gracefully. I did not.","how":"Hold a beat just before \"not\" so the laugh lands.","why":"Vinh: small, true, self-aware stories make people like you."},{"say":"I've been told I talk with my hands, so stand back.","how":"Say it deadpan and pause before \"stand back\"; the pause does the work.","why":""}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 18"],"status":"USED","how":["Self-aware, true, small stories are the easiest humour. You don't need jokes — you need honest moments told with timing (pause before the punchline)."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Humour”?","a":"Add a \"Humorous Stories\" section to your Story Bank."},{"id":"g19","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Don't be boring","mv":"Cut detours and corrections that lose people.","tr":"Start closer to the action and know your point before you begin.","w":"Vinh names two traps: the story that never takes off, and the story that never lands.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G19","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 19–20"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Stories lose people through detours, corrections (\"No wait, it was Tuesday\") and starting too early. Cut anything that doesn't serve the point. Vinh names two traps: the story that never takes off, and the story that never lands."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Don't be boring”?","a":"Cut detours and corrections that lose people."},{"id":"g20","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Spark–Point–Link formula","mv":"Start at a peak (emotion, action or insight), give the 4Ws, land one point, link it to them.","tr":"Ask \"So what?\" until you find your point, then ask \"Why does this matter to them?\" for the link.","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G20","d":{"lines":[{"say":"This reminds me of my first job. The point is, nobody gets it right first time.","how":"No fillers on the way to \"nobody\": if you need a moment, pause instead of \"so\" or \"like\".","why":"Vinh's story formula: start at the peak moment, land one point, then link it to the listener."},{"say":"What I took from it is simple: ask for help sooner.","how":"Palms down and steady on \"sooner\"; your voice will drop and sound sure without sounding sharp.","why":""}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 20"],"rel":["g21","n-vg-storytelling"],"status":"USED","how":["Vinh's story formula. Spark: start at a peak moment of emotion, action or insight, then fill in the 4Ws. Point: land one clear point. Link: connect it to the person listening, so it's about them too."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Spark–Point–Link formula”?","a":"Start at a peak (emotion, action or insight), give the 4Ws, land one point, link it to them."},{"id":"g21","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"The Link / Relinking","mv":"Tie a story to this listener; find a kinder meaning in an old painful story (\"second arrow\").","tr":"End with one sentence that starts \"And I think that's true for you too, because…\".","w":"Relinking means finding a kinder, truer meaning for an old painful story, so you stop firing second arrows at yourself.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G21","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I used to see that as my worst moment. Now I think it's where I learnt the most.","how":"Ease off the speed just for \"most\"; slowing down is what gives it weight.","why":"Vinh: you can choose a kinder meaning for a painful story. It helps others do the same."},{"say":"What if this isn't a failure, just the start of the story?","how":"Stop moving before you start; a calm body makes \"start\" come out calm too.","why":""}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 21–22"],"status":"USED","how":["The Link ties your story to this particular listener's life, so it isn't just about you.","Vinh's two arrows: the first arrow is what happened; the second is the meaning you add afterwards (\"I'm a failure\"). Relinking means finding a kinder, truer meaning for an old painful story, so you stop firing second arrows at yourself."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “The Link / Relinking”?","a":"End with one sentence that starts \"And I think that's true for you too, because…\"."},{"id":"g22","t":"vg","tp":"openers","ti":"Fill the shelf (High, Low, Buffalo)","mv":"Each share a high, a low and something unusual — six threads. Two-shelf exercise: go first with someone you barely know; ask someone you've known for years.","tr":"Use it at the start of a team meeting or a dinner.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Moderate (self-disclosure reciprocity)","code":"G22","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Let's do high, low, buffalo: a high, a low and something random from your week.","how":"Say it a touch quicker than usual, with a lift on \"random\".","why":"Vinh's game: share a high, a low and something random. Six easy threads to pull on, and everyone fills each other's shelf."},{"say":"I'll go first. High: new job. Low: no sleep. Buffalo: I've taken up pottery.","how":"Hold a beat just before \"pottery\" so the laugh lands.","why":""},{"say":"Same again next week? You go first this time.","how":"Slow your last words, \"You go first this time\", and let your pitch settle down like a warm full stop.","why":""},{"say":"So good to see you! Quick one: a high, a low and something random from your week. I'll go first.","how":"Give \"So good to see you\" more energy than feels necessary, then smile into \"I'll go first\".","why":"Vinh's game: going first with a real answer gives others permission to open up."}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 23","On Purpose with Jay Shetty"],"rel":["g23"],"status":"USED","how":["Each person shares a high (something good), a low (something hard) and a buffalo (something random about them). One turn gives three threads, so a group of four has a dozen places to go. Vinh says go first and give a rich answer — your depth gives others permission.","His two-shelf exercise: try it with someone you barely know, and with someone you've known for years."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Things to talk about","note":"FORD topics turned into High, Low, Buffalo: six threads from one easy round.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"YouTube @askvinh","text":"Family, Occupation, Recreation, Dreams; \"What do you do in your free time?\"","card":"g39"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Each share a high, a low and something unusual — six threads. Two-shelf exercise: go first with someone you barely know; ask someone you've known for years.","card":"g22"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Fill the shelf (High, Low, Buffalo)”?","a":"Each share a high, a low and something unusual — six threads. Two-shelf exercise: go first with someone you barely know; ask someone you've known for years."},{"id":"g23","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Conversational threads","mv":"Offer and pull on threads in words, voice and body.","tr":"Pick the thread with the most feeling in it and ask about that.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G23","d":{"lines":[{"say":"You mentioned Japan. What took you there?","how":"Let your voice lift gently on \"What took you there\" so it sounds like real curiosity, not a test.","why":"Vinh: people drop small threads. Notice one and follow it."},{"say":"You said 'finally'. Has it been a long road?","how":"Ask it once, ending on \"been a long road\", then leave the silence alone; don't add a second question.","why":""},{"say":"You mentioned it's been a big week. How are you doing with it all?","how":"Put on warm interest first, and it will come through in \"big\".","why":""},{"say":"I really enjoyed this. Same time next month? I want to hear how the move goes.","how":"Let your face show you mean it, and end \"move goes\" on a gentle rise.","why":"Vinh: pull gently on a thread they gave you, and carry it into next time."}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 23, 26","Jay Shetty","Vinh TikTok","Vinh on TikTok"],"status":"USED","how":["Every answer offers threads — a detail, a flash of emotion, a shift in tone, folded arms. Vinh says offer threads of your own and gently pull on theirs, in words, voice and body."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Conversational threads”?","a":"Offer and pull on threads in words, voice and body."},{"id":"g24","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Listening is the bigger half","mv":"Ask at the end: \"What seemed to matter most to them?\" Ask a friend how you could listen better.","tr":"After your next conversation, answer that question in one line.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G24","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Thanks for telling me all that. It sounds like the team matters most to you.","how":"Let \"matters most to you\" drift down in pitch and stop there; don't add anything after it.","why":"Vinh: listening is the bigger half. Afterwards, ask yourself what mattered most to them, and tell them."},{"say":"I'm trying to be a better listener. How did I do just then?","how":"Stop moving before you start; a calm body makes \"better\" come out calm too.","why":""},{"say":"Thank you. I learned more from you in ten minutes than from a week of reading about it.","how":"Slow right down on \"ten minutes\" and let the last words settle low.","why":"Vinh: listening is the bigger half. Afterwards, tell them what you took from them."},{"say":"What you said about the team really stuck with me. I'll be thinking about it on the way home.","how":"Hold eye contact through \"stuck with me\", then pause before the rest.","why":"Vinh: ask yourself what seemed to matter most to them, then tell them."}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 24"],"status":"USED","how":["Vinh calls listening the bigger half of connection. At the end of a conversation, ask yourself \"What seemed to matter most to them?\" — and ask a friend how you could listen better."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Follow-up questions","note":"Three follow-ups on strong feelings grew into a whole chapter on listening as the bigger half.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"On Purpose podcast","text":"When someone shows strong feeling, ask three follow-ups before you say anything about yourself.","card":"g31"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Ask at the end: \"What seemed to matter most to them?\" Ask a friend how you could listen better.","card":"g24"}]},{"title":"Listening is the bigger half","note":"His first workshops taught speaking only. A student pointed out the missing half, and listening became central to his teaching.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"First US workshop · 2016 (told in an Apr 2026 video)","text":"Three days on voice, body language and stories, with no listening at all. A student asked when they would learn it, and later wrote that he had missed half of communication.","card":null},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Ask at the end: \"What seemed to matter most to them?\" Ask a friend how you could listen better.","card":"g24"}]}],"vb":"2016 → 2026","q":"Finish the line: “What seemed to matter most …”","a":"“What seemed to matter most to them?”"},{"id":"g25","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Name your trap (five non-listening traps)","mv":"Judging, advising, one-upping, mind-reading, daydreaming.","tr":"Finish the sentence: \"My number one listening trap is…\"","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G25","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Do you want ideas, or would you rather I just listen?","how":"Ask it once, ending on \"rather I just listen\", then leave the silence alone; don't add a second question.","why":"Vinh's five traps: judging, advising, one-upping, mind-reading, daydreaming. Catch yourself and stay with them."},{"say":"I'm going to stop trying to fix it and just listen.","how":"Start at their speed so they know you're with them, then slow down on \"listen\" to bring the heat down.","why":""},{"say":"That's a lot. Before I say anything, tell me the rest.","how":"Ease off the speed just for \"rest\"; slowing down is what gives it weight.","why":""}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 25"],"rel":["g30"],"status":"USED (the antidote lines)","how":["Vinh's five passive listening traps: judging, giving advice, one-upping, mind reading and daydreaming. For a week, just notice which one you fall into most — you have to catch a habit before you can change it."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Name your trap (five non-listening traps)”?","a":"Judging, advising, one-upping, mind-reading, daydreaming."},{"id":"g26","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Reflection: notice → reflect → clarify","mv":"Give their exact word back (\"Trapped?\"), pause, then clarify.","tr":"Repeat one key word with a gentle rise, then wait.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Strong (reflective listening; Itzchakov)","code":"G26","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Trapped?","how":"Give \"Trapped?\" back at their volume, a touch slower, then let the silence work.","why":"Vinh: notice, reflect, clarify. Give their own word back, then pause. Reflective listening helps people feel understood."},{"say":"When you say it's complicated, what do you mean?","how":"Match their pace for the first few words, then gradually slow down as you reach \"complicated, what do you mean\".","why":""},{"say":"Doesn't feel right?","how":"Say \"Doesn't feel right?\" softly with a slight lift at the end, then go quiet and wait.","why":""},{"say":"Wait, \"stuck\"? Say more about that. I think you've put your finger on something.","how":"Lift your pitch on \"stuck\", then pause a full beat before \"Say more\".","why":"Vinh: notice a strong word, reflect it back, pause, then clarify."}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 26"],"rel":["g29"],"status":"USED","how":["Notice a strong word they used, reflect it back (\"Trapped?\"), pause, then clarify. Reflective listening has good research behind it and makes people feel genuinely understood."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Keeping them talking","note":"His 2025 'tell me more' became a full reflection method in the book.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Diary of a CEO · 2025","text":"Buys time and makes them feel heard.","card":"g40"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Give their exact word back (\"Trapped?\"), pause, then clarify.","card":"g26"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Reflection: notice → reflect → clarify”?","a":"Give their exact word back (\"Trapped?\"), pause, then clarify."},{"id":"g27","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Vocal radar (five vocal signals)","mv":"Notice when rate, volume, melody, tone or pauses change, and reflect it gently.","tr":"\"You slowed down when you mentioned your sister — what's going on there?\"","w":"Don't analyse every sentence — just notice when their voice doesn't match the rest of the conversation, then gently ask.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G27","d":{"lines":[{"say":"You went a bit quiet when you mentioned your manager. Everything all right?","how":"After \"Everything all right\", stop and hold a two-second pause so they have room to answer.","why":"Vinh: listen for when someone's pace, volume or tone shifts, and gently check in."},{"say":"You sound tired of all this. Am I reading that right?","how":"Say \"tired\" quieter than usual, as if you're sharing it just with them.","why":""}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 27 (Thread Pulling, pp. 260–262)"],"rel":["g28","g23"],"status":"USED","how":["The five vocal foundations, heard in someone else: a change in rate, volume, melody, tonality (warm to cold) or an unexpected pause. Don't analyse every sentence — just notice when their voice doesn't match the rest of the conversation, then gently ask."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Vocal radar (five vocal signals)”?","a":"\"You slowed down when you mentioned your sister — what's going on there?\""},{"id":"g28","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Visual radar","mv":"Notice what their body changes, and ask rather than assume.","tr":"Name what you saw as a question: \"You went quiet there — all good?\"","w":"Notice what their body changes, and ask rather than assume.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G28","d":{"lines":[{"say":"You smiled when you said that. Good memory?","how":"Settle into stillness first, then say \"Good memory\" quietly, like you've got all the time in the world.","why":"Vinh: notice what their face and body do, and ask rather than assume."},{"say":"You look like there's more you want to say. Go on.","how":"Say \"more\" quieter than usual, as if you're sharing it just with them.","why":""}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 28"],"status":"USED","how":["Notice what changes in their body — posture, hands, face — and ask about it rather than assuming what it means."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Visual radar”?","a":"Notice what their body changes, and ask rather than assume."},{"id":"g29","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Emotional radar / undercurrent","mv":"Ask yourself \"What might they be feeling or needing?\"; name the feeling as a question.","tr":"\"Sounds like that left you feeling a bit unseen?\"","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Strong (affect labelling)","code":"G29","d":{"lines":[{"say":"It sounds like you're feeling pretty overwhelmed. Is that it?","how":"Drop the volume a little for \"Is that it\" so it sounds private, not public.","why":"Vinh: ask yourself what they might be feeling or needing, then offer it as a question. Labelling feelings calms them."},{"say":"Sounds like you're feeling unheard. Is that fair?","how":"Start at their speed so they know you're with them, then slow down on \"unheard\" to bring the heat down.","why":""},{"say":"What would help most right now?","how":"Drop the volume a little for \"most right now\" so it sounds private, not public.","why":""},{"say":"That sounds like a lot to carry. What would help most right now?","how":"Drop your volume a notch and slow down; leave room after the question.","why":"Vinh: ask yourself what they might be feeling or needing, then name it gently."}],"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 29"],"status":"USED","how":["Ask yourself \"What might they be feeling or needing?\" Then name the feeling as a question. Labelling a feeling, research shows, can turn its intensity down."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “What might they be feeling …”","a":"“What might they be feeling or needing?”"},{"id":"g30","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Last Word, Last Letter","mv":"A game that stops you planning your reply while they talk.","tr":"Play it with a friend for five minutes.","w":"A theatre game Vinh used to stop himself planning his reply while someone was still talking: start your response with a word beginning with the last letter of the last word they said.","ev":"Coach's view","code":"G30","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 29 (p. 274)","On Purpose with Jay Shetty"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["A theatre game Vinh used to stop himself planning his reply while someone was still talking: start your response with a word beginning with the last letter of the last word they said. It forces you to listen to the end."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Last Word, Last Letter","note":"The listening game gained a score, so you can track it.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"A game that stops you planning your reply while they talk.","card":"g30"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Mel Robbins Podcast · Nov 2026","text":"After a round, rate from 1 to 10 how hard it felt. A high score means you were busy planning your reply instead of listening to the end.","card":null}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Last Word, Last Letter”?","a":"Play it with a friend for five minutes."},{"id":"g31","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Three follow-ups on strong emotion","mv":"When someone shows strong feeling, ask three follow-ups before you say anything about yourself.","tr":"Count them on your fingers if you need to.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Moderate (Huang 2017)","code":"G31","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Wow. How did you feel when that happened?","how":"After \"feel when that happened\", stop and hold a two-second pause so they have room to answer.","why":"Vinh: when someone shows strong feeling, ask three follow-up questions before you share anything of yours."},{"say":"And what happened after that?","how":"Ask it once, ending on \"after that\", then leave the silence alone; don't add a second question.","why":""}],"src":["On Purpose with Jay Shetty"],"status":"USED","how":["When someone shows strong feeling, ask three follow-up questions before you say anything about yourself."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Follow-up questions","note":"Three follow-ups on strong feelings grew into a whole chapter on listening as the bigger half.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"On Purpose podcast","text":"When someone shows strong feeling, ask three follow-ups before you say anything about yourself.","card":"g31"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Ask at the end: \"What seemed to matter most to them?\" Ask a friend how you could listen better.","card":"g24"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Three follow-ups on strong emotion”?","a":"When someone shows strong feeling, ask three follow-ups before you say anything about yourself."},{"id":"g32","t":"vg","tp":"tough","ti":"Argue at the situation, not the person","mv":"Reflect their emotion; say what upset you about the situation.","tr":"\"I can hear you're exhausted. What got to me was the plan changing last minute.\"","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G32","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I'm not upset with you. I'm upset about what happened.","how":"Let your hands rest open and upward as you reach \"not\"; it softens the line.","why":"Vinh: in a row, reflect their feeling and aim your frustration at the situation, not at them."},{"say":"Can we make this us against the problem?","how":"Let your hands rest open and upward as you reach \"us\"; it softens the line.","why":""},{"say":"You've clearly thought this through. Here's the one part I see differently, and it's about the timeline, not you.","how":"Palms down and steady on \"the one part\"; keep your volume level.","why":"Vinh: reflect their effort first, then aim at the situation, never the person."}],"src":["On Purpose with Jay Shetty"],"rel":["g37"],"status":"USED","how":["In a conflict, reflect their emotion first, then say what upset you about the situation, not what's wrong with them."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Argue at the situation, not the person”?","a":"Reflect their emotion; say what upset you about the situation."},{"id":"g33","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Share wins without arrogance","mv":"Credit the help and what you learned.","tr":"\"We got it — and honestly, Sam's idea made the difference.\"","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G33","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I'm really proud of it, and I had a lot of help.","how":"Keep \"had a lot of help\" low and level; a rising end would make it sound unsure.","why":"Vinh: share good news with credit to others and what you learned. It invites celebration, not envy."},{"say":"We did it, and honestly, your idea is what got us there.","how":"Slow your last words, \"what got us there\", and let your pitch settle down like a warm full stop.","why":""}],"src":["On Purpose with Jay Shetty"],"status":"USED","how":["Share a win by crediting the people who helped and what you learned along the way."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Share wins without arrogance”?","a":"\"We got it — and honestly, Sam's idea made the difference.\""},{"id":"g34","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Hard to interrupt","mv":"Visual and vocal presence: finish your sentence, steady volume.","tr":"Don't speed up or drop volume when someone tries to cut in.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G34","d":{"src":["On Purpose with Jay Shetty"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vinh's take: finish your sentence, keep your volume steady, and keep your body still and present. People interrupt less when your voice and body show you're mid-thought."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Hard to interrupt”?","a":"Visual and vocal presence: finish your sentence, steady volume."},{"id":"g35","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Match, Mirror and Lead","mv":"Match their vocal foundations and body first; then lead gently.","tr":"Start near their pace and volume, then lead a notch at a time. Obvious copying backfires.","w":"Don't yank a nervous person's energy up.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G35","d":{"lines":[{"say":"No rush at all. Take your time.","how":"Breathe instead of saying \"um\" before \"rush\", then say the rest in one clean go.","why":"Vinh: match their pace and energy first; once you're in step, you can gently lead."},{"say":"Okay. Let's slow down for a sec.","how":"Let your hands rest open and upward as you reach \"slow\"; it softens the line.","why":""}],"src":["blog \"How to Build Rapport with Absolutely Anyone\"","Vinh's blog"],"rel":["n-vg-calm-pace","g36"],"status":"USED","how":["Vinh's rapport method. Match and mirror their vocal foundations and body language first — if they're quiet and slow, lower and slow down — so they relax. Once you're in sync, lead gently with a question, a lighter tone or a decision. Don't yank a nervous person's energy up."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Matching people","note":"Match, mirror and lead from his blog became a precise volume habit in the book: match first, then add a notch.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Blog: build rapport with anyone","text":"Match their vocal foundations and body first; then lead gently.","card":"g35"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Match a quiet person's volume first; add a notch of volume to your hello.","card":"g6"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"What’s the trap with “Match, Mirror and Lead”?","a":"Don't yank a nervous person's energy up."},{"id":"g36","t":"vg","tp":"ideas","ti":"What they want from this chat","mv":"Information, connection, harmony or fun — then tailor.","tr":"Ask yourself in the first minute: which of the four is this?","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G36","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Do you fancy a proper catch-up, or just a quick hello?","how":"Give \"proper\" a bit more volume than you normally would; energy is catching.","why":"Vinh: people want different things from a chat: information, connection, harmony or fun. Ask, then give them that."},{"say":"What would make this chat useful for you?","how":"Stay still, maybe with a hand near your chin, and keep \"useful\" low and unhurried.","why":""},{"say":"What's on your mind today? I've got time.","how":"Ease off the speed just for \"mind\"; slowing down is what gives it weight.","why":""},{"say":"It's your call. Can I give you the one risk I see, short version, and then you decide?","how":"Say \"your call\" warmly and slowly, then keep the risk to one breath.","why":"Vinh: some people want information. Be clear and short, and leave the decision with them."}],"src":["blog / The Diary of a CEO 2025","Vinh's blog"],"rel":["g35"],"status":"USED","how":["Vinh says most people want one of four things from a conversation: information (be clear and short), connection (warmth), harmony (keep the peace) or fun. Spot it, then tailor how you talk."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “What they want from this chat”?","a":"Ask yourself in the first minute: which of the four is this?"},{"id":"g37","t":"vg","tp":"tough","ti":"\"Yes, and…\"","mv":"Build on what they said; \"yes, but\" wipes it out.","tr":"Swap one \"but\" for \"and\" in your next disagreement.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Plausible (improv)","code":"G37","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Yes, and we could try it your way for the first part.","how":"Palms up at rib height as you say \"your way\"; open hands signal you're not a threat.","why":"Vinh (from improv): build on what they said. 'Yes, but' wipes it out."},{"say":"Yes, and what if we did it on Saturday?","how":"Stay still, maybe with a hand near your chin, and keep \"Saturday\" low and unhurried.","why":""}],"src":["Instagram Jun 2025"],"rel":["g32"],"status":"USED","how":["Borrowed from improv: \"Yes, and…\" builds on what they said; \"yes, but…\" wipes it out."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “Build on what …”","a":"“ Build on what they said; ”"},{"id":"g38","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"3-2-1 / \"the one thing is\"","mv":"When put on the spot, give three steps, two types, or one thing.","tr":"Record five minutes today, watch it once, and write down three changes.","w":"Quiet students who turn up their volume notice they never move their hands, so that becomes the next thing.","ev":"Coach's view","code":"G38","d":{"lines":[{"say":"There's really one thing I'd change, and it's the timing.","how":"Use a calm palms-down gesture and keep \"change, and it's the timing\" level and firm.","why":"Vinh: when you're put on the spot, give three steps, two options or one thing. It sounds clear and calm."},{"say":"Three things to take away: start small, ask early, and keep going.","how":"Palms down and steady on \"Three\"; your voice will drop and sound sure without sounding sharp.","why":""}],"src":["YouTube @askvinh","Lewis Howes, Nov 2026","Mark Manson, Nov 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-practical-magic"],"status":"USED","how":["Vinh's ten-minute habit: record yourself talking for five minutes, watch it back for five, and pick three things to change. A generic top-ten list gives generic results; your own recording shows your list.","Fixing one thing often reveals the next. Quiet students who turn up their volume notice they never move their hands, so that becomes the next thing."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"When you are put on the spot","note":"The same core, refined through 2026: buy time, narrow the question, answer one thing.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"When put on the spot, give three steps, two types, or one thing.","card":"g38"},{"label":"Updated","when":"YouTube · Jun 2026","text":"Vinh's PAT framework for surprise questions. Pause for a couple of seconds instead of blurting.","card":"n-vg-m-pat"},{"label":"Latest","when":"Mel Robbins Podcast · Nov 2026","text":"When a question throws you, Vinh uses three steps: pause, ask a clarifying question, then answer slowly. Clarifying buys time, and more importantly it tells you what they actually want to know.","card":"n-vg-m-pause-clarify"}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"Finish the line: “the one …”","a":"“the one thing is”"},{"id":"g39","t":"vg","tp":"openers","ti":"FORD topics","mv":"Family, Occupation, Recreation, Dreams; \"What do you do in your free time?\"","tr":"If you're stuck, pick the next letter.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G39","d":{"lines":[{"say":"What do you get up to in your free time?","how":"Keep the start level and let the pitch rise only on \"free time\".","why":"Vinh's topic map: Family, Occupation, Recreation, Dreams."},{"say":"If money were no object, what would you be doing?","how":"Keep the start level and let the pitch rise only on \"what would you be doing\".","why":""}],"src":["YouTube @askvinh","@askvinh on YouTube"],"status":"USED","how":["Family, Occupation, Recreation, Dreams — four safe areas for small talk. Vinh's favourite question: \"What do you do in your free time?\""],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Things to talk about","note":"FORD topics turned into High, Low, Buffalo: six threads from one easy round.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"YouTube @askvinh","text":"Family, Occupation, Recreation, Dreams; \"What do you do in your free time?\"","card":"g39"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Each share a high, a low and something unusual — six threads. Two-shelf exercise: go first with someone you barely know; ask someone you've known for years.","card":"g22"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"Finish the line: “What do you do in your …”","a":"“What do you do in your free time?”"},{"id":"g40","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"\"Tell me more\"","mv":"Buys time and makes them feel heard.","tr":"Use it when you're not sure what to say next.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Moderate","code":"G40","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Tell me more. I want to get this right.","how":"Match their pace for the first few words, then gradually slow down as you reach \"want to get this right\".","why":"Vinh: 'Tell me more' buys you time and makes people feel heard."},{"say":"Ooh, tell me more.","how":"Put on warm interest first, and it will come through in \"more\".","why":""}],"src":["The Diary of a CEO 2025"],"status":"USED","how":["Vinh says \"Tell me more\" does two jobs: it makes the person feel heard and it buys you time to think."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Keeping them talking","note":"His 2025 'tell me more' became a full reflection method in the book.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Diary of a CEO · 2025","text":"Buys time and makes them feel heard.","card":"g40"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Give their exact word back (\"Trapped?\"), pause, then clarify.","card":"g26"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “\"Tell me more\"”?","a":"Buys time and makes them feel heard."},{"id":"g41","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Four stages of competence","mv":"The clunky stage is where people quit; \"it feels unfamiliar, not fake\".","tr":"When a new habit feels weird, tell yourself \"unfamiliar, not fake\".","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","code":"G41","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14","@askvinh on YouTube"],"rel":["g14"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Learning goes from not knowing you're bad, to knowing you're bad, to clunky and deliberate, to natural. The clunky third stage is where people quit. Vinh says when it feels fake, it's just unfamiliar — keep going."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"\"Fake it till you make it\"","note":"He taught it for years, then dropped it: new habits are exploring, not faking.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"His early teaching","text":"For years he told students to act confident until they became confident: fake it till you make it.","card":null},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"The clunky stage is where people quit; \"it feels unfamiliar, not fake\".","card":"g41"},{"label":"Latest","when":"\"Advice I've changed my mind about\" video · Apr 2026","text":"He now says faking backfires, because part of you knows and other people feel it. Call it exploring: trying on something unfamiliar, like new clothes, and keeping what fits.","card":null}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"Finish the line: “it feels unfamiliar, …”","a":"“it feels unfamiliar, not fake”"},{"id":"g42","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Low-status habits","mv":"Notice apologising, shrinking, rushing; replace them.","tr":"Replace your opening \"Sorry, quick question\" with a pause and the question.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G42","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 14 (\"start with a pause, not an apology\")","@askvinh on YouTube"],"rel":["g14"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Vinh points out habits that lower how people see you: over-apologising, shrinking your body, and rushing. Notice them, then replace them — start with a pause, not an apology."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Low-status habits”?","a":"Replace your opening \"Sorry, quick question\" with a pause and the question."},{"id":"g43","t":"vg","tp":"body","ti":"Video presence","mv":"Camera on at hello, torso and hands in shot, a bit more energy.","tr":"Raise your camera to eye level and sit back enough that your hands show.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G43","d":{"src":["@askvinh on YouTube","On Purpose with Jay Shetty"],"rel":["g15"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Camera on at hello, torso and hands in shot, good light and sound, and a bit more energy than in person."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Energy on video calls","note":"A simple picture for camera energy came first; the book folds it into a wider video checklist.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"YouTube · Aug 2025","text":"On video calls people slump, and slumped sounds low energy. Vinh's guide for energy on camera: imagine 10 to 20 people in the room with you and speak to that room.","card":"n-vg-m-camera-room"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Camera on at hello, torso and hands in shot, a bit more energy.","card":"g43"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Video presence”?","a":"Camera on at hello, torso and hands in shot, a bit more energy."},{"id":"g44","t":"vg","tp":"groups","ti":"Entering a group","mv":"Hover, catch an eye, wait for a natural gap, add to the topic.","tr":"Your first line should build on what they're discussing.","w":"Hover near the group, catch someone's eye, wait for a natural gap, then add to the topic they're already on rather than changing it.","ev":"Plausible","code":"G44","d":{"src":["YouTube \"How to enter conversations\"","@askvinh \"How to enter conversations\"","On Purpose with Jay Shetty"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Hover near the group, catch someone's eye, wait for a natural gap, then add to the topic they're already on rather than changing it."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Entering a group”?","a":"Hover near the group, catch someone's eye, wait for a natural gap, then add to the topic they're already on rather than changing it."},{"id":"h1","t":"ch","tp":"openers","ti":"Small-talk formula (Listen · Display · React · Ask · Offer)","mv":"Listen properly, show it on your face, react in a few words, ask one small question, offer a related story.","tr":"Run the loop once in your next chat: listen, show it, react, ask, then offer.","w":"He says most people \"wait to talk\" instead of listening.","ev":"Moderate (matches Huang 2017)","code":"H1","d":{"steps":["Listen","Display","React","Ask","Offer"],"lines":[{"say":"Hang on, that's brilliant. What happened next?","how":"","why":"Hughes' loop: listen, show it on your face, react briefly, ask one small question. Follow-up questions raise liking (Huang et al., 2017)."},{"say":"Oh, really? How did that come about?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Something similar happened to me once. But first, how did yours turn out?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 91–93, 448–450"],"rel":["h2"],"status":"USED","how":["Hughes' five-step loop: Listen properly; Display it on your face (react visibly to the high points); React with a few words; Ask one small follow-up question; then Offer a related story of your own. It matches research showing follow-up questions make people like you more.","His stated aim for small talk is that the other person leaves feeling interesting — not that you seem interesting.","Hughes' small-talk formula is a loop. Listen (be interested and remember names and stories). Display (let your face and body react). Ask (a small follow-up question). Offer (a related story or comment). Then repeat. Listen: he says remembering names is about motivation, not memory. Would you remember it for a thousand dollars? Try to get a \"full degree\" in someone's life, and keep notes in your phone contacts (children's names, favourite places, birthdays). Display: react visibly. \"Sounds like an amazing place to work!\" with raised eyebrows keeps them talking. Ask: a light follow-up such as \"planning to stay a while?\" makes them feel interesting. Offer: a short, related piece of your own life so it doesn't feel like an interview.","Hughes' small-talk cycle goes Listen, Display, Ask, Offer. Listen for the points they most want you to hear, then show interest, ask about those points and offer something of your own. When it slows down, start the circle again. He says most people \"wait to talk\" instead of listening. Spot what they want you to find most interesting about what they're saying. He calls small talk the gateway skill that makes everything else work."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"The small-talk loop","note":"The field guide's four-step loop gained a quick React step in the updated formula.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Behavior Ops Manual · 2023 (Read & Connect)","text":"Listen → Display (let your face react) → Ask a small follow-up → Offer a related story. Repeat.","card":null},{"label":"Updated","when":"Read Anyone formula · Nov 2026","text":"Listen properly, show it on your face, react in a few words, ask one small question, offer a related story.","card":"h1"}]}],"vb":"2023 → 2026","q":"Name the 5 steps of “Small-talk formula (Listen · Display · React · Ask · Offer)”.","a":"Listen → Display → React → Ask → Offer"},{"id":"h2","t":"ch","tp":"openers","ti":"Make them feel interesting, not interested","mv":"The aim of small talk is that they leave feeling interesting.","tr":"Ask yourself afterwards: \"Did they leave feeling interesting?\"","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H2","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I could listen to you talk about this all day. What's the best part?","how":"","why":"Hughes' aim for small talk: they leave feeling interesting, not impressed by you."},{"say":"I have a feeling you've got a good story or two. What's been keeping you busy?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"That was genuinely fascinating. Thanks for telling me about it.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 91"],"rel":["h33"],"status":"USED","how":["Hughes' goal for small talk: the other person should leave feeling interesting. Your curiosity does that — not your own clever stories."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Making people feel seen","note":"The book's 'make them feel interesting' became, in his 2026 interview, the most important people skill of all.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Behavior Ops Manual · 2023","text":"The aim of small talk is that they leave feeling interesting.","card":"h2"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Diary of a CEO · 2026","text":"The most important people skill: make people feel heard, resonate with them, don't judge.","card":"h19"}]}],"vb":"2023 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Make them feel interesting, not interested”?","a":"The aim of small talk is that they leave feeling interesting."},{"id":"h3","t":"ch","tp":"openers","ti":"Learn one new thing every conversation","mv":"Even with people you've known for years.","tr":"\"What's something you've got into lately that I don't know about?\"","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H3","d":{"lines":[{"say":"We've known each other ages and I've just realised I don't know this. What got you into it?","how":"","why":"Hughes: aim to learn one new thing in every conversation, even with people you know well. Curiosity shows."},{"say":"I've learned something today, thank you. Where did you pick that up?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 449"],"rel":["h2"],"status":"USED","how":["Set yourself a small goal: learn one new thing about the person in every conversation, even with people you've known for years. It keeps you curious and makes them feel interesting."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Learn one new thing every conversation","note":"Same habit, with a healthy caveat added.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"The Behavior Ops Manual","text":"Even with people you've known for years.","card":"h3"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Harder Not Smarter podcast · 2024","text":"Go into every conversation believing the person has something to teach you, while staying sceptical of what you are told.","card":null}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Learn one new thing every conversation”?","a":"Even with people you've known for years."},{"id":"h4","t":"ch","tp":"groups","ti":"Remember names and stories","mv":"It's motivation, not memory; keep notes.","tr":"After a conversation, jot down their name and one detail in your phone.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H4","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Sam, isn't it? Good to see you again.","how":"","why":"Hughes: remembering is about motivation, not memory. Using a name or detail shows they registered with you."},{"say":"Lovely to meet you, Priya. I'll remember the sailing story.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 448"],"status":"USED","how":["Hughes says remembering names and stories is about motivation, not memory. Decide it matters, and keep notes on what people tell you."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Remember names and stories”?","a":"After a conversation, jot down their name and one detail in your phone."},{"id":"h5","t":"ch","tp":"openers","ti":"Opening one-liners","mv":"\"How did you get started in the industry?\", \"What's your take on all this?\"","tr":"Use the setting: \"What's your take on the talk?\"","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H5","d":{"lines":[{"say":"How did you get started in this line of work?","how":"","why":"Hughes' starter questions invite opinion or origin stories, which people enjoy answering."},{"say":"What's your take on all this?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"What did you make of the talk?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 96"],"rel":["h6"],"status":"USED","how":["Hughes suggests simple, open starters like \"How did you get started in the industry?\" or \"What's your take on all this?\" — questions that invite a story or opinion.","Hughes offers simple openers: \"How did you get started in the industry?\", \"What's your opinion on all this?\", \"What did you think about…?\". For teens, ask about their favourite class today, or ask for help with something on your phone."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “How did you get started in …”","a":"“How did you get started in the industry?”"},{"id":"h6","t":"ch","tp":"openers","ti":"FORK topics","mv":"Family, Occupation, Recreation, Knowledge.","tr":"When you run dry, pick the next letter.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H6","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Have you got family nearby?","how":"","why":"Hughes' safe topic map: Family, Occupation, Recreation, Knowledge. Pick one and follow it."},{"say":"What do you do when you're not working?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"What's something you know loads about that most people don't?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 96"],"status":"USED","how":["A military acronym Hughes uses for small talk: Family (people close to them), Occupation (what they do and like most about it), Recreation (what they do for fun) and Knowledge (what they know a lot about).","A military acronym Hughes uses for the topics first conversations naturally flow around: Family, Occupation, Recreation and Knowledge. Use it to steer and to think of questions."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “FORK topics”?","a":"Family, Occupation, Recreation, Knowledge."},{"id":"h7","t":"ch","tp":"openers","ti":"Four Threads formula","mv":"Answer a basic question with four hooks they can grab.","tr":"Practise a four-thread answer to \"Where are you from?\" and \"What do you do?\"","w":"When someone asks you a basic question, answer with four hooks they can grab instead of one word.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H7","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Not bad. Busy week, a new puppy and far too little sleep. How about you?","how":"","why":"Hughes: answer a simple question with a few hooks, so the other person can pick whichever interests them."},{"say":"I'm in insurance, I've just got back from Lisbon, and I'm learning the guitar, badly. What about you?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 96–97, 450"],"status":"USED","how":["When someone asks you a basic question, answer with four hooks they can grab instead of one word. \"Where are you from?\" \"Houston\" ends the chat; \"Originally Houston, but I left at eighteen for the navy and spent years in Hawaii\" gives them four threads.","When asked a simple question, answer with the answer plus about four extra \"threads\" the other person can pick up. That gives them many ways to find common ground. His example: asked where he's from, he says Houston, then that he left as a teen to join the military, was stationed in Hawaii for thirteen years and loved it, and served as a navigator on a destroyer. Each detail (Houston, military, Hawaii, navigation) is a hook. A one-word answer kills the conversation. In the language section he calls this \"offering conversational threads\".","When someone asks a boring question (\"what do you do?\"), answer with a short story instead of a bare fact. That gives them threads to pull and keeps the conversation alive. He also describes connecting people at events: introduce one group to another until you're the common link everyone talks about."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Four Threads formula”?","a":"Practise a four-thread answer to \"Where are you from?\" and \"What do you do?\""},{"id":"h8","t":"ch","tp":"groups","ti":"\"Barkeep\": meet and introduce","mv":"Meet a group, then introduce them to another group.","tr":"At your next event, aim to introduce just two people who haven't met.","w":"You can stop at any phase.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H8","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Have you met Tom? You both run. Tom, tell her about the half-marathon.","how":"","why":"Hughes' 'barkeep' habit: meet people, then connect them to each other. Being the connector makes everyone feel included."},{"say":"Mind if I join you? I don't know a soul here.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 451–452"],"status":"USED","how":["Hughes' social challenge from military training: meet a small group and learn their names, then meet a second group, then introduce the second group to the first. Keep connecting people. Soon you're the thing everyone has in common. You can stop at any phase."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “\"Barkeep\": meet and introduce”?","a":"Meet a group, then introduce them to another group."},{"id":"h9","t":"ch","tp":"warmth","ti":"Sincere compliments","mv":"Compliment freely but never suck up.","tr":"Give the compliment and ask for nothing afterwards.","w":"Compliment freely but never suck up.","ev":"Moderate","code":"H9","d":{"lines":[{"say":"That was a really thoughtful way to handle it.","how":"","why":"Hughes: compliment freely but never suck up. Specific beats general."},{"say":"I like how you think. I'm glad we got talking.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 131"],"status":"USED","how":["Compliment freely, Hughes says, but never suck up. A compliment must be true and not tied to a request."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Sincere compliments”?","a":"Compliment freely but never suck up."},{"id":"h10","t":"ch","tp":"warmth","ti":"Share your insecurities","mv":"Openness invites openness; people like imperfect people.","tr":"I'll be honest, I'm hopeless at events like this. How are you finding it?","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Moderate (pratfall, self-disclosure)","code":"H10","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I'll be honest, I'm hopeless at events like this. How are you finding it?","how":"","why":"Hughes: people like imperfect people. A small, true admission invites the same back."},{"say":"I worry about that too, if I'm honest.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 132"],"status":"USED","how":["Openness invites openness. Hughes notes people like imperfect people, so sharing a real insecurity can make you easier to connect with.","Hughes recommends openly sharing your insecurities."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Share your insecurities”?","a":"I'll be honest, I'm hopeless at events like this. How are you finding it?"},{"id":"h11","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"Sincere reaction statement","mv":"Reflect what their day probably felt like (\"You must be shattered\") purely to show you understand them.","tr":"Say it, then stop and let them respond.","w":"Only to show understanding, never to fish.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H11","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Sounds like it's been a long week.","how":"","why":"Use it only to show you understand, never to fish."},{"say":"That must have been exhausting.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"That sounds like it's been really frustrating for you.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 348–349","The Diary of a CEO 2024"],"status":"USED (sincere form only)","how":["Keep only this sincere use, never using statements to fish for information."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “You must …”","a":"“You must be shattered”"},{"id":"h12","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"Verbal reflection / verbal mirror","mv":"Repeat their last few words or the theme back.","tr":"They: \"It's been a strange month.\" You: \"A strange month?\" Then wait.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Moderate (Voss; reflective listening)","code":"H12","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Completely out of the blue?","how":"","why":"Hughes (from FBI practice): repeat their last few words as a question. It shows you're listening and invites more."},{"say":"Not listened to?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"So the deadline's the real problem?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 354–356 (from FBI practice)"],"status":"USED","how":["Repeat the last few words someone said, or the theme, back to them. It shows you're listening and invites them to say more. Hughes draws it from FBI practice; Chris Voss teaches a similar mirror."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Verbal reflection / verbal mirror”?","a":"Repeat their last few words or the theme back."},{"id":"h13","t":"ch","tp":"warmth","ti":"Go first (sincere self-disclosure)","mv":"Share something real of your own first, at the depth you'd be happy for them to match, so it's easier for them to open up.","tr":"Share at the depth you'd like them to match, then ask.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Moderate","code":"H13","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I'll go first: my week's been a bit of a mess. How's yours been?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"I found my first year here really hard. Was it like that for you?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 350"],"status":"USED (sincere form only)","how":["Share something of your own to make it easier for them to share."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Go first (sincere self-disclosure)”?","a":"Share at the depth you'd like them to match, then ask."},{"id":"h14","t":"ch","tp":"openers","ti":"Sincere \"teach me\"","mv":"When you genuinely don't know, show real interest in their expertise and let them teach you.","tr":"Only ask what you really want to learn.","w":"Real interest in their expertise when you genuinely don't know.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H14","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I know nothing about this. Could you give me the two-minute version?","how":"","why":"Genuine interest in their expertise. Only ask when you really don't know."},{"say":"I might be missing something. Can you walk me through how you see it?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 356"],"status":"USED","how":["Show real interest in their expertise when you genuinely don't know."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Sincere \"teach me\"”?","a":"When you genuinely don't know, show real interest in their expertise and let them teach you."},{"id":"h15","t":"ch","tp":"needs","ti":"Needs Map as listening prompts","mv":"Significance, approval, acceptance, intelligence, pity (hardship), strength/power: hear the quiet question and answer it sincerely.","tr":"Listen for what they're showing right now; if you're unsure, pick Anyone and ask a follow-up.","w":"Never say \"you're a significance person\". The needs are hunches to test by listening, never labels.","ev":"Coach's view","code":"H15","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 366–372","The Diary of a CEO 2024","Chase's needs Short"],"status":"USED (the Lean chips)","how":["Hughes describes six social needs: significance, approval, acceptance, intelligence, pity (shown here as Hardship) and strength or power. they're the Lean chips — listening prompts for the quiet question someone might be asking, never labels for people. Most people show a mix.","The quiet questions: Significance — do people see my impact? Approval — am I doing this right? Acceptance — do I belong? Intelligence — do people see I'm sharp? Hardship — does anyone see how hard this has been? Power — do I get the final say? Answer the quiet question sincerely."],"go":[{"tab":"profile","label":"Open the six needs guide"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Needs Map as listening prompts”?","a":"Never say \"you're a significance person\". The needs are hunches to test by listening, never labels."},{"id":"h16","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"Values Map: \"so I can finally…\"","mv":"Listen for the end-goal (connection, information, recognition, experience, freedom, growth) and ask about it.","tr":"\"And once that's sorted, what will it let you do?\"","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Coach's view","code":"H16","d":{"lines":[{"say":"And once that's sorted, what will it let you finally do?","how":"","why":"Hughes' values map: listen for what someone wants to finally be free to do, and ask about it. It reaches what really matters to them."},{"say":"What's the bit of this that really matters to you?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 383–386"],"rel":["h15"],"status":"USED (honest questions only)","how":["Listen for the end goal behind what people want — often heard as \"so I can finally…\". Hughes lists six values: connection, information, recognition, experience, freedom and growth. Use this only to ask honest questions about what matters to them."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “so I …”","a":"“so I can finally…”"},{"id":"h17","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Decision style, spoken honestly","mv":"Give the reason that matters to how they decide (practical, return, new, what others do). Never to pressure.","tr":"If they care about practicality, lead with the practical reason, truthfully.","w":"Only ever give the honest reason. If it isn't true, don't say it.","ev":"Coach's view","code":"H17","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Here's the practical side, then it's your decision.","how":"","why":"Never to pressure."},{"say":"No rush. Have a look at the numbers and let me know what you think.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"For what it's worth, it's worked well for plenty of people in your position.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 378–382"],"status":"USED (honest framing only)","how":["Hughes describes six decision styles: deviance, novelty, social, conformity, necessity and investment (people often use two neighbouring ones). Use them only to give the honest reason that matters to how someone decides — practical, value, new, or what others do — never to pressure."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Decision style, spoken honestly”?","a":"Give the reason that matters to how they decide (practical, return, new, what others do). Never to pressure."},{"id":"h18","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"Use their words / pronouns","mv":"Notice their sensory words and I/we/they habits and speak in their terms — sincerely.","tr":"If they say \"the team\", talk about \"the team\", not \"you\".","w":"Speak in their terms to be understood, never to steer a pitch.","ev":"Plausible (VAK matching not supported)","code":"H18","d":{"lines":[{"say":"How's the team finding it?","how":"","why":"Hughes: notice their words and whether they say 'I' or 'we', then speak in their terms. Kept sincere: no steering."},{"say":"When you say it feels heavy, what's the heaviest part?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 393–400"],"status":"USED (no persuasion use)","how":["Notice the words and pronouns people use (I, we, they; see, hear, feel) and speak in their terms — sincerely, to be understood."],"go":[{"tab":"say","label":"Get more lines in Say"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Use their words / pronouns”?","a":"Speak in their terms to be understood, never to steer a pitch."},{"id":"h19","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Heard and seen, without judgment","mv":"The most important people skill: make people feel heard, resonate with them, don't judge.","tr":"Before responding, check: have I shown I heard them?","w":"The most important people skill: make people feel heard, resonate with them, don't judge.","ev":"Moderate","code":"H19","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I can tell this really matters to you. I'm listening.","how":"","why":"Hughes (DOAC 2026): the core people skill is making someone feel heard and seen, without judging."},{"say":"That makes complete sense, given everything you've dealt with.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Thanks for telling me. I'm glad you did.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Diary of a CEO 2026"],"rel":["h20"],"status":"USED","how":["In his 2026 Diary of a CEO interview, Hughes said the most important people skill is making people feel heard and seen — resonating with them without judging. He thinks it will matter more as AI grows."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Making people feel seen","note":"The book's 'make them feel interesting' became, in his 2026 interview, the most important people skill of all.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Behavior Ops Manual · 2023","text":"The aim of small talk is that they leave feeling interesting.","card":"h2"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Diary of a CEO · 2026","text":"The most important people skill: make people feel heard, resonate with them, don't judge.","card":"h19"}]}],"vb":"2023 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Heard and seen, without judgment”?","a":"Before responding, check: have I shown I heard them?"},{"id":"h20","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Resonate before you redirect","mv":"Acknowledge their view first, then offer yours (\"get into their river\").","tr":"\"I can see why that matters to you. Here's where I land…\"","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Moderate","code":"H20","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I can see why you'd see it that way. Can I tell you where I'm coming from?","how":"","why":"Hughes (DOAC 2026): get into their river first. Acknowledge their view before offering yours."},{"say":"You've got a fair point there. Here's the bit I'm still stuck on.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Before I say anything, I want to make sure I've got your side right.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Diary of a CEO 2026"],"status":"USED","how":["Acknowledge someone's view first, genuinely, before you offer yours. Hughes calls it getting into their river before trying to steer the boat."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “get into …”","a":"“get into their river”"},{"id":"h21","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"People are reasons (Four Ways of Seeing People; Laws of Behaviour)","mv":"Everyone is suffering and wearing a mask; see behaviour as having reasons, not as broken.","tr":"When someone's difficult, ask \"What might be behind this?\"","w":"Seeing hostility as self-protection removes your own anger and judgement, so you can respond calmly and with curiosity instead of escalating.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H21","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I don't think you're being difficult. Something about this is clearly weighing on you.","how":"","why":"Hughes' laws of behaviour: everyone is carrying something. Seeing behaviour as having reasons, not as 'difficult', lowers the heat."},{"say":"I'm sure you had your reasons. Can you tell me what they were?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 297–302"],"status":"USED","how":["Hughes asks you to see everyone as carrying something and wearing a mask: behaviour has reasons, so people aren't broken — something is driving them.","Hughes' laws are as follows. Everyone is suffering and insecure. Everyone wears a mask, and everyone pretends not to. Everyone is a product of childhood suffering and reward. The fifth, \"everyone is a drug addict with different drugs\", means each of us craves a particular social need. He estimates that most interpersonal patterns are set by about age 12. Its point is that people repeat whatever gives them their need-hit.","Hughes describes a ladder of viewpoints. At the bottom, people are broken (you try to fix or retaliate). Next they are different (you feel without acting), then they are facts (you calm down). At the top, people are reasons: every behaviour has a cause, and judgement disappears.","Hughes uses an eel that bites when cornered to reframe aggression. It's usually a product of what hurt someone, like an animal defending itself. Seeing hostility as self-protection removes your own anger and judgement, so you can respond calmly and with curiosity instead of escalating.","Hughes describes common \"masks\". One is the overachiever who can't stop moving: often not just ambitious but afraid of what surfaces if they stop performing. He also turns the question back on the viewer: ask why you want so badly to see behind other people's masks. The urge can be about your own gap. Everyone is hiding something, which calls for compassion, not leverage."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “People are reasons (Four Ways of Seeing People; Laws of Behaviour)”?","a":"Seeing hostility as self-protection removes your own anger and judgement, so you can respond calmly and with curiosity instead of escalating."},{"id":"h22","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Golden bridge","mv":"Let the other person back down without losing face.","tr":"Give them an easy, dignified way out: \"With what you knew then, that made sense.\"","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H22","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Let's both take a breath and come back to it tomorrow.","how":"","why":"Hughes: leave the other person a graceful way back. People climb down more easily when they can keep face."},{"say":"To be fair, none of us had the full picture at the time.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"No harm done. We're good.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 51"],"rel":["h20"],"status":"USED","how":["From Sun Tzu: build your opponent a golden bridge to retreat across. In everyday terms, let the other person back down without losing face."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Golden bridge”?","a":"Give them an easy, dignified way out: \"With what you knew then, that made sense.\""},{"id":"h23","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Handling interruptions","mv":"Calmly: \"One sec, James, let me finish this thought.\"","tr":"Keep your voice level and don't speed up.","w":"Then add \"One sec, James.\" Finally, stop, shift your attention elsewhere and excuse yourself.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H23","d":{"lines":[{"say":"One sec, James. Let me finish this thought, then I'm all yours.","how":"","why":"Hughes: handle interruptions calmly, using their name, and promise to hear them next."},{"say":"Hold that thought, I want to hear it. Let me finish this bit first.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 454–455"],"status":"USED","how":["Stay calm and use their name: \"One sec, James — let me finish this thought.\" Then finish it.","For repeated interruptions, Hughes escalates calmly. First keep talking and raise a hand. Then add \"One sec, James.\" Finally, stop, shift your attention elsewhere and excuse yourself."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “One sec, James, let me finish …”","a":"“One sec, James, let me finish this thought.”"},{"id":"h24","t":"ch","tp":"voice","ti":"Speak plainly (drop hedges)","mv":"Cut \"just\", \"honestly\", \"I'll try\", \"maybe\", needless tag questions.","tr":"Re-read your next email and delete every \"just\".","w":"Say a price, opinion or request once, calmly, then stop.","ev":"Moderate","code":"H24","d":{"lines":[{"say":"I'll send it over by Friday.","how":"","why":"Hughes: hedges like 'just', 'honestly' and 'I'll try' leak doubt. Plain words sound trustworthy."},{"say":"I don't agree, and here's why.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"I got that wrong. Here's what I'll do about it.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 453, 462–463"],"status":"USED","how":["Cut words that weaken what you say: \"just\", \"honestly\", \"I'll try\", \"maybe\" and needless tag questions (\"…, right?\"). Say a price, opinion or request once, calmly, then stop.","Hughes says modern speech is washed out by fear of offending, and leaders speak with candour instead. Direct doesn't mean rude. He also advises dropping tag questions like \"…right?\" because they signal you need others' confirmation.","Hughes says listeners stop focusing when they feel they've heard it before, know the outcome, or are buried in wordy fluff. He contrasts these with short attention-grabbing transitions (\"Here's the problem…\", \"That's only half the story…\", \"Make no mistake…\") that spike focus before a key point."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Speak plainly (drop hedges)”?","a":"Say a price, opinion or request once, calmly, then stop."},{"id":"h25","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Honest objection handling (Sympathise · Socialise · Select · Show · Secure)","mv":"I felt the same; many people do; which part worries you most; here's the real picture; does that help? Only with true statements.","tr":"Only say \"I felt the same\" if you did.","w":"Only with true statements.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H25","d":{"steps":["Sympathise: 'I had the same question when I first saw this.'","Socialise: 'Most people ask exactly that.'","Select: pinpoint the real issue with one question.","Show: what's actually true, in their language.","Secure: a simple check — 'Does that make sense for you?'"],"lines":[{"say":"I had the same worry at first. Which part bothers you most?","how":"","why":"Hughes' objection steps, kept honest: sympathise, normalise if true, find the real worry, show the facts, check it helped."},{"say":"You're not the first to ask that, and it's a fair question.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Does that help, or is something still nagging at you?","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 538–539"],"status":"USED (honest form)","how":["Hughes' five steps, used here only with true statements: Sympathise (\"I felt the same\"), Socialise (\"many people do\"), Select (\"which part worries you most?\"), Show (the real picture) and Secure (\"does that help?\").","Hughes' five-step formula for objections goes: Sympathise (you understand), Socialise (others felt the same), Select (pinpoint the real concern), Show (address it with evidence) and Secure (confirm and move forward). His example: a customer worried about the time commitment. You say you thought the same at first and many people assume it takes hours. Then you check that time is the real concern, show how little it takes and confirm the next step."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Handling a hesitation","note":"Same five S's; the updated version insists every step uses only true statements.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Behavior Ops Manual · 2023 (Talk playbook)","text":"Sympathise → Socialise → Select → Show → Secure, as a quick playbook for any hesitation.","card":null},{"label":"Updated","when":"Read Anyone · honest form","text":"I felt the same; many people do; which part worries you most; here's the real picture; does that help? Only with true statements.","card":"h25"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"Name the 5 steps of “Honest objection handling (Sympathise · Socialise · Select · Show · Secure)”.","a":"Sympathise → Socialise → Select → Show → Secure"},{"id":"h26","t":"ch","tp":"groups","ti":"Use names, answer the implication","mv":"Use people's names; answer both the question and its hidden implication calmly.","tr":"If asked \"Is this on schedule?\", answer that and the worry behind it.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"Plausible","code":"H26","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Fair question, Sarah. Yes, I did, and here's why.","how":"","why":"Hughes: use people's names, and answer both the question and what it implies, calmly."},{"say":"Thanks, Mark. That really helped.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 887–889"],"rel":["h4"],"status":"USED","how":["Use people's names, and when you're asked a question, answer both the question and its hidden implication — calmly."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Use names, answer the implication”?","a":"Use people's names; answer both the question and its hidden implication calmly."},{"id":"h27","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Gratitude and enjoyment (authority traits)","mv":"Interpersonal gratitude and visible enjoyment of the moment are magnetic.","tr":"Thank one person specifically today and let yourself enjoy the conversation.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Moderate (gratitude research)","code":"H27","d":{"lines":[{"say":"Thank you for making time for this. I know your diary's packed.","how":"","why":"Hughes lists gratitude and visible enjoyment as authority traits: people are drawn to someone who's glad to be there."},{"say":"I'm really enjoying this, by the way.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Thanks for coming. It's good to see you.","how":"","why":""}],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 148–153"],"status":"USED","how":["Two of Hughes' authority traits are gratitude towards people and visible enjoyment of the moment. He calls enjoyment highly magnetic. Gratitude has good research support.","Hughes defines gratitude as being able to \"zoom out\" and feel thankful for the big picture (high-altitude) and \"zoom in\" on small details of the moment (low-altitude). Interpersonal gratitude is an inner thankfulness for the people around you, which others can feel. His taco story: a friend got him to see the whole chain behind one taco, from the trained staff to the recipe investment to the farmers and their families. High-altitude thoughts include being grateful that the Earth is still here at all. He suggests a phone reminder to \"zoom out\" daily, and cites studies linking gratitude journals and gratitude letters to better mood, recovery, sleep and fewer pain symptoms. Gratitude makes the other authority traits more contagious and helps you keep perspective when stakes are high.","Hughes defines enjoyment as being able to find, and feel happy about, the details of an experience without depending on outside events. He says \"charisma is a by-product of enjoyment.\" It differs from excitement because it doesn't need something good to happen first. He uses Dan Millman's \"Way of the Peaceful Warrior\", where the mentor turns cleaning bathroom floors into a meditation, to show enjoyment as a matter of perspective. People who enjoy life become magnetic, which increases the effect of everything else they do."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Gratitude and enjoyment (authority traits)”?","a":"Interpersonal gratitude and visible enjoyment of the moment are magnetic."},{"id":"h28","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"ACSS: Authority, Comfort, Social skill, Skills","mv":"Fix comfort and authority before scripts.","tr":"Before an event, work on calm rather than lines.","w":"People avoid trying until they think it's perfect.","ev":"","code":"H28","d":{"src":["The Diary of a CEO 2024","The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 938–943"],"rel":["h30"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Hughes' order of work: Authority and Comfort come before Social skills and Skills. Fix your comfort first — aim to be the most comfortable person in the room — and scripts matter much less.","Hughes says problems with these skills almost always trace to observation, communication or self-management. He diagnoses them with the ACSS framework: Authority, Comfort, Social skills or Skill development. He first checks which side of the Taxonomy of Influence the trouble sits on: you (operator) or them (subject). Then he asks direct questions about each ACSS area and walks through a real scenario. A common finding is Comfort. People avoid trying until they think it's perfect. He notes that being honest isn't the same as being self-aware, so get outside input."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Fixing what isn't working","note":"The book's troubleshooting order became the four-letter ACSS check he teaches in interviews.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Behavior Ops Manual · 2023","text":"Hughes says problems with these skills almost always trace to observation, communication or self-management. He diagnoses them with the ACSS framework: Authority, Comfort, Social skills or Skill development.","card":null},{"label":"Updated","when":"Diary of a CEO · 2024","text":"Fix comfort and authority before scripts.","card":"h28"}]}],"vb":"2023 → 2024","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “ACSS: Authority, Comfort, Social skill, Skills”?","a":"Before an event, work on calm rather than lines."},{"id":"h29","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Authority Triangle (behaviour, effect, habits) and five authority traits (confidence, discipline, leadership, gratitude, enjoyment)","mv":"Self-work; the Hughes Authority Inventory.","tr":"Pick one area of life order to tidy this week.","w":"It's self-work, not a tactic, and it isn't validated by research.","ev":"","code":"H29","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 32, 122–198"],"rel":["h28"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Hughes sees authority as coming from behaviour, effect and habits, and from five traits: confidence, discipline, leadership, gratitude and enjoyment. He links it to order in your life (environment, time, appearance, social life, finances). It's self-work, not a tactic, and it isn't validated by research.","Hughes' recipe for real authority has three sides. Behaviour: your inner traits (confidence, discipline, leadership, gratitude, enjoyment). Effect: what others unconsciously read (movement, appearance, confidence, connection, intent). Habits: lifestyle areas that leak through your nonverbals (environment, time, appearance, social, financial). The Effect side holds the \"tripwires\" people check, without knowing it, before treating you as an authority. The Habits side matters because a messy environment, poor time control or money stress create nervous behaviour that others pick up as negative gut feelings. He gives a self-assessment, the Hughes Authority Inventory, to score each part.","Hughes' five traits that create authority and followership are Confidence, Discipline, Leadership, Gratitude and Enjoyment. He says it doesn't matter whether authority is genuine or only perceived, because both produce obedience. His focus is on building the genuine version. For each trait he covers what it means, how it trips the authority tripwires, the habits that build it, and what it looks like to others. He also gives a five-level self-assessment for each in the Hughes Authority Inventory.","The Hughes Authority Inventory is a self-assessment that scores the five authority traits on five levels each, plus a 1-5 lifestyle assessment across environment, time, appearance, social and financial. Its purpose is to show your weakest areas so you know what to work on. Each trait has checklists by level. A low-confidence level two includes not putting the phone down socially, backing down easily, comparing yourself to others and allowing put-downs. Level five confidence is contagious, takes criticism well and has no need for reassurance. Leadership level one takes credit for others' work and blames others; level four gives credit, cares about others' growth and makes people feel significant. The rule: aim one level higher than where most of your marks fall, and if you scored one or two, aim for three or four quickly. Each low score is a prompt to find the limiting belief or habit behind it.","On Diary of a CEO, Hughes named the elements that make people see you as an authority: confidence, discipline, leadership, gratitude and enjoyment. He then recapped Milgram to show how strongly people respond to authority."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"The authority traits","note":"Same traits, retold more simply on his podcast.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Behavior Ops Manual · 2023","text":"Hughes' five traits that create authority and followership are Confidence, Discipline, Leadership, Gratitude and Enjoyment. He says it doesn't matter whether authority is genuine or only perceived, because both produce obedience.","card":null},{"label":"Updated","when":"Podcast","text":"On Diary of a CEO, Hughes named the elements that make people see you as an authority: confidence, discipline, leadership, gratitude and enjoyment. He then recapped Milgram to show how strongly people respond to authority.","card":null}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Authority Triangle (behaviour, effect, habits) and five authority traits (confidence, discipline, leadership, gratitude, enjoyment)”?","a":"Self-work; the Hughes Authority Inventory."},{"id":"h30","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Composure: avoid collapse and posturing","mv":"Composure rating scale and pendulum; \"this is part of life\".","tr":"Notice which way you swing under pressure, and aim for the middle.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","code":"H30","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 154–158, 164"],"rel":["h32"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Hughes describes a pendulum with two traps at either end: collapse (shrinking, apologising, going small) and posturing (puffing up, acting big). Composure sits in the middle. When things go wrong, tell yourself \"this is part of life\".","Hughes' pendulum has Composure in the centre and two insecure extremes. Collapse means shrinking mentally and physically to please others. Posturing means inflating your importance to impress or intimidate. Both come from the fear of not being enough. His restaurant example: given an undercooked steak, the collapser hesitates, then apologises for mentioning it and says it's fine. The posturer makes a scene. The composed person calmly asks for it to be fixed. Both extremes try to get something from the other person (respect, love, money), hide their agenda behind a mask, live in a competitive frame and are exhausting. He says posturers often feel like impostors because they are acting. He quotes \"Stress is who you think you should be. Relaxation is who you are.\" Exercise: rate yourself on the composure scale every day for a month, just measuring, without judging. He repeats a line from his training: \"you judge, you lose.\""]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Composure","note":"The pendulum (collapse, composure, posturing) gained a daily practice: treat everyone the same and score yourself each night.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"The Behavior Ops Manual","text":"Composure rating scale and pendulum; \"this is part of life\".","card":"h30"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Doug Bopst podcast · 2025","text":"The mistake Hughes says sinks most people is ranking yourself above or below the person in front of you.","card":"k-m-same-with-everyone"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"Finish the line: “this is part …”","a":"“this is part of life”"},{"id":"h31","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Performance vs connection mode","mv":"Talking to people is not a performance; focus on them; third-person camera view.","tr":"When you feel self-conscious, ask them a question.","w":"Talking to people is not a performance; focus on them; third-person camera view.","ev":"","code":"H31","d":{"steps":["Talking to people is not a performance","focus on them","third-person camera view"],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 164"],"rel":["h33"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Talking to people isn't a performance. Hughes says shift your focus onto them instead of on how you're coming across — like switching from a camera pointed at you to one pointed at them.","Hughes says talking to people isn't a performance. In performance mode you're tense, watching yourself, analysing afterwards, feeling evaluated and focused on yourself. In connection mode you're relaxed, curious and focused on the other person."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Name the 3 steps of “Performance vs connection mode”.","a":"Talking to people is not a performance → focus on them → third-person camera view"},{"id":"h32","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Nervous = excited","mv":"Reframe the same arousal as excitement (Brooks 2014).","tr":"Before you walk in, say \"I'm excited\" to yourself.","w":"Relabelling the arousal as excitement works with your physiology instead of against it.","ev":"","code":"H32","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 164–165"],"rel":["h30","h42"],"status":"DELIVERY (Moderate)","how":["Nerves and excitement feel almost the same in the body. Hughes suggests reframing nerves as excitement — research by Alison Wood Brooks supports saying \"I'm excited\" over \"calm down\".","Hughes cites research summarised by Weisinger and Pawliw-Fry: people told to say \"I'm excited\" before a stressful task did better than people told to calm down. Nerves and excitement feel almost the same in the body, so choose the interpretation deliberately. Trying and failing to calm down feels like a failure and makes things worse. Relabelling the arousal as excitement works with your physiology instead of against it."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Nervous = excited”?","a":"Reframe the same arousal as excitement (Brooks 2014)."},{"id":"h33","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Role application","mv":"Give yourself a role before you walk in: \"My job is to make them feel interesting.\"","tr":"Pick a role for your next event — host, connector or question-asker.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","code":"H33","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 130"],"rel":["h2"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Give yourself a role before you walk in: \"My job is to make them feel interesting.\" A role takes the focus off you.","Hughes notes discomfort vanishes when you're playing a clear role. You might feel like an impostor walking into a luxury shop, but not if your boss sent you there with an unlimited company card. Build roles around your strengths and step into them."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “My job is to make them …”","a":"“My job is to make them feel interesting.”"},{"id":"h34","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Rules of confidence","mv":"Everyone's faking it; expect good outcomes; worst case is small; don't compare.","tr":"Before something scary, name the realistic worst case — it's usually small.","w":"Everyone's faking it; expect good outcomes; worst case is small; don't compare.","ev":"","code":"H34","d":{"steps":["Everyone's faking it","expect good outcomes","worst case is small","don't compare"],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 129, 134"],"rel":["h32"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Hughes' rules: everyone's faking it to some degree; expect good outcomes; the worst case is usually small; don't compare yourself with others.","Hughes defines confidence as feeling comfortable in a situation and sure of your future safety and a good outcome. He calls it \"having a stellar reputation with yourself\", built through repetition and control, not faked externally. Confident people move without hesitation, which trips the movement tripwire. Their self-assurance makes others comfortable. Signs he lists: open, comfortable posture, relaxed lowered shoulders, ease tilting the head or being physically vulnerable, a comfortable stance, relaxed breathing, a walk with conviction, comfort with appropriate touch, and genuine visible reactions to others' stories. Tips: define what confidence looks like for you; use role-application (you'd walk into a luxury shop confidently if your boss sent you with the company card); do one good thing for your body daily; share your insecurities, because people like imperfect people and it disarms judgement; try cold showers; work on your voice; use a to-do list to build proof you get things done; don't compare yourself to others; let future regret drive you.","Hughes' everyday confidence builders: do one thing for your body daily, take cold showers (he cites studies on energy, metabolism and absenteeism), work on your voice, use a to-do list to collect evidence you get things done, stop comparing yourself to others (\"comparison is the thief of confidence\"), and let future regret, not present comfort, drive your choices. He adds that people get addicted to others' approval when they don't approve of themselves. Self-approval through kept promises is the cure."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Rules of confidence”?","a":"Before something scary, name the realistic worst case — it's usually small."},{"id":"h35","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Limiting beliefs, self-talk, growth mindset","mv":"Identify and rewrite limiting beliefs.","tr":"Write one limiting belief and a truer rewrite.","w":"\"You're not a finished product until you're dead.\"","ev":"","code":"H35","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 166–178"],"rel":["h34"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Spot the beliefs that hold you back (\"I'm bad with people\"), question them and rewrite them. Hughes pairs this with a growth mindset.","Hughes' \"Limiting Belief Impeachment Process\" means questioning the validity of beliefs that hold you back. Most fall into a few groups: comparing yourself to others, worrying about status, negative talk about your ability, self-judgement and self-doubt. The key question is \"Is this helpful?\", not \"Is this true?\" The easiest way to spot one is to listen for thoughts that justify not being able to do something. He gives internal and external worksheets (finish sentences like \"When I meet someone with far more money than me, I assume they…\") and insists you actually write them, noting that resisting the exercise may itself reveal a limiting belief. He lists common limiting beliefs about money (often learned from parents), relationships, health, work and yourself, for example \"I've failed before, so I'll fail again\" or \"there will always be people better than me\".","Hughes says the unsuccessful people he's known saw themselves as a \"finished product\". The successful ones had what Carol Dweck calls a growth mindset: skills come from effort and can always be developed. \"You're not a finished product until you're dead.\"","Hughes loved \"bloom where you're planted\" as a child, but as an adult realised it kept him stuck. Adults can choose where they're planted. Many of us keep beliefs that protected or rewarded us once but no longer fit. He notes some limiting beliefs hide or wear a \"helpful costume\". You rarely delete a belief. What changes is your relationship to it and how you hear it when it speaks.","Hughes says the single trait he'd give a salesperson isn't scripts or techniques but confidence. Limiting beliefs are the \"soil\" that must be dealt with first. There's no five-step trick: step outside your comfort zone, because that's what surfaces the beliefs so you can confront them."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Limiting beliefs","note":"The book's step-by-step impeachment of a belief; the later video adds that the soil comes first.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Behavior Ops Manual · 2023","text":"Hughes' \"Limiting Belief Impeachment Process\" means questioning the validity of beliefs that hold you back.","card":null},{"label":"Updated","when":"YouTube","text":"Hughes says the single trait he'd give a salesperson isn't scripts or techniques but confidence. Limiting beliefs are the \"soil\" that must be dealt with first.","card":null}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Limiting beliefs, self-talk, growth mindset”?","a":"Identify and rewrite limiting beliefs."},{"id":"h36","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Lifestyle traits (environment, time, appearance, social, finances)","mv":"Life order shows up in presence.","tr":"Pick the area that feels messiest and make one small fix.","w":"What you do when nobody's looking shows up in how you come across.","ev":"","code":"H36","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 179–183"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Hughes believes order in five areas — environment, time, appearance, social life and finances — shows up in how you carry yourself.","Hughes' five lifestyle \"mastery zones\" are, in order: Environment, Time, Appearance, Social and Financial. Mastering each one aligns your unconscious signals so people sense authority. What you do when nobody's looking shows up in how you come across. Environment: never walk past a mess, clear the car when you get out, keep digital files tidy. Time: put everything in a calendar, set priorities, say no to lower-priority requests, find calm-enjoyment in tasks you'd rather skip. Appearance: health, hygiene, posture and dressing in line with respected people in your world. He says appearance often matters more socially than achievements, and cites attractiveness research. Social: accept compliments gracefully, introduce people, tell stories, make strangers laugh, handle difficult conversations, ask favours, stay present. Financial: plan ahead, resist impulse buys, pay bills on time, avoid unnecessary debt. Each has a 1-5 self-rating list."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Lifestyle traits (environment, time, appearance, social, finances)”?","a":"What you do when nobody's looking shows up in how you come across."},{"id":"h37","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Move slower than the room; calm-assertive energy","mv":"Slow, deliberate movement reads as trust.","tr":"Walk in at pool speed.","w":"Hughes' rule of thumb: never move faster than you would in a swimming pool.","ev":"","code":"H37","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 144, 880"],"rel":["h30"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Fear speeds the body up. Hughes' rule of thumb: never move faster than you would in a swimming pool. Slow, deliberate movement reads as calm and trustworthy.","Never move faster than you would in a swimming pool. Fast movements trigger the same fear response (fast eyelid shutter) in others."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Move slower than the room; calm-assertive energy”?","a":"Walk in at pool speed."},{"id":"h38","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Display (face shows their story)","mv":"React visibly to the high points of their story.","tr":"Let your eyebrows and smile move when they hit the good part.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"","code":"H38","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 92, 129"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["React visibly to the high points of their story. Your face tells them you're with them — the \"Display\" step of the small-talk formula."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Display (face shows their story)”?","a":"React visibly to the high points of their story."},{"id":"h39","t":"ch","tp":"voice","ti":"Tonality and pauses","mv":"Emphasis changes meaning; pause around key words.","tr":"Say \"I didn't say she took it\" stressing a different word each time.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","code":"H39","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 498–499"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Where you put the emphasis changes the meaning of a sentence, so choose the word you stress, and pause around the key words.","Hughes says pauses before and after key phrases make them stand out, and tone carries meaning beyond the words. Statements spoken with a downward inflection sound certain."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Tonality and pauses”?","a":"Emphasis changes meaning; pause around key words."},{"id":"h40","t":"ch","tp":"stories","ti":"Speak to the mammal / descriptive language / move impact to the right","mv":"Concrete pictures; put the emotional reveal at the end; Journey–Connection–Reveal.","tr":"Replace one abstract word with a picture in your next story.","w":"His craft tips are to show rather than tell (\"leaves crunched underfoot\", not \"it was autumn\") and to cut redundant words (\"sat down on the seat\").","ev":"","code":"H40","d":{"steps":["Concrete pictures","put the emotional reveal at the end","Journey–Connection–Reveal"],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 452, 456–461, 473–484"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Use concrete pictures people can see, and move the emotional reveal to the end of the sentence or story so it lands. Hughes outlines a Journey–Connection–Reveal shape.","Put the emotional punch at the end of the sentence. Right-branching sentences land harder because the listener's focus builds up to the reveal.","Hughes says stories capture focus and carry tribal authority. They bring the listener \"to the fire\" with the tribe and transfer emotion. While a story runs, the critical mind relaxes and the listener feels what's described. His craft tips are to show rather than tell (\"leaves crunched underfoot\", not \"it was autumn\") and to cut redundant words (\"sat down on the seat\"). Open with sudden, confusing conflict and use cliffhangers. Avoid clichés and phrases that weaken conviction. A captivating story has six basic steps, and he simplifies the Hero's Journey for speaking.","Hughes summarises Campbell's 17-step Hero's Journey and simpler versions. For talking, he boils it down to Journey–Connection–Reveal: take them on a short journey, connect it to them, then deliver the reveal at the end. In trial work he treats the client as the hero (or anti-hero) of their own story. He asks what makes each person the hero in their own eyes."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Speak to the mammal / descriptive language / move impact to the right”?","a":"Replace one abstract word with a picture in your next story."},{"id":"h41","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Baseline and change (the \"five Cs\" in NCI write-ups)","mv":"Notice changes from their normal, read clusters in context, then ask — never conclude.","tr":"Gently ask: \"Is this a comfortable thing to talk about?\"","w":"A change usually means stress or discomfort. Ask gently; never jump to a conclusion.","ev":"","code":"H41","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 216–217, 302","NCI"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Notice changes from someone's normal (their baseline), read clusters of signals in context and culture rather than one gesture — then ask, never conclude."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Baseline and change (the \"five Cs\" in NCI write-ups)”?","a":"Notice changes from their normal, read clusters in context, then ask — never conclude."},{"id":"h42","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Stop-worrying questions and day-tight compartments","mv":"Two questions (what's worrying me, what can I do about it); live one day at a time.","tr":"Write both answers down, do the first step, let the rest wait.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","code":"H42","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 165–166"],"rel":["h32","h30"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Two questions: what's worrying me, and what can I do about it? Then live in \"day-tight compartments\" — one day at a time.","Hughes borrows the image of a ship's watertight compartments: seal off yesterday and tomorrow and put all your effort into today's compartment. \"If I take care of today, tomorrow will take care of itself.\""]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Stop-worrying questions and day-tight compartments”?","a":"Two questions (what's worrying me, what can I do about it); live one day at a time."},{"id":"h43","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Coaching checklist","mv":"A short self-coaching checklist to review your own interactions.","tr":"After an important chat, ask: did I listen, did they feel interesting, did I stay composed?","w":"Hughes' eight points for coaching yourself: set the example when no one's looking, track every day and practise calm enjoyment.","ev":"","code":"H43","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 946"],"status":"DELIVERY","how":["Hughes ends with a short self-coaching checklist for reviewing your own interactions afterwards.","Hughes' eight points for coaching yourself: set the example when no one's looking, track every day and practise calm enjoyment. Look after your health and prioritise your future self. Keep going back to the Authority Assessment, never complain, and approach every issue with leadership, enjoyment and composure."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Coaching checklist”?","a":"After an important chat, ask: did I listen, did they feel interesting, did I stay composed?"},{"id":"n-vve-about","t":"vve","tp":"teachers","ti":"Who is Vanessa Van Edwards?","mv":"Vanessa Van Edwards runs Science of People, a behaviour research lab, and writes about the science of connection.","tr":"Pick Van Edwards on the Teacher chips when you want openers, warmth and practical cues.","w":"Treat it as a coach's view: test it in real life and keep what works.","ev":"","d":{"src":["scienceofpeople.com"],"how":["Vanessa Van Edwards runs Science of People, a behaviour research lab, and writes about the science of connection. On this page she's the teacher for openers, warmth and competence cues, follow-up questions and the nine power questions.","Her three books here are Captivate (2017, fourteen \"hacks\" for first impressions and connection), Cues (2022, the body, voice and word signals behind charisma) and Conversation (2026, the three-level Conversation Blueprint). She curates a lot of research, but a few of her exact numbers are illustrations, so hold those loosely."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Who is Vanessa Van Edwards?”?","a":"Vanessa Van Edwards runs Science of People, a behaviour research lab, and writes about the science of connection."},{"id":"n-vve-power-questions","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"The nine power questions","mv":"Vanessa's nine questions, three per level. Ease: exciting work, fun plans, passion project.","tr":"Use them in order. Save the Level 3 ones for people who already feel safe with you.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Conversation, Levels 1–3"],"rel":["v2"],"how":["Vanessa's nine questions, three per level. Ease: exciting work, fun plans, passion project. Connect: biggest goal, what you've always wanted to learn, what you love to talk about. Meaning: which character you're most like, how you feel misunderstood, what you'd do if you weren't afraid."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Conversation starters","note":"Same aim in both books: skip autopilot small talk. Captivate gave you novel 'sparkers' and taught you to watch for what lights people up; Conversation turns that into nine power questions across three levels.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Novel questions; notice what makes them lean in and dig there.","card":"v62"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Nine power questions in three levels — put people at ease, connect deeply, create meaning.","card":"v2"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The nine power questions”?","a":"Vanessa's nine questions, three per level. Ease: exciting work, fun plans, passion project."},{"id":"n-vve-magic-phrases","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"The five magic phrases","mv":"Vanessa's five short phrases that build warmth: \"I was just thinking of you\", \"Tell me more?\", \"Last time you mentioned…\", \"Same here!\" and \"You're so ___!\" Each one only works when it's true.","tr":"Look for honest moments to use each one this week.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Conversation, Level 1 \"Five Magic Phrases\""],"how":["Vanessa's five short phrases that build warmth: \"I was just thinking of you\", \"Tell me more?\", \"Last time you mentioned…\", \"Same here!\" and \"You're so ___!\" Each one only works when it's true."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “I was just thinking …”","a":"“I was just thinking of you”"},{"id":"n-vve-curious-critical","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Curious before critical","mv":"When someone frustrates you, ask what explanation you'd give if you'd done the same thing. It turns judgment into curiosity.","tr":"Ask \"What might be going on for them?\" before you react.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"how":["When someone frustrates you, ask what explanation you'd give if you'd done the same thing. It turns judgment into curiosity."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Curious before critical”?","a":"When someone frustrates you, ask what explanation you'd give if you'd done the same thing. It turns judgment into curiosity."},{"id":"n-vve-compliments","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Memorable compliments","mv":"If you think something nice, say it. Make it specific, skip the obvious, and praise what they worked on rather than what they were born with.","tr":"\"The way you handled that question was so calm\" beats \"You're great\".","w":"Make it specific, skip the obvious, and praise what they worked on rather than what they were born with.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa on X, Sep 2026"],"how":["If you think something nice, say it. Make it specific, skip the obvious, and praise what they worked on rather than what they were born with."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Compliments, two ways","note":"How to give a good compliment, and what the research says it does for you.","chain":[{"label":"How to give one","when":"Book · Cues","text":"If you think something nice, say it. Make it specific, skip the obvious, and praise what they worked on rather than what they were born with.","card":"n-vve-compliments"},{"label":"It lifts the giver too","when":"Video · Feb 2026","text":"Vanessa shares research on the warmth and competence model. Giving a sincere compliment raised how warm and competent both people seemed, the giver as well as the receiver.","card":"n-vve-m-compliment-both-ways"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"What’s the trap with “Memorable compliments”?","a":"Make it specific, skip the obvious, and praise what they worked on rather than what they were born with."},{"id":"n-vve-go-deeper","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Go one level deeper","mv":"Research cited by Vanessa shows people expect deep talk to be more awkward than it is, and enjoy it more than they predict. Go one level deeper than feels comfortable — at their pace.","tr":"Swap \"What do you do?\" for \"What's something you're working on that you actually care about?\"","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Conversation research guide","Kardas, Kumar & Epley 2022, as cited in the handbook"],"rel":["n-vve-power-questions"],"how":["Research cited by Vanessa shows people expect deep talk to be more awkward than it is, and enjoy it more than they predict. Go one level deeper than feels comfortable — at their pace."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Go one level deeper”?","a":"Swap \"What do you do?\" for \"What's something you're working on that you actually care about?\""},{"id":"n-vve-triple-nod","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"The slow triple nod","mv":"A slow nod in threes while someone talks encourages them to keep going and shows you're with them. Speed matters: slow nods feel thoughtful, fast nods look like \"hurry up\".","tr":"Use it when you want someone to open up more.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Cues ch. 4","Conversation, Level 1"],"rel":["v81","n-vve-head-tilt","v87"],"how":["A slow nod in threes while someone talks encourages them to keep going and shows you're with them. Speed matters: slow nods feel thoughtful, fast nods look like \"hurry up\".","Vanessa's exercise: say \"I hear you… I hear you… I hear you\" slowly, about one per second, nodding with each. The often-quoted \"67% longer\" figure is best held loosely. To show quiet disagreement, she says simply withhold your nods."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The slow triple nod”?","a":"A slow nod in threes while someone talks encourages them to keep going and shows you're with them. Speed matters: slow nods feel thoughtful, fast nods look like \"hurry up\"."},{"id":"n-vve-head-tilt","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"The head tilt","mv":"A slight head tilt while someone talks says \"I'm listening, tell me more\". Vanessa says time your tilts for the moments that matter rather than holding one all the time.","tr":"Tilt when they get to the important part.","w":"Vanessa says time your tilts for the moments that matter rather than holding one all the time.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Cues ch. 4"],"rel":["n-vve-triple-nod","v81"],"how":["A slight head tilt while someone talks says \"I'm listening, tell me more\". Vanessa says time your tilts for the moments that matter rather than holding one all the time."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “I'm listening, tell …”","a":"“I'm listening, tell me more”"},{"id":"n-vg-about","t":"vg","tp":"teachers","ti":"Who is Vinh Giang?","mv":"Vinh Giang is a communication coach (and former magician) who teaches voice, body language, storytelling and listening. His book is Before We Forget How to Connect (2026).","tr":"Pick Giang on the Teacher chips when you want delivery-first lines.","w":"Treat it as a coach's view: test it in real life and keep what works.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect, contents","Vinh's book page"],"rel":["g1","n-vg-practical-magic"],"how":["Vinh Giang is a communication coach (and former magician) who teaches voice, body language, storytelling and listening. His book is Before We Forget How to Connect (2026). On this page he's the teacher for how you say things.","The book has four parts: vocal skills (tonality, rate, melody, volume, pause), body language (presence, posture, gestures, expression, movement, eye contact), storytelling, and active listening. Every chapter ends with a \"Practical Magic\" exercise: record a Look Back, a Stretch, then Compare, and finish the sentence \"The one thing I need to work on is…\". Most \"How to say it\" cues on the Generate tab come from his book."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Who is Vinh Giang?”?","a":"Pick Giang on the Teacher chips when you want delivery-first lines."},{"id":"n-vg-calm-pace","t":"vg","tp":"tough","ti":"Calm someone by matching, then slowing","mv":"When someone's upset and talking fast, Vinh's approach is to match their rate first, then slow down step by step. They tend to follow your pace down.","tr":"Match, then slow, then lower your volume a touch.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 4"],"rel":["g35"],"how":["When someone's upset and talking fast, Vinh's approach is to match their rate first, then slow down step by step. They tend to follow your pace down."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Calm someone by matching, then slowing”?","a":"When someone's upset and talking fast, Vinh's approach is to match their rate first, then slow down step by step. They tend to follow your pace down."},{"id":"n-vg-practical-magic","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Practical Magic: Look Back, Stretch, Compare","mv":"Every chapter in Vinh's book ends with a recorded exercise: Look Back at a baseline video, Stretch by exaggerating the skill, then Compare the two. You finish by writing \"The one thing I need to work on is…\".","tr":"Do one Practical Magic a week, focused on a single skill.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect, all chapters"],"rel":["g14"],"how":["Every chapter in Vinh's book ends with a recorded exercise: Look Back at a baseline video, Stretch by exaggerating the skill, then Compare the two. You finish by writing \"The one thing I need to work on is…\"."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Record and review","note":"The book ends each chapter with one thing to work on; in his late-2026 interviews he says pick three from a single recording, because fixing one often reveals the next.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Every chapter in Vinh's book ends with a recorded exercise: Look Back at a baseline video, Stretch by exaggerating the skill, then Compare the two. You finish by writing \"The one thing I need to work on is…\".","card":"n-vg-practical-magic"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Lewis Howes and Mark Manson interviews · Nov 2026","text":"Vinh's ten-minute habit: record yourself talking for five minutes, watch it back for five, and pick three things to change. A generic top-ten list gives generic results; your own recording shows your list.","card":"n-vg-m-pick-three"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"Finish the line: “The one thing I need to …”","a":"“The one thing I need to work on is…”"},{"id":"n-vg-storytelling","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Storytelling: the human superpower","mv":"Vinh calls storytelling the human superpower. Start a \"Story Bank\" note on your phone: whenever something moves you — frustration, surprise, pride, embarrassment, joy — write down what happened.","tr":"Add one entry to your Story Bank this week.","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 15"],"how":["Vinh calls storytelling the human superpower. Start a \"Story Bank\" note on your phone: whenever something moves you — frustration, surprise, pride, embarrassment, joy — write down what happened. Then fill in the 4Ws: Who, What, Where, When."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Storytelling: the human superpower”?","a":"Add one entry to your Story Bank this week."},{"id":"n-vg-story-bank","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Building your Story Bank","mv":"Collect stories in categories: high-stakes moments, vulnerable moments and funny moments. Pick ones where you still remember exactly how you felt.","tr":"Write the working title and the 4Ws before you write the story itself.","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Before We Forget How to Connect ch. 15–18"],"rel":["n-vg-storytelling","g17"],"how":["Collect stories in categories: high-stakes moments, vulnerable moments and funny moments. Pick ones where you still remember exactly how you felt."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Building your Story Bank”?","a":"Collect stories in categories: high-stakes moments, vulnerable moments and funny moments. Pick ones where you still remember exactly how you felt."},{"id":"n-all-liking-gap","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"The liking gap","mv":"After a conversation, people usually liked you more than you think (Boothby and colleagues, 2018). Vanessa uses this to encourage you to signal liking more clearly and to assume people already like you a little.","tr":"If you enjoyed it, say so — \"I really enjoyed that.\"","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Conversation, Level 1"],"rel":["n-all-assume-liked"],"how":["After a conversation, people usually liked you more than you think (Boothby and colleagues, 2018). Vanessa uses this to encourage you to signal liking more clearly and to assume people already like you a little."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The liking gap”?","a":"After a conversation, people usually liked you more than you think (Boothby and colleagues, 2018). Vanessa uses this to encourage you to signal liking more clearly and to assume people already like you a little."},{"id":"n-all-assume-liked","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Assume they already like you","mv":"Vanessa's \"power of assumption\": walk in assuming people already like you a little more than you fear. The liking gap research suggests that's often true.","tr":"Before you walk in, tell yourself \"They're going to like me.\"","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"rel":["n-all-liking-gap","v65"],"how":["Vanessa's \"power of assumption\": walk in assuming people already like you a little more than you fear. The liking gap research suggests that's often true."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Expecting the best","note":"Expecting the best of others became walking in assuming they already like you.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate · 2017","text":"Your expectations shape people; listen for the good. Challenge: a one-day vow of silence.","card":"v65"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Vanessa's \"power of assumption\": walk in assuming people already like you a little more than you fear. The liking gap research suggests that's often true.","card":"n-all-assume-liked"}]}],"vb":"2017 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Assume they already like you”?","a":"Before you walk in, tell yourself \"They're going to like me.\""},{"id":"n-all-end-on-purpose","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"End on purpose","mv":"A large study found conversations almost never end when both people want them to — only about 2% of the time (Mastroianni and colleagues, 2021). So ending warmly and on purpose is a kindness.","tr":"Leave on a high rather than waiting for it to fizzle.","w":"A large study found conversations almost never end when both people want them to — only about 2% of the time (Mastroianni and colleagues, 2021).","ev":"","d":{"src":["Mastroianni et al. 2021, as cited in the handbook"],"how":["A large study found conversations almost never end when both people want them to — only about 2% of the time (Mastroianni and colleagues, 2021). So ending warmly and on purpose is a kindness. Vanessa's exit: something in the future, a specific kindness, then go."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “End on purpose”?","a":"A large study found conversations almost never end when both people want them to — only about 2% of the time (Mastroianni and colleagues, 2021). So ending warmly and on purpose is a kindness."},{"id":"n-all-warmth-first","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Warmth first, then competence","mv":"People size you up on two questions: can I trust you, and can I rely on you? Research (Fiske, Cuddy and Glick, 2007) shows warmth is judged first.","tr":"In the first minute, be warm; in the second, be clear.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Fiske, Cuddy & Glick 2007"],"how":["People size you up on two questions: can I trust you, and can I rely on you? Research (Fiske, Cuddy and Glick, 2007) shows warmth is judged first. So lead with interest, their name and a real smile, then make one clear point."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Warmth first, then competence”?","a":"People size you up on two questions: can I trust you, and can I rely on you? Research (Fiske, Cuddy and Glick, 2007) shows warmth is judged first."},{"id":"n-all-voice-competence","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Your voice signals competence","mv":"People who heard someone's pitch rated them more competent and thoughtful than people who only read the same words (Schroeder and Epley, 2015). That's why Vinh puts voice first.","tr":"For anything important, call or speak rather than only writing.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"rel":["g1"],"how":["People who heard someone's pitch rated them more competent and thoughtful than people who only read the same words (Schroeder and Epley, 2015). That's why Vinh puts voice first."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Your voice and how you are judged","note":"The idea that people judge your competence by your voice goes back years: in 2019 he framed it as a vocal image you have to earn; the book backs it with research.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Unstoppable podcast · 2019","text":"Most people polish how they look, but Vinh points out that clothes, shoes and a watch can be bought. Your vocal image has to be earned through practice, and people still judge your competence from it.","card":"n-vg-m-vocal-image"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"People who heard someone's pitch rated them more competent and thoughtful than people who only read the same words (Schroeder and Epley, 2015). That's why Vinh puts voice first.","card":"n-all-voice-competence"}]}],"vb":"2019 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Your voice signals competence”?","a":"For anything important, call or speak rather than only writing."},{"id":"n-ch-about","t":"ch","tp":"teachers","ti":"Who is Chase Hughes?","mv":"A former US Navy behaviour trainer who teaches body language, composure and people skills through The Behavior Ops Manual (2023) and NCI University.","tr":"Tap the Chase Hughes filter above to study his cards on their own.","w":"Treat it as a coach's view: test it in real life and keep what works.","ev":"","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual","NCI University"],"rel":["h15"],"how":["On this page he's the teacher for the small-talk loop, composure, plain speech and the six needs as listening prompts.","His credentials and claims are disputed by critics and none of his models has published validation, so treat his ideas as a coach's view: useful, untested.","Chase Hughes is a behaviour-profiling and influence trainer and author. His site says he served over 20 years in the US Navy, retiring as a Chief in 2019. He founded Applied Behavior Research and NCI University (Neuro-Cognitive Intelligence) and is a member of The Behavior Panel on YouTube. His site lists podcast appearances such as Diary of a CEO, Danny Jones, PBD and Joe Rogan. This assistant only draws on his work: the Ops Manual plus his public videos and site."],"go":[{"tab":"watch","label":"Watch Hughes read real people"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Who is Chase Hughes?”?","a":"A former US Navy behaviour trainer who teaches body language, composure and people skills through The Behavior Ops Manual (2023) and NCI University."},{"id":"n-vve-m-like-to-do","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Ask \"What do you like to do?\"","mv":"Swap \"What do you do?\" for \"What do you like to do?\". Vanessa found it in a veteran TV interviewer's memoir: one word turns a job report into a topic people enjoy.","tr":"At your next event, open with \"What do you like to do when you're not working?\"","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"","d":{"src":["The Learning Leader Show with Ryan Hawk, Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vve-power-questions","n-vve-magic-phrases"],"how":["Swap \"What do you do?\" for \"What do you like to do?\". Vanessa found it in a veteran TV interviewer's memoir: one word turns a job report into a topic people enjoy. She calls these micro-swaps: the question stays safe, but it leans towards what lights someone up.","Memoirs are one of her research sources, because people describe their conversation habits there without meaning to. The aim is the same treasure hunt as always: look for the topic that makes their face change."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Answering and asking \"What do you do?\"","note":"First she fixed your answer with a logline; in 2026 she also fixes the question itself.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"A short, hooky one-liner plus a hook back to them.","card":"v23"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Learning Leader interview · Oct 2026","text":"Swap \"What do you do?\" for \"What do you like to do?\". Vanessa found it in a veteran TV interviewer's memoir: one word turns a job report into a topic people enjoy.","card":"n-vve-m-like-to-do"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"Finish the line: “What do you like …”","a":"“What do you like to do?”"},{"id":"n-vve-m-work-goals","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Two doors to deeper talk at work: goals and learning","mv":"Vanessa says the two easiest doors from small talk into real connection at work are goals and learning, because a goal taps motivation and values.","tr":"Ask one colleague this week: \"What are you working towards right now, at work or outside it?\"","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"","d":{"src":["The Learning Leader Show with Ryan Hawk, Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vve-power-questions"],"how":["Vanessa says the two easiest doors from small talk into real connection at work are goals and learning, because a goal taps motivation and values. Her quick test: do you know the current work goal and the personal goal of the five people you work with most?","Her own team calls open with \"Tell me something good\" as a short warm-up, and goals are a normal topic, so she knows who is training for a marathon as well as who is chasing a quarterly target."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Two doors to deeper talk at work: goals and learning”?","a":"Ask one colleague this week: \"What are you working towards right now, at work or outside it?\""},{"id":"n-vve-m-relationship-audit","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"The relationship audit: heck-yes, drainers and \"meh\"","mv":"Make a private list in three columns: the people who give you energy (the ones you would happily cook for tonight), the people who drain you, and the ambivalent middle you leave feeling \"meh\".","tr":"Tonight, write three names in each column, then plan one call to a heck-yes person.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"","d":{"src":["The Dan Buettner Podcast, Sep 2025"],"rel":["v49","n-vve-m-one-uppers"],"how":["Make a private list in three columns: the people who give you energy (the ones you would happily cook for tonight), the people who drain you, and the ambivalent middle you leave feeling \"meh\". Vanessa says the meh group matters most, because it quietly uses up time you could give to the people who fuel you.","Ambivalent ties often run on habit, or on someone you're not sure is truly happy for you. To make room for closer friends, she suggests seeing less of the meh column and more of your heck-yes people."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Making and keeping friends","note":"Making friends was about social hours and shared places; by 2025 she adds a first step: sort who fuels you before you add more.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Captivate and Conversation","text":"Schedule regular time, build shared contexts, try small invitations.","card":"v49"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Dan Buettner Podcast · 2025","text":"Make a private list in three columns: the people who give you energy (the ones you would happily cook for tonight), the people who drain you, and the ambivalent middle you leave feeling \"meh\".","card":"n-vve-m-relationship-audit"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The relationship audit: heck-yes, drainers and \"meh\"”?","a":"Make a private list in three columns: the people who give you energy (the ones you would happily cook for tonight), the people who drain you, and the ambivalent middle you leave feeling \"meh\"."},{"id":"n-vve-m-one-uppers","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"One-uppers and one-downers","mv":"A pattern Vanessa flags in ambivalent relationships: someone whose good news is always better than yours and whose bad news is always worse.","tr":"When you hear a bigger story, try \"That sounds rough. How are you doing now?\" and let it go.","w":"When you share a win and get a flat \"oh, great\" or a bigger story back, notice it instead of competing.","ev":"","d":{"src":["The Dan Buettner Podcast, Sep 2025"],"rel":["n-vve-m-relationship-audit"],"how":["A pattern Vanessa flags in ambivalent relationships: someone whose good news is always better than yours and whose bad news is always worse. When you share a win and get a flat \"oh, great\" or a bigger story back, notice it instead of competing.","You don't have to cut anyone off. Stop taking your best news there first, and check you're not doing it yourself: when someone shares, stay on their story before adding yours."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “One-uppers and one-downers”?","a":"When you share a win and get a flat \"oh, great\" or a bigger story back, notice it instead of competing."},{"id":"n-vve-m-impression-triggers","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Know who brings out your best first impression","mv":"Pick three words: your worst first impression, your current one, and the one you want. Then list the people and places that bring out each.","tr":"Write your three first-impression words, plus one person who brings out the good one.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Interview on Voxera Production, Jul 2026"],"rel":["v49","n-vve-m-relationship-audit"],"how":["Pick three words: your worst first impression, your current one, and the one you want. Then list the people and places that bring out each. Vanessa says awkwardness dresses up two ways: some people shrink and go quiet, others get bigger, louder and start name-dropping.","Spend more time with the people who bring out your best version, and have a small plan ready for the places that make you tight, like arriving early or finding one person to talk to first."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Know who brings out your best first impression”?","a":"Write your three first-impression words, plus one person who brings out the good one."},{"id":"n-vve-m-outbreath-hello","t":"vve","tp":"voice","ti":"Say hello on the out-breath","mv":"For a warmer, more relaxed voice, breathe in, then say \"hello\" as you breathe out. Vanessa says speaking on the exhale relaxes your vocal cords and keeps you out of a tight, high pitch.","tr":"Before your next call, breathe in and let your \"hello\" ride out on the exhale.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Interview on Voxera Production, Jul 2026"],"rel":["n-vve-m-name-statement"],"how":["For a warmer, more relaxed voice, breathe in, then say \"hello\" as you breathe out. Vanessa says speaking on the exhale relaxes your vocal cords and keeps you out of a tight, high pitch.","Try it on the phone first: a pinched, high hello next to a relaxed one on the out-breath. Same person, very different first impression."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"A confident voice","note":"The book gives the vocal power rules; her interviews boil them down to two quick habits you can use today.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Cues · 2022","text":"Statements end down; pause instead of \"um\".","card":"v85"},{"label":"Updated","when":"BigDeal interview · 2024","text":"Vanessa describes a pitch that went wrong in its first line: the founder said his own name with a rising, question-like tone. A name, a number or a price said like a question invites people to doubt you or bargain.","card":"n-vve-m-name-statement"},{"label":"Latest","when":"Interview · Jul 2026","text":"For a warmer, more relaxed voice, breathe in, then say \"hello\" as you breathe out. Vanessa says speaking on the exhale relaxes your vocal cords and keeps you out of a tight, high pitch.","card":"n-vve-m-outbreath-hello"}]}],"vb":"2022 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Say hello on the out-breath”?","a":"For a warmer, more relaxed voice, breathe in, then say \"hello\" as you breathe out. Vanessa says speaking on the exhale relaxes your vocal cords and keeps you out of a tight, high pitch."},{"id":"n-vve-m-own-baseline","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Film your own baseline","mv":"To learn your own cues, film yourself twice: once telling what you had for breakfast yesterday (a calm memory), and once retelling an embarrassing moment as if you're living it again. Compare your face, hands and voice.","tr":"Record both clips on your phone tonight and note the first thing that changes.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Modern Wisdom with Chris Williamson, Mar 2025"],"rel":["n-vve-m-impression-triggers"],"how":["To learn your own cues, film yourself twice: once telling what you had for breakfast yesterday (a calm memory), and once retelling an embarrassing moment as if you're living it again. Compare your face, hands and voice. That shows you your normal next to your stressed self.","Vanessa uses a version of this in her lab. Once you know your own stress signals, you can catch them mid-conversation and reset your breathing and posture."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Film your own baseline”?","a":"To learn your own cues, film yourself twice: once telling what you had for breakfast yesterday (a calm memory), and once retelling an embarrassing moment as if you're living it again. Compare your face, hands and voice."},{"id":"n-vve-m-name-statement","t":"vve","tp":"voice","ti":"Say your name as a statement","mv":"Vanessa describes a pitch that went wrong in its first line: the founder said his own name with a rising, question-like tone. A name, a number or a price said like a question invites people to doubt you or bargain.","tr":"Say \"Hi, I'm ___.\" and let your voice drop on your name.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["BigDeal with Codie Sanchez, Sep 2024"],"rel":["n-vve-m-outbreath-hello"],"how":["Vanessa describes a pitch that went wrong in its first line: the founder said his own name with a rising, question-like tone. A name, a number or a price said like a question invites people to doubt you or bargain.","She sees the same thing in sales training all the time. Let your pitch drop at the end of statements you mean."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"A confident voice","note":"The book gives the vocal power rules; her interviews boil them down to two quick habits you can use today.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Cues · 2022","text":"Statements end down; pause instead of \"um\".","card":"v85"},{"label":"Updated","when":"BigDeal interview · 2024","text":"Vanessa describes a pitch that went wrong in its first line: the founder said his own name with a rising, question-like tone. A name, a number or a price said like a question invites people to doubt you or bargain.","card":"n-vve-m-name-statement"},{"label":"Latest","when":"Interview · Jul 2026","text":"For a warmer, more relaxed voice, breathe in, then say \"hello\" as you breathe out. Vanessa says speaking on the exhale relaxes your vocal cords and keeps you out of a tight, high pitch.","card":"n-vve-m-outbreath-hello"}]}],"vb":"2022 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Say your name as a statement”?","a":"Say \"Hi, I'm ___.\" and let your voice drop on your name."},{"id":"n-vg-m-pause-clarify","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Pause, clarify, then answer slowly","mv":"When a question throws you, Vinh uses three steps: pause, ask a clarifying question, then answer slowly. Clarifying buys time, and more importantly it tells you what they actually want to know.","tr":"Next time you're put on the spot, try \"Good question. Do you mean ___ or ___?\"","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["The Mel Robbins Podcast, Nov 2026"],"how":["When a question throws you, Vinh uses three steps: pause, ask a clarifying question, then answer slowly. Clarifying buys time, and more importantly it tells you what they actually want to know.","Lots of people ask vague questions. \"When you say an update on the campaign, is there a platform you'd like me to start with?\" Each clarifying step sharpens your answer, and answering more slowly makes you look calm and in control."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"When you are put on the spot","note":"The same core, refined through 2026: buy time, narrow the question, answer one thing.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"When put on the spot, give three steps, two types, or one thing.","card":"g38"},{"label":"Updated","when":"YouTube · Jun 2026","text":"Vinh's PAT framework for surprise questions. Pause for a couple of seconds instead of blurting.","card":"n-vg-m-pat"},{"label":"Latest","when":"Mel Robbins Podcast · Nov 2026","text":"When a question throws you, Vinh uses three steps: pause, ask a clarifying question, then answer slowly. Clarifying buys time, and more importantly it tells you what they actually want to know.","card":"n-vg-m-pause-clarify"}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Pause, clarify, then answer slowly”?","a":"When a question throws you, Vinh uses three steps: pause, ask a clarifying question, then answer slowly. Clarifying buys time, and more importantly it tells you what they actually want to know."},{"id":"n-vg-m-be-engaged","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"To be engaging, be engaged","mv":"Asked what most people get wrong with their voice, Vinh says: a very limited range. If you want to be more engaging, engage your instrument more.","tr":"In your next meeting, speak one notch louder than feels normal and notice what your hands do.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Jefferson Fisher podcast, Nov 2026"],"rel":["g6"],"how":["Asked what most people get wrong with their voice, Vinh says: a very limited range. If you want to be more engaging, engage your instrument more. When quiet speakers simply raise their volume, their hands start moving and their face comes alive too.","Variety is the key. Each part of the voice (rate, volume, pitch and melody, tonality and the pause) has a range, and most of us only use a narrow strip of it."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “To be engaging, be engaged”?","a":"Asked what most people get wrong with their voice, Vinh says: a very limited range. If you want to be more engaging, engage your instrument more."},{"id":"n-vg-m-stretch-range","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Stretch your range: one paragraph, three speeds","mv":"Take one paragraph and say it slowly, then in 20 seconds, then in 10. Vinh has you stretch every vocal foundation like this so you own the whole range: slower for weight, faster when you want passion and excitement to come through.","tr":"Read today's paragraph three times: slow, 20 seconds, then 10 seconds.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Jefferson Fisher podcast, Nov 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-be-engaged"],"how":["Take one paragraph and say it slowly, then in 20 seconds, then in 10. Vinh has you stretch every vocal foundation like this so you own the whole range: slower for weight, faster when you want passion and excitement to come through."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Stretch your range: one paragraph, three speeds”?","a":"Read today's paragraph three times: slow, 20 seconds, then 10 seconds."},{"id":"n-vg-m-clean-thinking","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Clean thinking, clear speaking","mv":"Vinh says smart people often ramble because they have lots to give but have never practised choosing. If you think messy, you speak messy.","tr":"Pick the question you're asked most at work and record a 30-second answer today.","w":"Vinh says smart people often ramble because they have lots to give but have never practised choosing.","ev":"","d":{"src":["BigDeal with Codie Sanchez, Nov 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pause-clarify"],"how":["Vinh says smart people often ramble because they have lots to give but have never practised choosing. If you think messy, you speak messy. His fix: list the 20 things you get asked about most, answer each out loud, record it, and keep only the one point that really matters.","Then put answers into a simple shape, like three steps, two types or one thing, so you can find it again under pressure."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Clean thinking, clear speaking”?","a":"Vinh says smart people often ramble because they have lots to give but have never practised choosing. If you think messy, you speak messy."},{"id":"n-vg-m-practise-as-you-deliver","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"How you practise is how you deliver","mv":"If you rehearse sitting down, reading flatly from a script, that is how you'll deliver. Vinh's rule: rehearse out loud as if you're already there, with the energy, pauses and movement you'll use, around ten times.","tr":"Rehearse your opening line standing up, at full energy, ten times.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["BigDeal with Codie Sanchez, Nov 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-practical-magic","n-vg-m-clean-thinking"],"how":["If you rehearse sitting down, reading flatly from a script, that is how you'll deliver. Vinh's rule: rehearse out loud as if you're already there, with the energy, pauses and movement you'll use, around ten times. High effort in rehearsal is what makes it stick."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “How you practise is how you deliver”?","a":"If you rehearse sitting down, reading flatly from a script, that is how you'll deliver. Vinh's rule: rehearse out loud as if you're already there, with the energy, pauses and movement you'll use, around ten times."},{"id":"n-vg-m-vocal-image","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Your vocal image is earned","mv":"Most people polish how they look, but Vinh points out that clothes, shoes and a watch can be bought. Your vocal image has to be earned through practice, and people still judge your competence from it.","tr":"Spend the time you'd give to an outfit on one recorded practice round.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Unstoppable with Kerwin Rae, Dec 2019"],"rel":["g1"],"how":["Most people polish how they look, but Vinh points out that clothes, shoes and a watch can be bought. Your vocal image has to be earned through practice, and people still judge your competence from it."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Your voice and how you are judged","note":"The idea that people judge your competence by your voice goes back years: in 2019 he framed it as a vocal image you have to earn; the book backs it with research.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Unstoppable podcast · 2019","text":"Most people polish how they look, but Vinh points out that clothes, shoes and a watch can be bought. Your vocal image has to be earned through practice, and people still judge your competence from it.","card":"n-vg-m-vocal-image"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"People who heard someone's pitch rated them more competent and thoughtful than people who only read the same words (Schroeder and Epley, 2015). That's why Vinh puts voice first.","card":"n-all-voice-competence"}]}],"vb":"2019 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Your vocal image is earned”?","a":"Spend the time you'd give to an outfit on one recorded practice round."},{"id":"n-vg-m-pick-three","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Record once, fix three things","mv":"Vinh's ten-minute habit: record yourself talking for five minutes, watch it back for five, and pick three things to change. A generic top-ten list gives generic results; your own recording shows your list.","tr":"Record five minutes today, watch it once, and write down three changes.","w":"Quiet students who turn up their volume notice they never move their hands, so that becomes the next thing.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Lewis Howes, Nov 2026","Mark Manson, Nov 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-practical-magic"],"how":["Vinh's ten-minute habit: record yourself talking for five minutes, watch it back for five, and pick three things to change. A generic top-ten list gives generic results; your own recording shows your list.","Fixing one thing often reveals the next. Quiet students who turn up their volume notice they never move their hands, so that becomes the next thing."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Record and review","note":"The book ends each chapter with one thing to work on; in his late-2026 interviews he says pick three from a single recording, because fixing one often reveals the next.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Every chapter in Vinh's book ends with a recorded exercise: Look Back at a baseline video, Stretch by exaggerating the skill, then Compare the two. You finish by writing \"The one thing I need to work on is…\".","card":"n-vg-practical-magic"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Lewis Howes and Mark Manson interviews · Nov 2026","text":"Vinh's ten-minute habit: record yourself talking for five minutes, watch it back for five, and pick three things to change. A generic top-ten list gives generic results; your own recording shows your list.","card":"n-vg-m-pick-three"}]},{"title":"Record and review","note":"Two versions of Vinh's self-review habit.","chain":[{"label":"Fix three things","when":"Podcasts · Nov 2026","text":"Vinh's ten-minute habit: record yourself talking for five minutes, watch it back for five, and pick three things to change. A generic top-ten list gives generic results; your own recording shows your list.","card":"n-vg-m-pick-three"},{"label":"Three passes","when":"Video · Aug 2026","text":"Vinh's record-and-review routine: film five minutes of unscripted speaking, then wait a day so you're less harsh. Review it three ways.","card":"n-vg-m-three-pass-review"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Record once, fix three things”?","a":"Vinh's ten-minute habit: record yourself talking for five minutes, watch it back for five, and pick three things to change. A generic top-ten list gives generic results; your own recording shows your list."},{"id":"n-vve-m-platinum-rule","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"The Platinum Rule for difficult people","mv":"Vanessa likes the Golden Rule but prefers what she calls the Platinum Rule: treat others as they would treat themselves. \"I'd never do that\" is true, and it's also beside the point, because they aren't wired like you.","tr":"Think of one person who frustrates you and ask: \"How would they describe what happened?\"","w":"\"I'd never do that\" is true, and it's also beside the point, because they aren't wired like you.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Apr 2019"],"how":["Vanessa likes the Golden Rule but prefers what she calls the Platinum Rule: treat others as they would treat themselves. \"I'd never do that\" is true, and it's also beside the point, because they aren't wired like you. Ask instead: how would they see this situation?","She pairs it with the Big Five personality traits, sketching where a difficult person sits on each so their behaviour becomes easier to predict, without trying to change who they are."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “I'd never …”","a":"“I'd never do that”"},{"id":"n-vve-m-name-story","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Ask about the story behind their name","mv":"Everyone shares their name, so Vanessa suggests using it: \"That's a lovely name. Is there a story behind it?\" It's safe, it's personal without being intrusive, and it often leads to family, places and traditions.","tr":"Next time you're introduced, ask about the story behind their name.","w":"Don't jump levels too fast, especially with introverts.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Question Everything Podcast, Oct 2026"],"how":["Everyone shares their name, so Vanessa suggests using it: \"That's a lovely name. Is there a story behind it?\" It's safe, it's personal without being intrusive, and it often leads to family, places and traditions.","She checks every opener against one test: is it still safe? Don't jump levels too fast, especially with introverts. The goal is to be easy to talk to."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “That's a lovely name. Is there a …”","a":"“That's a lovely name. Is there a story behind it?”"},{"id":"n-vve-m-warm-word","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"One warm word changes how people see you","mv":"Vanessa's lab re-ran a classic 1946 study with hundreds of adults. Swapping one word, \"cold\" for \"warm\", in a description of a person changed everything: with \"warm\", 92% saw the person as generous; with \"cold\", 14%.","tr":"Reread your bio or email sign-off and add one genuinely warm word that's true of you.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Oct 2026"],"rel":["v81"],"how":["Vanessa's lab re-ran a classic 1946 study with hundreds of adults. Swapping one word, \"cold\" for \"warm\", in a description of a person changed everything: with \"warm\", 92% saw the person as generous; with \"cold\", 14%. Warmth lifted wisdom, humour and good nature too, and didn't lower competence.","Her takeaway: we over-polish competence and undervalue warmth. Warmth works like an amplifier for every other good trait."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “, in a description of a person …”","a":"“, in a description of a person changed everything: with ”"},{"id":"n-vve-m-social-reps","t":"vve","tp":"practice","ti":"Social reps: go to the social gym","mv":"On Vanessa's channel she argues that every small chat at work, on campus or at the shop is a social rep. Fewer casual chats means fewer reps, so each conversation feels harder and matters more.","tr":"Before a big conversation this week, have two small chats first: the barista, a neighbour, a colleague.","w":"Like the gym, you don't have to enjoy it; you go because it keeps you strong.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Sep 2026"],"rel":["n-vve-m-practice-spots"],"how":["On Vanessa's channel she argues that every small chat at work, on campus or at the shop is a social rep. Fewer casual chats means fewer reps, so each conversation feels harder and matters more. Like the gym, you don't have to enjoy it; you go because it keeps you strong.","An athlete warms up before game day. If an interview is your first face-to-face chat in weeks, you've skipped the warm-up."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Social reps: go to the social gym”?","a":"Like the gym, you don't have to enjoy it; you go because it keeps you strong."},{"id":"n-vve-m-practice-spots","t":"vve","tp":"practice","ti":"Low-stakes places to practise","mv":"Don't practise on your boss first.","tr":"Pick one daily spot this week and aim for one exchange beyond \"thanks\".","w":"Don't practise on your boss first.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Oct 2020"],"rel":["n-vve-m-social-reps"],"how":["Don't practise on your boss first. Vanessa's low-pressure practice spots: get haircuts more often, because hairdressers and barbers are small-talk pros with a captive audience; and pop into a shop or café once a day instead of one big weekly trip.","Try going back to the same person to go deeper each time, or try new ones to practise first impressions."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Where to practise","note":"The low-stakes practice idea carried through to her 2026 book.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"YouTube · Oct 2020","text":"Don't practise on your boss first.","card":"n-vve-m-practice-spots"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Conversation · 2026","text":"Practise with friendly people before important ones; you can even tell them you're practising. She thanks hairdressers as small-talk champions.","card":null}]}],"vb":"2020 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Low-stakes places to practise”?","a":"Pick one daily spot this week and aim for one exchange beyond \"thanks\"."},{"id":"n-vve-m-energy-shortlist","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"A short list of people who don't get your energy","mv":"Some people will underestimate you whatever you do. Vanessa keeps a short mental list of people who don't get her energy.","tr":"If someone keeps replaying in your head tonight, say \"No, thank you\" and turn to something you enjoy.","w":"Vanessa keeps a short mental list of people who don't get her energy.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), May 2021"],"rel":["n-vve-m-relationship-audit"],"how":["Some people will underestimate you whatever you do. Vanessa keeps a short mental list of people who don't get her energy. When one pops into her head late at night, she thinks \"No, thank you\" and takes her attention back.","It's a mental boundary, not a feud: nothing is said to them. It sits alongside saying no and setting boundaries in person."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “A short list of people who don't get your energy”?","a":"Some people will underestimate you whatever you do. Vanessa keeps a short mental list of people who don't get her energy."},{"id":"n-vve-m-script-dictate","t":"vve","tp":"voice","ti":"Read a script without sounding like a robot","mv":"Vanessa's script tips: dictate it instead of typing it, so it sounds like you; keep sentences and words short, and cut any word you'd never say out loud; smile on the lines that excite you, because a smile changes the sound of your voice.","tr":"Next time you prepare a talk, record yourself saying it, then use the transcript as the script.","w":"She says one fancy word she'd never use ruined take after take until it was cut.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Aug 2022"],"rel":["v85"],"how":["Vanessa's script tips: dictate it instead of typing it, so it sounds like you; keep sentences and words short, and cut any word you'd never say out loud; smile on the lines that excite you, because a smile changes the sound of your voice.","She says one fancy word she'd never use ruined take after take until it was cut."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Read a script without sounding like a robot”?","a":"She says one fancy word she'd never use ruined take after take until it was cut."},{"id":"n-vg-m-art-not-science","t":"vg","tp":"ideas","ti":"Communication is an art, not a science","mv":"Vinh used to treat communication like chemistry: louder voice plus bigger gestures equals influence, every time.","tr":"Before your next meeting, ask yourself: \"Who's in this room, and how much energy suits them today?\"","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Apr 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-be-engaged"],"how":["Vinh used to treat communication like chemistry: louder voice plus bigger gestures equals influence, every time. In real rooms the same moves came across as confident in one place and too much in another, and a warm joke landed in one room and offended in the next. He now teaches principles, not rules.","Art means reading the context, feeling the room and adjusting as you go. That's why he puts emotional intelligence above any formula for eye contact, gestures or tone."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Communication is an art, not a science”?","a":"Before your next meeting, ask yourself: \"Who's in this room, and how much energy suits them today?\""},{"id":"n-vg-m-connection-mode","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Swap performance questions for connection questions","mv":"In \"performance mode\" the questions in your head are all about you: Am I coming across well? Do they think I'm smart?","tr":"Walking into your next conversation, ask yourself one connection question instead of \"How am I doing?\"","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Apr 2026","Vinh Giang (YouTube), May 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pause-clarify"],"how":["In \"performance mode\" the questions in your head are all about you: Am I coming across well? Do they think I'm smart? Vinh switches to \"connection mode\" questions: What are they feeling? What aren't they saying? What would make them feel seen?","He learned performance from magic and theatre and later realised he was speaking to impress, not to connect. Before a talk he reminds himself it isn't about him: if the message reaches one person, it's worth it. It feels uncomfortable at first because you lose your scripts and actually have to listen."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Swap performance questions for connection questions”?","a":"In \"performance mode\" the questions in your head are all about you: Am I coming across well? Do they think I'm smart?"},{"id":"n-vg-m-instrument-not-type","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Introvert or extrovert, the instrument is the same","mv":"Vinh's vocal teacher asked him: would a world-class introvert pianist play differently from an extrovert one? No.","tr":"If you call yourself quiet, try one meeting this week at a notch more volume and see how it feels.","w":"Your voice and body don't care about your personality type.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Apr 2026","Jay Shetty Podcast, Aug 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-stretch-range"],"how":["Vinh's vocal teacher asked him: would a world-class introvert pianist play differently from an extrovert one? No. Your voice and body don't care about your personality type. Being an introvert is about where you get your energy, not about how well you can speak.","He admits he used to go easy on introverted students and let them stay quiet, and says he was only serving half his class. Better questions than \"Am I an introvert?\": Have I actually practised with my instrument? Am I letting who I was decide who I become?"]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Introverts and speaking","note":"He stopped letting introverted students off the hook.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"His early classes","text":"He went easy on introverted students and let them stay small and quiet, believing that was simply who they were.","card":null},{"label":"Updated","when":"YouTube · Apr 2026 and Jay Shetty Podcast · Aug 2026","text":"Vinh's vocal teacher asked him: would a world-class introvert pianist play differently from an extrovert one? No.","card":"n-vg-m-instrument-not-type"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"What’s the trap with “Introvert or extrovert, the instrument is the same”?","a":"Your voice and body don't care about your personality type."},{"id":"n-vg-m-complainer","t":"vg","tp":"tough","ti":"When someone keeps complaining: \"What else?\"","mv":"Vinh borrows a coaching approach for serial complainers. First let them take a full \"emotional breath\": ask what's on their mind, then keep asking \"What else?\" until they're done.","tr":"Next time someone vents, ask \"What else?\" twice before you offer anything.","w":"You don't have to solve everyone's problems; you help them find their own answer.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Oct 2024"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pause-clarify"],"how":["Vinh borrows a coaching approach for serial complainers. First let them take a full \"emotional breath\": ask what's on their mind, then keep asking \"What else?\" until they're done. Then ask what they think the main problem is. Finally make it practical: \"How can I help?\"","It moves people from venting to thinking, and it's hard to keep complaining to someone who keeps asking good questions. You don't have to solve everyone's problems; you help them find their own answer."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “Vinh borrows a coaching approach for serial complainers. First let …”","a":"“ Vinh borrows a coaching approach for serial complainers. First let them take a full ”"},{"id":"n-vg-m-adrenaline","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Shaky hands? Burn off the adrenaline first","mv":"Vinh says shaking hands, a wobbly voice and rushing usually come from spare adrenaline. Before a talk, interview or big meeting, burn it off: a brisk walk, 20 star jumps or a few push-ups.","tr":"Before your next high-stakes moment, find a corridor and walk briskly for two minutes.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Aug 2024","Vinh Giang (YouTube), Nov 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pause-clarify","g8"],"how":["Vinh says shaking hands, a wobbly voice and rushing usually come from spare adrenaline. Before a talk, interview or big meeting, burn it off: a brisk walk, 20 star jumps or a few push-ups. Then, before you answer a question, take a two to three second pause and breathe.","Unspent adrenaline also makes speakers pace the stage for no reason and talk so fast their thinking can't keep up, which is where rambling starts."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Shaky hands? Burn off the adrenaline first”?","a":"Vinh says shaking hands, a wobbly voice and rushing usually come from spare adrenaline. Before a talk, interview or big meeting, burn it off: a brisk walk, 20 star jumps or a few push-ups."},{"id":"n-vg-m-para","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Answer with PARA: Point, Action, Result, Ask","mv":"Vinh compares an unstructured answer to a parcel arriving as loose parts: the listener has to assemble it. PARA puts it in a box.","tr":"Answer the next \"Why did you...?\" at work with one sentence each for Point, Action, Result and Ask.","w":"Frameworks stop you speaking your thinking process out loud.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), May 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-clean-thinking"],"how":["Vinh compares an unstructured answer to a parcel arriving as loose parts: the listener has to assemble it. PARA puts it in a box. Point: your answer in one line. Action: what you did. Result: what came of it. Ask: invite the next step.","It's one of several frameworks he uses, alongside 3-2-1, to turn thinking into words before you speak. Frameworks stop you speaking your thinking process out loud."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Answer with PARA: Point, Action, Result, Ask”?","a":"Frameworks stop you speaking your thinking process out loud."},{"id":"n-vg-m-small-slips","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Small slip, keep going; big slip, name it","mv":"Vinh's rule: if you don't make a big deal of a small slip, nor will the audience. Fix the word and carry on.","tr":"Next time you trip over a word, just say the right word and keep going.","w":"Vinh's rule: if you don't make a big deal of a small slip, nor will the audience.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Nov 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pause-clarify"],"how":["Vinh's rule: if you don't make a big deal of a small slip, nor will the audience. Fix the word and carry on. But if something big happens, like a fall or a loud interruption, name it with a light line, because pretending it didn't happen makes everyone uneasy."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Small slip, keep going; big slip, name it”?","a":"Next time you trip over a word, just say the right word and keep going."},{"id":"n-vg-m-prime-the-change","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Tell people close to you that you're practising","mv":"When Vinh's students go home speaking with more energy, partners often react badly: \"Why are you talking like that?\" He says it's because they gave no context, so others invent their own.","tr":"Tell one person at home: \"I'm practising speaking with more energy. Tell me if it feels odd.\"","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Sep 2024"],"rel":["g41","n-vg-m-pick-three"],"how":["When Vinh's students go home speaking with more energy, partners often react badly: \"Why are you talking like that?\" He says it's because they gave no context, so others invent their own. Tell the people who know you best what you're practising and why, before you try it on them.","Expect the clunky stage. Changing how you sound around people who know your old habits is harder than with strangers."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “Why are you talking …”","a":"“Why are you talking like that?”"},{"id":"n-vg-m-pragmatic-mentors","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Find mentors one or two steps ahead","mv":"Vinh separates aspirational mentors (famous names who inspire you) from pragmatic mentors who are just one or two steps ahead.","tr":"Name one person a step or two ahead of you in a skill you want, and send them one specific question this week.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Mamak Sessions Podcast, Nov 2023"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pick-three"],"how":["Vinh separates aspirational mentors (famous names who inspire you) from pragmatic mentors who are just one or two steps ahead. The pragmatic ones are more likely to reply, and they still remember the exact steps between where you are and where they are."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Find mentors one or two steps ahead”?","a":"Vinh separates aspirational mentors (famous names who inspire you) from pragmatic mentors who are just one or two steps ahead."},{"id":"n-vg-m-camera-room","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"On camera, speak to a room of 10 to 20","mv":"On video calls people slump, and slumped sounds low energy. Vinh's guide for energy on camera: imagine 10 to 20 people in the room with you and speak to that room.","tr":"In your next video call, sit up and pitch your hello to an imagined room of 15.","w":"Not a stadium, which looks over the top, and not one person on a sofa.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Aug 2025"],"rel":["g43","n-vg-m-be-engaged"],"how":["On video calls people slump, and slumped sounds low energy. Vinh's guide for energy on camera: imagine 10 to 20 people in the room with you and speak to that room. Not a stadium, which looks over the top, and not one person on a sofa."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Energy on video calls","note":"A simple picture for camera energy came first; the book folds it into a wider video checklist.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"YouTube · Aug 2025","text":"On video calls people slump, and slumped sounds low energy. Vinh's guide for energy on camera: imagine 10 to 20 people in the room with you and speak to that room.","card":"n-vg-m-camera-room"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"Camera on at hello, torso and hands in shot, a bit more energy.","card":"g43"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “On camera, speak to a room of 10 to 20”?","a":"In your next video call, sit up and pitch your hello to an imagined room of 15."},{"id":"n-vg-m-pat","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Put on the spot? Use PAT: Pause, Ask, The one thing","mv":"Vinh's PAT framework for surprise questions. Pause for a couple of seconds instead of blurting.","tr":"Next time a question throws you, count two seconds, ask \"Do you mean X or Y?\", then give one thing.","w":"Pause for a couple of seconds instead of blurting.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jun 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pause-clarify"],"how":["Vinh's PAT framework for surprise questions. Pause for a couple of seconds instead of blurting. Ask a clarifying question, which shrinks a vague question into a smaller, clearer one and buys time. Then start your answer with \"The one thing is...\", which forces you to choose.","He says answering instantly can make you look as if you put no thought in. A focused question gets a focused answer, in fewer words."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"When you are put on the spot","note":"The same core, refined through 2026: buy time, narrow the question, answer one thing.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Before We Forget How to Connect · 2026","text":"When put on the spot, give three steps, two types, or one thing.","card":"g38"},{"label":"Updated","when":"YouTube · Jun 2026","text":"Vinh's PAT framework for surprise questions. Pause for a couple of seconds instead of blurting.","card":"n-vg-m-pat"},{"label":"Latest","when":"Mel Robbins Podcast · Nov 2026","text":"When a question throws you, Vinh uses three steps: pause, ask a clarifying question, then answer slowly. Clarifying buys time, and more importantly it tells you what they actually want to know.","card":"n-vg-m-pause-clarify"}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Put on the spot? Use PAT: Pause, Ask, The one thing”?","a":"Vinh's PAT framework for surprise questions. Pause for a couple of seconds instead of blurting."},{"id":"n-vg-m-ccc","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Explain anything with CCC: Context, Core, Connect","mv":"For complex ideas, Vinh uses CCC. Context: set the scene and give the why before the what.","tr":"Before your next update to your boss, write one line each for Context, Core and Connect.","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jul 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-para","n-vg-m-clean-thinking"],"how":["For complex ideas, Vinh uses CCC. Context: set the scene and give the why before the what. Core: one main idea, said simply. Connect: show why it matters to this listener.","In his example, the context is a launch with a tight deadline, the core is a request for extra editors, and the connect is finishing on time without burning out the team."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Explain anything with CCC: Context, Core, Connect”?","a":"For complex ideas, Vinh uses CCC. Context: set the scene and give the why before the what."},{"id":"n-vg-m-offer-and-echo","t":"vg","tp":"openers","ti":"Go first, then listen for the echo","mv":"When someone mentions a hobby you don't share, like fishing, the urge is to switch to yours. Vinh's fix starts with a small offer: share something real about yourself first, so they know how deep they can go.","tr":"Next time someone names a hobby you don't share, ask what they love about it and listen for what it stands for.","w":"When someone mentions a hobby you don't share, like fishing, the urge is to switch to yours.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jul 2026"],"rel":["g23"],"how":["When someone mentions a hobby you don't share, like fishing, the urge is to switch to yours. Vinh's fix starts with a small offer: share something real about yourself first, so they know how deep they can go. Then listen for the \"echo\": what their interest might stand for. Fishing might mean nature, quiet time or self-sufficiency, and you probably share one of those.","He compares going first to serving in tennis: there's no rally until someone serves. Go shallow and they'll stay shallow."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Go first, then listen for the echo”?","a":"Next time someone names a hobby you don't share, ask what they love about it and listen for what it stands for."},{"id":"n-vg-m-anchor-invite","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Don't drop your idea at the first frown","mv":"Two habits Vinh says drain a smart person's authority: checking for approval after every sentence, and abandoning an idea the moment someone looks doubtful.","tr":"In your next meeting, finish one idea fully before you check how it landed.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Dec 2025"],"rel":["g42","n-vg-m-pause-clarify"],"how":["Two habits Vinh says drain a smart person's authority: checking for approval after every sentence, and abandoning an idea the moment someone looks doubtful. Instead, finish your sentence on a lower pitch and pause, then ask for clarity, not reassurance (\"Any questions?\"). To hold an idea and stay open, anchor and invite: \"I believe this is the right direction because... and I'd like to hear your view too.\"","He calls the second habit \"disappearing spine\": a brief frown and you're saying \"Forget I said anything.\" Commit to finishing the idea once you've started it."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Don't drop your idea at the first frown”?","a":"Two habits Vinh says drain a smart person's authority: checking for approval after every sentence, and abandoning an idea the moment someone looks doubtful."},{"id":"n-vg-m-firm-friendly","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Firm and friendly: the middle way","mv":"Vinh sees people stuck at two extremes: stern requests that put backs up, and requests so soft no one acts on them.","tr":"Next time you ask for a change, keep the words direct and lift the melody and your eyebrows.","w":"Vinh sees people stuck at two extremes: stern requests that put backs up, and requests so soft no one acts on them.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Oct 2026"],"rel":["g11"],"how":["Vinh sees people stuck at two extremes: stern requests that put backs up, and requests so soft no one acts on them. The middle is the same clear message with more melody in your voice, a slight smile and eyebrows that aren't lowered, and \"we\" language that includes you (\"I need to do it too\").","The words can stay firm. It's the delivery that tells them you're reminding them with care, not telling them off."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Firm and friendly: the middle way”?","a":"Vinh sees people stuck at two extremes: stern requests that put backs up, and requests so soft no one acts on them."},{"id":"n-vg-m-criticism","t":"vg","tp":"tough","ti":"Taking criticism without shutting down","mv":"Vinh's two mistakes with criticism: getting defensive straight away, and treating all of it the same. Most people giving feedback are trying to help but say it clumsily, so listen for the useful part.","tr":"Next time you get criticism, say \"Thanks, let me think about that\" before you explain anything.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jul 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-prime-the-change"],"how":["Vinh's two mistakes with criticism: getting defensive straight away, and treating all of it the same. Most people giving feedback are trying to help but say it clumsily, so listen for the useful part. But mocking comments about how you look aren't feedback; the danger is the belief you start to carry because of them.","He once got so stung by comments on his early videos that he stopped showing his face on camera for years, and later saw that the belief, not the comments, had held him back."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Taking criticism without shutting down”?","a":"Next time you get criticism, say \"Thanks, let me think about that\" before you explain anything."},{"id":"n-vg-m-avoid-overcorrect","t":"vg","tp":"tough","ti":"Between bottling it up and blurting it out","mv":"Vinh says he used to avoid hard conversations until something tiny, like a cup left without a coaster, set off an explosion. Then he overcorrected and said everything, forcefully, and people got defensive or shut down.","tr":"In your next tricky talk, notice the first sign they're going quiet or tense, and pause to reassure.","w":"Vinh says he used to avoid hard conversations until something tiny, like a cup left without a coaster, set off an explosion.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Aug 2026"],"rel":["g32","g29"],"how":["Vinh says he used to avoid hard conversations until something tiny, like a cup left without a coaster, set off an explosion. Then he overcorrected and said everything, forcefully, and people got defensive or shut down. The skill in between is watching the other person as well as the topic: the moment they stop feeling safe, slow down and rebuild that first.","He credits the book Crucial Conversations for the idea of tracking both the content and how safe the other person feels."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Between bottling it up and blurting it out”?","a":"Vinh says he used to avoid hard conversations until something tiny, like a cup left without a coaster, set off an explosion. Then he overcorrected and said everything, forcefully, and people got defensive or shut down."},{"id":"n-vg-m-cant-get-wrong","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Practise until you can't get it wrong","mv":"Vinh's line on repetition: amateurs practise until they get it right; masters practise until they can't get it wrong.","tr":"Pick one fundamental, like pausing after your key point, and repeat it in every conversation this week.","w":"People skip repetition because familiar feels like competent, and they hunt for new tricks instead of finer detail in the basics.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Aug 2025"],"rel":["g14","g41"],"how":["Vinh's line on repetition: amateurs practise until they get it right; masters practise until they can't get it wrong. People skip repetition because familiar feels like competent, and they hunt for new tricks instead of finer detail in the basics."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Practise until you can't get it wrong”?","a":"People skip repetition because familiar feels like competent, and they hunt for new tricks instead of finer detail in the basics."},{"id":"n-vg-m-impromptu-drill","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"A five-minute speaking-on-the-spot drill","mv":"Vinh's home drill: write (or generate) ten easy, open questions about everyday life and work, then record yourself answering them out loud for five minutes, moving on when you run dry.","tr":"Do one five-minute round tonight with three questions you've written.","w":"Stick a photo of a friend above your phone so you talk to a person, not a lens.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Nov 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pick-three"],"how":["Vinh's home drill: write (or generate) ten easy, open questions about everyday life and work, then record yourself answering them out loud for five minutes, moving on when you run dry. Stick a photo of a friend above your phone so you talk to a person, not a lens. Then review it: listen first, then watch."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “A five-minute speaking-on-the-spot drill”?","a":"Do one five-minute round tonight with three questions you've written."},{"id":"n-vg-m-count-three","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Count to three before you reply","mv":"Vinh's tip for quiet authority: when someone finishes speaking, count to three in your head while you look interested, then reply. It stops you jumping in with your own story, and it makes what you do say land harder.","tr":"In your next conversation, count to three silently before every reply.","w":"Speaking less isn't the same as never speaking: say less, and make it count.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Oct 2025"],"how":["Vinh's tip for quiet authority: when someone finishes speaking, count to three in your head while you look interested, then reply. It stops you jumping in with your own story, and it makes what you do say land harder. Speaking less isn't the same as never speaking: say less, and make it count."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Pause first, then share","note":"Two of Vinh's listening habits that work together.","chain":[{"label":"Before you reply","when":"Video · Oct 2025","text":"Vinh's tip for quiet authority: when someone finishes speaking, count to three in your head while you look interested, then reply. It stops you jumping in with your own story, and it makes what you do say land harder.","card":"n-vg-m-count-three"},{"label":"When to share","when":"Video · Sep 2026","text":"Vinh's flow for an easy conversation: reflect what you noticed, then pause and say nothing. Pull on their threads, but after they've shared about three things, share one of your own, or it starts to feel like an interview.","card":"n-vg-m-three-to-one"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Count to three before you reply”?","a":"Vinh's tip for quiet authority: when someone finishes speaking, count to three in your head while you look interested, then reply. It stops you jumping in with your own story, and it makes what you do say land harder."},{"id":"n-vg-m-check-level","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Ask how much they already know","mv":"Vinh admits he used big words and acronyms to sound clever when explaining tax returns. Now he checks first.","tr":"Before your next explanation, ask how familiar they are with the topic, and drop one acronym.","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jun 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-ccc","n-vg-m-clean-thinking"],"how":["Vinh admits he used big words and acronyms to sound clever when explaining tax returns. Now he checks first. One to one: \"Before I go any further, how familiar are you with this?\" Before a talk, he asks the organiser where most of the audience sits, from beginner to expert."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Ask how much they already know”?","a":"Vinh admits he used big words and acronyms to sound clever when explaining tax returns. Now he checks first."},{"id":"n-vg-m-three-to-one","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Reflect, pause, and the 3-to-1 ratio","mv":"Vinh's flow for an easy conversation: reflect what you noticed, then pause and say nothing. Pull on their threads, but after they've shared about three things, share one of your own, or it starts to feel like an interview.","tr":"In your next catch-up, count three things they share before you offer one of yours.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Sep 2026"],"rel":["g23"],"how":["Vinh's flow for an easy conversation: reflect what you noticed, then pause and say nothing. Pull on their threads, but after they've shared about three things, share one of your own, or it starts to feel like an interview. When you share, offer an experience, not advice."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Pause first, then share","note":"Two of Vinh's listening habits that work together.","chain":[{"label":"Before you reply","when":"Video · Oct 2025","text":"Vinh's tip for quiet authority: when someone finishes speaking, count to three in your head while you look interested, then reply. It stops you jumping in with your own story, and it makes what you do say land harder.","card":"n-vg-m-count-three"},{"label":"When to share","when":"Video · Sep 2026","text":"Vinh's flow for an easy conversation: reflect what you noticed, then pause and say nothing. Pull on their threads, but after they've shared about three things, share one of your own, or it starts to feel like an interview.","card":"n-vg-m-three-to-one"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Reflect, pause, and the 3-to-1 ratio”?","a":"In your next catch-up, count three things they share before you offer one of yours."},{"id":"n-vve-m-subtle-drainers","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"The most draining people aren't the obvious ones","mv":"Vanessa says the people who do the most damage are rarely the openly mean ones. They're the subtle ones: the passive-aggressive comment, or the friend who answers your good news with questions that make you doubt it.","tr":"Think of who you'd tell good news to first, and who you'd avoid. Spend more time with the first.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["The Unplanned Podcast, Mar 2025"],"rel":["n-vve-m-one-uppers","n-vve-m-relationship-audit"],"how":["Vanessa says the people who do the most damage are rarely the openly mean ones. They're the subtle ones: the passive-aggressive comment, or the friend who answers your good news with questions that make you doubt it. She calls them \"dream killers\".","Notice who you hesitate to tell good news to. That hesitation is information."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The most draining people aren't the obvious ones”?","a":"Vanessa says the people who do the most damage are rarely the openly mean ones. They're the subtle ones: the passive-aggressive comment, or the friend who answers your good news with questions that make you doubt it."},{"id":"n-vve-m-face-at-rest","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Check what your face does at rest","mv":"Vanessa's mirror check: relax completely and look at your face at rest. Mouth corners that turn down read as sad.","tr":"Tonight, look at your relaxed face in a mirror for ten seconds and note one thing to soften when it counts.","w":"You don't need to hold a smile all day.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Mar 2026"],"rel":["v84","n-vve-m-own-baseline"],"how":["Vanessa's mirror check: relax completely and look at your face at rest. Mouth corners that turn down read as sad. Vertical lines between the eyebrows read as angry, even when you feel fine. Some people also default to a slight frown or a wrinkled nose without knowing it.","She learned this the hard way. On a video podcast she was listening hard, and the host told her she looked bored. Her resting face just looks low. You don't need to hold a smile all day. When it matters, at an interview, a networking event or the break room, lift the corners of your mouth a touch and relax your brow. It's a muscle, so practise it."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Check what your face does at rest”?","a":"You don't need to hold a smile all day."},{"id":"n-vve-m-compliment-both-ways","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Compliments lift the giver too","mv":"Vanessa shares research on the warmth and competence model. Giving a sincere compliment raised how warm and competent both people seemed, the giver as well as the receiver.","tr":"Give one small, specific compliment today that you'd normally keep to yourself.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Feb 2026"],"rel":["n-vve-compliments"],"how":["Vanessa shares research on the warmth and competence model. Giving a sincere compliment raised how warm and competent both people seemed, the giver as well as the receiver. Yet people give far fewer compliments than they think of, mostly because they fear it will be awkward. The research says it usually goes better than we expect.","Skip the obvious. Someone praised for the same thing over and over stops hearing it. Be specific, and praise what they value or worked hard at."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Compliments, two ways","note":"How to give a good compliment, and what the research says it does for you.","chain":[{"label":"How to give one","when":"Book · Cues","text":"If you think something nice, say it. Make it specific, skip the obvious, and praise what they worked on rather than what they were born with.","card":"n-vve-compliments"},{"label":"It lifts the giver too","when":"Video · Feb 2026","text":"Vanessa shares research on the warmth and competence model. Giving a sincere compliment raised how warm and competent both people seemed, the giver as well as the receiver.","card":"n-vve-m-compliment-both-ways"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Compliments lift the giver too”?","a":"Give one small, specific compliment today that you'd normally keep to yourself."},{"id":"n-vve-m-social-battery","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Know your social battery","mv":"Vanessa checked the popular claim that introverts have exactly 2.3 hours of social energy and couldn't find the research behind it. The useful point stands, though: everyone's battery is different.","tr":"Before your next big social event, pick one battery pack: a person, a topic or a setting that gives you energy.","w":"Choose settings that help too, like a walk or a meal you love instead of a plain coffee.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Feb 2026"],"rel":["n-vve-m-relationship-audit"],"how":["Vanessa checked the popular claim that introverts have exactly 2.3 hours of social energy and couldn't find the research behind it. The useful point stands, though: everyone's battery is different. Work out your own. How long can you last at a loud party compared with dinner with close friends? Who and what fills you up, and who drains you?","Then plan around it. If an event will drain you fast, plan recharge time, bring someone who lifts your energy, or steer toward topics you enjoy. Choose settings that help too, like a walk or a meal you love instead of a plain coffee. She calls these your \"battery packs\"."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Know your social battery”?","a":"Vanessa checked the popular claim that introverts have exactly 2.3 hours of social energy and couldn't find the research behind it. The useful point stands, though: everyone's battery is different."},{"id":"n-vve-m-ask-preferences","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Ask how people like to work","mv":"Vanessa's first step to psychological safety is the simplest: ask about preferences. Do they like to brainstorm by email or in person?","tr":"Ask one colleague this week: \"Do you prefer a quick call or a message when I need something?\"","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Dec 2025"],"rel":["v77"],"how":["Vanessa's first step to psychological safety is the simplest: ask about preferences. Do they like to brainstorm by email or in person? Do they want the agenda ahead of time to think it over? Do they prefer a quick call or a message? Many people who feel unsafe at work have had their preferences ignored before.","Asking shows you want to meet them where they work best. For someone you want to invest in more deeply, she suggests you both take a free Big Five personality quiz and talk through your results together."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Ask how people like to work”?","a":"Vanessa's first step to psychological safety is the simplest: ask about preferences. Do they like to brainstorm by email or in person?"},{"id":"n-vve-m-warm-voice","t":"vve","tp":"voice","ti":"Warm voice comes from what you say","mv":"Vanessa says the easiest way to warm up your voice is through content, not technique. Talk about real feelings: something that made you happy, sad or amazed.","tr":"In your next talk or pitch, swap one list of points for a short story you actually care about.","w":"Tell a story rather than reciting points.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Oct 2025"],"rel":["n-vve-m-outbreath-hello"],"how":["Vanessa says the easiest way to warm up your voice is through content, not technique. Talk about real feelings: something that made you happy, sad or amazed. Tell a story rather than reciting points. Choose material you truly believe helps people, and the conviction comes through in your voice.","The body helps too. Keep space between your ears and shoulders and between your arms and torso, so you can breathe deeply. Add pauses, since you'll breathe naturally in them, and slow down."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Warm voice comes from what you say”?","a":"In your next talk or pitch, swap one list of points for a short story you actually care about."},{"id":"n-vve-m-charged-talks","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Three moves for an emotionally charged talk","mv":"Vanessa's three moves. First, pause when either of you is in fight-or-flight: suggest picking it up later in the week, or take a short break for a coffee.","tr":"Next time a talk heats up, ask \"What do we agree on here?\" before you argue the next point.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Oct 2025"],"how":["Vanessa's three moves. First, pause when either of you is in fight-or-flight: suggest picking it up later in the week, or take a short break for a coffee. Second, go back to what you agree on, like the final goal, and build from there. Third, get to next steps quickly so the talk moves forward, not round in circles.","You can hear when the other person is charged, from a shaky voice or tense face, even if you're calm. That's a good moment to offer the pause."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Three moves for an emotionally charged talk”?","a":"Vanessa's three moves. First, pause when either of you is in fight-or-flight: suggest picking it up later in the week, or take a short break for a coffee."},{"id":"n-vve-m-friend-signals","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Early signs a new friendship may not suit you","mv":"Vanessa lists patterns worth noticing in a budding friendship. They never ask you anything back.","tr":"Think of a new friendship and ask yourself honestly: do I look forward to seeing them?","w":"They never ask you anything back.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Aug 2025"],"rel":["n-vve-m-relationship-audit","n-vve-m-subtle-drainers","v49"],"how":["Vanessa lists patterns worth noticing in a budding friendship. They never ask you anything back. Every story is about money, status or name-dropping. Nothing is ever their responsibility. Every part of their life is in conflict. They say yes and then cancel or turn up very late. They're unkind to servers or drivers.","Her bonus check is your own body. Do you look forward to seeing them, or feel a knot when their message pops up? Notice patterns over time, not one bad day, and use them to decide where to put your energy, not to judge the person."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Early signs a new friendship may not suit you”?","a":"They never ask you anything back."},{"id":"n-vve-m-host-easy","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"When you host, make connecting easy","mv":"When Vanessa hosts, her main worry is whether guests will find it easy to connect, so she builds that in. At her wedding, every table had a folded paper game with conversation starters inside.","tr":"For your next gathering, put a few good questions on the table or in a game.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Jul 2025"],"rel":["v48"],"how":["When Vanessa hosts, her main worry is whether guests will find it easy to connect, so she builds that in. At her wedding, every table had a folded paper game with conversation starters inside. At her event, she set rounds of questions and hid starters around the room.","She also thinks about guests who like structure. Even at her wedding there was a printed agenda for the people who feel calmer knowing what's next."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “When you host, make connecting easy”?","a":"For your next gathering, put a few good questions on the table or in a game."},{"id":"n-vve-m-feature-story","t":"vve","tp":"listening","ti":"Listen as if they'll be in your next book","mv":"Vanessa says one sign of emotional intelligence is being able to get curious about almost anyone. When she gets trapped in her own head, she plays a trick.","tr":"In your next conversation, tell yourself they have a story worth retelling, and look for it.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Jul 2025"],"rel":["v51"],"how":["Vanessa says one sign of emotional intelligence is being able to get curious about almost anyone. When she gets trapped in her own head, she plays a trick. She tells herself this person has a story she'll want to feature in her next book, so she has to find it.","That changes the questions you ask, because now you're hunting for something. You can also imagine you're about to introduce them to a friend and need one great fact."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Listen as if they'll be in your next book”?","a":"Vanessa says one sign of emotional intelligence is being able to get curious about almost anyone. When she gets trapped in her own head, she plays a trick."},{"id":"n-vve-m-name-it-precisely","t":"vve","tp":"ideas","ti":"Name the feeling precisely","mv":"Vanessa sees emotional intelligence partly as noticing what you feel while you feel it, instead of shutting it down. A wider vocabulary helps.","tr":"The next time you feel \"angry\", pause and pick a more exact word.","w":"Vanessa sees emotional intelligence partly as noticing what you feel while you feel it, instead of shutting it down.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Jul 2025"],"how":["Vanessa sees emotional intelligence partly as noticing what you feel while you feel it, instead of shutting it down. A wider vocabulary helps. Is it anger, or frustration, disappointment or irritation? Each means something slightly different, and naming it exactly helps you understand it and choose what to do.","She also keeps a \"failure file\". For each mistake, big or small, she writes what happened, why, and what she learned, which turns failures into lessons."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Name the feeling precisely”?","a":"Vanessa sees emotional intelligence partly as noticing what you feel while you feel it, instead of shutting it down."},{"id":"n-vve-m-larger-mission","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Help each person see their part in the mission","mv":"Vanessa tells a well-known story from a NASA visit. A janitor asked what he did said he was helping put a man on the moon.","tr":"Tell one person this week how their work connects to the bigger goal.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Jun 2025"],"rel":["v65"],"how":["Vanessa tells a well-known story from a NASA visit. A janitor asked what he did said he was helping put a man on the moon. Her leadership lesson is that leading isn't about your own big impact. It's helping every person, at work or at home, see how their part, however small, matters to the bigger goal.","She openly admits the photo she used for the story was AI-generated. The story is real; there was simply no picture of it."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Help each person see their part in the mission”?","a":"Tell one person this week how their work connects to the bigger goal."},{"id":"n-vve-m-just-hey","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"The simplest opener: \"Hey\"","mv":"Asked how to approach someone you're interested in, Vanessa's answer is a warm, simple \"Hey.\" If they're taken or not interested, you both move on with no awkwardness.","tr":"Next time you'd like to talk to someone, try a relaxed \"Hey\" and see what comes back.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Jun 2025"],"rel":["n-vve-head-tilt","v19"],"how":["Asked how to approach someone you're interested in, Vanessa's answer is a warm, simple \"Hey.\" If they're taken or not interested, you both move on with no awkwardness. If they're interested, they'll say hey back and the door is open. It works for anyone.","Add a small head tilt if you like, as it signals engagement. The simpler the opener, the less there is to go wrong."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “Asked how to approach someone you're interested in, Vanessa's answer …”","a":"“ Asked how to approach someone you're interested in, Vanessa's answer is a warm, simple ”"},{"id":"n-vve-m-greeting-signals","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Signal hug or handshake clearly","mv":"Vanessa says awkward greetings come from mixed signals. If you want a hug, come in with both palms open and your chest toward them.","tr":"At your next greeting, decide first, hug or handshake, and make your body say it.","w":"For the handshake: thumb up, hand vertical, never flipped on top.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Jun 2025"],"rel":["v82","v81"],"how":["Vanessa says awkward greetings come from mixed signals. If you want a hug, come in with both palms open and your chest toward them. If you want a handshake, turn your body slightly to the side and offer one hand. If they angle away, that's your cue to switch to a handshake.","For the handshake: thumb up, hand vertical, never flipped on top. Keep your hand dry (holding a drink in a napkin helps), squeeze firmly but not hard, and give one to three pumps. A wave or fist bump is fine if you'd rather not touch."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Signal hug or handshake clearly”?","a":"For the handshake: thumb up, hand vertical, never flipped on top."},{"id":"n-vve-m-space-touch","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Space, eye contact and touch: the top pet peeves","mv":"In a poll of about 6,000 of Vanessa's followers, standing too close was the top nonverbal pet peeve. Her guide: about 18 inches is the closest, and around three feet is more comfortable.","tr":"In your next chat, check your distance and try noticing their eye colour.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Jun 2025"],"rel":["v84"],"how":["In a poll of about 6,000 of Vanessa's followers, standing too close was the top nonverbal pet peeve. Her guide: about 18 inches is the closest, and around three feet is more comfortable. Space norms vary by culture, so watch where people stand. Also aim for roughly 60 to 70 percent eye contact in Western settings, not 100.","She's a natural arm-toucher, so she now reaches out and stops just short, a \"hover\". It shows warmth without crossing a boundary. Her eye-contact check: if you notice their eye colour, you're making enough."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Space, eye contact and touch: the top pet peeves”?","a":"In your next chat, check your distance and try noticing their eye colour."},{"id":"n-vve-m-mute-listening","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Look engaged on mute","mv":"Vanessa says most people look bored on mute, even when they aren't.","tr":"In your next video call, give the speaker one clear eyebrow raise when they make a good point.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), May 2025"],"rel":["n-vve-triple-nod","n-vve-head-tilt"],"how":["Vanessa says most people look bored on mute, even when they aren't. Her listening cues: raise your eyebrows when something's interesting, mouth a silent \"wow\", lean toward the camera, and narrow your lower lids slightly as if focusing on something good. Finish with a slow triple nod or a head tilt.","The speaker's eye goes to whoever looks most engaged on the grid, so they'll start speaking to you. The speaker feels good, and you look good."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Look engaged on mute”?","a":"Vanessa says most people look bored on mute, even when they aren't."},{"id":"n-vve-m-interested-wins","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Be interested to be interesting","mv":"Vanessa tells a famous story of two rival British politicians. A woman who dined with each said that after sitting with Gladstone she thought he was the cleverest person in England.","tr":"At your next gathering, aim to leave one person feeling clever, not to look clever yourself.","w":"Don't worry about being the funniest or smartest in the room.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Apr 2025"],"how":["Vanessa tells a famous story of two rival British politicians. A woman who dined with each said that after sitting with Gladstone she thought he was the cleverest person in England. After sitting with Disraeli, she thought she was. Disraeli made her feel interesting, and Vanessa's point is that's what wins people.","Don't worry about being the funniest or smartest in the room. Arrive curious, ask good questions and show empathy."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Be interested to be interesting”?","a":"Don't worry about being the funniest or smartest in the room."},{"id":"n-vve-m-friend-questions","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Questions that help you know a friend better","mv":"Vanessa's friend questions: What was your best birthday ever? What's your favourite place in the world?","tr":"Ask a friend two of these this week, starting with the light ones.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Mar 2025"],"rel":["v49"],"how":["Vanessa's friend questions: What was your best birthday ever? What's your favourite place in the world? What was your first concert? What are your biggest pet peeves? What's your guilty pleasure? What was the best gift you ever got, and the worst? Are you an introvert, extrovert or in between? How do you feel most misunderstood?","Many answers are useful later. A favourite place shows you how they recharge. The birthday answer quietly gives you ideas for their next one. The misunderstood question surprised her with almost every friend she asked."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Questions that help you know a friend better”?","a":"Ask a friend two of these this week, starting with the light ones."},{"id":"n-vve-m-date-levels","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Move a date past the basics","mv":"Vanessa describes three levels of knowing someone. Level one is basics: job, hometown.","tr":"On your next date, swap one basic question for \"What excites you most in your life right now?\"","w":"A bad second date never leaves it.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Feb 2025"],"rel":["v52","n-vve-go-deeper"],"how":["Vanessa describes three levels of knowing someone. Level one is basics: job, hometown. A bad second date never leaves it. Level two is what drives and worries them: \"What's your big goal this year?\" \"Are you learning anything right now?\" \"What's on your bucket list?\" Level three is the story they tell about themselves: \"Who's your role model?\" \"What shaped who you are?\"","Don't rush to level three. Hint at it once level two is going well, perhaps by the third date."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Move a date past the basics”?","a":"Vanessa describes three levels of knowing someone. Level one is basics: job, hometown."},{"id":"n-vve-m-year-end-questions","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Year-end questions to ask each other","mv":"Vanessa's year-end prompts, for yourself, a partner or friends: What did you learn about yourself this year? What habit did you form, or break?","tr":"Pick three of these for your next dinner with friends or family.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Dec 2024"],"rel":["v37"],"how":["Vanessa's year-end prompts, for yourself, a partner or friends: What did you learn about yourself this year? What habit did you form, or break? What was the highlight, and the low point? What surprised you with joy? What risk did you take? What was your most meaningful conversation? How do you want to feel this time next year?","Two she likes: who were the five people you spent most time with this year, and who would you like it to be next year?"]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Year-end questions to ask each other”?","a":"Vanessa's year-end prompts, for yourself, a partner or friends: What did you learn about yourself this year? What habit did you form, or break?"},{"id":"n-vve-m-meeting-first","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Get heard in meetings: go first, say something new","mv":"Vanessa's meeting tips for quieter people. Volunteer to go first, because early ideas get referred back to, and credited, all meeting long.","tr":"In your next meeting, put your hand up to go first, or say one new idea in a steady, downward tone.","w":"When you speak, add something new rather than \"just building on what they said\".","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Dec 2024"],"how":["Vanessa's meeting tips for quieter people. Volunteer to go first, because early ideas get referred back to, and credited, all meeting long. When you speak, add something new rather than \"just building on what they said\". Use a downward tone and don't apologise first. And use the start and end of meetings, when people remember most and the chat is easier.","Arriving a few minutes early to chat, or staying to wish people well, counts as time in the meeting with far less pressure."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Get heard in meetings: go first, say something new”?","a":"In your next meeting, put your hand up to go first, or say one new idea in a steady, downward tone."},{"id":"n-vve-m-subject-lines","t":"vve","tp":"ideas","ti":"Email subject lines people want to open","mv":"After writing thousands of newsletters, Vanessa's first rule is to drop generic subject lines like \"Follow up\", \"Meeting\" or \"Overview\". People open those on autopilot, if at all.","tr":"Rewrite the subject line of your next email so it states the main point.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Nov 2024"],"rel":["v71"],"how":["After writing thousands of newsletters, Vanessa's first rule is to drop generic subject lines like \"Follow up\", \"Meeting\" or \"Overview\". People open those on autopilot, if at all. Instead, either put your main point in the subject, so they know what's coming before they open it, or make it intriguing: a question, a teaser or a surprise.","Keep the intrigue honest, not clickbait. You want people to feel good when your name appears in their inbox."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Email subject lines people want to open”?","a":"After writing thousands of newsletters, Vanessa's first rule is to drop generic subject lines like \"Follow up\", \"Meeting\" or \"Overview\". People open those on autopilot, if at all."},{"id":"n-vve-m-past-relationship","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Answering \"Tell me about your last relationship\"","mv":"Vanessa's three-part answer. Start by saying you won't go into too much detail, which protects everyone's privacy.","tr":"Draft your three lines now, so a date doesn't catch you off guard.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Dec 2024"],"rel":["n-vve-m-name-statement","v57"],"how":["Vanessa's three-part answer. Start by saying you won't go into too much detail, which protects everyone's privacy. Then share two or three things you learned or how you grew. End with what you hope for next, such as a best friend with similar values.","It works whether the relationship was good or bad, and it helps you reframe the past for yourself too."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “Tell me about your …”","a":"“Tell me about your last relationship”"},{"id":"n-vve-m-pause-is-normal","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"A short pause before you answer is normal","mv":"Vanessa says being fine with pausing took her a long time and was a confidence issue.","tr":"In your next conversation, take one full breath before you answer a question.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"how":["Vanessa says being fine with pausing took her a long time and was a confidence issue. It's okay to say \"Let me think about that for a second.\" Large studies of real conversation suggest a one to two second pause before replying is normal. It feels like five seconds to you but sounds natural to them.","Taking that space reads as intelligence and confidence, not hesitation."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “A short pause before you answer is normal”?","a":"In your next conversation, take one full breath before you answer a question."},{"id":"n-vve-m-ramble-know-a-lot","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Rambling? It's because you know a lot","mv":"Vanessa calls rambling the curse of smart people: when you know a subject well, it's hard to explain to someone who doesn't. When you catch yourself rambling, don't get annoyed at yourself.","tr":"Next time you hear yourself ramble, think \"I know a lot\", then give the one-line version.","w":"When you catch yourself rambling, don't get annoyed at yourself.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"how":["Vanessa calls rambling the curse of smart people: when you know a subject well, it's hard to explain to someone who doesn't. When you catch yourself rambling, don't get annoyed at yourself. Tell yourself, \"That's because I know a lot. I just need to simplify.\"","Being kind to yourself in the moment gets you back on track faster than an inner scolding."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Rambling? It's because you know a lot”?","a":"Vanessa calls rambling the curse of smart people: when you know a subject well, it's hard to explain to someone who doesn't. When you catch yourself rambling, don't get annoyed at yourself."},{"id":"n-vve-m-shared-goals","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"You don't have to like someone to talk well","mv":"Vanessa's view: you can have a productive conversation without liking someone, and you can't fake liking. So aim for effective, not friendly.","tr":"With someone you find hard, open with a shared goal: \"What do you think of the new plan?\"","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vve-m-platinum-rule","n-vve-curious-critical"],"how":["Vanessa's view: you can have a productive conversation without liking someone, and you can't fake liking. So aim for effective, not friendly. Build warmth through shared goals: industry news, the next quarter, a prediction you can debate. That's real common ground, not forced chat about their kids.","She dropped \"how to be instantly likable\" as a book subtitle when she realised people can work well together without being friends."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “You don't have to like someone to talk well”?","a":"Vanessa's view: you can have a productive conversation without liking someone, and you can't fake liking. So aim for effective, not friendly."},{"id":"n-vg-m-exit-one-more","t":"vg","tp":"openers","ti":"Leave with a reflection and one last question","mv":"Vinh's two-step exit.","tr":"Next time you need to leave, pick one thing they mentioned and ask one final question about it.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"rel":["g23","g30"],"how":["Vinh's two-step exit. First, reflect back something they mentioned, to show you listened: \"I have to try that pizza place.\" Then give a time limit and ask one last question about it: \"I've got to jump into a meeting, but before I go, what should I order there?\"","Ending on their topic means the last thing they remember is you being interested in them."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Leave with a reflection and one last question”?","a":"Next time you need to leave, pick one thing they mentioned and ask one final question about it."},{"id":"n-vg-m-overarticulate","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Exaggerate your mouth to speak more clearly","mv":"Vinh says clarity starts with clear words before any framework. English is his third language, and he used to carry over mouth shapes from Vietnamese and Chinese, so \"three\" came out as \"free\".","tr":"Read one page aloud tonight, over-moving your mouth on every word.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-vocal-image"],"how":["Vinh says clarity starts with clear words before any framework. English is his third language, and he used to carry over mouth shapes from Vietnamese and Chinese, so \"three\" came out as \"free\". A speech pathologist told him to fix the words first. The drill: open any book at a random page and read aloud with exaggerated lip and mouth movements, five minutes a day.","To level up, read with a pen held across your mouth, which makes your lips and tongue work harder. Take it out and your normal speech feels crisper."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Exaggerate your mouth to speak more clearly”?","a":"Vinh says clarity starts with clear words before any framework. English is his third language, and he used to carry over mouth shapes from Vietnamese and Chinese, so \"three\" came out as \"free\"."},{"id":"n-vg-m-vocal-archetypes","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Four voices: motivator, educator, coach, friend","mv":"Vinh's four vocal archetypes at work. Motivator inspires.","tr":"Before your next meeting, decide which archetype you'll open in and which you'll move to.","w":"Problems come from being stuck in one: always friend, and people don't take you seriously; always coach, and they find you intimidating; always educator, and you sound like a lecture.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026","Coaching on stage, Jun 2026"],"rel":["g35"],"how":["Vinh's four vocal archetypes at work. Motivator inspires. Educator teaches, slower, in steps. Coach pushes, firm and direct. Friend chats warmly. Problems come from being stuck in one: always friend, and people don't take you seriously; always coach, and they find you intimidating; always educator, and you sound like a lecture.","Changing gear isn't fake. You already talk differently to a child, a boss and a mate. What looks fake is a sudden jump, so ease from one to the next. For \"Tell me about yourself\" in an interview, he starts in friend, then shows competence."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Four voices: motivator, educator, coach, friend”?","a":"Problems come from being stuck in one: always friend, and people don't take you seriously; always coach, and they find you intimidating; always educator, and you sound like a lecture."},{"id":"n-vg-m-headline-first","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Give senior people the result, not the process","mv":"Vinh says smart people often ramble because they think more detail means more value, or want to prove how hard they worked. Senior leaders usually want the distilled answer.","tr":"Write your next status update as one headline sentence, then stop.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pause-clarify"],"how":["Vinh says smart people often ramble because they think more detail means more value, or want to prove how hard they worked. Senior leaders usually want the distilled answer. Asked \"How's the campaign going?\", say \"We're getting double our spend back. It's going well,\" and let them ask for the process if they want it.","If you need a structure, he reaches for 3-2-1: three steps, two types, or the one thing."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Give senior people the result, not the process”?","a":"Write your next status update as one headline sentence, then stop."},{"id":"n-vg-m-rehearse-three-ways","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Rehearse in three rounds: read, improvise, hide cues","mv":"Vinh's answer to blanking out is better rehearsal, not less. Round one is a table read: read the whole talk aloud with full enthusiasm, even sitting down.","tr":"Do one improv run of your next talk with the notes face down.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-practise-as-you-deliver","n-vg-m-cant-get-wrong"],"how":["Vinh's answer to blanking out is better rehearsal, not less. Round one is a table read: read the whole talk aloud with full enthusiasm, even sitting down. Round two is an improv read: put the notes aside and deliver it from memory. When you lose your place, pause and practise finding it again. Round three is hiding a few key points where only you can see them.","The effort of recalling it without notes is what makes it stick. If you go blank on the day, you've already practised calmly finding your way back."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Rehearse in three rounds: read, improvise, hide cues”?","a":"Vinh's answer to blanking out is better rehearsal, not less. Round one is a table read: read the whole talk aloud with full enthusiasm, even sitting down."},{"id":"n-vg-m-interrupted-kindly","t":"vg","tp":"tough","ti":"When someone keeps interrupting you","mv":"Vinh starts by giving the benefit of the doubt: most interrupters fear forgetting a thought they think is valuable. If it keeps happening, he stops, looks at them, and stays calm and silent for three or four seconds.","tr":"Next time you're cut off, try the calm three-second pause before you speak.","w":"Say you know they don't mean it, explain that it undermines you in front of the team, and that you're raising it because you trust them.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Sep 2026"],"how":["Vinh starts by giving the benefit of the doubt: most interrupters fear forgetting a thought they think is valuable. If it keeps happening, he stops, looks at them, and stays calm and silent for three or four seconds. Then he says firmly, \"Thanks for your input. Do you mind if I finish my thought, then I'll hand over to you?\"","If it still continues, talk privately. Say you know they don't mean it, explain that it undermines you in front of the team, and that you're raising it because you trust them. That beats complaining about them to colleagues. Also check your own voice and posture, since quiet, still speakers get cut off more."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “When someone keeps interrupting you”?","a":"Say you know they don't mean it, explain that it undermines you in front of the team, and that you're raising it because you trust them."},{"id":"n-vg-m-meet-their-level","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Listen to find their level, then meet them there","mv":"Vinh says leaders who don't listen talk to everyone at the same level, and people get lost. Start with an open question like \"How has your journey here been?\" and let them talk.","tr":"When you explain something today, watch for a confused look and offer to simplify.","w":"Vinh says leaders who don't listen talk to everyone at the same level, and people get lost.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-check-level","g28"],"how":["Vinh says leaders who don't listen talk to everyone at the same level, and people get lost. Start with an open question like \"How has your journey here been?\" and let them talk. A few follow-ups (\"What's hard? What's easy?\") show you where they are, so you can pitch to it.","His old accountant talked over his head every month and never noticed his confused face. His new one sees a raised eyebrow and says, \"Looks like I should simplify that.\" Listen with your eyes too."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “How has your journey …”","a":"“How has your journey here been?”"},{"id":"n-vg-m-breath-support","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Nine minutes a day for breath support","mv":"Many people start a sentence strong and trail into a creaky \"vocal fry\" because they run out of air. Vinh's nine-minute routine, three minutes each.","tr":"Do three minutes of belly breathing and three of hissing before your next important call.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Aug 2026"],"rel":["g6"],"how":["Many people start a sentence strong and trail into a creaky \"vocal fry\" because they run out of air. Vinh's nine-minute routine, three minutes each. First, breathe slowly through your nose into your belly with jaw, neck and shoulders relaxed. Second, breathe in the same way and let it out as a long steady hiss. Third, breathe in and count aloud on one breath.","The aim is to give your last word as much air as your first. A steady stream of air is what gives your voice stamina, volume and resonance."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Nine minutes a day for breath support”?","a":"Many people start a sentence strong and trail into a creaky \"vocal fry\" because they run out of air. Vinh's nine-minute routine, three minutes each."},{"id":"n-vg-m-review-right-after","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Think of it too late? Review right after","mv":"Vinh says being quick starts with the basics. When voice and body are second nature, you're calm and present instead of in your head.","tr":"After your next meeting, spend five minutes writing what you'd say differently.","w":"When voice and body are second nature, you're calm and present instead of in your head.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jul 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-practical-magic","n-vg-m-pick-three"],"how":["Vinh says being quick starts with the basics. When voice and body are second nature, you're calm and present instead of in your head. The other half is reviewing straight after: take five to ten minutes after a meeting to ask what you could have said or done better. Don't wait three days.","At first the good idea arrives an hour later. With regular reviews, the gap shrinks to minutes, and eventually the idea comes while you're still talking. He also reviews his whole year each December to spot what to do more and less of."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Think of it too late? Review right after”?","a":"When voice and body are second nature, you're calm and present instead of in your head."},{"id":"n-vg-m-speak-to-one","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Speak to many as you would to one","mv":"Coaching a speaker on stage, Vinh had him swap group language for singular: instead of \"You all know what I mean?\", ask \"Have you been through that?\" while looking at one person.","tr":"In your next talk, say one line to one person using \"you\", not \"you all\".","w":"Coaching a speaker on stage, Vinh had him swap group language for singular: instead of \"You all know what I mean?\", ask \"Have you been through that?\" while looking at one person.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jun 2026"],"how":["Coaching a speaker on stage, Vinh had him swap group language for singular: instead of \"You all know what I mean?\", ask \"Have you been through that?\" while looking at one person. Pair one sentence with one set of eyes, then move on. He also had him open in a warm, conversational tone before moving into a firmer coaching tone.","He suggests using the stage in layers: start in the middle, step forward only for your key point, then step back. And watch for gestures, like a wagging finger, that can feel like talking down to a child."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “You all know what …”","a":"“You all know what I mean?”"},{"id":"n-vg-m-ask-for-time","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"It's fine to ask for more time","mv":"Vinh's three steps for a high-pressure question. Pause as long as you need, looking composed: glance aside, hand near your chin, not slouched or fidgeting.","tr":"Practise saying \"Let me come back to you on that\" so it feels natural when you need it.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), May 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pat"],"how":["Vinh's three steps for a high-pressure question. Pause as long as you need, looking composed: glance aside, hand near your chin, not slouched or fidgeting. If nothing good comes after a few seconds, say so: \"I'd love to give you a thoughtful answer. Can I come back to you after lunch?\" If you do answer, give one thing, then ask \"Want me to go deeper?\"","Answering instantly doesn't make you look smart. It can look as though you didn't think. People would rather wait for a useful answer than get a rushed one."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “It's fine to ask for more time”?","a":"Vinh's three steps for a high-pressure question. Pause as long as you need, looking composed: glance aside, hand near your chin, not slouched or fidgeting."},{"id":"n-vg-m-acq","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Answer, context, question","mv":"Vinh's ACQ framework stops you losing the question halfway through. Answer in one clear sentence.","tr":"Use ACQ for the next question you're asked at work.","w":"Add a little context only if needed.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Mar 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-headline-first","n-vg-m-para"],"how":["Vinh's ACQ framework stops you losing the question halfway through. Answer in one clear sentence. Add a little context only if needed. Then ask a clarifying question such as \"Do you need more detail?\" For \"Are we on track for Friday?\": \"Yes, we're on track. We started a bit late, but it fits next week's testing. Need more detail?\"","People usually want a quick status check, not every detail as proof you're on top of things."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Answer, context, question”?","a":"Add a little context only if needed."},{"id":"n-vg-m-pro","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Take a position: position, reason, open","mv":"Vinh says staying neutral on everything (\"I don't mind, whatever the team wants\") feels safe, but people slowly stop asking for your view. His PRO framework: state your Position, give one Reason, then Open it up with a question.","tr":"The next time you're asked \"What do you think?\", give a position and one reason.","w":"Vinh says staying neutral on everything (\"I don't mind, whatever the team wants\") feels safe, but people slowly stop asking for your view.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Mar 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-anchor-invite"],"how":["Vinh says staying neutral on everything (\"I don't mind, whatever the team wants\") feels safe, but people slowly stop asking for your view. His PRO framework: state your Position, give one Reason, then Open it up with a question. \"My view is twice a week, because subscribers are up. What do you think?\"","He also warns against thinking out loud when people want a decision. Pause, settle on your answer, then deliver the final version, not the rough draft."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “I don't mind, whatever the …”","a":"“I don't mind, whatever the team wants”"},{"id":"n-vg-m-scar-not-wound","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Tell it from the scar, not the wound","mv":"Vinh separates a wound, pain still raw, from a scar, pain you've processed. Told from a wound, a story overwhelms you and leaves listeners feeling they must comfort you.","tr":"Before sharing a personal story, ask yourself: can I tell this calmly, and what's the lesson?","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jan 2026"],"rel":["g17"],"how":["Vinh separates a wound, pain still raw, from a scar, pain you've processed. Told from a wound, a story overwhelms you and leaves listeners feeling they must comfort you. He learned this when he broke down mid-workshop telling a story about his grandmother. His fix is Test, Stabilise, Lead. Try it on trusted friends first, practise until you can feel it without being swept away, and make the lesson the focus.","Emotion is fine. The point is that you guide it, so the audience leaves with the lesson and not just the heaviness."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Tell it from the scar, not the wound”?","a":"Vinh separates a wound, pain still raw, from a scar, pain you've processed. Told from a wound, a story overwhelms you and leaves listeners feeling they must comfort you."},{"id":"n-vg-m-three-pass-review","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Review a recording three ways","mv":"Vinh's record-and-review routine: film five minutes of unscripted speaking, then wait a day so you're less harsh. Review it three ways.","tr":"Record five minutes today and do the listen-only pass tomorrow.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Aug 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-pick-three","n-vg-practical-magic"],"how":["Vinh's record-and-review routine: film five minutes of unscripted speaking, then wait a day so you're less harsh. Review it three ways. Listen only, with the screen face down, for your voice. Watch only, on mute, for your face and body. Then read a transcript with the ums and ahs left in, and highlight every filler.","Separating sound, sight and words shows habits you'd miss watching normally. He still sends every fourth talk to a mentor for feedback."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Record and review","note":"Two versions of Vinh's self-review habit.","chain":[{"label":"Fix three things","when":"Podcasts · Nov 2026","text":"Vinh's ten-minute habit: record yourself talking for five minutes, watch it back for five, and pick three things to change. A generic top-ten list gives generic results; your own recording shows your list.","card":"n-vg-m-pick-three"},{"label":"Three passes","when":"Video · Aug 2026","text":"Vinh's record-and-review routine: film five minutes of unscripted speaking, then wait a day so you're less harsh. Review it three ways.","card":"n-vg-m-three-pass-review"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Review a recording three ways”?","a":"Vinh's record-and-review routine: film five minutes of unscripted speaking, then wait a day so you're less harsh. Review it three ways."},{"id":"n-vg-m-join-with-question","t":"vg","tp":"groups","ti":"Join a conversation with a question","mv":"Vinh says you don't need knowledge of the topic to join in. A question is a contribution: \"How did you find this place?\" opens a new angle and often leads somewhere personal.","tr":"At your next group chat, ask one question even if you know nothing about the topic.","w":"Vinh says you don't need knowledge of the topic to join in.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Sep 2026"],"how":["Vinh says you don't need knowledge of the topic to join in. A question is a contribution: \"How did you find this place?\" opens a new angle and often leads somewhere personal. If you're too polite to get in, give people a signal, a small \"Oh!\" and a lift of the hand, so they can see you want to speak.","He suggests a personal target: in every meeting or meal, ask at least one question and contribute at least one thing, so you're never invisible."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “How did you find …”","a":"“How did you find this place?”"},{"id":"n-vg-m-filler-drill","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"A three-step drill to cut \"um\"","mv":"Vinh's three steps. Awareness: record ten unscripted minutes, get a transcript, and highlight your ums and fillers.","tr":"Ask a friend to clap at every \"um\" for five minutes of chat.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Aug 2026"],"rel":["g14"],"how":["Vinh's three steps. Awareness: record ten unscripted minutes, get a transcript, and highlight your ums and fillers. Deliberate practice: speak for ten minutes while a friend claps every time you use one. His mentor used an air horn. Then bring it into daily conversation.","A fun extra step: deliberately overdo the fillers in a practice run. Acting them out makes you much more aware of them."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “A three-step drill to cut \"um\"”?","a":"Vinh's three steps. Awareness: record ten unscripted minutes, get a transcript, and highlight your ums and fillers."},{"id":"n-vg-m-engagement-tools","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Beyond slides: tools that keep an audience with you","mv":"Vinh says most presenters rely on content alone. His toolkit adds: questions (online, ask people to type A, B or C), a prop that shows the point, a short activity linked back to the message, a video, a story, or an analogy.","tr":"Add one question or one prop to your next presentation.","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Feb 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-storytelling"],"how":["Vinh says most presenters rely on content alone. His toolkit adds: questions (online, ask people to type A, B or C), a prop that shows the point, a short activity linked back to the message, a video, a story, or an analogy. In a 30-minute talk, pick one or two, not all of them.","Activities work best when energy dips, such as later in the day. Asking people to choose A, B or C also tells you what's landing."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Beyond slides: tools that keep an audience with you”?","a":"Vinh says most presenters rely on content alone. His toolkit adds: questions (online, ask people to type A, B or C), a prop that shows the point, a short activity linked back to the message, a video, a story, or an analogy."},{"id":"n-vg-m-match-weight","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Match your delivery to the weight of the question","mv":"Coaching a lively speaker, Vinh noticed she brought the same bubbly energy to everything.","tr":"Next time you're asked something serious, slow down and let your hands rest for the key line.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Feb 2026"],"rel":["g8","n-vg-m-vocal-archetypes"],"how":["Coaching a lively speaker, Vinh noticed she brought the same bubbly energy to everything. For a weighty question, he had her open playful, then shift to stillness: fewer gestures, slower pace, longer pauses as she reached the meaning. He also had her cut the warm-up line (\"Great question, I've been thinking about this\") and start with the answer.","Many people lock in one version of themselves that they think people like. Showing a stiller, deeper side when it fits gives your answer real weight."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Match your delivery to the weight of the question”?","a":"Next time you're asked something serious, slow down and let your hands rest for the key line."},{"id":"n-vg-m-explain-loop","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Explaining is its own skill: capture, structure, practise, repeat","mv":"Vinh says knowing a topic and explaining it are separate skills. Many experts have done thousands of hours of learning and almost no explaining.","tr":"Pick one thing you know well, write it out, and explain it aloud in two minutes.","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jul 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-ccc","n-vg-m-clean-thinking"],"how":["Vinh says knowing a topic and explaining it are separate skills. Many experts have done thousands of hours of learning and almost no explaining. His loop: capture everything you know on paper, structure it into a simple order, practise saying it aloud, then refine it and repeat.","Messy speaking usually comes from messy thinking. Putting your thoughts on paper first is what makes the spoken version clear."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Explaining is its own skill: capture, structure, practise, repeat”?","a":"Vinh says knowing a topic and explaining it are separate skills. Many experts have done thousands of hours of learning and almost no explaining."},{"id":"n-vg-m-three-peaks","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Tell the story from its peak","mv":"Asked why people drift halfway through a story, Vinh says it's usually the setup: we take too long to reach the moment that matters.","tr":"Take a story you tell often and start it at the peak moment instead of at the beginning.","w":"Like a film, drop the listener straight into the scene instead of explaining what happened the day before.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-explain-loop"],"how":["Asked why people drift halfway through a story, Vinh says it's usually the setup: we take too long to reach the moment that matters. His fix is to find one of three peaks (the emotional peak, the action peak, or the moment the lesson landed) and tell only the part around it. Like a film, drop the listener straight into the scene instead of explaining what happened the day before.","He still defends stories: three quick tips may be useful, but people remember what they felt, and stories are what make them feel."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Tell the story from its peak”?","a":"Like a film, drop the listener straight into the scene instead of explaining what happened the day before."},{"id":"n-vg-m-gesture-vocab","t":"vg","tp":"body","ti":"Widen your gesture vocabulary","mv":"Vinh says that after learning about open palms, he used that one gesture for everything. His advice is to grow a wider vocabulary of gestures so your hands can do different jobs.","tr":"Next time you explain something, try one new gesture on purpose, such as showing size, direction or a list on your fingers.","w":"He also realised his old monotone came from never moving his body: voice and body are one instrument, and when one is locked the other goes flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vg-m-be-engaged"],"how":["Vinh says that after learning about open palms, he used that one gesture for everything. His advice is to grow a wider vocabulary of gestures so your hands can do different jobs. He also realised his old monotone came from never moving his body: voice and body are one instrument, and when one is locked the other goes flat."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Widen your gesture vocabulary”?","a":"Next time you explain something, try one new gesture on purpose, such as showing size, direction or a list on your fingers."},{"id":"n-vg-m-apology-prefix","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Drop the apology before your idea","mv":"Vinh calls it the apology prefix: \"Sorry, just a quick thought\", \"This might sound stupid\", \"I'm probably wrong\". Its twin is the trailing qualifier tacked on the end: \"I think\", \"could be wrong\", \"but that's just my opinion\".","tr":"In your next meeting, catch one apology prefix before it comes out and start with \"Here's my thought\" instead.","w":"Both are a habit of protecting yourself from criticism, but they shrink the idea and signal that you don't trust it.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Dec 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-headline-first"],"how":["Vinh calls it the apology prefix: \"Sorry, just a quick thought\", \"This might sound stupid\", \"I'm probably wrong\". Its twin is the trailing qualifier tacked on the end: \"I think\", \"could be wrong\", \"but that's just my opinion\". Both are a habit of protecting yourself from criticism, but they shrink the idea and signal that you don't trust it. His swaps: \"Here's my thought.\" \"One idea we could explore is…\" \"Here's my recommendation.\" \"Before we continue, I want to clarify one point.\"","You can still be open to being wrong. Say the idea cleanly, then invite pushback: \"What am I missing?\""]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “Sorry, just a …”","a":"“Sorry, just a quick thought”"},{"id":"n-vg-m-speak-for-work","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Let your work speak, and speak for your work","mv":"Vinh's point is that quiet good work often goes unseen. You don't need to brag or make yourself the hero; you need to give context for the value you created without shrinking it.","tr":"At your next check-in, name one result and the specific part you played in it.","w":"You don't need to brag or make yourself the hero; you need to give context for the value you created without shrinking it.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Dec 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-apology-prefix"],"how":["Vinh's point is that quiet good work often goes unseen. You don't need to brag or make yourself the hero; you need to give context for the value you created without shrinking it. Something like: \"Here's the part of the results I was able to drive\" or \"I'd love to share the impact of the changes I made.\"","Sharing what you did is information your team needs, not showing off."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Let your work speak, and speak for your work”?","a":"You don't need to brag or make yourself the hero; you need to give context for the value you created without shrinking it."},{"id":"n-vg-m-two-beats","t":"vg","tp":"listening","ti":"Wait two extra beats","mv":"When someone finishes talking, Vinh suggests staying quiet for two extra beats before you reply. People usually fill that small gap themselves, and what they add is often the deeper, more personal reason behind what they just said.","tr":"In your next chat, count two silent beats after the other person stops, and see what they add.","w":"Keep a warm, interested face so the pause reads as attention rather than judgement.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Dec 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-offer-and-echo"],"how":["When someone finishes talking, Vinh suggests staying quiet for two extra beats before you reply. People usually fill that small gap themselves, and what they add is often the deeper, more personal reason behind what they just said.","It can feel awkward at first. Keep a warm, interested face so the pause reads as attention rather than judgement."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Wait two extra beats”?","a":"In your next chat, count two silent beats after the other person stops, and see what they add."},{"id":"n-vg-m-one-clean-sentence","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Shorter sentences carry more conviction","mv":"Vinh notes that long, looping sentences drain conviction: by the time you land, people have lost the thread.","tr":"Before your next message or update, cut your longest sentence in half.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Dec 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-apology-prefix"],"how":["Vinh notes that long, looping sentences drain conviction: by the time you land, people have lost the thread. His example turns a stumbling explanation about ducklings into one clean line: ducklings are bright yellow, but their feathers darken as they grow. Say the point in the shortest sentence that still makes sense."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Shorter sentences carry more conviction”?","a":"Vinh notes that long, looping sentences drain conviction: by the time you land, people have lost the thread."},{"id":"n-vg-m-open-loop","t":"vg","tp":"stories","ti":"Open a loop and close it later","mv":"Vinh's storytelling device: open a loop early with a question or a small mystery, and don't close it until later. Our brains hang on to unfinished things, so an open loop keeps people with you while you get through the middle.","tr":"Start your next story with the strange detail (\"I ended up apologising to a parrot\") and explain it at the end.","w":"Vinh's storytelling device: open a loop early with a question or a small mystery, and don't close it until later.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Oct 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-three-peaks"],"how":["Vinh's storytelling device: open a loop early with a question or a small mystery, and don't close it until later. Our brains hang on to unfinished things, so an open loop keeps people with you while you get through the middle.","Always close it. A loop left open feels like a trick rather than a story."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Open a loop and close it later”?","a":"Vinh's storytelling device: open a loop early with a question or a small mystery, and don't close it until later."},{"id":"n-vg-m-voice-notes","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Send a voice note instead of a text","mv":"Vinh sends voice notes where most people would type, from friends to people he contacts on LinkedIn, and says most reply warmly, often surprised the feature exists.","tr":"Today, reply to one message with a short voice note instead of typing.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Oct 2025"],"rel":["g14","n-vg-m-random-word"],"how":["Vinh sends voice notes where most people would type, from friends to people he contacts on LinkedIn, and says most reply warmly, often surprised the feature exists. It's daily speaking practice that costs nothing, and hearing a real voice builds rapport faster than text."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Send a voice note instead of a text”?","a":"Today, reply to one message with a short voice note instead of typing."},{"id":"n-vg-m-numbers-context","t":"vg","tp":"ideas","ti":"Give numbers a picture","mv":"The more technical the number, the faster people switch off, says Vinh.","tr":"Take one number from your next update and turn it into a comparison someone could picture.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Sep 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-one-clean-sentence"],"how":["The more technical the number, the faster people switch off, says Vinh. Swap decimals and big exact figures for simple comparisons: instead of a 7.3-point score change, \"for every ten customers, seven now love us, compared with five before\"; instead of a twelve-million reach figure, \"more people than live in Belgium\"."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Give numbers a picture”?","a":"The more technical the number, the faster people switch off, says Vinh."},{"id":"n-vg-m-look-sound-first","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"Work on looking and sounding confident, not feeling it","mv":"Vinh's look, feel and sound framework. Almost everyone says they want to feel more confident, yet feeling is the hardest of the three to control.","tr":"Before your next nervous moment, fix two things you can control: stand tall and slow your first sentence.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Sep 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-be-engaged","n-vg-m-apology-prefix"],"how":["Vinh's look, feel and sound framework. Almost everyone says they want to feel more confident, yet feeling is the hardest of the three to control. So start with the two you can control: how you look (posture, eye contact, gestures) and how you sound (volume, pace, pitch). He says the feeling tends to follow the behaviour."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Work on looking and sounding confident, not feeling it”?","a":"Vinh's look, feel and sound framework. Almost everyone says they want to feel more confident, yet feeling is the hardest of the three to control."},{"id":"n-vg-m-random-word","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"The random word drill","mv":"Vinh's favourite drill for linking mind and mouth: open a random word generator, take whatever word comes up, and speak about it for a minute straight away.","tr":"Do one random word today and talk about it for 60 seconds out loud.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Aug 2025"],"rel":["g14","n-vg-m-voice-notes"],"how":["Vinh's favourite drill for linking mind and mouth: open a random word generator, take whatever word comes up, and speak about it for a minute straight away. Pair it with a simple structure, such as one thing, two types or three steps, so you practise organising your thoughts as you speak."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “The random word drill”?","a":"Do one random word today and talk about it for 60 seconds out loud."},{"id":"n-vg-m-try-on-styles","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Try on other people's styles like outfits","mv":"Vinh's fast track: choose three to five communicators you admire (a speaker, a colleague, someone online), study how they move, sound and handle hard questions, then try their habits on like outfits.","tr":"Pick one communicator you admire and borrow just one habit of theirs in a conversation this week.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Nov 2025"],"rel":["g14","n-vg-m-random-word"],"how":["Vinh's fast track: choose three to five communicators you admire (a speaker, a colleague, someone online), study how they move, sound and handle hard questions, then try their habits on like outfits. It feels odd at first, but over time you keep what fits and your own style forms from the mix."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Try on other people's styles like outfits”?","a":"Vinh's fast track: choose three to five communicators you admire (a speaker, a colleague, someone online), study how they move, sound and handle hard questions, then try their habits on like outfits."},{"id":"n-vve-m-maybe-a-point","t":"vve","tp":"stories","ti":"Maybe it shouldn't be a story","mv":"Vanessa's question for a story that keeps losing people: should it be a story at all? Sometimes the listener just wants the lesson, and saying it plainly works better than a long setup.","tr":"Before your next story, ask yourself whether a one-line point would do. If not, start with a hook that tells them why to listen.","w":"Spot it when eyes start drifting while you're talking.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vve-m-slides-failed"],"how":["Vanessa's question for a story that keeps losing people: should it be a story at all? Sometimes the listener just wants the lesson, and saying it plainly works better than a long setup. When it does need to be a story, open an information gap first, such as telling them it's the weirdest thing that has ever happened to you, so they want to know where it's going."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Maybe it shouldn't be a story”?","a":"Vanessa's question for a story that keeps losing people: should it be a story at all? Sometimes the listener just wants the lesson, and saying it plainly works better than a long setup."},{"id":"n-vve-m-slides-failed","t":"vve","tp":"stories","ti":"The day the slides failed","mv":"Vanessa tells of a big talk where her slides wouldn't work, so she had to give an hour on data-heavy research from memory. With nothing to show, she told every lab study as a story, plus stories from her own life and her students.","tr":"Take one fact you want to share this week and tell it as a short story about one person.","w":"It became her highest-rated talk, and she realised she had been giving audiences a long list of tips instead of something to feel.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vve-m-maybe-a-point","n-vve-m-warm-competent-switch"],"how":["Vanessa tells of a big talk where her slides wouldn't work, so she had to give an hour on data-heavy research from memory. With nothing to show, she told every lab study as a story, plus stories from her own life and her students. It became her highest-rated talk, and she realised she had been giving audiences a long list of tips instead of something to feel."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “The day the slides failed”?","a":"Take one fact you want to share this week and tell it as a short story about one person."},{"id":"n-vve-m-warm-competent-switch","t":"vve","tp":"ideas","ti":"Switch between warm and competent every minute","mv":"Vanessa asks the advanced speakers she coaches to switch roughly every minute between warmth and competence: a case study about one student, then the research, then something funny, then the data, then a takeaway.","tr":"Plan your next talk or update in one-minute blocks, and alternate a human moment with a fact.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube, joint video), Oct 2026"],"rel":["n-vve-m-slides-failed"],"how":["Vanessa asks the advanced speakers she coaches to switch roughly every minute between warmth and competence: a case study about one student, then the research, then something funny, then the data, then a takeaway. She also varies how she presents a single study: the numbers, then telling it as if she were a participant, then having the audience try it, then a video or cartoon of it.","Staying in one mode for too long, whether all data or all jokes, is what loses people."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Switch between warm and competent every minute”?","a":"Vanessa asks the advanced speakers she coaches to switch roughly every minute between warmth and competence: a case study about one student, then the research, then something funny, then the data, then a takeaway."},{"id":"n-vve-m-ingroup-reading","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"You read your own group best","mv":"Vanessa points to a large review of 97 studies: people everywhere recognise basic emotions better than chance, but they are more accurate with people from their own national, ethnic or regional group.","tr":"With a colleague from a different background, ask one clarifying question before acting on how you think they feel.","w":"The fix isn't to stop reading people.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Sep 2024"],"how":["Vanessa points to a large review of 97 studies: people everywhere recognise basic emotions better than chance, but they are more accurate with people from their own national, ethnic or regional group. That gives you a home advantage when reading cues, and a real blind spot with people whose background differs from yours.","The fix isn't to stop reading people. Take more time to learn someone's baseline, and check with a question before trusting your read."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “You read your own group best”?","a":"The fix isn't to stop reading people."},{"id":"n-vve-m-mentor-offer","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Find a mentor for knowledge, then offer first","mv":"Vanessa names two mistakes. The first is choosing a mentor because they inspire you; instead, look for someone whose specific knowledge can help with your next step.","tr":"Think of one person whose know-how you need, and work out one useful thing you could offer them before you ask.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Jun 2024"],"rel":["n-vve-m-expert-power"],"how":["Vanessa names two mistakes. The first is choosing a mentor because they inspire you; instead, look for someone whose specific knowledge can help with your next step. The second is approaching them only to take. Mentors are busy, so offer something first: every mentee she has taken on began by being generous to her before asking for anything."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Find a mentor for knowledge, then offer first”?","a":"Think of one person whose know-how you need, and work out one useful thing you could offer them before you ask."},{"id":"n-vve-m-expert-power","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Become the go-to person for one useful skill","mv":"Vanessa built her early career by becoming the person who ran a new tool nobody else in her office understood. Her advice: pick a skill tied to your job and become a real power user of it.","tr":"Ask your manager this week: \"Which skill would be most useful for me to level up?\"","w":"Vanessa built her early career by becoming the person who ran a new tool nobody else in her office understood.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), May 2024"],"rel":["n-vve-m-mentor-offer"],"how":["Vanessa built her early career by becoming the person who ran a new tool nobody else in her office understood. Her advice: pick a skill tied to your job and become a real power user of it. You can even ask your manager which skill would help the team most. Being the go-to person also builds relationships, like the colleague who loved finding cheap flights and got invited to plenty of lunches for it."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Become the go-to person for one useful skill”?","a":"Vanessa built her early career by becoming the person who ran a new tool nobody else in her office understood. Her advice: pick a skill tied to your job and become a real power user of it."},{"id":"n-vve-m-team-questions","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Two questions for your next team meeting","mv":"Two of Vanessa's team-meeting questions. For fun: \"What's something you don't like that everyone else seems to like?\", which sparks a light debate about real tastes.","tr":"At your next one-to-one, ask how they like to be supported when things get stressful, and write it down.","w":"For fun: \"What's something you don't like that everyone else seems to like?\", which sparks a light debate about real tastes.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Apr 2024"],"rel":["n-vve-m-ask-preferences"],"how":["Two of Vanessa's team-meeting questions. For fun: \"What's something you don't like that everyone else seems to like?\", which sparks a light debate about real tastes. For support: \"What can I do to help when you're stressed?\" Some people want space to process alone, while others want company, and you can only know which by asking."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “What's something you don't like that everyone else …”","a":"“What's something you don't like that everyone else seems to like?”"},{"id":"n-vve-m-abcd-delegate","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"Delegate with A, B, C and D work","mv":"Vanessa's ABCD sort. List your tasks in four columns: A is work you're excellent at and love, B is work you're good at, C is work you're slow at, D is work you dread.","tr":"Write your own ABCD list this week and pick one D task to pass to someone who'd enjoy it.","w":"Never give away your A work.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Mar 2024"],"rel":["n-vve-m-team-questions","n-vve-m-expert-power"],"how":["Vanessa's ABCD sort. List your tasks in four columns: A is work you're excellent at and love, B is work you're good at, C is work you're slow at, D is work you dread. Never give away your A work. Hand off your D and C work first, ideally to someone for whom it is A or B work, which you can find out by asking them to do the same exercise. When you hand it over, explain why: \"You're great at this and I'm not, so it'll be done faster and better with you.\"","Framed that way, delegation lands as a compliment rather than a dumped chore."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Delegate with A, B, C and D work”?","a":"Write your own ABCD list this week and pick one D task to pass to someone who'd enjoy it."},{"id":"n-vve-m-happy-not-impressive","t":"vve","tp":"warmth","ti":"Think \"what makes me happy\", not \"how do I impress\"","mv":"Asked how to start flirting, Vanessa takes the pressure off: you don't need lines or to be funny. In research she cites, people caught a genuine smile and their mood lifted, while a fake smile passed on nothing.","tr":"Before your next social event, spend a minute thinking of something that genuinely makes you happy, and let it show.","w":"Asked how to start flirting, Vanessa takes the pressure off: you don't need lines or to be funny.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Mar 2024"],"rel":["n-vve-m-ingroup-reading"],"how":["Asked how to start flirting, Vanessa takes the pressure off: you don't need lines or to be funny. In research she cites, people caught a genuine smile and their mood lifted, while a fake smile passed on nothing. So instead of thinking \"I've got to be impressive\", walk into the call, shop or bar thinking about what makes you happy. A real smile is what spreads."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “what makes …”","a":"“what makes me happy”"},{"id":"n-vg-m-tongue-twisters","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Tongue twisters, slow first","mv":"Most people can only speak fast by mumbling, says Vinh.","tr":"Pick one tongue twister and say it slowly five times, then a little faster each time.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jul 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-overarticulate"],"how":["Most people can only speak fast by mumbling, says Vinh. His drill is three to five minutes of tongue twisters, but read slowly first, the way you'd learn a new lift with light weights, so your lips, tongue and jaw learn the exact shapes. Only then build up speed. The aim isn't speaking fast all the time; it's having the full range of speed available without losing clarity."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Tongue twisters, slow first”?","a":"Most people can only speak fast by mumbling, says Vinh."},{"id":"n-vg-m-playful-questions","t":"vg","tp":"openers","ti":"Lighter, more curious questions","mv":"Vinh's fix for flat chats is a lighter, more curious question.","tr":"Write down three open questions for the situation you're in most often, and use one tomorrow.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jun 2025"],"rel":["g23","n-vg-m-two-beats"],"how":["Vinh's fix for flat chats is a lighter, more curious question. Instead of \"How are you?\", try \"What's been the most fun thing that's happened today?\" Instead of \"Did you enjoy the concert?\" (yes, no, done), try \"What did you love most about the concert?\" He also says to prepare once for the situations you meet every week, such as new people at work, so you have a few good open questions ready."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Lighter, more curious questions”?","a":"Write down three open questions for the situation you're in most often, and use one tomorrow."},{"id":"n-vg-m-feedback-sandwich","t":"vg","tp":"tough","ti":"The feedback sandwich still works, if it's real","mv":"Vinh admits the feedback sandwich has become a cliché, which is exactly why people stopped using it. His reasoning: the moment you pull someone aside, they're on alert.","tr":"Before your next piece of feedback, note one specific thing they truly did well and start with it.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jun 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-interrupted-kindly"],"how":["Vinh admits the feedback sandwich has become a cliché, which is exactly why people stopped using it. His reasoning: the moment you pull someone aside, they're on alert. Opening with something they genuinely did well lowers that guard so the correction can land. Then finish by showing confidence in them.","The praise must be true and specific. A made-up compliment makes the whole thing feel like a trick."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The feedback sandwich still works, if it's real”?","a":"Vinh admits the feedback sandwich has become a cliché, which is exactly why people stopped using it. His reasoning: the moment you pull someone aside, they're on alert."},{"id":"n-vg-m-record-wait","t":"vg","tp":"practice","ti":"Record five minutes, then wait a day","mv":"Vinh's starting point for a 30-day plan: record a five-minute, unplanned video of yourself talking, and don't redo it to make it perfect, because the flaws are the point. Then don't watch it straight away.","tr":"Record five minutes on any topic today and put a reminder in to watch it tomorrow.","w":"Vinh's starting point for a 30-day plan: record a five-minute, unplanned video of yourself talking, and don't redo it to make it perfect, because the flaws are the point.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), May 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-review-right-after"],"how":["Vinh's starting point for a 30-day plan: record a five-minute, unplanned video of yourself talking, and don't redo it to make it perfect, because the flaws are the point. Then don't watch it straight away. Leave it at least a day, because we are far too harsh on ourselves in the moment. When you do watch, list what you'd change in how you sound and how you look.","This differs from his quick review after a conversation. That review is about what you said; the recording is about how you come across, and that needs some distance."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Record five minutes, then wait a day”?","a":"Vinh's starting point for a 30-day plan: record a five-minute, unplanned video of yourself talking, and don't redo it to make it perfect, because the flaws are the point."},{"id":"n-vg-m-name-association","t":"vg","tp":"groups","ti":"Make names stick with a wild picture","mv":"Vinh links a new name to something that stands out about the person, and the wilder the picture, the better it sticks: \"Vinh is wearing vintage glasses.\" If your own name is unusual, be kind to strangers.","tr":"At your next meeting, make one silly picture for one new name, and use the name once before you leave.","w":"Spot it when someone is hovering on the edge of the circle.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), May 2025"],"how":["Vinh links a new name to something that stands out about the person, and the wilder the picture, the better it sticks: \"Vinh is wearing vintage glasses.\" If your own name is unusual, be kind to strangers. Say it more than once, and give them a hook or a sound-alike to hold on to."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “Vinh is wearing …”","a":"“Vinh is wearing vintage glasses.”"},{"id":"n-vg-m-voice-tune-up","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"A daily tune-up: siren, lip trills, yawn","mv":"Vinh treats vocal exercises like tuning a guitar before you play it. His routine is three minutes each.","tr":"Before your next important call, do one minute each of sirens, lip trills and a slow yawn.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Apr 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-breath-support"],"how":["Vinh treats vocal exercises like tuning a guitar before you play it. His routine is three minutes each. Sirens: glide your voice from low to high and back, with your mouth closed and then open, to widen your range. Lip trills: blow air through relaxed lips, playing with pitch and volume, to bring your voice forward. Yawning: open the soft palate for a fuller, more resonant sound."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “A daily tune-up: siren, lip trills, yawn”?","a":"Vinh treats vocal exercises like tuning a guitar before you play it. His routine is three minutes each."},{"id":"n-vve-m-assume-a-story","t":"vve","tp":"deeper","ti":"Assume they have a fascinating story","mv":"Vanessa says most conversations run on social scripts: how are you, what do you do, where are you from. Her mental trick for getting past them is to assume the person has a fascinating story and that her job is to find it.","tr":"In your next conversation, silently assume there's a great story somewhere in this person, and ask one more question to find it.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Dec 2023"],"rel":["n-vve-m-friend-questions"],"how":["Vanessa says most conversations run on social scripts: how are you, what do you do, where are you from. Her mental trick for getting past them is to assume the person has a fascinating story and that her job is to find it. That makes her far more curious, and that curiosity is where empathy starts."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Assume they have a fascinating story”?","a":"Vanessa says most conversations run on social scripts: how are you, what do you do, where are you from. Her mental trick for getting past them is to assume the person has a fascinating story and that her job is to find it."},{"id":"n-vve-m-micromanager","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Working with a micromanager","mv":"Vanessa says micromanagers are usually anxious themselves: secretly afraid the work will go wrong.","tr":"Write down how often and how you'd like to be checked in on, and bring it to your next one-to-one.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Dec 2023"],"rel":["n-vve-m-abcd-delegate","n-vve-m-team-questions"],"how":["Vanessa says micromanagers are usually anxious themselves: secretly afraid the work will go wrong. First, work out what level of management helps you thrive: how often you like check-ins, by which channel, and what helps you work best. Then ask for a meeting to agree it together. Build trust with some human check-ins too (\"What was the highlight of your weekend?\"). When they ease off, thank them for trusting you so the better pattern sticks."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Working with a micromanager”?","a":"Write down how often and how you'd like to be checked in on, and bring it to your next one-to-one."},{"id":"n-vve-m-intro-formula","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"A simple formula for introducing yourself","mv":"Vanessa's introduction in order: a quick greeting, your name said slowly (most of us rush or swallow it), a positive word such as \"excited\" or \"thrilled\", then your reason for being there or your role that day.","tr":"Practise saying your own name slowly and clearly three times before your next event.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Nov 2023"],"rel":["n-vve-m-fun-fact-prompts"],"how":["Vanessa's introduction in order: a quick greeting, your name said slowly (most of us rush or swallow it), a positive word such as \"excited\" or \"thrilled\", then your reason for being there or your role that day. If you're brave, add a fun fact. Use first name only in casual settings and full name in formal ones."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “A simple formula for introducing yourself”?","a":"Vanessa's introduction in order: a quick greeting, your name said slowly (most of us rush or swallow it), a positive word such as \"excited\" or \"thrilled\", then your reason for being there or your role that day."},{"id":"n-vve-m-one-to-one","t":"vve","tp":"groups","ti":"A shape for one-to-ones: wins, challenges, goals, help","mv":"Vanessa gives her team check-ins a little structure. Start on a high by asking for a win.","tr":"Use the four questions (win, challenge, goal, help) in your next one-to-one, whichever side of the table you're on.","w":"Then ask what they are struggling with and what you can help with, which lets issues come up naturally and makes them a shared problem rather than a complaint.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Oct 2023"],"rel":["n-vve-m-team-questions","n-vve-m-abcd-delegate"],"how":["Vanessa gives her team check-ins a little structure. Start on a high by asking for a win. Then ask what they are struggling with and what you can help with, which lets issues come up naturally and makes them a shared problem rather than a complaint. Finish on growth: ask about their goals this month and how you can support them."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “A shape for one-to-ones: wins, challenges, goals, help”?","a":"Then ask what they are struggling with and what you can help with, which lets issues come up naturally and makes them a shared problem rather than a complaint."},{"id":"n-vve-m-real-weakness","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Answering \"What's your greatest weakness?\"","mv":"Vanessa's rule: never give a fake positive like \"I'm too detail-oriented\". Give a real weakness as a short story that's a little funny, endearing or vulnerable, then say what you learned and how you work on it now.","tr":"Draft your honest weakness as a 30-second story with a lesson, before your next interview.","w":"Vanessa's rule: never give a fake positive like \"I'm too detail-oriented\".","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Oct 2023"],"rel":["n-vve-m-intro-formula"],"how":["Vanessa's rule: never give a fake positive like \"I'm too detail-oriented\". Give a real weakness as a short story that's a little funny, endearing or vulnerable, then say what you learned and how you work on it now. Her own answer is being optimistic to the point of being unrealistic, told through a funny story from school."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “What's your …”","a":"“What's your greatest weakness?”"},{"id":"n-vve-m-summarise-next","t":"vve","tp":"listening","ti":"Sum up the feeling, then ask what's next","mv":"Vanessa's way of sounding smart in a meeting is to be the person who sums up and moves things on.","tr":"In your next stuck discussion, sum up what people feel and why, then ask what the next step should be.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Jul 2023"],"how":["Vanessa's way of sounding smart in a meeting is to be the person who sums up and moves things on. Name what people seem to feel and why, then ask: \"What can we do next?\" It shows people they were heard and gives the group a next step. She pairs it with short, plain sentences: clarity, not big words, is what makes people sound smart."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Sum up the feeling, then ask what's next”?","a":"Vanessa's way of sounding smart in a meeting is to be the person who sums up and moves things on."},{"id":"n-vve-m-fun-fact-prompts","t":"vve","tp":"openers","ti":"Have a fun fact ready","mv":"Vanessa keeps a couple of fun facts ready, using prompts like \"When I was young I wanted to be…\" or \"The first concert I ever went to was…\". A good fun fact opens a story and gives the other person something to ask about.","tr":"Fill in two prompts for yourself tonight so you have an answer ready next time someone asks.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Aug 2023"],"rel":["n-vve-m-intro-formula","n-vve-m-assume-a-story"],"how":["Vanessa keeps a couple of fun facts ready, using prompts like \"When I was young I wanted to be…\" or \"The first concert I ever went to was…\". A good fun fact opens a story and gives the other person something to ask about. Hers: as a child she wanted to be a teacher, which led to how she ended up teaching on YouTube."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “When I was young I wanted …”","a":"“When I was young I wanted to be…”"},{"id":"n-vg-m-grammar-check","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Ask a speech pathologist for your top ten errors","mv":"English is Vinh's third language.","tr":"Record yourself answering three questions out loud, and read the transcript for one pattern you'd like to fix.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Mar 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-overarticulate","n-vg-m-record-wait"],"how":["English is Vinh's third language. One of his best investments was recording several minutes of himself talking freely, getting it transcribed, and paying a local speech pathologist (about 150 dollars at the time) to list the ten grammar errors he made most often. Tenses and plurals are common ones. Small, repeated slips can quietly cost credibility, and a short list tells you exactly what to practise."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Ask a speech pathologist for your top ten errors”?","a":"Record yourself answering three questions out loud, and read the transcript for one pattern you'd like to fix."},{"id":"n-vg-m-arena-feedback","t":"vg","tp":"presence","ti":"You can't please everyone, so choose whose feedback counts","mv":"Vinh's reminder: if a hundred people watch and six dislike it, trying to win those six over usually makes you blander for the ninety-four who liked you. Let some people not like you.","tr":"Name the two or three people whose feedback on your work you most trust, and ask one of them for it this week.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Feb 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-welcome-disagreement"],"how":["Vinh's reminder: if a hundred people watch and six dislike it, trying to win those six over usually makes you blander for the ninety-four who liked you. Let some people not like you. When it comes to feedback, he gives most weight to people who are actually in the arena doing the same kind of work, not to those commenting from the stands.","That isn't a licence to ignore all criticism. Seek out sharp feedback from people who do the work, and welcome it."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “You can't please everyone, so choose whose feedback counts”?","a":"Vinh's reminder: if a hundred people watch and six dislike it, trying to win those six over usually makes you blander for the ninety-four who liked you. Let some people not like you."},{"id":"n-vg-m-welcome-disagreement","t":"vg","tp":"tough","ti":"Thank people for disagreeing with you","mv":"Vinh says friends and colleagues who disagree with him are some of the most valuable people around him; without them he would be living in an echo chamber.","tr":"Next time someone pushes back on your idea, start your reply with \"Thanks for disagreeing. Tell me more.\"","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jan 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-arena-feedback","n-vg-m-feedback-sandwich"],"how":["Vinh says friends and colleagues who disagree with him are some of the most valuable people around him; without them he would be living in an echo chamber. When his new head of marketing pushed back on a rigid plan of his, the first thing Vinh did was thank him. He says the same about his wife: when she disagrees, she may be saving him from a bad decision."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Thank people for disagreeing with you”?","a":"Vinh says friends and colleagues who disagree with him are some of the most valuable people around him; without them he would be living in an echo chamber."},{"id":"n-vg-m-breath-then-speak","t":"vg","tp":"voice","ti":"Breathe in, let a little out, then speak","mv":"Vinh notes that some people stumble when they start talking at the very top of a big breath, when the pressure is highest. His tip is to take a deep breath, let a little air out for a second or two, and then begin.","tr":"Before your next sentence that matters, breathe in, let a little out, then start.","w":"Spot it when you hear yourself rush, trail off, or go flat.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Jan 2025"],"rel":["n-vg-m-breath-support"],"how":["Vinh notes that some people stumble when they start talking at the very top of a big breath, when the pressure is highest. His tip is to take a deep breath, let a little air out for a second or two, and then begin. He pairs it with slowing down and allowing pauses, which only seem weird if you treat them as weird.","Stuttering has many causes. If it is persistent, a speech therapist is the right person to see. This is a simple tip some people find helps."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Breathe in, let a little out, then speak”?","a":"Before your next sentence that matters, breathe in, let a little out, then start."},{"id":"n-vg-m-calm-isnt-universal","t":"vg","tp":"tough","ti":"Staying calm isn't always what they need","mv":"Vinh's lesson from early marriage: when his wife was upset and he answered in a very calm, reasoned voice, it made her more frustrated. Some people want you to meet their energy first, while others want the opposite.","tr":"Think of one person close to you: when they're upset, do they want you to match them or steady them? Ask them on a calm day.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vinh Giang (YouTube), Dec 2024"],"rel":["g35"],"how":["Vinh's lesson from early marriage: when his wife was upset and he answered in a very calm, reasoned voice, it made her more frustrated. Some people want you to meet their energy first, while others want the opposite. With people you're close to, you learn which by trial and error. With strangers in a heated moment, he defaults to the safer route of not escalating."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Staying calm isn't always what they need”?","a":"Vinh's lesson from early marriage: when his wife was upset and he answered in a very calm, reasoned voice, it made her more frustrated. Some people want you to meet their energy first, while others want the opposite."},{"id":"n-vve-m-nervous-laugh-rule","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Swap the nervous laugh for an if-then rule","mv":"Vanessa's approach to nervous laughter is to replace it rather than just trying to stop. Pick a positive nonverbal to do instead, such as a nod, a head tilt or a deep breath.","tr":"Write one if-then rule for your most common nervous habit and use it this week.","w":"Vanessa's approach to nervous laughter is to replace it rather than just trying to stop.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Apr 2023"],"rel":["n-vve-m-pause-is-normal"],"how":["Vanessa's approach to nervous laughter is to replace it rather than just trying to stop. Pick a positive nonverbal to do instead, such as a nod, a head tilt or a deep breath. Then make a small rule for the situation where it happens most: \"If I feel a nervous laugh coming with my boss, I'll ask a question instead.\" The rule gives your brain something to do, so it doesn't fall back on its default."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Swap the nervous laugh for an if-then rule”?","a":"Vanessa's approach to nervous laughter is to replace it rather than just trying to stop."},{"id":"n-vve-m-close-talker","t":"vve","tp":"body","ti":"Handling a close talker kindly","mv":"People stand close for many reasons, so Vanessa keeps it kind.","tr":"At your next event, keep a drink in hand. It gives you an easy, friendly buffer.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Mar 2023"],"rel":["n-vve-m-space-touch"],"how":["People stand close for many reasons, so Vanessa keeps it kind. Her gentle ways to win back space: step back a little; hold a drink in front of you, which naturally sets a distance; or use slightly bigger hand gestures, which widen your personal bubble so the other person tends to ease back on their own."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Handling a close talker kindly”?","a":"At your next event, keep a drink in hand. It gives you an easy, friendly buffer."},{"id":"n-vve-m-awkward-truth","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Telling someone about bad breath or body odour","mv":"Vanessa's script starts from compassion, because you don't know why it's happening.","tr":"If you ever need to have this talk, do it privately and early, and lead with why you care.","w":"Vanessa's script starts from compassion, because you don't know why it's happening.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Feb 2023"],"rel":["v40"],"how":["Vanessa's script starts from compassion, because you don't know why it's happening. Ask permission privately (\"Can I share something a bit uncomfortable?\"), then name it plainly with \"I've noticed…\", then give your reason: you work closely together and you want them to do well. End with \"I know this is awkward, but I wanted to tell you privately.\" If they seem open, you can gently suggest a dentist or doctor."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Telling someone about bad breath or body odour”?","a":"Vanessa's script starts from compassion, because you don't know why it's happening."},{"id":"n-vve-m-dont-click","t":"vve","tp":"tough","ti":"Talking to someone you don't click with","mv":"Vanessa's first move with someone you don't click with is to ask for their advice or opinion, since people tend to warm to those who ask, and know-it-alls especially enjoy it.","tr":"Ask the person you find hardest one advice question this week, and really listen.","w":"Vanessa's first move with someone you don't click with is to ask for their advice or opinion, since people tend to warm to those who ask, and know-it-alls especially enjoy it.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Oct 2022"],"rel":["n-vve-m-assume-a-story"],"how":["Vanessa's first move with someone you don't click with is to ask for their advice or opinion, since people tend to warm to those who ask, and know-it-alls especially enjoy it. Keep a few questions ready, such as asking what they've read or watched lately that they'd recommend. For someone who gives one-word answers, ask what they could happily talk about for hours, remember it, and bring it up next time.","Her quiet hope is that the right question uncovers something you actually like about them."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Talking to someone you don't click with”?","a":"Vanessa's first move with someone you don't click with is to ask for their advice or opinion, since people tend to warm to those who ask, and know-it-alls especially enjoy it."},{"id":"n-vve-m-trait-stories","t":"vve","tp":"presence","ti":"Talk about yourself through stories, not boasts","mv":"Vanessa's method: list the traits you're proud of and want people to know, such as organised or detail-oriented.","tr":"Pick your top trait and write a three-sentence story that shows it.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"","d":{"src":["Vanessa Van Edwards (YouTube), Sep 2022"],"rel":["n-vve-m-real-weakness"],"how":["Vanessa's method: list the traits you're proud of and want people to know, such as organised or detail-oriented. Then show each one through a short story that uses the word, like a project where being detail-oriented made it succeed. Another way is to share what someone else said about you, for example a boss's comment, which sounds less like boasting and can be checked."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Talk about yourself through stories, not boasts”?","a":"Pick your top trait and write a three-sentence story that shows it."},{"id":"need-sig","t":"ch","tp":"needs","ti":"👑 Significance","mv":"Wants to feel they matter and make an impact. Approach by noticing their impact out loud, and asking their view first.","tr":"I'd value your take on this before anyone else's.","w":"Avoid: Talking over them or one-upping their story. It hits their quiet fear — being ignored, made to feel small, ridiculed or left behind.","ev":"Coach's view","need":"significance","d":{"q":"Do people see me as important and making an impact?","signs":["Talks about achievements, titles, who they know","Status brands, cars, watches, latest tech","Wants to lead or decide first","Steers talk back to themselves"],"lines":[{"say":"I'd value your take on this before anyone else's.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"What you built there is genuinely rare.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"It's obvious people here look to you when it counts.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"You don't strike me as someone who just follows the crowd.","how":"","why":""}],"traps":["Talking over them or one-upping their story","Treating them as 'just another' person in the room","Correcting them in front of others"],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 367","Read the Room six needs guide"],"rel":["h15","need-app","need-acc"],"go":[{"tab":"profile","label":"Open the six needs guide"},{"tab":"say","need":"significance","label":"Lines for this need in Say"}],"how":["The quiet question behind it: \"Do people see me as important and making an impact?\"","Call out a specific impact they actually had, and ask for their view before you give yours. The aim is to make them feel interesting — not to impress them.","What hurts most: Being ignored, made to feel small, ridiculed or left behind."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"How this need has been worded","note":"Same need, three wordings. The latest keeps the listening-prompt framing and drops anything that could steer.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Read Anyone v1 · 10 Nov 2026","text":"Significance: Wants to matter. Listen for: Talks about what they built, their impact, or standing out. Do: Name their specific impact. Then ask what they are proudest of."},{"label":"Updated","when":"Read & Connect field guide","text":"Significance — the quiet question: \"Do people see me as important and making an impact?\" Show them their impact matters. Notice their influence out loud."},{"label":"Latest","when":"Read the Room · 18 Nov 2026","text":"Wants to feel they matter and make an impact. Approach by noticing their impact out loud, and asking their view first."}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"What quiet question is someone with this need asking?","a":"“Do people see me as important and making an impact?”"},{"id":"need-app","t":"ch","tp":"needs","ti":"🤝 Approval","mv":"Wants to know they’re doing OK. Approach with reassurance, praise the effort, and make yes and no equally safe.","tr":"You've clearly put real care into this.","w":"Avoid: Sarcasm or teasing.","ev":"Coach's view","need":"approval","d":{"q":"Will people recognise me so I can move forward with confidence?","signs":["Self put-downs ('I'm rubbish at this')","Asks permission when they don't need to","Shifts their view if you disagree","Lots of compliments; rarely complains"],"lines":[{"say":"You've clearly put real care into this.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"There's no wrong answer here — I'd just love your honest view.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Honestly, you're better at this than you give yourself credit for.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"That's a completely reasonable thing to want.","how":"","why":""}],"traps":["Sarcasm or teasing","Silence after they put themselves down","Pushing them to decide on the spot"],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 368","Read the Room six needs guide"],"rel":["h15","need-sig","need-acc"],"go":[{"tab":"profile","label":"Open the six needs guide"},{"tab":"say","need":"approval","label":"Lines for this need in Say"}],"how":["The quiet question behind it: \"Will people recognise me so I can move forward with confidence?\"","Reassure them, and notice the care they put in, not only the result. Make it safe to say yes, and equally safe to say no. Don't leave a self-put-down hanging in silence."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"How this need has been worded","note":"Same need, three wordings. The latest keeps the listening-prompt framing and drops anything that could steer.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Read Anyone v1 · 10 Nov 2026","text":"Approval: Wants to get it right. Listen for: Checks in, seeks reassurance, plays themselves down before they start. Do: Give clear, specific validation. Say what is good and why."},{"label":"Updated","when":"Read & Connect field guide","text":"Approval — the quiet question: \"Will people recognise me so I can move forward with confidence?\" Reassurance and permission. Recognise effort, not just results."},{"label":"Latest","when":"Read the Room · 18 Nov 2026","text":"Wants to know they’re doing OK. Approach with reassurance, praise the effort, and make yes and no equally safe."}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"What quiet question is someone with this need asking?","a":"“Will people recognise me so I can move forward with confidence?”"},{"id":"need-acc","t":"ch","tp":"needs","ti":"🫂 Acceptance","mv":"Wants to belong. Approach by including them and bringing them into the group.","tr":"Want me to introduce you to a few people here?","w":"Avoid: Singling them out in front of others. It hits their quiet fear — gossip, social criticism, or not fitting in with their people.","ev":"Coach's view","need":"acceptance","d":{"q":"Do I belong — and can others see that I do?","signs":["Clubs, teams, groups, 'we' and 'us'","Helping or caring roles, volunteering, pets","Team shirts, memberships, community talk","Returns every smile and eyebrow flash"],"lines":[{"say":"Want me to introduce you to a few people here?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"It's people like you that make a group actually work.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"You'd fit right in with the people I work with.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Everyone I've introduced you to has said the same thing — you're easy to be around.","how":"","why":""}],"traps":["Singling them out in front of others","Anything that could embarrass them with their group","'Nobody else does it this way'"],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 369","Read the Room six needs guide"],"rel":["h15","need-sig","need-app"],"go":[{"tab":"profile","label":"Open the six needs guide"},{"tab":"say","need":"acceptance","label":"Lines for this need in Say"}],"how":["The quiet question behind it: \"Do I belong — and can others see that I do?\"","Include them in the 'we'. If you can do it honestly, introduce them to your people so they feel they belong. Hughes' habit here is to meet people and make introductions.","What hurts most: Gossip, social criticism, or not fitting in with their people."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"How this need has been worded","note":"Same need, three wordings. The latest keeps the listening-prompt framing and drops anything that could steer.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Read Anyone v1 · 10 Nov 2026","text":"Acceptance: Wants to belong. Listen for: We, us, our. Teams, groups, who is in and who is out. Do: Use shared language. Make room for them. Talk about the 'we'."},{"label":"Updated","when":"Read & Connect field guide","text":"Acceptance — the quiet question: \"Do I belong — and can others see that I do?\" Make them feel part of the group — including yours."},{"label":"Latest","when":"Read the Room · 18 Nov 2026","text":"Wants to belong. Approach by including them and bringing them into the group."}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"What quiet question is someone with this need asking?","a":"“Do I belong — and can others see that I do?”"},{"id":"need-int","t":"ch","tp":"needs","ti":"🧠 Intelligence","mv":"Wants to be seen as smart. Approach by asking them to explain, staying curious, and letting them reach the conclusion.","tr":"I'd love to hear how you'd approach it.","w":"Avoid: Over-simplifying or lecturing them. It hits their quiet fear — looking stupid, being questioned, or being called out.","ev":"Coach's view","need":"intelligence","d":{"q":"Do people see me as smart?","signs":["Mentions education, books, research, technical detail","Says 'actually…', corrects or explains","Calls others idiots or incompetent","Asks if you know things they know you don't"],"lines":[{"say":"I'd love to hear how you'd approach it.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"I'll be honest, I know next to nothing about this — how does it actually work?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"You clearly think this through more than most people.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"That's a sharper way of looking at it than I'd considered.","how":"","why":""}],"traps":["Over-simplifying or lecturing them","Catching them out in public","Vague claims with no reasoning"],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 370","Read the Room six needs guide"],"rel":["h15","need-sig","need-app"],"go":[{"tab":"profile","label":"Open the six needs guide"},{"tab":"say","need":"intelligence","label":"Lines for this need in Say"}],"how":["The quiet question behind it: \"Do people see me as smart?\"","Ask how they would approach it, then stay curious long enough to hear the answer. A sincere 'teach me' — only when you want to learn — lets them show their reasoning.","What hurts most: Looking stupid, being questioned, or being called out."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"How this need has been worded","note":"Same need, three wordings. The latest keeps the listening-prompt framing and drops anything that could steer.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Read Anyone v1 · 10 Nov 2026","text":"Intelligence: Wants to be seen as smart. Listen for: Mentions expertise, research, how it works. Tests whether you know it too. Do: Ask for their expert view, and mean it. Let them explain."},{"label":"Updated","when":"Read & Connect field guide","text":"Intelligence — the quiet question: \"Do people see me as smart?\" Respect their reasoning. Ask them to explain — and be genuinely curious."},{"label":"Latest","when":"Read the Room · 18 Nov 2026","text":"Wants to be seen as smart. Approach by asking them to explain, staying curious, and letting them reach the conclusion."}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"What quiet question is someone with this need asking?","a":"“Do people see me as smart?”"},{"id":"need-pit","t":"ch","tp":"needs","ti":"🌧️ Pity (hardship)","mv":"Wants someone to see how hard it’s been. Approach by acknowledging it, then pausing so they feel heard, without rushing to fix it.","tr":"That sounds genuinely exhausting… (pause)","w":"Avoid: 'Look on the bright side' or 'could be worse'. It hits their quiet fear — being dismissed, disbelieved, or misunderstood.","ev":"Coach's view","need":"pity","d":{"q":"Does anyone realise how hard I've had it?","signs":["Complaints about traffic, weather, health or workload","Bad-luck stories, or being treated unfairly","A stressed or hunched look","Reacts strongly to small pains"],"lines":[{"say":"That sounds genuinely exhausting… (pause)","how":"","why":""},{"say":"You've had a lot more on your plate than people realise.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"I honestly don't know how you've kept going with all that.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Most people wouldn't have handled half of that.","how":"","why":""}],"traps":["'Look on the bright side' or 'could be worse'","Rushing to fix it","Topping their story with yours"],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 370","Read the Room six needs guide"],"rel":["h15","need-sig","need-app"],"go":[{"tab":"profile","label":"Open the six needs guide"},{"tab":"say","need":"pity","label":"Lines for this need in Say"}],"how":["The quiet question behind it: \"Does anyone realise how hard I've had it?\"","Confirm that it has been hard, then pause so they feel heard. Don't rush to fix it or bright-side it. Reflect a word they used, and ask a little more before you talk about yourself.","What hurts most: Being dismissed, disbelieved, or misunderstood."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"How this need has been worded","note":"Same need, three wordings. The latest keeps the listening-prompt framing and drops anything that could steer.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Read Anyone v1 · 10 Nov 2026","text":"Hardship: Wants it known it has been hard. Listen for: Talks about what they have been through. Seeks a moment of recognition. Do: Confirm it was hard. Pause so they feel heard. Then gently move on."},{"label":"Updated","when":"Read & Connect field guide","text":"Pity — the quiet question: \"Does anyone realise how hard I've had it?\" Confirm how tough it's been — then pause so they feel heard."},{"label":"Latest","when":"Read the Room · 18 Nov 2026","text":"Wants someone to see how hard it’s been. Approach by acknowledging it, then pausing so they feel heard, without rushing to fix it."}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"What quiet question is someone with this need asking?","a":"“Does anyone realise how hard I've had it?”"},{"id":"need-str","t":"ch","tp":"needs","ti":"🦁 Strength / Power","mv":"Wants to be seen as strong and in control. Approach with calm respect, and give them the choice.","tr":"Your call — how would you want to play it?","w":"Avoid: Dominance contests or 'putting them in their place'. It hits their quiet fear — being disrespected, challenged, or ignored.","ev":"Coach's view","need":"power","d":{"q":"Do people see me as strong and powerful?","signs":["Loud voice, big posture, a crushing handshake","Stories of toughness, or getting one over on people","Status and pecking order matter to them","Bristles when challenged; can be sharp with staff"],"lines":[{"say":"Your call — how would you want to play it?","how":"","why":""},{"say":"I'd rather have you on my side of this.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"You're obviously the one who gets things done here.","how":"","why":""},{"say":"Most people would have backed down there. You didn't.","how":"","why":""}],"traps":["Dominance contests or 'putting them in their place'","Embarrassing them in front of others","Ultimatums"],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 371","Read the Room six needs guide"],"rel":["h15","need-sig","need-app"],"go":[{"tab":"profile","label":"Open the six needs guide"},{"tab":"say","need":"power","label":"Lines for this need in Say"}],"how":["The quiet question behind it: \"Do people see me as strong and powerful?\"","Stay calm and respectful, and put the choice in their hands. Don't make it a contest. Offer clear options and let them decide.","What hurts most: Being disrespected, challenged, or ignored."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"How this need has been worded","note":"Same need, three wordings. The latest keeps the listening-prompt framing and drops anything that could steer.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Read Anyone v1 · 10 Nov 2026","text":"Power: Wants to steer. Listen for: Directs the conversation, wants the final say, asks for the headline. Do: Be short and clear. Let them make the call. Offer options, not a lecture."},{"label":"Updated","when":"Read & Connect field guide","text":"Strength / Power — the quiet question: \"Do people see me as strong and powerful?\" Let them feel in control. Calm respect, never a contest."},{"label":"Latest","when":"Read the Room · 18 Nov 2026","text":"Wants to be seen as strong and in control. Approach with calm respect, and give them the choice."}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"What quiet question is someone with this need asking?","a":"“Do people see me as strong and powerful?”"},{"id":"play-rapport","t":"ch","tp":"openers","ti":"Build rapport fast (Talk playbook)","mv":"Eyebrow flash and a warm hello in the first seconds — most people return it. Move slowly (the 'swimming pool' rule): speed reads as nerves.","tr":"Eyebrow-flash and warmly greet everyone you meet today, then count who flashes back.","w":"Spot it in the first minute, or the moment a chat starts running dry.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"steps":["Eyebrow flash and a warm hello in the first seconds — most people return it.","Move slowly (the 'swimming pool' rule): speed reads as nerves.","Small-talk loop: Listen → Display (let your face react) → Ask a small follow-up → Offer a related story. Repeat.","Answer their questions with four 'threads' they can grab (place, job, story, interest).","Mirror the last 2–3 words they said, or reflect the theme: 'Fifteen years. You must have seen it all.'","Use FORK topics: Family, Occupation, Recreation, Knowledge.","Connection mode, not performance mode: attention on them, curious, relaxed.","Give their need (see Profile) — that's the fastest rapport of all."],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 91–97, 314, 355, 431 (Talk tab playbook)"],"go":[{"tab":"talk","label":"Open the Talk playbooks"}],"how":["Eyebrow flash and a warm hello in the first seconds — most people return it. Move slowly (the 'swimming pool' rule): speed reads as nerves. Small-talk loop: Listen → Display (let your face react) → Ask a small follow-up → Offer a related story. Repeat. Answer their questions with four 'threads' they can grab (place, job, story, interest). Mirror the last 2–3 words they said, or reflect the theme: 'Fifteen years. You must have seen it all.' Use FORK topics: Family, Occupation, Recreation, Knowledge. Connection mode, not performance mode: attention on them, curious, relaxed. Give their need — that's the fastest rapport of all."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Name the 8 steps of “Build rapport fast (Talk playbook)”.","a":"Eyebrow flash and a warm hello in the first seconds — most p → Move slowly (the 'swimming pool' rule) → Small-talk loop → Answer their questions with four 'threads' they can grab (pl → Mirror the last 2–3 words they said, or reflect the theme → Use FORK topics → Connection mode, not performance mode → Give their need (see Profile) — that's the fastest rapport o"},{"id":"play-calm","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Calm someone down (Talk playbook)","mv":"Lens first: anger is usually fear. 'People are reasons', not broken. Re-orient: break the loop with something unexpected or a step to the side.","tr":"Next time someone is upset, say one true thing they can't disagree with, then pause.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"steps":["Lens first: anger is usually fear. 'People are reasons', not broken.","Re-orient: break the loop with something unexpected or a step to the side.","Validate: say something true they can't disagree with ('It's Friday, and everyone's trying to get home').","Observe: give their need — Strength: respect and control; Pity: 'that's genuinely awful' + pause; Approval: reassurance.","Action: ask their advice or offer a choice, so they feel in control again.","Stay composed — no collapse (grovelling) and no posturing (matching their volume)."],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 280, 300, 812 (Talk tab playbook)"],"go":[{"tab":"talk","label":"Open the Talk playbooks"}],"how":["Lens first: anger is usually fear. 'People are reasons', not broken. Re-orient: break the loop with something unexpected or a step to the side. Validate: say something true they can't disagree with ('It's Friday, and everyone's trying to get home'). Observe: give their need — Strength: respect and control; Pity: 'that's genuinely awful' + pause; Approval: reassurance. Action: ask their advice or offer a choice, so they feel in control again. Stay composed — no collapse (grovelling) and no posturing (matching their volume)."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Calm someone down (Talk playbook)”?","a":"Lens first: anger is usually fear. 'People are reasons', not broken. Re-orient: break the loop with something unexpected or a step to the side."},{"id":"play-support","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"Support someone who's struggling (Talk playbook)","mv":"Don't try to fix them. Help them put words to what's going on. Swap 'why' for 'How did that end up happening?' or 'What was going on around then?'","tr":"Next time someone's replies get shorter, stop talking and count to nine in your head.","w":"Make it free to say no: 'You don't have to answer that.' Ask what's in the way rather than adding more reasons.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"steps":["Don't try to fix them. Help them put words to what's going on.","Swap 'why' for 'How did that end up happening?' or 'What was going on around then?'","Name the feeling you see, so they can say 'that's right'.","If their answers get shorter, you're adding too much. Stop and count to nine.","Make it free to say no: 'You don't have to answer that.'","Ask what's in the way rather than adding more reasons."],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. videos 2025–26 (Talk tab playbook)"],"go":[{"tab":"talk","label":"Open the Talk playbooks"}],"how":["Don't try to fix them. Help them put words to what's going on. Swap 'why' for 'How did that end up happening?' or 'What was going on around then?' Name the feeling you see, so they can say 'that's right'. If their answers get shorter, you're adding too much. Stop and count to nine. Make it free to say no: 'You don't have to answer that.' Ask what's in the way rather than adding more reasons."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Name the 6 steps of “Support someone who's struggling (Talk playbook)”.","a":"Don't try to fix them. Help them put words to what's going o → Swap 'why' for 'How did that end up happening?' or 'What was → Name the feeling you see, so they can say 'that's right'. → If their answers get shorter, you're adding too much. Stop a → Make it free to say no → Ask what's in the way rather than adding more reasons."},{"id":"play-argue","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"When an argument gets heated (Talk playbook)","mv":"Ask yourself what feels threatened for them: identity, status and control, or safety. Stay calm. Don't match their emotion or raise your voice to be heard.","tr":"In your next disagreement, ask what a heavy word means to them before you answer it.","w":"Don't match their emotion or raise your voice to be heard.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"steps":["Ask yourself what feels threatened for them: identity, status and control, or safety.","Stay calm. Don't match their emotion or raise your voice to be heard.","Don't explain yourself under pressure or debate your character. Leave them a way back: 'Maybe you didn't mean it that way.'","If they use a heavy word like 'betrayal', ask what they mean by it.","Don't tell them their feelings are wrong, and don't try to fix them mid-argument.","Put yourselves on the same side: 'What's the next step that would fix this?'","Afterwards, if your reaction was bigger than the moment, ask when you first felt exactly this way."],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. videos 2025–26 (Talk tab playbook)"],"go":[{"tab":"talk","label":"Open the Talk playbooks"}],"how":["Ask yourself what feels threatened for them: identity, status and control, or safety. Stay calm. Don't match their emotion or raise your voice to be heard. Don't explain yourself under pressure or debate your character. Leave them a way back: 'Maybe you didn't mean it that way.' If they use a heavy word like 'betrayal', ask what they mean by it. Don't tell them their feelings are wrong, and don't try to fix them mid-argument. Put yourselves on the same side: 'What's the next step that would fix this?' Afterwards, if your reaction was bigger than the moment, ask when you first felt exactly this way."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “When an argument gets heated (Talk playbook)”?","a":"Ask yourself what feels threatened for them: identity, status and control, or safety. Stay calm. Don't match their emotion or raise your voice to be heard."},{"id":"k-ethics","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"The scalpel rule: ethics first","mv":"Hughes frames these skills like a scalpel: the same tool can heal or harm, so the user carries the responsibility. His stated aim is to use influence to lift people up, and he says life isn't a zero-sum game.","tr":"Before using any technique, ask whether the other person would be glad you used it if they knew. If not, don't.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"ex":["A nurse uses calm, slow movement and expectancy language to settle a frightened patient. That is the \"scalpel\" used to help."],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 2","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 5"],"rel":["k-time-distance"],"how":["Hughes frames these skills like a scalpel: the same tool can heal or harm, so the user carries the responsibility. His stated aim is to use influence to lift people up, and he says life isn't a zero-sum game.","The book opens with a note on ethics and an \"Author's Code of Honor\" built around responsibility, stewardship, leadership and conduct. He also says the book is for education and that readers are responsible for what they do with it."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The scalpel rule: ethics first”?","a":"Hughes frames these skills like a scalpel: the same tool can heal or harm, so the user carries the responsibility. His stated aim is to use influence to lift people up, and he says life isn't a zero-sum game."},{"id":"k-time-distance","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"The time/distance problem","mv":"The core question Hughes says he built his system to solve: how do you move someone a long way from their normal behaviour in a short time, instead of only small changes over a long time?","tr":"The core question Hughes says he built his system to solve: how do you move someone a long way from their normal behaviour in a short time, instead of only small changes over a long time?","w":"The core question Hughes says he built his system to solve: how do you move someone a long way from their normal behaviour in a short time, instead of only small changes over a long time?","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"ex":["Being asked to commit on the spot, in an unfamiliar setting, by someone acting like an authority is a classic \"short time, big distance\" setup."],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 7-9"],"rel":["k-ethics"],"how":["The core question Hughes says he built his system to solve: how do you move someone a long way from their normal behaviour in a short time, instead of only small changes over a long time?","He plots \"distance\" (how far from normal someone acts) against \"time\" (how long it takes). Ordinary persuasion training gets small shifts slowly. The \"missing bridge\" is the skill gap between those two points. He says his answer is the Hierarchy of Influence: working at the deeper levels (impulse and behaviour patterns) rather than words."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The time/distance problem”?","a":"The core question Hughes says he built his system to solve: how do you move someone a long way from their normal behaviour in a short time, instead of only small changes over a long time?"},{"id":"k-yt-render-of-you","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"People respond to their \"render\" of you","mv":"Hughes describes the brain as a prediction machine: each person carries their own \"render\" of you and hears everything you say as that character saying it. Some of the worst arguments are sequels to old ones.","tr":"To change how someone sees you, make one clear, consistent change they can't miss.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – everyone's render of you @18:00","One clean violation @30:00"],"how":["Hughes describes the brain as a prediction machine: each person carries their own \"render\" of you and hears everything you say as that character saying it. Some of the worst arguments are sequels to old ones.","Because the brain prefers predicting to looking, gradual change often goes unnoticed. One clear, clean break from the expected pattern updates someone's picture of you faster than years of slow drift."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “of you Hughes describes the brain as a prediction machine: …”","a":"“ of you Hughes describes the brain as a prediction machine: each person carries their own ”"},{"id":"k-yt-translator-not-fix","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"People need a translator, not a fix","mv":"Hughes suggests that much of what runs people is old, unprocessed grief and childhood patterns, not current beliefs.","tr":"Instead of advice, try \"I wonder if this feels like something from before?\"","w":"He applies it to yourself too: notice when a reaction belongs to the past and name it, rather than labelling yourself \"too sensitive\".","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – What it Feels like to SEE INTO People @0:00","A translator, not a fix @7:30","Three steps to spot it @6:02"],"rel":["k-yt-argument-ghost"],"how":["Hughes suggests that much of what runs people is old, unprocessed grief and childhood patterns, not current beliefs. What a struggling person needs is a translator who helps them see what the pattern is, not someone trying to fix them.","He applies it to yourself too: notice when a reaction belongs to the past and name it, rather than labelling yourself \"too sensitive\". His three steps: listen for a reaction bigger than the situation, ask what the person (or you) is afraid to lose, then speak to that loss rather than arguing the logic."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “People need a translator, not a fix”?","a":"He applies it to yourself too: notice when a reaction belongs to the past and name it, rather than labelling yourself \"too sensitive\"."},{"id":"k-yt-feelings-before-beliefs","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Feelings first, beliefs explain afterwards","mv":"Hughes pushes back on \"beliefs drive behaviour\". In his view you feel first, and the brain then attaches a belief to explain the feeling.","tr":"When someone states a strong belief, ask what feeling it protects.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – You're Talking to Scar Tissue @0:00"],"rel":["k-scripts-vs-emotions"],"how":["Hughes pushes back on \"beliefs drive behaviour\". In his view you feel first, and the brain then attaches a belief to explain the feeling. To understand someone, watch what they need emotionally, such as approval as a currency or shame as a prison.","This fits his wider point that the emotional brain moves faster than the logical one."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Feelings first, beliefs explain afterwards”?","a":"When someone states a strong belief, ask what feeling it protects."},{"id":"k-yt-being-influential","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Being influential vs doing influence","mv":"Hughes separates doing influence (techniques and scripts) from being influential. Influence lives in the person, so a script used by someone without control over their own state and body falls flat.","tr":"Work on your own composure before collecting more lines to say.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – being vs doing influence @0:00","Seeing people as reasons @2:00"],"how":["Hughes separates doing influence (techniques and scripts) from being influential. Influence lives in the person, so a script used by someone without control over their own state and body falls flat.","He describes lenses for seeing people. The more advanced ones move past traits to the reasons and fuel behind behaviour, which is where profiling pays off."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Being influential vs doing influence”?","a":"Hughes separates doing influence (techniques and scripts) from being influential. Influence lives in the person, so a script used by someone without control over their own state and body falls flat."},{"id":"k-pod-argument-common-ground","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Resolving an argument with a partner (podcast)","mv":"On Diary of a CEO (Dec 2024), Hughes said to establish common ground early in a disagreement, even by asking \"do we both want the same thing from this?\". Let the other person get everything out first.","tr":"\"Before we go on, do we both want the same outcome here?\" Then listen fully before correcting anything.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Diary of a CEO – Chase Hughes (2024): How to win an argument @1:10:33"],"rel":["k-yt-conflict-frame"],"how":["On Diary of a CEO (Dec 2024), Hughes said to establish common ground early in a disagreement, even by asking \"do we both want the same thing from this?\". Let the other person get everything out first. Hold back corrections of small wrong details until the end, because by then they've often defused."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “do we both want the same …”","a":"“do we both want the same thing from this?”"},{"id":"k-yt-argument-ghost","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Every argument is with a ghost","mv":"Hughes says most fights with a partner aren't about the dishes or the tone. The comment hits an old wound from someone who once made you feel you didn't matter.","tr":"When a reaction is bigger than what happened, ask yourself when you first felt exactly this way. Whatever surfaces first is probably the ghost, and it is yours to find, not theirs to carry.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Every Argument is With a GHOST @0:00","Old code, new hardware @7:30"],"rel":["k-yt-core-shame"],"how":["Hughes says most fights with a partner aren't about the dishes or the tone. The comment hits an old wound from someone who once made you feel you didn't matter. The nervous system has no calendar, so the old moment is happening again now.","In his couple example, a wife's remark sends the husband back to how his father spoke to him. He isn't really fighting his wife. A reaction bigger than the moment is the tell. Seeing that it isn't personal also means it isn't permanent."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Every argument is with a ghost”?","a":"When a reaction is bigger than what happened, ask yourself when you first felt exactly this way. Whatever surfaces first is probably the ghost, and it is yours to find, not theirs to carry."},{"id":"k-yt-thats-right","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"Aim for \"that's right\": name the emotion","mv":"Hughes borrows the negotiation idea that the goal is the other person thinking \"that's right\", meaning they accept your description as their reality. Naming the emotion they're feeling loosens its grip, and they feel seen.","tr":"\"Sounds like you're exhausted from carrying this alone.\" Then stop talking.","w":"He calls this a \"micro-collapse\", and it's the moment to listen rather than push.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – waiting for \"that's right\" @2:00","Naming emotions @8:00"],"how":["Hughes borrows the negotiation idea that the goal is the other person thinking \"that's right\", meaning they accept your description as their reality. Naming the emotion they're feeling loosens its grip, and they feel seen.","He says unnamed emotions run people. When you name one accurately, the person's guarded expression often softens for about a second. He calls this a \"micro-collapse\", and it's the moment to listen rather than push."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Aim for \"that's right\": name the emotion”?","a":"Hughes borrows the negotiation idea that the goal is the other person thinking \"that's right\", meaning they accept your description as their reality. Naming the emotion they're feeling loosens its grip, and they feel seen."},{"id":"k-yt-avoid-why","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"Why to drop \"why\" from your questions","mv":"Hughes puts \"why\" at the top of his list of words to avoid when you're trying to influence someone. A \"why\" question asks the person to defend themselves, so they dig in.","tr":"Swap \"Why did you do that?\" for \"What was going on for you then?\"","w":"Hughes puts \"why\" at the top of his list of words to avoid when you're trying to influence someone.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – the word to avoid @12:30"],"rel":["k-yt-remove-reasons"],"how":["Hughes puts \"why\" at the top of his list of words to avoid when you're trying to influence someone. A \"why\" question asks the person to defend themselves, so they dig in.","He presents it as one of several lines that work by taking pressure off a person rather than adding it. \"What\" and \"how\" questions invite description instead of justification."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “from your questions …”","a":"“ from your questions Hughes puts ”"},{"id":"k-yt-function-not-trait","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Name the function, not the trait","mv":"Hughes' rule of thumb: if the other person ever has to think \"No, I'm not\", you've lost them. So describe what a behaviour does, not what the person is.","tr":"Describe the behaviour's purpose kindly. Avoid personality labels in tense moments.","w":"Instead of \"You're angry\", try \"That reaction makes sense as a protective move.\" Naming the function avoids a label they have to reject.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – the \"No, I'm not\" rule @22:00","Function, not trait @24:00"],"rel":["k-yt-thats-right"],"how":["Hughes' rule of thumb: if the other person ever has to think \"No, I'm not\", you've lost them. So describe what a behaviour does, not what the person is.","Instead of \"You're angry\", try \"That reaction makes sense as a protective move.\" Naming the function avoids a label they have to reject."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Name the function, not the trait”?","a":"Describe the behaviour's purpose kindly. Avoid personality labels in tense moments."},{"id":"k-yt-remove-reasons","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Influence removes reasons, it doesn't add pressure","mv":"Hughes argues that nobody is really talked into anything. People already want things, and influence works by removing the reasons not to act.","tr":"Instead of adding arguments, ask what's in the way and help take that one thing off their plate.","w":"Hughes argues that nobody is really talked into anything.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Helping Makes It Worse @0:00","Pushing creates refutations @2:30"],"rel":["k-yt-avoid-why"],"how":["Hughes argues that nobody is really talked into anything. People already want things, and influence works by removing the reasons not to act. The harder you push, the more you hand them something to argue against.","In his phone-call example, the more the caller pushed, the more the other person came back with short one-word refutations. That is a sign she had already thought it through and was defending her position. Deadlines and added pressure, even with your own kids, tend to make people dig in."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Influence removes reasons, it doesn't add pressure”?","a":"Hughes argues that nobody is really talked into anything. People already want things, and influence works by removing the reasons not to act."},{"id":"k-yt-disclosure-ladder","t":"ch","tp":"deeper","ti":"The honesty ladder: deepen disclosure in steps","mv":"Hughes lays out honesty in levels. It starts with mildly questioning a social rule, moves to admitting something about yourself, and ends with a personal inner reveal.","tr":"Go one level more honest than polite small talk, and let them match before you go further.","w":"Spot it when their answers get longer and warmer: they are ready for a bit more.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – honesty levels @26:00","Bridging levels @32:00"],"how":["Hughes lays out honesty in levels. It starts with mildly questioning a social rule, moves to admitting something about yourself, and ends with a personal inner reveal. Each step invites the other person to match it.","Jumping straight to a deep reveal makes people's guard go up (\"where did that come from?\"). He suggests bridging between levels: slow down, lower your voice, then share."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The honesty ladder: deepen disclosure in steps”?","a":"Hughes lays out honesty in levels. It starts with mildly questioning a social rule, moves to admitting something about yourself, and ends with a personal inner reveal."},{"id":"k-yt-metaphor-prisons","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Metaphors that become prisons","mv":"Hughes argues everyday metaphors (\"time is money\", \"love is a journey\", \"the mind is a container\") are not facts. They quietly limit how we think.","tr":"In a disagreement, check definitions early: \"When you say 'respect', what does that look like to you?\"","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – metaphors as prisons @2:30","Same word, different meaning @5:00"],"how":["Hughes argues everyday metaphors (\"time is money\", \"love is a journey\", \"the mind is a container\") are not facts. They quietly limit how we think. Many fights are really about the words used, where two people mean different things by the same word.","He also notes that the most alive moments in life are bigger than language can hold. Words shrink them afterwards."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Metaphors that become prisons”?","a":"In a disagreement, check definitions early: \"When you say 'respect', what does that look like to you?\""},{"id":"k-yt-memory-rewrite","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Memory is rewritten and arousal gets misread","mv":"Hughes cites the 1974 bridge study, in which men on a scary bridge read their racing hearts as attraction. Gut feelings are assembled, not simply true.","tr":"Treat strong gut feelings and vivid memories as data to check, not verdicts.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – the bridge study @10:00","Recall overwrites memory @14:00","Chase Hughes – memories have no source tag @10:00"],"how":["Hughes cites the 1974 bridge study, in which men on a scary bridge read their racing hearts as attraction. Gut feelings are assembled, not simply true. Each time you recall a memory and tell it, the new version overwrites the old one.","That matters for profiling and interviews: confident detail isn't proof of accuracy, and leading retellings can reshape memory."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Memory is rewritten and arousal gets misread”?","a":"Hughes cites the 1974 bridge study, in which men on a scary bridge read their racing hearts as attraction. Gut feelings are assembled, not simply true."},{"id":"k-yt-context-shift","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Why you become someone else in some places","mv":"Hughes explains that familiar places (family dinners, old workplaces) trigger old roles before anyone speaks. People stack small requests (\"come on, it's family, it's tradition\") that pull you back into them.","tr":"When a stack of requests lands, answer just one: \"I'm not saying no to you, just to this,\" then stop talking. If someone leans on who you're supposed to be, ask what part feels like a big deal to them.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Why You Become Someone ELSE in Some Places @2:00","Saying no to one brick @4:33"],"how":["Hughes explains that familiar places (family dinners, old workplaces) trigger old roles before anyone speaks. People stack small requests (\"come on, it's family, it's tradition\") that pull you back into them.","His counters are to disrupt predictability early (where you sit or stand, whom you talk to first) and to slow down, because speed belongs to habit and slowness to choice. He also says to watch fatigue, which makes you fall back on old scripts, and to state your boundary calmly without explaining."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “come on, it's family, …”","a":"“come on, it's family, it's tradition”"},{"id":"k-yt-behavioral-magnetics","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Behavioural magnetics: why you keep attracting the same people","mv":"Hughes calls it \"emotional gravity\" and describes four magnets. The mirror magnet pulls you toward people who treat you the way you were treated growing up.","tr":"Write down your loop in one sentence. Awareness is the first break in the pattern.","w":"His steps: spot the loop (\"what keeps happening that I say I don't want, but I keep allowing?\"), name the magnet, then trace it to its source.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Behavioral Magnetics @3:13","Steps to rewire @9:01"],"rel":["k-yt-emotional-debt"],"how":["Hughes calls it \"emotional gravity\" and describes four magnets. The mirror magnet pulls you toward people who treat you the way you were treated growing up. The echo magnet makes you recreate familiar pain for a false sense of control. The reversal magnet flips the roles, so you become what hurt you. The completion magnet keeps finding people like those who hurt you, hoping this time the story ends differently.","His steps: spot the loop (\"what keeps happening that I say I don't want, but I keep allowing?\"), name the magnet, then trace it to its source. Who taught you this was normal, and whose love came with conditions?"]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Behavioural magnetics: why you keep attracting the same people”?","a":"Write down your loop in one sentence. Awareness is the first break in the pattern."},{"id":"k-yt-bullies","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Dealing with bullies","mv":"Hughes uses his animal profiles for bullies. Some are Chihuahuas, loud to keep themselves safe, and some are Porcupines, keeping people away after being hurt.","tr":"Ask what the bully is protecting, and respond calmly to that, not to the noise.","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Deal with Bullies the RIGHT way @0:45","Four types @7:21","Redirecting in the moment @12:22"],"rel":["k-yt-core-shame"],"how":["Hughes uses his animal profiles for bullies. Some are Chihuahuas, loud to keep themselves safe, and some are Porcupines, keeping people away after being hurt. He lists four drivers: feeling insignificant, fear of being hurt, a secret shame projected onto others, and a hidden fear surfacing.","He'd rather teach children empathy for why bullies act that way than tactics to make them feel small. In the moment, his redirect is to use the person's name, state a few plain facts about what's happening right now, then ask them to do something else."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Dealing with bullies”?","a":"Hughes uses his animal profiles for bullies. Some are Chihuahuas, loud to keep themselves safe, and some are Porcupines, keeping people away after being hurt."},{"id":"k-yt-core-shame","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Insults as \"reverse sentences\": reading core shame","mv":"Hughes calls shame a survival system, not a moral issue. Everyone keeps answering one question: what part of me has to stay hidden?","tr":"When someone insults you, ask what they're afraid of being. It takes the sting out and keeps you kind.","w":"Hughes calls shame a survival system, not a moral issue.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – See Their Core Shame Instantly @0:00","Insults as clues @8:00"],"how":["Hughes calls shame a survival system, not a moral issue. Everyone keeps answering one question: what part of me has to stay hidden? He reads insults as reverse sentences. The insult someone reaches for (weak, fake, needy, stupid, cringe) points to their own deepest fear.","An offhand insult suggests mild insecurity, and a repeated one a heavily reinforced defence. He describes personas built on it. Charming performers fear being exposed as social frauds, and dominators fear being weak. Seeing it lets you understand people \"without contempt\"."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Insults as \"reverse sentences\": reading core shame”?","a":"Hughes calls shame a survival system, not a moral issue."},{"id":"k-yt-emotional-debt","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Emotional debt","mv":"Hughes describes \"emotional debt\" as the build-up of many small unprocessed emotional moments. Like debt, it charges \"payments\" in restlessness, not being able to sit in silence, numbing and strained relationships.","tr":"If this resonates strongly, a therapist is the \"witness\" he describes. Talk to a professional, especially if you're self-medicating.","w":"His steps: see the payments (make them conscious), name the pattern to create distance, let the body finish what it started, stop taking on new debt and get a witness, someone to be seen by.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Why You Can't Relax: Emotional DEBT @5:30","The steps out @18:39"],"rel":["k-yt-behavioral-magnetics","k-roots-of-anxiety"],"how":["Hughes describes \"emotional debt\" as the build-up of many small unprocessed emotional moments. Like debt, it charges \"payments\" in restlessness, not being able to sit in silence, numbing and strained relationships. The brain keeps scanning for unresolved threats, and the prefrontal cortex loses ground.","His steps: see the payments (make them conscious), name the pattern to create distance, let the body finish what it started, stop taking on new debt and get a witness, someone to be seen by. He says you can't think your way out; you have to feel your way out. Body-based tremor release exercises (TRE) come up in the video, as does the claim that numbing works short-term, which is why it's hard to stop."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Emotional debt”?","a":"If this resonates strongly, a therapist is the \"witness\" he describes. Talk to a professional, especially if you're self-medicating."},{"id":"k-yt-conflict-frame","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Heated conflict: three threats, ten nevers","mv":"Hughes says heated conflict usually comes from three threats: to identity, to status or control, and to social safety. Once one fires, the brain shifts into limbic mode and logic gets bypassed.","tr":"\"I want us on the same side of the table on this. What's the next step that would fix it?\"","w":"His ten \"nevers\" when someone attacks your frame: don't explain yourself under pressure, argue about your motive or debate your character.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – The Cheat Code for Controlling Conflict @1:40","The ten nevers @14:00"],"how":["Hughes says heated conflict usually comes from three threats: to identity, to status or control, and to social safety. Once one fires, the brain shifts into limbic mode and logic gets bypassed. Your job is to work out which threat it is and reduce it.","His ten \"nevers\" when someone attacks your frame: don't explain yourself under pressure, argue about your motive or debate your character. Don't apologise falsely just to end it, or match their emotion. Don't accept heavy words like \"betrayal\" without asking what they mean, answer either-or traps, or try to win on logic alone. Don't argue that their feelings are wrong, and don't try to fix the person mid-conflict. Keep the frame on process, not blame, leave them a bridge to retreat (\"maybe you didn't mean to\"), and put yourselves on the same side of the table."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Heated conflict: three threats, ten nevers”?","a":"Hughes says heated conflict usually comes from three threats: to identity, to status or control, and to social safety. Once one fires, the brain shifts into limbic mode and logic gets bypassed."},{"id":"k-neurology-overview","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"The neurology of influence (Section 02 overview)","mv":"Section 02 gives a plain-language tour of the brain so you understand why the methods work. His analogy: a pilot can fly without knowing the fuel lines, but mastery needs both.","tr":"Section 02 gives a plain-language tour of the brain so you understand why the methods work. His analogy: a pilot can fly without knowing the fuel lines, but mastery needs both.","w":"The main lessons: language networks are evolutionarily new, emotion and memory live in the limbic system, and gut feelings come from systems that don't use words.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 63-88"],"rel":["k-limbic-system","k-cortex-vs-limbic","k-ras","k-vta-drive","k-brain-stem"],"how":["Section 02 gives a plain-language tour of the brain so you understand why the methods work. His analogy: a pilot can fly without knowing the fuel lines, but mastery needs both.","It covers the cerebrum, the cerebellum (movement and balance), the brain stem (automatic functions), the left and right hemispheres, the lobes, the cortex, deep structures (hypothalamus, pituitary, pineal, thalamus, basal ganglia), the limbic system, memory, neurons and glia, resting potential, the reticular activating system and the VTA (drive). The main lessons: language networks are evolutionarily new, emotion and memory live in the limbic system, and gut feelings come from systems that don't use words. Treat this as Hughes' explanatory framing for influence, not a neuroscience textbook."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “The neurology of influence (Section 02 overview)”?","a":"The main lessons: language networks are evolutionarily new, emotion and memory live in the limbic system, and gut feelings come from systems that don't use words."},{"id":"k-brain-stem","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Brain stem","mv":"The brain stem links the brain and spinal cord and runs automatic survival functions: breathing, heart rate, swallowing, sleep and waking.","tr":"The brain stem links the brain and spinal cord and runs automatic survival functions: breathing, heart rate, swallowing, sleep and waking.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 65","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 71","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 951"],"rel":["k-neurology-overview"],"how":["The brain stem links the brain and spinal cord and runs automatic survival functions: breathing, heart rate, swallowing, sleep and waking. Hughes' rule is that the closer a method gets to the brain stem and spinal cord, the stronger its effect.","Its three parts are the medulla oblongata (breathing, heart and digestion), the pons and the midbrain. He likens the brain to a flower: the cerebrum is the bloom, the brain stem the stem and the spinal cord the root. In training he puts up a slide saying you're \"in the business of manufacturing impulse and identity\", not information."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Brain stem”?","a":"The brain stem links the brain and spinal cord and runs automatic survival functions: breathing, heart rate, swallowing, sleep and waking."},{"id":"k-limbic-system","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Limbic system (the \"mammal brain\")","mv":"The limbic system handles emotion, memory and bonding. Hughes says it \"thrives on feeling, not thinking\".","tr":"Change environments to change habits. New cues weaken old scripts. When communicating, make people feel safe and understood before giving them facts.","w":"It communicates through gut feelings rather than words, and powerful communication targets it rather than the cortex.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"ex":["A therapist uses lavender in the office so the usual \"medical building\" smell doesn't set off a fear script."],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 68","The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 82-84","The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 959-960","Chase Hughes short – limbic signals and gut feelings @0:00"],"rel":["k-cortex-vs-limbic"],"how":["The limbic system handles emotion, memory and bonding. Hughes says it \"thrives on feeling, not thinking\". It communicates through gut feelings rather than words, and powerful communication targets it rather than the cortex.","Its main parts are the hippocampus (memory), amygdala (fear, anger, jealousy), hypothalamus and thalamus. It was once called the \"nose brain\" because early research linked it to smell. It records which behaviours led to good or bad experiences, is where we fall in and out of love, and holds many unconscious value judgements. It runs memory through scripts and uses the surroundings to store and retrieve them. That's why a smoker on holiday abroad can find it easier to skip cigarettes: the usual triggers aren't there."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Limbic system (the \"mammal brain\")”?","a":"The limbic system handles emotion, memory and bonding. Hughes says it \"thrives on feeling, not thinking\"."},{"id":"k-cortex-vs-limbic","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Cortex vs limbic: weak vs powerful communication","mv":"Hughes puts it simply: weak communication focuses on the cortex (information and reasoning), and powerful communication focuses on the limbic system (feeling). Thoughts are electrical; emotions are chemical and stronger.","tr":"When persuading, pair the facts with a concrete human story and a felt benefit. When deciding for yourself, give your reasoning a moment to catch up with the feeling.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 80-83"],"rel":["k-limbic-system"],"how":["Hughes puts it simply: weak communication focuses on the cortex (information and reasoning), and powerful communication focuses on the limbic system (feeling). Thoughts are electrical; emotions are chemical and stronger.","The neocortex is the newest part of the brain: language, planning, reasoning, personality. It can only rationalise decisions the emotional brain has already leaned toward. Influence that changes feelings (chemistry) outlasts influence that only adds facts (electricity)."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Cortex vs limbic: weak vs powerful communication”?","a":"Hughes puts it simply: weak communication focuses on the cortex (information and reasoning), and powerful communication focuses on the limbic system (feeling). Thoughts are electrical; emotions are chemical and stronger."},{"id":"k-ras","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Reticular activating system (RAS)","mv":"The RAS is a crayon-sized network in the brain stem that filters sensory input for what you've decided matters. Hughes' example: after you research and buy a new car, you suddenly see that model everywhere.","tr":"Use it on yourself. Decide each week which two cues to watch for and you'll start noticing them without effort.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 79-80"],"rel":["k-neurology-overview"],"how":["The RAS is a crayon-sized network in the brain stem that filters sensory input for what you've decided matters. Hughes' example: after you research and buy a new car, you suddenly see that model everywhere.","The RAS learns from repeated focus and emotion. Whatever you keep attending to, especially with strong feeling, it flags in future. He links this to training your eye for behaviour: practise a few cues and you'll start spotting them automatically. The bias section's \"frequency illusion\" describes the same effect."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Reticular activating system (RAS)”?","a":"Use it on yourself. Decide each week which two cues to watch for and you'll start noticing them without effort."},{"id":"k-vta-drive","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"The VTA, dopamine and drive","mv":"The ventral tegmental area is one of the brain's main dopamine sources and feeds the reward and motivation circuits. Hughes uses it to explain drive: what we chase, and why anticipation can matter more than the reward itself.","tr":"The ventral tegmental area is one of the brain's main dopamine sources and feeds the reward and motivation circuits. Hughes uses it to explain drive: what we chase, and why anticipation can matter more than the reward itself.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 78-79","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 967"],"rel":["k-magic-pathway"],"how":["The ventral tegmental area is one of the brain's main dopamine sources and feeds the reward and motivation circuits. Hughes uses it to explain drive: what we chase, and why anticipation can matter more than the reward itself.","In the glossary he notes the VTA's two main pathways, to limbic and to cortical areas, and that the nucleus accumbens is central to reward, balancing dopamine (desire, anticipation) against serotonin (satiety)."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The VTA, dopamine and drive”?","a":"The ventral tegmental area is one of the brain's main dopamine sources and feeds the reward and motivation circuits. Hughes uses it to explain drive: what we chase, and why anticipation can matter more than the reward itself."},{"id":"k-scripts-vs-emotions","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Scripts vs emotions (Section 02 review)","mv":"Hughes closes the neurology section with everyday examples. Small sensory cues (a sound, a smell, a topic) can silently start a script and change someone's mood or behaviour, and a skilled person notices and adjusts.","tr":"If someone suddenly goes cold, ask yourself \"what just happened?\" It's often a trigger unrelated to you.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 87-88"],"rel":["k-limbic-system"],"how":["Hughes closes the neurology section with everyday examples. Small sensory cues (a sound, a smell, a topic) can silently start a script and change someone's mood or behaviour, and a skilled person notices and adjusts.","In his examples a doctor goes serious at dinner when a text tone resembles a hospital monitor, and a clinician primes closed-off patients with memories of close friends. The takeaway: when someone suddenly changes, look for what just triggered it before deciding it's about you."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Scripts vs emotions (Section 02 review)”?","a":"Hughes closes the neurology section with everyday examples. Small sensory cues (a sound, a smell, a topic) can silently start a script and change someone's mood or behaviour, and a skilled person notices and adjusts."},{"id":"k-neurotransmitters","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Neurotransmitter reference (overview)","mv":"Hughes includes a reference on neurotransmitters. Dopamine drives seeking and reward, GABA calms activity, glutamate supports learning and memory and oxytocin supports bonding.","tr":"Hughes includes a reference on neurotransmitters. Dopamine drives seeking and reward, GABA calms activity, glutamate supports learning and memory and oxytocin supports bonding.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 824-835"],"rel":["k-vta-drive"],"how":["Hughes includes a reference on neurotransmitters. Dopamine drives seeking and reward, GABA calms activity, glutamate supports learning and memory and oxytocin supports bonding.","The book lists foods and supplements for each. This site doesn't repeat those lists. Talk to a doctor before changing diet or supplements to alter brain chemistry."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Neurotransmitter reference (overview)”?","a":"Hughes includes a reference on neurotransmitters. Dopamine drives seeking and reward, GABA calms activity, glutamate supports learning and memory and oxytocin supports bonding."},{"id":"k-amygdala","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Amygdala","mv":"The amygdala handles basic emotions such as fear, anger and jealousy, along with some memory processing. It's part of the limbic system.","tr":"The amygdala handles basic emotions such as fear, anger and jealousy, along with some memory processing. It's part of the limbic system.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 949","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 68"],"rel":["k-limbic-system","k-brain-stem"],"how":["The amygdala handles basic emotions such as fear, anger and jealousy, along with some memory processing. It's part of the limbic system."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Amygdala”?","a":"The amygdala handles basic emotions such as fear, anger and jealousy, along with some memory processing. It's part of the limbic system."},{"id":"k-hippocampus","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Hippocampus","mv":"The hippocampus converts short-term memories into long-term ones and handles spatial memory.","tr":"The hippocampus converts short-term memories into long-term ones and handles spatial memory.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 955"],"rel":["k-limbic-system"],"how":["The hippocampus converts short-term memories into long-term ones and handles spatial memory."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Hippocampus”?","a":"The hippocampus converts short-term memories into long-term ones and handles spatial memory."},{"id":"k-hypothalamus","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Hypothalamus","mv":"Hughes calls the coffee-bean-sized hypothalamus perhaps the brain's most important structure. It controls thirst, hunger, emotions, temperature, arousal and sleep rhythms.","tr":"Hughes calls the coffee-bean-sized hypothalamus perhaps the brain's most important structure. It controls thirst, hunger, emotions, temperature, arousal and sleep rhythms.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 955","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 67"],"rel":["k-limbic-system","k-pituitary"],"how":["Hughes calls the coffee-bean-sized hypothalamus perhaps the brain's most important structure. It controls thirst, hunger, emotions, temperature, arousal and sleep rhythms."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Hypothalamus”?","a":"Hughes calls the coffee-bean-sized hypothalamus perhaps the brain's most important structure. It controls thirst, hunger, emotions, temperature, arousal and sleep rhythms."},{"id":"k-pituitary","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Pituitary gland","mv":"The pituitary is the \"master gland\" that controls other hormone glands, including growth, puberty and thyroid hormones.","tr":"The pituitary is the \"master gland\" that controls other hormone glands, including growth, puberty and thyroid hormones.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 960","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 67"],"rel":["k-hypothalamus"],"how":["The pituitary is the \"master gland\" that controls other hormone glands, including growth, puberty and thyroid hormones."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Pituitary gland”?","a":"The pituitary is the \"master gland\" that controls other hormone glands, including growth, puberty and thyroid hormones."},{"id":"k-thalamus","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Thalamus","mv":"The thalamus is the brain's switchboard. All senses except smell go there first and get routed onward.","tr":"The thalamus is the brain's switchboard. All senses except smell go there first and get routed onward.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 965","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 67"],"rel":["k-neurology-overview"],"how":["The thalamus is the brain's switchboard. All senses except smell go there first and get routed onward."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Thalamus”?","a":"The thalamus is the brain's switchboard. All senses except smell go there first and get routed onward."},{"id":"k-spinal-cord","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Spinal cord","mv":"The spinal cord carries movement, sensation and organ signals between brain and body. In Hughes' model it sits at the root of the \"Impulse\" lever.","tr":"The spinal cord carries movement, sensation and organ signals between brain and body. In Hughes' model it sits at the root of the \"Impulse\" lever.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 964"],"rel":["k-brain-stem"],"how":["The spinal cord carries movement, sensation and organ signals between brain and body. In Hughes' model it sits at the root of the \"Impulse\" lever."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Spinal cord”?","a":"The spinal cord carries movement, sensation and organ signals between brain and body. In Hughes' model it sits at the root of the \"Impulse\" lever."},{"id":"k-nucleus-accumbens","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Nucleus accumbens","mv":"The nucleus accumbens is central to the reward circuit. Dopamine there drives desire and anticipation, and serotonin is linked to satisfaction.","tr":"The nucleus accumbens is central to the reward circuit. Dopamine there drives desire and anticipation, and serotonin is linked to satisfaction.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 958"],"rel":["k-vta-drive"],"how":["The nucleus accumbens is central to the reward circuit. Dopamine there drives desire and anticipation, and serotonin is linked to satisfaction."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Nucleus accumbens”?","a":"The nucleus accumbens is central to the reward circuit. Dopamine there drives desire and anticipation, and serotonin is linked to satisfaction."},{"id":"k-mammalian-brain","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Mammalian brain","mv":"Hughes uses \"mammalian brain\" for the limbic system. It constantly reads others' behaviour and produces gut feelings it can't put into words.","tr":"Hughes uses \"mammalian brain\" for the limbic system. It constantly reads others' behaviour and produces gut feelings it can't put into words.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 957"],"rel":["k-limbic-system"],"how":["Hughes uses \"mammalian brain\" for the limbic system. It constantly reads others' behaviour and produces gut feelings it can't put into words."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Mammalian brain”?","a":"Hughes uses \"mammalian brain\" for the limbic system. It constantly reads others' behaviour and produces gut feelings it can't put into words."},{"id":"k-resting-potential","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Resting potential","mv":"Resting potential is the electrical imbalance across a neuron's membrane while it's at rest. Hughes uses it to explain \"residual electricity\", the idea that recently activated pathways fire more easily.","tr":"Resting potential is the electrical imbalance across a neuron's membrane while it's at rest. Hughes uses it to explain \"residual electricity\", the idea that recently activated pathways fire more easily.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 962","The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 72-73"],"how":["Resting potential is the electrical imbalance across a neuron's membrane while it's at rest. Hughes uses it to explain \"residual electricity\", the idea that recently activated pathways fire more easily."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Resting potential”?","a":"Resting potential is the electrical imbalance across a neuron's membrane while it's at rest. Hughes uses it to explain \"residual electricity\", the idea that recently activated pathways fire more easily."},{"id":"k-dacc-dmpfc","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Dorsal anterior cingulate and dmPFC","mv":"Hughes' glossary covers two brain regions. The dorsomedial prefrontal cortex (dmPFC) helps with sense of self, social impressions, empathy, morality and decisions.","tr":"Hughes' glossary covers two brain regions. The dorsomedial prefrontal cortex (dmPFC) helps with sense of self, social impressions, empathy, morality and decisions.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 952-953"],"rel":["k-neurology-overview"],"how":["Hughes' glossary covers two brain regions. The dorsomedial prefrontal cortex (dmPFC) helps with sense of self, social impressions, empathy, morality and decisions. The dorsal anterior cingulate (area 32) is linked to conflict monitoring and emotion."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Dorsal anterior cingulate and dmPFC”?","a":"Hughes' glossary covers two brain regions. The dorsomedial prefrontal cortex (dmPFC) helps with sense of self, social impressions, empathy, morality and decisions."},{"id":"k-impulse-cycle","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Impulse cycle","mv":"The impulse cycle is how an urge builds and gets acted on. Hughes uses it to explain how cravings and quick decisions bypass reasoning.","tr":"The impulse cycle is how an urge builds and gets acted on. Hughes uses it to explain how cravings and quick decisions bypass reasoning.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 965"],"how":["The impulse cycle is how an urge builds and gets acted on. Hughes uses it to explain how cravings and quick decisions bypass reasoning."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Impulse cycle”?","a":"The impulse cycle is how an urge builds and gets acted on. Hughes uses it to explain how cravings and quick decisions bypass reasoning."},{"id":"k-pleasure-rich","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Pleasure-rich, happy-poor","mv":"Pleasure-rich, happy-poor describes someone who confuses pleasure with happiness. They keep chasing pleasure hits and still feel unfulfilled.","tr":"Ask yourself whether you're chasing a hit or building something that lasts.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 960"],"how":["Pleasure-rich, happy-poor describes someone who confuses pleasure with happiness. They keep chasing pleasure hits and still feel unfulfilled."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Pleasure-rich, happy-poor”?","a":"Ask yourself whether you're chasing a hit or building something that lasts."},{"id":"k-cause-blindness","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Cause-blindness","mv":"Cause-blindness is fixing symptoms while ignoring causes.","tr":"When you want a trait, ask what causes it, not what it looks like. Then work on the cause.","w":"He says coaches and clinicians often fail because they target thoughts and ideas instead of the dopamine-driven habit loop.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 200-203","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 952"],"rel":["k-skill-placebo","k-brilliant-basics"],"how":["Cause-blindness is fixing symptoms while ignoring causes. Hughes' examples: copying the \"body language of confidence\" instead of building confidence, telling people to make their bed instead of building drive, and listing productive people's routines instead of developing focus.","He also gives policy examples, saying laws often target symptoms while deeper social decay is ignored. He says coaches and clinicians often fail because they target thoughts and ideas instead of the dopamine-driven habit loop. This ties to his warning about \"skill placebos\"."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Cause-blindness”?","a":"Cause-blindness is fixing symptoms while ignoring causes."},{"id":"k-agentic-shift","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"The agentic shift","mv":"The agentic shift is the drop in personal responsibility and critical thinking that happens around an authority figure. People start seeing themselves as an instrument of someone else's will.","tr":"The protective question is \"Would I do this if it were entirely my call?\" Watch for \"I'll take responsibility\" or \"the rules require it\" being used to override your conscience.","w":"In Milgram's setup the experimenter never shouted or threatened.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"ex":["An employee told \"legal has approved it, just send it\" stops checking whether it's honest. That's a small agentic shift."],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 106-107","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 949"],"rel":["k-hard-wired-obedience"],"how":["The agentic shift is the drop in personal responsibility and critical thinking that happens around an authority figure. People start seeing themselves as an instrument of someone else's will.","In Milgram's setup the experimenter never shouted or threatened. He used four calm prods, escalating from a polite request to continue to telling the participant they had no other choice. Experts predicted almost nobody would go to the maximum. In reality about 65% did, and Hughes notes everyone went to at least the 210-volt level. The study has been replicated many times with similar results. Participants repeatedly asked who would take responsibility, and relaxed when told the experimenter would."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “The agentic shift”?","a":"In Milgram's setup the experimenter never shouted or threatened."},{"id":"k-social-resonance","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Social resonance","mv":"Hughes compares people to pianos in a showroom. Strike middle C on one piano and the C strings on the others vibrate too.","tr":"Your calm or panic spreads to others. Use that deliberately: be the steady note in a tense room.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 109-110"],"rel":["k-hard-wired-obedience","k-bystander-effect","k-uniform-power"],"how":["Hughes compares people to pianos in a showroom. Strike middle C on one piano and the C strings on the others vibrate too. People likewise resonate with the signals of those around them, especially status symbols and uniforms.","He follows this with research on how groups and authority signals spread behaviour, including the smoke-filled room experiment, in which students stayed put while smoke filled the room because others stayed calm."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Social resonance”?","a":"Your calm or panic spreads to others. Use that deliberately: be the steady note in a tense room."},{"id":"k-smoke-room","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"The smoke-filled room experiment","mv":"In Latané and Darley's 1968 study, students filling in forms in a waiting room often failed to report smoke pouring in when others around them (actors) stayed calm. Hughes uses it to show our \"authority radar\" and crowd scripts.","tr":"In an emergency, act on what you see, not on how calm others look. Someone has to move first.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 110-111"],"rel":["k-bystander-effect","k-social-resonance"],"how":["In Latané and Darley's 1968 study, students filling in forms in a waiting room often failed to report smoke pouring in when others around them (actors) stayed calm. Hughes uses it to show our \"authority radar\" and crowd scripts.","He adds that modern life multiplies vague authority (policy, corporate rules, staff \"just following procedure\"), so it's often hard to see where authority comes from. He calls this the \"authority loophole\" at play."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The smoke-filled room experiment”?","a":"In Latané and Darley's 1968 study, students filling in forms in a waiting room often failed to report smoke pouring in when others around them (actors) stayed calm. Hughes uses it to show our \"authority radar\" and crowd scripts."},{"id":"k-crosswalk-study","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Crosswalk and suit studies","mv":"Hughes cites studies in which people were more likely to cross against the signal if someone else went first, especially when the rule-breaker wore a suit and tie. We use clothing to spot authority without thinking.","tr":"Notice when you follow someone mainly because they look official or confident.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 112-113"],"rel":["k-uniform-power","k-social-resonance"],"how":["Hughes cites studies in which people were more likely to cross against the signal if someone else went first, especially when the rule-breaker wore a suit and tie. We use clothing to spot authority without thinking."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Crosswalk and suit studies”?","a":"Hughes cites studies in which people were more likely to cross against the signal if someone else went first, especially when the rule-breaker wore a suit and tie. We use clothing to spot authority without thinking."},{"id":"k-bystander-effect","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"The bystander effect","mv":"The more people present, the less likely any one of them is to help someone in distress, because responsibility gets spread out. Hughes adds that once one person breaks the norm and helps, others follow.","tr":"Be the first to help. It gives everyone else permission. When you need help in a crowd, point at one specific person and give them a specific task.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"ex":["\"You in the blue jacket, call an ambulance\" works far better than shouting \"Someone call for help!\""],"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 113-114","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 951"],"rel":["k-smoke-room","k-social-resonance"],"how":["The more people present, the less likely any one of them is to help someone in distress, because responsibility gets spread out. Hughes adds that once one person breaks the norm and helps, others follow.","He tells the Kitty Genovese case and describes filmed experiments in which dozens of people walked past a person collapsed on steps until one man stepped in, after which others rushed to help. In one variation a man in a business suit got help within seconds, which shows how appearance changes responses."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “The bystander effect”?","a":"Be the first to help. It gives everyone else permission. When you need help in a crowd, point at one specific person and give them a specific task."},{"id":"k-uniform-power","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"The power of a uniform","mv":"Hughes describes filmed demonstrations in which a man in a police-style uniform got passers-by to guard a \"prisoner\" and even use a fake taser on him when he ran. Uniforms switch on obedience scripts almost instantly.","tr":"Treat a uniform as a claim, not proof. Ask for ID or verify through official channels before obeying anything unusual.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 114-116"],"rel":["k-agentic-shift","k-hard-wired-obedience"],"how":["Hughes describes filmed demonstrations in which a man in a police-style uniform got passers-by to guard a \"prisoner\" and even use a fake taser on him when he ran. Uniforms switch on obedience scripts almost instantly.","The \"taser\" was only a stick with a light. Ordinary people gave orders and chased the actor because a uniformed figure asked them to. He connects this to his Stanford Prison Experiment and agentic shift material."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The power of a uniform”?","a":"Hughes describes filmed demonstrations in which a man in a police-style uniform got passers-by to guard a \"prisoner\" and even use a fake taser on him when he ran. Uniforms switch on obedience scripts almost instantly."},{"id":"k-three-authority-types","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Three types of authority: social, forceful, perceived","mv":"Hughes names three kinds of authority. Social authority is built through cultural norms and relationships.","tr":"Hughes names three kinds of authority. Social authority is built through cultural norms and relationships.","w":"He opens the chapter with a description of a leader so kind, loyal and good-humoured that nobody felt superior or inferior around him, the ideal his authority traits aim at.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 116"],"rel":["k-leadership"],"how":["Hughes names three kinds of authority. Social authority is built through cultural norms and relationships. Forceful authority is backed by the power to punish. Perceived authority comes from cues such as clothing, posture and confidence. Perceived authority can be as effective as the real thing.","He opens the chapter with a description of a leader so kind, loyal and good-humoured that nobody felt superior or inferior around him, the ideal his authority traits aim at."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Three types of authority: social, forceful, perceived”?","a":"He opens the chapter with a description of a leader so kind, loyal and good-humoured that nobody felt superior or inferior around him, the ideal his authority traits aim at."},{"id":"k-charisma-weber","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Charisma and crowd psychology","mv":"Hughes quotes Max Weber's classic definition: charisma is a quality that makes someone seem extraordinary, almost superhuman, to followers.","tr":"Be wary when a group treats its leader as extraordinary and beyond question. That's where charisma turns into control.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 168"],"how":["Hughes quotes Max Weber's classic definition: charisma is a quality that makes someone seem extraordinary, almost superhuman, to followers. Weber saw charismatic figures as emotional and energising, unlike the cold authority of a bureaucrat."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Charisma and crowd psychology”?","a":"Be wary when a group treats its leader as extraordinary and beyond question. That's where charisma turns into control."},{"id":"k-unstable-leader","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"\"Humans will follow an unstable leader\"","mv":"Hughes was struck by Cesar Millan's remark that humans are the only animals that will follow an unstable leader. He takes it as proof that real followership comes from calm, balanced behaviour, not words.","tr":"Before leading others through stress, settle yourself. Your state spreads.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 148","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 154"],"rel":["k-leadership"],"how":["Hughes was struck by Cesar Millan's remark that humans are the only animals that will follow an unstable leader. He takes it as proof that real followership comes from calm, balanced behaviour, not words. It underpins his composure model."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “Humans will follow an …”","a":"“Humans will follow an unstable leader”"},{"id":"k-hard-wired-obedience","t":"ch","tp":"brain","ti":"Hard-wired for obedience","mv":"Hughes argues our brains evolved to obey authority because it kept our ancestors alive and breeding, and that brain shape hasn't changed in roughly 180,000 years.","tr":"Get into the habit of asking \"on whose authority?\" when a request is unusual.","w":"Spot it when a reaction seems bigger than the moment, in them or in you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 108-109","Chase Hughes with Mark Bowden – why our ancestors obeyed @23:30"],"rel":["k-agentic-shift","k-uniform-power","k-social-resonance"],"how":["Hughes argues our brains evolved to obey authority because it kept our ancestors alive and breeding, and that brain shape hasn't changed in roughly 180,000 years. The lab coat in Milgram carried no real power, only perceived authority, and it worked just as well.","He notes the brain doesn't check whether authority is genuine unless something obviously seems off. Milgram also tested whether the Yale setting mattered by moving to a plain office, and obedience stayed high."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Hard-wired for obedience”?","a":"Hughes argues our brains evolved to obey authority because it kept our ancestors alive and breeding, and that brain shape hasn't changed in roughly 180,000 years."},{"id":"k-followership","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Followership","mv":"Followership is a strong desire to follow someone's behaviour. Hughes says it comes from the leader's own behaviour, not from a technique.","tr":"Followership is a strong desire to follow someone's behaviour. Hughes says it comes from the leader's own behaviour, not from a technique.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 954"],"rel":["k-leadership"],"how":["Followership is a strong desire to follow someone's behaviour. Hughes says it comes from the leader's own behaviour, not from a technique."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Followership”?","a":"Followership is a strong desire to follow someone's behaviour. Hughes says it comes from the leader's own behaviour, not from a technique."},{"id":"k-veering","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"Veering","mv":"Veering is Hughes' word for a listener losing focus. Their attention drifts and distraction behaviours increase.","tr":"Veering is Hughes' word for a listener losing focus. Their attention drifts and distraction behaviours increase.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 966"],"how":["Veering is Hughes' word for a listener losing focus. Their attention drifts and distraction behaviours increase. It's your cue to change pace, tell a story or ask something."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Veering”?","a":"Veering is Hughes' word for a listener losing focus. Their attention drifts and distraction behaviours increase."},{"id":"k-imitate-communicators","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Learning from great communicators: copy their worldview","mv":"When studying great communicators, Hughes says eye contact, posture and humour are only symptoms. Study their worldview instead: how they see the world and how they see the person in front of them.","tr":"Watch a speaker you admire and write one sentence about how they seem to see their audience. Practise holding that view.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 94"],"how":["When studying great communicators, Hughes says eye contact, posture and humour are only symptoms. Study their worldview instead: how they see the world and how they see the person in front of them."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Learning from great communicators: copy their worldview”?","a":"When studying great communicators, Hughes says eye contact, posture and humour are only symptoms. Study their worldview instead: how they see the world and how they see the person in front of them."},{"id":"k-fear-speaking","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"The biggest fear: negative reaction, not public speaking","mv":"Hughes argues nobody actually fears public speaking. They fear the negative reaction they might get.","tr":"Before a talk, write down the exact reaction you fear and how likely it really is. It usually shrinks on paper.","w":"Hughes argues nobody actually fears public speaking.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 95"],"how":["Hughes argues nobody actually fears public speaking. They fear the negative reaction they might get. Like fear of heights (really fear of falling), naming the real fear makes it easier to handle.","He links this to limiting beliefs that run without your consent. Getting them out into the open strips their power, echoing his rule that a script discussed openly loses its strength."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “The biggest fear: negative reaction, not public speaking”?","a":"Before a talk, write down the exact reaction you fear and how likely it really is. It usually shrinks on paper."},{"id":"k-brilliant-basics","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Brilliant on the basics","mv":"Hughes' law 11: get brilliant on the basics while others chase \"skill placebos\". His test is that if a kindergartner couldn't understand a solution, it's probably not the right one.","tr":"Pick the weakest basic in your life (sleep, tidiness, kindness) and fix it before learning any advanced technique.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 97-99","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 12"],"rel":["k-skill-placebo","k-rock-in-shoe","k-discipline"],"how":["Hughes' law 11: get brilliant on the basics while others chase \"skill placebos\". His test is that if a kindergartner couldn't understand a solution, it's probably not the right one.","He lists childhood rules most adults don't follow: make your bed, be kind, show interest in others, sleep and wake early, exercise, eat well, take it one step at a time, don't put others down, practise, enjoy yourself, own your mood. He also says to fix points of failure first, then build on strengths. Tinting the windows won't help a failing engine."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Brilliant on the basics”?","a":"Hughes' law 11: get brilliant on the basics while others chase \"skill placebos\". His test is that if a kindergartner couldn't understand a solution, it's probably not the right one."},{"id":"k-rock-in-shoe","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"The rock in the shoe and demon exposition","mv":"The \"rock in the shoe\" is the small, nagging problem that undermines everything else. \"Demon exposition\" means dragging hidden weaknesses and fears into the open so they lose power.","tr":"List the three things you avoid thinking about (an unpaid bill, an unfinished task, a difficult conversation). Handle one this week.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 98-99"],"rel":["k-brilliant-basics","k-fear-speaking"],"how":["The \"rock in the shoe\" is the small, nagging problem that undermines everything else. \"Demon exposition\" means dragging hidden weaknesses and fears into the open so they lose power. Hughes says to deal with points of failure before polishing strengths.","His school analogy: a struggling student needs help in the failing subject, not extra credit in their best one. Hidden flaws leak into your nonverbal behaviour and reduce authority."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “rock in …”","a":"“rock in the shoe”"},{"id":"k-factors-inhibit-authority","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Factors that inhibit authority","mv":"Hughes lists behaviours that stop people seeing you as an authority.","tr":"Pick the two items you most recognise in yourself and work on those first. Slowing down fixes several at once.","w":"Hughes lists behaviours that stop people seeing you as an authority.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 123"],"how":["Hughes lists behaviours that stop people seeing you as an authority. They include negativity, putting others down, anger, poor self-control, poor hygiene, touching your face, rapid movements and speech, a hurried pace, poor posture, a fast blink rate, constant adjustments, looking rushed, loudness, selfishness, scattered attention, fear of confrontation, complaining or seeking pity, bragging, blaming, bullying, dishonesty and over-conforming."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Factors that inhibit authority”?","a":"Pick the two items you most recognise in yourself and work on those first. Slowing down fixes several at once."},{"id":"k-bate","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"BATE: the Behavior Applied To Everything","mv":"Hughes' \"BATE\" idea is that the way you do one thing is the way you do everything. Behaviour patterns carry across life areas, from paying bills to planning a holiday.","tr":"Look at one small habit (how you leave the kitchen) for clues about bigger patterns (how you leave projects). Change the small one first.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 124-126"],"rel":["k-discipline"],"how":["Hughes' \"BATE\" idea is that the way you do one thing is the way you do everything. Behaviour patterns carry across life areas, from paying bills to planning a holiday.","He admits it has exceptions, but argues most behaviour runs on social or evolutionary scripts, so patterns overlap. He suggests journalling where your own tendencies repeat, as part of building authority."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “BATE: the Behavior Applied To Everything”?","a":"Hughes' \"BATE\" idea is that the way you do one thing is the way you do everything. Behaviour patterns carry across life areas, from paying bills to planning a holiday."},{"id":"k-escalatory-pyramid","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"The escalatory pyramid (confidence ladder)","mv":"A step-by-step ladder for building social confidence through gradual discomfort.","tr":"Do one rung a day for a few weeks. Keep it kind and respectful. Compliments should be sincere, and stop if someone seems uncomfortable.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 135-136"],"how":["A step-by-step ladder for building social confidence through gradual discomfort. Say hello to a stranger, then add eye contact, then a smile, then good posture, then a compliment, longer eye contact, comfortable silence, a follow-up question, and so on.","Once a step feels easy, Hughes suggests layering in more stress: longer eye contact, longer pauses, slightly bigger requests, or topics that are a little less socially comfortable. He insists confidence is developed, not born, and calls it the most important element of persuasion."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The escalatory pyramid (confidence ladder)”?","a":"A step-by-step ladder for building social confidence through gradual discomfort."},{"id":"k-discipline","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Discipline: serving your future self","mv":"Hughes defines discipline as putting the needs of your future self ahead of the wants of your present self. He frames it as being your future self's \"butler\": prepare things so tomorrow-you is grateful.","tr":"Tonight, do one thing for tomorrow-you: lay out clothes, set the coffee, clear the desk.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 136-144","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 954"],"rel":["k-magic-pathway","k-calm-enjoyment"],"how":["Hughes defines discipline as putting the needs of your future self ahead of the wants of your present self. He frames it as being your future self's \"butler\": prepare things so tomorrow-you is grateful.","The \"disciplinarian\" vetoes the snooze button, the binge-watching and the junk food. The \"butler\" lays out tomorrow's clothes, tidies before bed, puts the phone away, saves money and keeps a journal. You only need \"a teaspoon of discipline\" to start a habit; fix one or two small behaviours at a time. Suggested habits: an earlier bedtime and wake time, staying out of the news, tidying before you work. Discipline shows in your behaviour and makes you more outgoing, because you're not carrying the \"I'm irresponsible\" reminder signal. Step two is future projection: get totally clear on goals, using vision boards and daily rehearsal."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Discipline and the future self","note":"The future-self idea has stayed constant for years; early on he paired it with a simple aged-photo exercise.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Eric Hunley podcast · 2021","text":"In 2021 Hughes defined discipline as putting your future self ahead of your present self, and said most bad habits are the present self winning.","card":"k-m-future-self"},{"label":"Updated","when":"The Behavior Ops Manual and videos","text":"Hughes defines discipline as putting the needs of your future self ahead of the wants of your present self. He frames it as being your future self's \"butler\": prepare things so tomorrow-you is grateful.","card":"k-discipline"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Discipline: serving your future self”?","a":"Tonight, do one thing for tomorrow-you: lay out clothes, set the coffee, clear the desk."},{"id":"k-calm-enjoyment","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Calm-enjoyment","mv":"Hughes says the one universal trait he's seen in successful people is \"calm-enjoyment\": quiet pleasure in getting done what has to be done, without rushing or overwhelm.","tr":"Slow down on necessary tasks and find something to enjoy in doing them well.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 141"],"rel":["k-discipline"],"how":["Hughes says the one universal trait he's seen in successful people is \"calm-enjoyment\": quiet pleasure in getting done what has to be done, without rushing or overwhelm. Unsuccessful people tend to neglect what must be done and work in a frantic, scattered way."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Calm-enjoyment”?","a":"Hughes says the one universal trait he's seen in successful people is \"calm-enjoyment\": quiet pleasure in getting done what has to be done, without rushing or overwhelm."},{"id":"k-leadership","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Leadership (calm-assertive, know your pack)","mv":"Hughes defines leadership as the trait that automatically triggers people's wiring to follow someone. It's marked by above-average composure and enjoyment and an absence of judgement.","tr":"Lead by being the calmest, least judgemental person in the room, setting clear boundaries kindly, and learning what each person on your team needs.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 144-148","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 959"],"how":["Hughes defines leadership as the trait that automatically triggers people's wiring to follow someone. It's marked by above-average composure and enjoyment and an absence of judgement. Its true capacity shows when the person is under stress in front of others.","He borrows from dog trainer Cesar Millan: the mother dog sets limits on food and play, leaders project calm-assertive energy, set rules, boundaries and limits, correct at the right moment, and \"know their pack\", meaning what fulfils each member. Millan's list for a true pack leader: honest, real, accepting, in touch, present, respectful, balanced. Followership, in his glossary, is the natural by-product of good leadership and authority, not of technique."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Leadership (calm-assertive, know your pack)”?","a":"Hughes defines leadership as the trait that automatically triggers people's wiring to follow someone. It's marked by above-average composure and enjoyment and an absence of judgement."},{"id":"k-roots-of-anxiety","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"The roots of anxiety","mv":"In Hughes' opinion anxiety grows from three things: a decline in your reputation with yourself, not recognising what you can and can't control, and unmet expectations, or the fear of them going unmet.","tr":"When anxious, write two columns, \"in my control\" and \"not in my control\", and act only on the first.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 162-163"],"how":["In Hughes' opinion anxiety grows from three things: a decline in your reputation with yourself, not recognising what you can and can't control, and unmet expectations, or the fear of them going unmet.","His fixes: dress the part, keep a \"resume notecard\" of your accomplishments to read before challenges, and rehearse upcoming events, because rehearsal gives your body a preview so you become a creator instead of a worrier. He says monitoring a behaviour is the first step to controlling it, and to start with awareness and rehearsal. Composed leaders meet stress with the lens \"this is part of life.\""]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “The roots of anxiety”?","a":"When anxious, write two columns, \"in my control\" and \"not in my control\", and act only on the first."},{"id":"k-yt-default-leader","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Becoming the \"default leader\": stop justifying","mv":"Hughes says that justifying yourself makes you shrink, and many of us do it for \"crumbs of approval\". Each time you hold back an unnecessary explanation, you build authority and self-respect \"muscle\".","tr":"Next time you're about to give three reasons for a simple decision, give none and see how it lands.","w":"Praise from others can feel nice but should never change how you see yourself.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Become the DEFAULT Leader @2:00"],"rel":["k-yt-conflict-frame"],"how":["Hughes says that justifying yourself makes you shrink, and many of us do it for \"crumbs of approval\". Each time you hold back an unnecessary explanation, you build authority and self-respect \"muscle\".","He insists it's clarity, not coldness. Praise from others can feel nice but should never change how you see yourself."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Becoming the \"default leader\": stop justifying”?","a":"Hughes says that justifying yourself makes you shrink, and many of us do it for \"crumbs of approval\". Each time you hold back an unnecessary explanation, you build authority and self-respect \"muscle\"."},{"id":"k-yt-authority-recipe","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"The recipe for authority (video)","mv":"In this video, Hughes says you're always creating gut feelings in others, so have a clear, good intention. He advises relaxed but upright posture, being in control of yourself and the composure pendulum.","tr":"In this video, Hughes says you're always creating gut feelings in others, so have a clear, good intention. He advises relaxed but upright posture, being in control of yourself and the composure pendulum.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – The EXACT Recipe for Authority @1:40","Composure pendulum @6:40"],"how":["In this video, Hughes says you're always creating gut feelings in others, so have a clear, good intention. He advises relaxed but upright posture, being in control of yourself and the composure pendulum. Composure sits in the middle, between posturing on one side and collapse on the other."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The recipe for authority (video)”?","a":"In this video, Hughes says you're always creating gut feelings in others, so have a clear, good intention. He advises relaxed but upright posture, being in control of yourself and the composure pendulum."},{"id":"k-yt-confidence-permission","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Confidence as permission","mv":"Hughes says confident people hold an internal belief that they have permission to act a certain way, and their lack of hesitation gives others permission too. \"We're more in the business of permission than persuasion.\"","tr":"Hughes says confident people hold an internal belief that they have permission to act a certain way, and their lack of hesitation gives others permission too. \"We're more in the business of permission than persuasion.\"","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – How Confidence Gives People an Unfair Advantage @3:40"],"how":["Hughes says confident people hold an internal belief that they have permission to act a certain way, and their lack of hesitation gives others permission too. \"We're more in the business of permission than persuasion.\""]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “We're more in the business of …”","a":"“We're more in the business of permission than persuasion.”"},{"id":"k-yt-five-authority","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Five things destroying your authority (video)","mv":"In this talk Hughes starts with confidence, which he says mostly means feeling safe, knowing there are no threats you can't handle. He recommends doing tiny things for your future self every day to build that.","tr":"In this talk Hughes starts with confidence, which he says mostly means feeling safe, knowing there are no threats you can't handle. He recommends doing tiny things for your future self every day to build that.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Fix these FIVE things DESTROYING your AUTHORITY @3:20"],"how":["In this talk Hughes starts with confidence, which he says mostly means feeling safe, knowing there are no threats you can't handle. He recommends doing tiny things for your future self every day to build that. He links calm and gratitude practices with lowering stress chemistry."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Five things destroying your authority (video)”?","a":"In this talk Hughes starts with confidence, which he says mostly means feeling safe, knowing there are no threats you can't handle. He recommends doing tiny things for your future self every day to build that."},{"id":"k-yt-confidence-543","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Confidence in 5:43: self-forgiveness and not outsourcing self-worth","mv":"Hughes' short confidence recipe has three parts. Be radically self-forgiving, since people try to build confidence while carrying old guilt.","tr":"Say his three commitments to yourself: forgive fast, choose roles on purpose, don't wait for permission.","w":"Stop \"subcontracting\" your self-belief to strangers' reactions.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Confidence Fixed in Exactly 05:43 @1:40"],"rel":["k-yt-default-leader"],"how":["Hughes' short confidence recipe has three parts. Be radically self-forgiving, since people try to build confidence while carrying old guilt. Stop \"subcontracting\" your self-belief to strangers' reactions. Choose your roles deliberately, and never wait for social permission."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Confidence in 5:43: self-forgiveness and not outsourcing self-worth”?","a":"Stop \"subcontracting\" your self-belief to strangers' reactions."},{"id":"k-yt-first-seconds","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"The first seconds: do you look like you need something?","mv":"Hughes says influence starts before you speak, in the other person's nervous system. In the opening seconds the biggest signal is whether you look like you need something from the interaction.","tr":"Arrive slower than usual, keep movements unhurried and don't open with a request.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – 90-Second Brain Capture @0:00","Looking like you need something @2:00"],"rel":["k-yt-xba-room","k-bate"],"how":["Hughes says influence starts before you speak, in the other person's nervous system. In the opening seconds the biggest signal is whether you look like you need something from the interaction. A calm, still person who isn't needy reads as authority.","He describes the next 10–30 seconds as a \"novelty break\": something unexpected but grounded that loosens the other person's script. When people sense you see them clearly without fear or judgement, they lean in."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “The first seconds: do you look like you need something?”?","a":"Arrive slower than usual, keep movements unhurried and don't open with a request."},{"id":"k-yt-confidence-worldview","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Confidence as a worldview, not a behaviour","mv":"Hughes describes real confidence as a way of seeing the world, not a set of behaviours. It grows when you stop looking for your reflection in other people's approval and start deciding for yourself.","tr":"Pick one small decision today and make it without asking anyone's opinion.","w":"Hughes describes real confidence as a way of seeing the world, not a set of behaviours.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – The Hidden Exit @0:00","Decide, don't seek mirrors @6:00"],"rel":["k-yt-confidence-543"],"how":["Hughes describes real confidence as a way of seeing the world, not a set of behaviours. It grows when you stop looking for your reflection in other people's approval and start deciding for yourself.","He notes that the better you get at something, the fewer people understand what you're doing. More compliments and feedback won't build confidence. Making your own decisions will."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Confidence as a worldview, not a behaviour”?","a":"Hughes describes real confidence as a way of seeing the world, not a set of behaviours. It grows when you stop looking for your reflection in other people's approval and start deciding for yourself."},{"id":"k-yt-default-state","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Calm and confidence are default states","mv":"Hughes says most states worth having, confidence and calm included, are already there by default. They show up when interference stops, not when you pile on more techniques.","tr":"When you feel tense, ask \"What am I adding?\" rather than \"What do I need to add?\"","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – default states @15:00"],"rel":["k-yt-remove-reasons"],"how":["Hughes says most states worth having, confidence and calm included, are already there by default. They show up when interference stops, not when you pile on more techniques.","He contrasts this with the habit of adding new tactics. The work is spotting and removing what gets in the way, such as old fears or the need for approval."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Calm and confidence are default states”?","a":"Hughes says most states worth having, confidence and calm included, are already there by default. They show up when interference stops, not when you pile on more techniques."},{"id":"k-skill-placebo","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Skill placebo and the \"magic pill mirage\"","mv":"The skill placebo is the comforting feeling that you have a skill because you have the information. Hughes' son thought Minecraft taught him to survive in the wild.","tr":"After reading about a skill, test it live. Failure tells you where to practise.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 937-940"],"rel":["k-knowledge-vs-skill","k-brilliant-basics"],"how":["The skill placebo is the comforting feeling that you have a skill because you have the information. Hughes' son thought Minecraft taught him to survive in the wild. The related \"magic pill\" trap is learning a few techniques and assuming authority, confidence and composure aren't needed."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Skill placebo and the \"magic pill mirage\"”?","a":"After reading about a skill, test it live. Failure tells you where to practise."},{"id":"k-habit-timelines","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Habit timelines","mv":"Hughes gives rough timelines for change. A psychological pattern takes about 90 days, a physiological one 40, a behavioural habit 21 and a specific skill about 12.","tr":"Track one habit every day for three weeks, and practise one phrase with real people for twelve days.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual pp. 945-946"],"rel":["k-discipline"],"how":["Hughes gives rough timelines for change. A psychological pattern takes about 90 days, a physiological one 40, a behavioural habit 21 and a specific skill about 12. Behavioural habits mostly change through rigorous daily monitoring.","For skills he prescribes heavy repetition with real people, up to hundreds of reps a day. For behavioural habits (authority traits like confidence, discipline and gratitude) an accountability partner helps. His psychological and physiological recommendations involve medical-type interventions this site doesn't repeat. Talk to a doctor about anything like that."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Habit timelines”?","a":"Hughes gives rough timelines for change. A psychological pattern takes about 90 days, a physiological one 40, a behavioural habit 21 and a specific skill about 12."},{"id":"k-magic-pathway","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"The magic pathway to habit","mv":"Hughes' habit formula: Desire, then Dopamine, then Reward, then Belief, adds up to a Habit. He notes many people with addictions are ordinary people whose reward circuits got out of their control.","tr":"Build good habits deliberately: want it, make the first steps rewarding, then notice and believe the evidence that you're \"someone who does this\".","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 142"],"rel":["k-discipline","k-vta-drive","k-habit-timelines"],"how":["Hughes' habit formula: Desire, then Dopamine, then Reward, then Belief, adds up to a Habit. He notes many people with addictions are ordinary people whose reward circuits got out of their control."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The magic pathway to habit”?","a":"Hughes' habit formula: Desire, then Dopamine, then Reward, then Belief, adds up to a Habit. He notes many people with addictions are ordinary people whose reward circuits got out of their control."},{"id":"k-pod-habits-byproducts","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Habits, not goals: plan the by-products (podcast)","mv":"Hughes told Diary of a CEO that life is about habits, not goals. Decide what by-products you want this year, then build the small habits that produce them, starting small.","tr":"Hughes told Diary of a CEO that life is about habits, not goals. Decide what by-products you want this year, then build the small habits that produce them, starting small.","w":"He noted most New Year's resolutions don't stick.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Diary of a CEO – tricks for discipline @1:38:32","Chase Hughes – goals as by-products @2:00"],"rel":["k-discipline","k-habit-timelines"],"how":["Hughes told Diary of a CEO that life is about habits, not goals. Decide what by-products you want this year, then build the small habits that produce them, starting small. He noted most New Year's resolutions don't stick."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Habits, not goals: plan the by-products (podcast)”?","a":"Hughes told Diary of a CEO that life is about habits, not goals. Decide what by-products you want this year, then build the small habits that produce them, starting small."},{"id":"k-yt-flattened","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"The \"flattened\" person: attention without depth","mv":"Hughes describes a \"flattened\" person whose attention has nowhere deep to go, like staring at a dead-end wall. Little time outdoors and few encounters with things bigger than us shrink perception.","tr":"Once a week, spend ten minutes with something old and well made, or outdoors, with your phone away.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – What is a 'FLATTENED Person'? @0:00","Stand in front of crafted things @17:30"],"how":["Hughes describes a \"flattened\" person whose attention has nowhere deep to go, like staring at a dead-end wall. Little time outdoors and few encounters with things bigger than us shrink perception.","His remedy is deliberate exposure to depth: time in nature, and standing in front of something carefully made by hand, like an old building's doorway or metalwork, and noticing the care in it."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “person: attention without depth Hughes …”","a":"“ person: attention without depth Hughes describes a ”"},{"id":"k-yt-predictability-change","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"The brain values predictability over success","mv":"Hughes says the brain prefers predictability to success, which is why people slide back into familiar patterns even when they hurt. He calls willpower as a fixed trait a myth.","tr":"Make the new behaviour the predictable one: same time, same place, no decision needed.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – predictability over success @6:00","Identity distance from the old self @8:00"],"rel":["k-habit-timelines","k-yt-default-state"],"how":["Hughes says the brain prefers predictability to success, which is why people slide back into familiar patterns even when they hurt. He calls willpower as a fixed trait a myth.","He frames habits as an identity that produces predictable behaviour, which is why change can feel like a threat to belonging. His practical levers: routines at fixed times, fewer choices during stressful periods, and treating the old behaviour as no longer who you are. For anything to do with brain chemistry or supplements, talk to a doctor."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The brain values predictability over success”?","a":"Hughes says the brain prefers predictability to success, which is why people slide back into familiar patterns even when they hurt. He calls willpower as a fixed trait a myth."},{"id":"k-yt-guilt-shame","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Guilt and shame keep you stuck","mv":"Hughes argues guilt can become a self-centred loop that keeps you replaying the past, and carrying shame doesn't make you virtuous. Real power starts with power over yourself.","tr":"Name the mistake, the lesson and the repair, then let the loop close.","w":"He says others tend to follow once you stop holding it against yourself.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – guilt as a loop @2:00","Shame is not virtue @8:00"],"rel":["k-yt-core-shame","k-yt-emotional-debt"],"how":["Hughes argues guilt can become a self-centred loop that keeps you replaying the past, and carrying shame doesn't make you virtuous. Real power starts with power over yourself.","He walks viewers through forgiving themselves out loud. He says others tend to follow once you stop holding it against yourself."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Guilt and shame keep you stuck”?","a":"Name the mistake, the lesson and the repair, then let the loop close."},{"id":"k-yt-insert-pause","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Insert a pause between trigger and reaction","mv":"Hughes says your freedom lives in the gap between a trigger and your reaction. Many adult decisions still run on old wounds, like a bullied child's hurt.","tr":"When you feel the flinch, breathe out slowly before you say anything.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – insert the pause @4:00"],"rel":["k-yt-argument-ghost","k-yt-default-state"],"how":["Hughes says your freedom lives in the gap between a trigger and your reaction. Many adult decisions still run on old wounds, like a bullied child's hurt. Deliberately inserting a pause is how you take that gap back.","He describes the small \"flinch\" you feel when something lands as recognition of an old pattern, not new information."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Insert a pause between trigger and reaction”?","a":"Hughes says your freedom lives in the gap between a trigger and your reaction. Many adult decisions still run on old wounds, like a bullied child's hurt."},{"id":"k-yt-anti-vision","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Write your anti-vision and fight entropy","mv":"In his \"Earth as a video game\" talk, Hughes says top players write their own quests instead of waiting for the game to assign them.","tr":"Write a one-page anti-vision, then set decade, year and month checkpoints.","w":"In his \"Earth as a video game\" talk, Hughes says top players write their own quests instead of waiting for the game to assign them.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – background programs and entropy @4:00","The anti-vision @10:00"],"rel":["k-discipline","k-pod-habits-byproducts","k-yt-predictability-change"],"how":["In his \"Earth as a video game\" talk, Hughes says top players write their own quests instead of waiting for the game to assign them. Almost nobody writes an anti-vision, the future you absolutely don't want, even though it's a strong motivator.","He warns about \"background programs\" (unfinished tasks, emotional baggage, idle habits) and entropy: systems drift unless you keep revisiting them."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"Finish the line: “Earth as a …”","a":"“Earth as a video game”"},{"id":"k-yt-discipline-future","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Discipline as living ahead of yourself","mv":"Hughes defines discipline as your mind being in the future: you put the needs of the person you'll be in five years ahead of what you want right now. Before you eat, work or spend, ask how it will affect that future you.","tr":"Pick one daily decision and ask \"what would five-years-from-now me choose?\" before you make it.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – getting ahead of yourself @1:00","Directing your attention @3:00","Chase Hughes – future-self neglect @5:00","Discipline only starts a habit @7:00"],"rel":["k-ras","k-habit-timelines"],"how":["Hughes defines discipline as your mind being in the future: you put the needs of the person you'll be in five years ahead of what you want right now. Before you eat, work or spend, ask how it will affect that future you.","His second lever is attention. Whatever you keep telling yourself to look for, your brain starts finding, like spotting your new car model everywhere. A steady diet of negative input trains it to hunt for more of the same, so choose your inputs on purpose. He also says goals are hard to reach if your body is run down; for anything about diet or health, talk to a doctor."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Discipline as living ahead of yourself”?","a":"Pick one daily decision and ask \"what would five-years-from-now me choose?\" before you make it."},{"id":"k-knowledge-vs-skill","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Knowledge vs skill","mv":"Hughes repeats that rehearsing in your head gives you knowledge, but only practice with real people builds skill. Holding a 737 flight manual doesn't make you a pilot.","tr":"Practise each technique with real humans in low-stakes settings until it's automatic.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Ops Manual p. 448","The Behavior Ops Manual p. 939"],"rel":["k-skill-placebo","k-habit-timelines"],"how":["Hughes repeats that rehearsing in your head gives you knowledge, but only practice with real people builds skill. Holding a 737 flight manual doesn't make you a pilot."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Knowledge vs skill”?","a":"Hughes repeats that rehearsing in your head gives you knowledge, but only practice with real people builds skill. Holding a 737 flight manual doesn't make you a pilot."},{"id":"k-nci-levels","t":"ch","tp":"teachers","ti":"NCI University levels","mv":"NCI University is Hughes' training institution with four tiers. NCI-1 Behavior Observer covers nonverbals and baselining, and NCI-2 Behavior Profiler covers patterns and motivations.","tr":"NCI University is Hughes' training institution with four tiers. NCI-1 Behavior Observer covers nonverbals and baselining, and NCI-2 Behavior Profiler covers patterns and motivations.","w":"Treat it as a coach's view: test it in real life and keep what works.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"how":["NCI University is Hughes' training institution with four tiers. NCI-1 Behavior Observer covers nonverbals and baselining, and NCI-2 Behavior Profiler covers patterns and motivations. NCI-3 Behavior Scientist covers applied influence, and NCI-4 Author & Elite Operator is the full library plus live training.","This site isn't affiliated. It's just a study aid built from his book and public material."],"go":[{"tab":"watch","label":"Watch Hughes read real people"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “NCI University levels”?","a":"NCI University is Hughes' training institution with four tiers. NCI-1 Behavior Observer covers nonverbals and baselining, and NCI-2 Behavior Profiler covers patterns and motivations."},{"id":"k-behavior-panel","t":"ch","tp":"teachers","ti":"The Behavior Panel","mv":"The Behavior Panel is a YouTube body-language analysis show. Chase Hughes appears alongside other analysts, including Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden (whose \"truth plane\" Hughes cites).","tr":"The Behavior Panel is a YouTube body-language analysis show. Chase Hughes appears alongside other analysts, including Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden (whose \"truth plane\" Hughes cites).","w":"Treat it as a coach's view: test it in real life and keep what works.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel on YouTube"],"how":["The Behavior Panel is a YouTube body-language analysis show. Chase Hughes appears alongside other analysts, including Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden (whose \"truth plane\" Hughes cites). This assistant uses only Hughes' own material, not the other panellists'."],"go":[{"tab":"watch","label":"Watch Hughes read real people"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The Behavior Panel”?","a":"The Behavior Panel is a YouTube body-language analysis show. Chase Hughes appears alongside other analysts, including Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden (whose \"truth plane\" Hughes cites)."},{"id":"k-m-ethics-three","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Three questions before you influence anyone","mv":"In a 2025 interview Hughes gave a three-step check: Am I hiding my intent? Do I genuinely feel I'm helping this person?","tr":"Before a big ask, run the three questions honestly.","w":"If any answer is wrong, stop.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["WiM Media interview (2025): the three-question check @1:09:06"],"rel":["k-ethics"],"how":["In a 2025 interview Hughes gave a three-step check: Am I hiding my intent? Do I genuinely feel I'm helping this person? If they could see inside my head and see the outcome, would they still choose the same thing? If any answer is wrong, stop.","He repeats his scalpel idea: the ethics are never in the tool, always in the person using it. He adds that studying how influence works is also the best protection, because you notice it happening to you."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Three questions before you influence anyone”?","a":"In a 2025 interview Hughes gave a three-step check: Am I hiding my intent? Do I genuinely feel I'm helping this person?"},{"id":"k-m-old-broadcast","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"The inner critic is an old broadcast","mv":"Hughes says confident and anxious people hear the same \"you're not enough\" voice; the difference is knowing it's fiction. If you know the disaster on the radio is a drama, your heart stays calm.","tr":"When the voice starts, label it: \"That's the old broadcast,\" then carry on.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["WiM Media interview (2025): the inner voice @0:09:42"],"rel":["k-m-same-with-everyone"],"how":["Hughes says confident and anxious people hear the same \"you're not enough\" voice; the difference is knowing it's fiction. If you know the disaster on the radio is a drama, your heart stays calm. He says even he still hears it, and it often sounds like a much younger you."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The inner critic is an old broadcast”?","a":"Hughes says confident and anxious people hear the same \"you're not enough\" voice; the difference is knowing it's fiction. If you know the disaster on the radio is a drama, your heart stays calm."},{"id":"k-m-same-with-everyone","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Treat a CEO and a barista the same","mv":"The mistake Hughes says sinks most people is ranking yourself above or below the person in front of you.","tr":"Tonight, score today's composure out of 10 and note one moment you rushed.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Doug Bopst podcast (2025): the hierarchy mistake @0:19:46"],"rel":["k-m-old-broadcast"],"how":["The mistake Hughes says sinks most people is ranking yourself above or below the person in front of you. His fix: agree to treat everyone the same, be the slowest and most relaxed person in the conversation, and score your composure from 1 to 10 each night (did you collapse, or posture?).","He calls composure a lifelong practice that starts in the body: slowness and relaxation first, then the mind follows. A big client and the person making your coffee get the same calm you."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Composure","note":"The pendulum (collapse, composure, posturing) gained a daily practice: treat everyone the same and score yourself each night.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"The Behavior Ops Manual","text":"Composure rating scale and pendulum; \"this is part of life\".","card":"h30"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Doug Bopst podcast · 2025","text":"The mistake Hughes says sinks most people is ranking yourself above or below the person in front of you.","card":"k-m-same-with-everyone"}]}],"vb":"2 versions","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Treat a CEO and a barista the same”?","a":"Tonight, score today's composure out of 10 and note one moment you rushed."},{"id":"k-m-connect-not-analyse","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Connect first, analyse only when it matters","mv":"Hughes says constantly reading people in everyday life pulls you back behind your eyes and out of the conversation.","tr":"Tonight, pick a TV interview and match the guest's posture and breathing for 30 seconds.","w":"He keeps careful baselining for high-stakes moments, like choosing someone to look after your children, and says to connect human to human the rest of the time.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Doug Bopst podcast (2026): practise on video, connect in person @0:04:48"],"rel":["k-m-joy-at-news"],"how":["Hughes says constantly reading people in everyday life pulls you back behind your eyes and out of the conversation. He keeps careful baselining for high-stakes moments, like choosing someone to look after your children, and says to connect human to human the rest of the time.","To practise, he suggests videos: copy an interviewee's posture and breathing pace and ask how it would feel to be them right now. Do this with people on screen, not with the person in front of you."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Connect first, analyse only when it matters”?","a":"Hughes says constantly reading people in everyday life pulls you back behind your eyes and out of the conversation."},{"id":"k-m-joy-at-news","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Notice joy, or its absence, at your good news","mv":"Hughes says one of the most telling moments is when you share a win. A friend who is glad for you shows real joy; notice instead a flat \"oh, cool\" or a quick one-sided smirk.","tr":"Next time you share good news, watch the first second of their face, then just keep talking normally.","w":"Treat it as one clue to explore, never proof on its own.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Doug Bopst podcast (2026): joy versus contempt @0:33:31"],"rel":["k-m-connect-not-analyse"],"how":["Hughes says one of the most telling moments is when you share a win. A friend who is glad for you shows real joy; notice instead a flat \"oh, cool\" or a quick one-sided smirk. Treat it as one clue to explore, never proof on its own.","He links it to relationship research where contempt was one of the strongest warning signs between partners."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Notice joy, or its absence, at your good news”?","a":"Treat it as one clue to explore, never proof on its own."},{"id":"k-m-words-matter","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Words still carry the message (the 93% myth)","mv":"Hughes pushes back on the popular claim that 93% of communication is nonverbal. You can't follow a film in a language you don't speak, and the original researcher never meant it that way.","tr":"When you read someone, listen to the exact words first, then check the body against them.","w":"You can't follow a film in a language you don't speak, and the original researcher never meant it that way.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Doug Bopst podcast (2026): the 93% myth @0:07:05"],"rel":["k-m-connect-not-analyse"],"how":["Hughes pushes back on the popular claim that 93% of communication is nonverbal. You can't follow a film in a language you don't speak, and the original researcher never meant it that way. Body language adds to words; it doesn't replace them."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Words, scripts and who you are","note":"Two sides of one idea: words matter, but no script works without the person behind it.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Courtroom Confidential podcast · 2025","text":"Hughes jokes about \"Harry Potter believers\": people hunting for the perfect spell of words. Hand a flawless script to someone frozen by anxiety and they'll get the same results as everyone else.","card":"k-m-no-magic-script"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Doug Bopst podcast · 2026","text":"Hughes pushes back on the popular claim that 93% of communication is nonverbal. You can't follow a film in a language you don't speak, and the original researcher never meant it that way.","card":"k-m-words-matter"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Words still carry the message (the 93% myth)”?","a":"When you read someone, listen to the exact words first, then check the body against them."},{"id":"k-m-skill-of-boredom","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Practise being bored","mv":"Hughes asks: can you let yourself be bored for a while? That's what meditation trains.","tr":"Sit for five minutes today with no phone and nothing to do.","w":"The adrenaline may still come, but if you don't see it as a threat, it stops running you.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Doug Bopst podcast (2026): the skill of being bored @0:55:01"],"rel":["k-m-same-with-everyone"],"how":["Hughes asks: can you let yourself be bored for a while? That's what meditation trains. He argues most emotional control isn't a technique; it's a change of perspective. The adrenaline may still come, but if you don't see it as a threat, it stops running you."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Practise being bored”?","a":"Hughes asks: can you let yourself be bored for a while? That's what meditation trains."},{"id":"k-m-future-self","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Meet your future self","mv":"In 2021 Hughes defined discipline as putting your future self ahead of your present self, and said most bad habits are the present self winning.","tr":"Before a choice you usually regret, ask \"What would 80-year-old me want here?\"","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Eric Hunley podcast (2021): discipline and the future self @0:04:31"],"rel":["k-m-skill-of-boredom"],"how":["In 2021 Hughes defined discipline as putting your future self ahead of your present self, and said most bad habits are the present self winning. One idea he gave: look at an aged photo of yourself (apps can make one) and ask what that person needs from you today."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Discipline and the future self","note":"The future-self idea has stayed constant for years; early on he paired it with a simple aged-photo exercise.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Eric Hunley podcast · 2021","text":"In 2021 Hughes defined discipline as putting your future self ahead of your present self, and said most bad habits are the present self winning.","card":"k-m-future-self"},{"label":"Updated","when":"The Behavior Ops Manual and videos","text":"Hughes defines discipline as putting the needs of your future self ahead of the wants of your present self. He frames it as being your future self's \"butler\": prepare things so tomorrow-you is grateful.","card":"k-discipline"}]},{"title":"The future self","note":"The future-self exercise gained a reason: the brain stores imagined futures much like memories.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Eric Hunley podcast · 2021","text":"In 2021 Hughes defined discipline as putting your future self ahead of your present self, and said most bad habits are the present self winning.","card":"k-m-future-self"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Chase Hughes video · Jan 2026","text":"In a 2026 video Hughes points to research showing the brain imagines the future using the same networks it uses to remember the past.","card":"k-m-future-memory"}]}],"vb":"2021 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Meet your future self”?","a":"In 2021 Hughes defined discipline as putting your future self ahead of your present self, and said most bad habits are the present self winning."},{"id":"k-m-causes-not-symptoms","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Work on causes, not symptoms","mv":"When success isn't coming, Hughes says we often chase the symptoms (money, status, recognition) instead of working on the causes. The world will never be fully fair, but if you keep working on the causes, results tend to follow.","tr":"Write down one result you want, then the daily cause behind it, and do that cause today.","w":"When success isn't coming, Hughes says we often chase the symptoms (money, status, recognition) instead of working on the causes.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Doug Bopst podcast (2026): causes, not symptoms @0:08:28"],"rel":["k-m-future-self"],"how":["When success isn't coming, Hughes says we often chase the symptoms (money, status, recognition) instead of working on the causes. The world will never be fully fair, but if you keep working on the causes, results tend to follow."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Work on causes, not symptoms”?","a":"Write down one result you want, then the daily cause behind it, and do that cause today."},{"id":"k-m-no-magic-script","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"No script is a magic spell","mv":"Hughes jokes about \"Harry Potter believers\": people hunting for the perfect spell of words. Hand a flawless script to someone frozen by anxiety and they'll get the same results as everyone else.","tr":"Next time you prepare for a hard call, spend half the time on your state (slow, relaxed, clear aim) and half on what you'll say.","w":"Spot it when someone's behaviour puzzles you. There is usually a reason.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Courtroom Confidential podcast (2025): Harry Potter believers @0:18:11"],"rel":["k-m-causes-not-symptoms"],"how":["Hughes jokes about \"Harry Potter believers\": people hunting for the perfect spell of words. Hand a flawless script to someone frozen by anxiety and they'll get the same results as everyone else. Who you are and how you carry it matters more than the exact wording.","He links this to his point about causes and symptoms: work on your belief and composure, then the words come out better on their own."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"Words, scripts and who you are","note":"Two sides of one idea: words matter, but no script works without the person behind it.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Courtroom Confidential podcast · 2025","text":"Hughes jokes about \"Harry Potter believers\": people hunting for the perfect spell of words. Hand a flawless script to someone frozen by anxiety and they'll get the same results as everyone else.","card":"k-m-no-magic-script"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Doug Bopst podcast · 2026","text":"Hughes pushes back on the popular claim that 93% of communication is nonverbal. You can't follow a film in a language you don't speak, and the original researcher never meant it that way.","card":"k-m-words-matter"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “No script is a magic spell”?","a":"Hughes jokes about \"Harry Potter believers\": people hunting for the perfect spell of words. Hand a flawless script to someone frozen by anxiety and they'll get the same results as everyone else."},{"id":"k-m-use-it-today","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Knowing a framework isn't using it","mv":"Hughes says people write a framework on a sticky note and call it learned. Understanding it means little; using it, getting feedback and using it again is how you master it.","tr":"Pick one idea from today and use it in a real conversation before bedtime, then note what happened.","w":"Skip the fine details until you've tried it for real.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Breakthrough Podcast (2024): use it, get feedback @0:04:41"],"rel":["k-m-skill-of-boredom"],"how":["Hughes says people write a framework on a sticky note and call it learned. Understanding it means little; using it, getting feedback and using it again is how you master it. Skip the fine details until you've tried it for real."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Knowing a framework isn't using it”?","a":"Skip the fine details until you've tried it for real."},{"id":"k-m-first-stair","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Take the stair in front of you","mv":"Hughes compares self-improvement to a staircase: we stare at the top wondering how to hack our way up, ignoring the step an inch from our foot. Diet, fitness and people skills all work the same way.","tr":"Name the smallest next step in the skill you're working on and do it today.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Pete A Turner podcast (2021): the staircase @0:51:12"],"rel":["k-m-use-it-today"],"how":["Hughes compares self-improvement to a staircase: we stare at the top wondering how to hack our way up, ignoring the step an inch from our foot. Diet, fitness and people skills all work the same way. Do the next step properly."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Take the stair in front of you”?","a":"Name the smallest next step in the skill you're working on and do it today."},{"id":"k-m-authority-not-rank","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Authority isn't rank","mv":"Hughes says the biggest mistake is reading authority as status. You can be the most junior person at work and still carry real authority, built from confidence, discipline, leadership, gratitude and enjoyment.","tr":"In your next meeting with someone senior, keep your pace slow and your manner warm, and drop any urge to shrink.","w":"Spot it when your shoulders rise, your pace speeds up, or you start over-explaining.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Easy Prey podcast (2023): authority vs hierarchy @0:37:07"],"rel":["k-m-same-with-everyone"],"how":["Hughes says the biggest mistake is reading authority as status. You can be the most junior person at work and still carry real authority, built from confidence, discipline, leadership, gratitude and enjoyment. You can be more confident than your boss and still be respectful.","He separates a leader from the person in charge: one is earned from the inside, the other is a job title."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Authority isn't rank”?","a":"Hughes says the biggest mistake is reading authority as status. You can be the most junior person at work and still carry real authority, built from confidence, discipline, leadership, gratitude and enjoyment."},{"id":"k-m-show-dont-tell","t":"ch","tp":"stories","ti":"Paint the picture, don't label it","mv":"Hughes says people decide with the older, image-driven part of the brain, so give it pictures. Instead of \"I was stressed\", describe the clenched fists and sweat-soaked shirt.","tr":"In your next story, swap one label (stressed, messy, confident) for what you actually saw.","w":"Instead of \"I was stressed\", describe the clenched fists and sweat-soaked shirt.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["356labs podcast (2020): vivid images @0:20:42"],"how":["Hughes says people decide with the older, image-driven part of the brain, so give it pictures. Instead of \"I was stressed\", describe the clenched fists and sweat-soaked shirt. Instead of saying someone was confident, show them striding in while heads turned."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Paint the picture, don't label it”?","a":"Instead of \"I was stressed\", describe the clenched fists and sweat-soaked shirt."},{"id":"k-m-do-right","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Do right instead of being right","mv":"For heated family topics, Hughes suggests choosing to do right over being right. Find common ground fast, thank them for sharing their view, and ask to pick it up another time.","tr":"Have one sentence ready: \"We agree on this much. Thanks for telling me. Can we save the rest for another day?\"","w":"Spot it when voices rise, or someone suddenly goes quiet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Dr. Phil Podcast (2025): do right, not be right @0:28:34"],"how":["For heated family topics, Hughes suggests choosing to do right over being right. Find common ground fast, thank them for sharing their view, and ask to pick it up another time. If it really matters it will still matter later. You can dislike someone's view and still love them."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Do right instead of being right”?","a":"Have one sentence ready: \"We agree on this much. Thanks for telling me. Can we save the rest for another day?\""},{"id":"k-m-value-not-convince","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Offer value, don't convince","mv":"Hughes says the first thing he teaches salespeople is that they are never trying to convince anyone. Their job is to deliver value and answer every question honestly.","tr":"Before your next pitch, write the one way this genuinely helps them. If you can't, don't pitch.","w":"Hughes says the first thing he teaches salespeople is that they are never trying to convince anyone.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Ryan Blair podcast (2025): value, not convincing @0:07:18"],"rel":["k-m-ethics-three"],"how":["Hughes says the first thing he teaches salespeople is that they are never trying to convince anyone. Their job is to deliver value and answer every question honestly. Pushing people into a decision brings regret, returns and bad reviews."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Offer value, don't convince”?","a":"Hughes says the first thing he teaches salespeople is that they are never trying to convince anyone. Their job is to deliver value and answer every question honestly."},{"id":"k-m-future-memory","t":"ch","tp":"practice","ti":"Your future is stored like a memory","mv":"In a 2026 video Hughes points to research showing the brain imagines the future using the same networks it uses to remember the past.","tr":"Spend two minutes picturing one ordinary day as the person you're becoming, in detail.","w":"Spot it when you've read about something but haven't tried it with a real person yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes (2026): living inside a prediction @0:01:06"],"rel":["k-m-future-self"],"how":["In a 2026 video Hughes points to research showing the brain imagines the future using the same networks it uses to remember the past. So a future you picture often and in detail starts to feel familiar, and your brain filters for chances that match it."]},"wl":"Spot it","vers":[{"title":"The future self","note":"The future-self exercise gained a reason: the brain stores imagined futures much like memories.","chain":[{"label":"Earlier","when":"Eric Hunley podcast · 2021","text":"In 2021 Hughes defined discipline as putting your future self ahead of your present self, and said most bad habits are the present self winning.","card":"k-m-future-self"},{"label":"Updated","when":"Chase Hughes video · Jan 2026","text":"In a 2026 video Hughes points to research showing the brain imagines the future using the same networks it uses to remember the past.","card":"k-m-future-memory"}]}],"vb":"2021 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Your future is stored like a memory”?","a":"In a 2026 video Hughes points to research showing the brain imagines the future using the same networks it uses to remember the past."},{"id":"k-m-costly-honesty","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Trust honesty that costs them something","mv":"Hughes calls the usual answers (consistency, eye contact) brochure answers. Signals like a badge or a kind post are cheap and anyone can fake warmth.","tr":"This week, notice one person who kept a promise when it cost them. That's the trust signal to weigh, not how warm they seem.","w":"What can't be faked is price: people who tell you a truth that makes them look bad, keep a promise after it's become inconvenient, or correct you quietly rather than in front of others.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel (2026): what makes you trust someone @0:04:12"],"rel":["k-m-incoherence","k-m-joy-at-news"],"how":["Hughes calls the usual answers (consistency, eye contact) brochure answers. Signals like a badge or a kind post are cheap and anyone can fake warmth. What can't be faked is price: people who tell you a truth that makes them look bad, keep a promise after it's become inconvenient, or correct you quietly rather than in front of others."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"What earns trust, and what loses it","note":"Three angles from one Behavior Panel discussion on trust.","chain":[{"label":"Earns it","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Hughes calls the usual answers (consistency, eye contact) brochure answers. Signals like a badge or a kind post are cheap and anyone can fake warmth.","card":"k-m-costly-honesty"},{"label":"Loses it","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Asked what makes him mistrust someone, Hughes doesn't point to one slip. He picks incoherence: the moment someone's layers stop lining up.","card":"k-m-incoherence"},{"label":"Check yourself","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Hughes adds a caution to gut reads: a big share of \"I don't trust that guy\" is that he's openly doing something we'd never admit we do ourselves. Sometimes we're mistrusting our own reflection wearing someone else's face.","card":"k-m-mistrust-reflection"}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Trust honesty that costs them something”?","a":"This week, notice one person who kept a promise when it cost them. That's the trust signal to weigh, not how warm they seem."},{"id":"k-m-incoherence","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Mistrust starts when the layers stop matching","mv":"Asked what makes him mistrust someone, Hughes doesn't point to one slip. He picks incoherence: the moment someone's layers stop lining up.","tr":"Next time a person feels off, ask yourself which two layers didn't match: words and face, or warmth and usefulness.","w":"He picks incoherence: the moment someone's layers stop lining up.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel (2026): incoherence @0:22:26"],"rel":["k-m-costly-honesty"],"how":["Asked what makes him mistrust someone, Hughes doesn't point to one slip. He picks incoherence: the moment someone's layers stop lining up. The words say one thing and the expression lands late or slightly off, or their warmth switches on and off depending on whether you're useful to them. Most of us register it as a vague \"I don't want to turn my back on this person\".","Treat it as a reason to look closer, not proof. Check it against their baseline and look for a cluster."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"What earns trust, and what loses it","note":"Three angles from one Behavior Panel discussion on trust.","chain":[{"label":"Earns it","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Hughes calls the usual answers (consistency, eye contact) brochure answers. Signals like a badge or a kind post are cheap and anyone can fake warmth.","card":"k-m-costly-honesty"},{"label":"Loses it","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Asked what makes him mistrust someone, Hughes doesn't point to one slip. He picks incoherence: the moment someone's layers stop lining up.","card":"k-m-incoherence"},{"label":"Check yourself","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Hughes adds a caution to gut reads: a big share of \"I don't trust that guy\" is that he's openly doing something we'd never admit we do ourselves. Sometimes we're mistrusting our own reflection wearing someone else's face.","card":"k-m-mistrust-reflection"}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Mistrust starts when the layers stop matching”?","a":"Asked what makes him mistrust someone, Hughes doesn't point to one slip. He picks incoherence: the moment someone's layers stop lining up."},{"id":"k-m-mistrust-reflection","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Sometimes the mistrust is your own reflection","mv":"Hughes adds a caution to gut reads: a big share of \"I don't trust that guy\" is that he's openly doing something we'd never admit we do ourselves. Sometimes we're mistrusting our own reflection wearing someone else's face.","tr":"The next time you take an instant dislike to someone, ask: is there anything they're doing that I quietly do too?","w":"Hughes adds a caution to gut reads: a big share of \"I don't trust that guy\" is that he's openly doing something we'd never admit we do ourselves.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel (2026): our own reflection @0:35:10"],"rel":["k-m-incoherence","k-yt-render-of-you"],"how":["Hughes adds a caution to gut reads: a big share of \"I don't trust that guy\" is that he's openly doing something we'd never admit we do ourselves. Sometimes we're mistrusting our own reflection wearing someone else's face.","That doesn't mean ignoring caution where you or others are at risk. It means checking whether the read is about them or about you."]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"What earns trust, and what loses it","note":"Three angles from one Behavior Panel discussion on trust.","chain":[{"label":"Earns it","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Hughes calls the usual answers (consistency, eye contact) brochure answers. Signals like a badge or a kind post are cheap and anyone can fake warmth.","card":"k-m-costly-honesty"},{"label":"Loses it","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Asked what makes him mistrust someone, Hughes doesn't point to one slip. He picks incoherence: the moment someone's layers stop lining up.","card":"k-m-incoherence"},{"label":"Check yourself","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Hughes adds a caution to gut reads: a big share of \"I don't trust that guy\" is that he's openly doing something we'd never admit we do ourselves. Sometimes we're mistrusting our own reflection wearing someone else's face.","card":"k-m-mistrust-reflection"}]}],"vb":"3 versions","q":"Finish the line: “I don't trust …”","a":"“I don't trust that guy”"},{"id":"k-m-sincerity-messy","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Three signs of sincerity: messy, in rhythm, fitting","mv":"Hughes picks three signs that someone is congruent. First, it's messy: a perfectly timed, polished delivery can mean rehearsal.","tr":"When you next hear a polished apology or pitch, check for a little mess and whether gestures land on the key words.","w":"He adds that heavy performance can put people off because it forces them to become an audience they never agreed to be.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel (2026): congruence show and tell @0:02:39"],"rel":["k-m-incoherence"],"how":["Hughes picks three signs that someone is congruent. First, it's messy: a perfectly timed, polished delivery can mean rehearsal. Second, the head, eyes and hands land on the same words as the speech, in rhythm. Third, the emotion fits the relationship, even when it clashes with the setting, like grief showing for someone they're fighting in court.","He adds that heavy performance can put people off because it forces them to become an audience they never agreed to be."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Genuine or rehearsed?","note":"What Chase looks for before deciding someone means it, and the changes he watches when they explain themselves.","chain":[{"label":"Sincere","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Hughes picks three signs that someone is congruent. First, it's messy: a perfectly timed, polished delivery can mean rehearsal.","card":"k-m-sincerity-messy"},{"label":"Genuine distress","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2025","text":"Looking at a father pleading for his missing child, Hughes points to what genuine looks like: recall that shows up where you'd expect it, answers that come straight away, and a face whose emotion matches the words.","card":"k-m-genuine-signs"},{"label":"What changes","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2025","text":"Hughes lists eight things he checks when someone gives an account: stress signs, a change in blink rate, losing fluency, a shift in tone or pitch, a change of posture, protective gestures (shoulders up, limbs pulled in), inconsistencies such as qualifiers, small admissions or backtracking, and repeating the question.","card":"k-m-testimony-eight"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Three signs of sincerity: messy, in rhythm, fitting”?","a":"When you next hear a polished apology or pitch, check for a little mess and whether gestures land on the key words."},{"id":"k-m-testimony-eight","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Eight changes to watch when someone explains themselves","mv":"Hughes lists eight things he checks when someone gives an account: stress signs, a change in blink rate, losing fluency, a shift in tone or pitch, a change of posture, protective gestures (shoulders up, limbs pulled in), inconsistencies such as qualifiers, small admissions or backtracking, and repeating the question.","tr":"Next time someone tells you a story that matters, watch for just one of these changing, and note what was being said.","w":"A fast blink rate that never changes isn't on the list.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel (2025): the eight things @0:33:48"],"rel":["k-m-genuine-signs"],"how":["Hughes lists eight things he checks when someone gives an account: stress signs, a change in blink rate, losing fluency, a shift in tone or pitch, a change of posture, protective gestures (shoulders up, limbs pulled in), inconsistencies such as qualifiers, small admissions or backtracking, and repeating the question.","Every item is a change, measured against how that person was a moment earlier. A fast blink rate that never changes isn't on the list."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Genuine or rehearsed?","note":"What Chase looks for before deciding someone means it, and the changes he watches when they explain themselves.","chain":[{"label":"Sincere","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Hughes picks three signs that someone is congruent. First, it's messy: a perfectly timed, polished delivery can mean rehearsal.","card":"k-m-sincerity-messy"},{"label":"Genuine distress","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2025","text":"Looking at a father pleading for his missing child, Hughes points to what genuine looks like: recall that shows up where you'd expect it, answers that come straight away, and a face whose emotion matches the words.","card":"k-m-genuine-signs"},{"label":"What changes","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2025","text":"Hughes lists eight things he checks when someone gives an account: stress signs, a change in blink rate, losing fluency, a shift in tone or pitch, a change of posture, protective gestures (shoulders up, limbs pulled in), inconsistencies such as qualifiers, small admissions or backtracking, and repeating the question.","card":"k-m-testimony-eight"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Eight changes to watch when someone explains themselves”?","a":"Hughes lists eight things he checks when someone gives an account: stress signs, a change in blink rate, losing fluency, a shift in tone or pitch, a change of posture, protective gestures (shoulders up, limbs pulled in), inconsistencies such as qualifiers, small admissions or backtracking, and repeating the question."},{"id":"k-m-genuine-signs","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"What genuine distress looks like","mv":"Looking at a father pleading for his missing child, Hughes points to what genuine looks like: recall that shows up where you'd expect it, answers that come straight away, and a face whose emotion matches the words.","tr":"When someone you know is upset, notice whether their attention is on the problem or on how they come across.","w":"The panel stress that these are signs to weigh, never a verdict either way.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel (2025): signs of a genuine appeal @0:46:33"],"rel":["k-m-testimony-eight","k-m-sincerity-messy"],"how":["Looking at a father pleading for his missing child, Hughes points to what genuine looks like: recall that shows up where you'd expect it, answers that come straight away, and a face whose emotion matches the words. His energy goes into finding the person, not into a campaign about his own innocence.","The panel stress that these are signs to weigh, never a verdict either way."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","vers":[{"title":"Genuine or rehearsed?","note":"What Chase looks for before deciding someone means it, and the changes he watches when they explain themselves.","chain":[{"label":"Sincere","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2026","text":"Hughes picks three signs that someone is congruent. First, it's messy: a perfectly timed, polished delivery can mean rehearsal.","card":"k-m-sincerity-messy"},{"label":"Genuine distress","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2025","text":"Looking at a father pleading for his missing child, Hughes points to what genuine looks like: recall that shows up where you'd expect it, answers that come straight away, and a face whose emotion matches the words.","card":"k-m-genuine-signs"},{"label":"What changes","when":"The Behavior Panel · 2025","text":"Hughes lists eight things he checks when someone gives an account: stress signs, a change in blink rate, losing fluency, a shift in tone or pitch, a change of posture, protective gestures (shoulders up, limbs pulled in), inconsistencies such as qualifiers, small admissions or backtracking, and repeating the question.","card":"k-m-testimony-eight"}]}],"vb":"2025 → 2026","q":"What’s the trap with “What genuine distress looks like”?","a":"The panel stress that these are signs to weigh, never a verdict either way."},{"id":"k-m-interview-excited","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Job interviews: call it excitement, talk outcomes","mv":"Hughes' two notes for a job interview: excitement and outcomes. Nerves and excitement feel the same in the body (faster heart and so on), so stop calling it nerves and call it excitement; you'll come across more comfortable.","tr":"Before your next interview, write the three outcomes they most want from the role and plan one example for each.","w":"Nerves and excitement feel the same in the body (faster heart and so on), so stop calling it nerves and call it excitement; you'll come across more comfortable.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel (2024): job interview body language @0:00:44"],"rel":["k-yt-first-seconds","k-m-same-with-everyone"],"how":["Hughes' two notes for a job interview: excitement and outcomes. Nerves and excitement feel the same in the body (faster heart and so on), so stop calling it nerves and call it excitement; you'll come across more comfortable. Then keep talking about the outcomes the employer wants from the role, so they picture that result while they're looking at you."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Job interviews: call it excitement, talk outcomes”?","a":"Before your next interview, write the three outcomes they most want from the role and plan one example for each."},{"id":"k-m-regrets-text","t":"ch","tp":"deeper","ti":"The five regrets, and the message to send tonight","mv":"Hughes retells hospice carer Bronnie Ware's list of the regrets she heard most: not living true to yourself, working too hard, not expressing feelings, losing touch with friends, and not letting yourself be happier.","tr":"Think of the first person who comes to mind and send them a short message tonight.","w":"He notes the last regret is \"let myself\", as if happiness was there all along and never picked up.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes (2026): the regrets video @0:08:34"],"rel":["k-m-future-self","k-yt-anti-vision"],"how":["Hughes retells hospice carer Bronnie Ware's list of the regrets she heard most: not living true to yourself, working too hard, not expressing feelings, losing touch with friends, and not letting yourself be happier. He looks back on missing years of his own children's lives during deployments. His ask is simple: if someone's face came to mind while you listened, message them now.","He notes the last regret is \"let myself\", as if happiness was there all along and never picked up."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The five regrets, and the message to send tonight”?","a":"Hughes retells hospice carer Bronnie Ware's list of the regrets she heard most: not living true to yourself, working too hard, not expressing feelings, losing touch with friends, and not letting yourself be happier."},{"id":"k-bp-open-close","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"The one-question body read: opening or closing?","mv":"If Hughes had to fit body language on a sticky note, it would be one question: is the body closing up or opening out?","tr":"In your next conversation, notice one moment the other person's body draws in and one moment it opens again. What was being said each time?","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel: opening or closing @0:05:01"],"how":["If Hughes had to fit body language on a sticky note, it would be one question: is the body closing up or opening out? Arms, legs and shoulders drawing together signal someone pulling in; the same parts loosening and spreading signal comfort returning. He suggests starting there, for example when interviewing a babysitter or a new colleague.","The change matters more than the posture itself. Compare against how they sat when the conversation was easy."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “The one-question body read: opening or closing?”?","a":"If Hughes had to fit body language on a sticky note, it would be one question: is the body closing up or opening out?"},{"id":"k-bp-body-narration","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Illustrators and body narration (Behavior Panel)","mv":"Illustrators are the hand, head and eyebrow movements that stress words as we speak.","tr":"Next time a friend tells you a story, watch whether their hands act it out. Narrating with the body is often a sign they're seeing the scene as they talk.","w":"Hughes' favourite thing to check is whether they match the tone and land on the words; when they don't, something is off, though that doesn't tell you what.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel: illustrators and body narration @0:08:46"],"rel":["k-m-sincerity-messy","k-m-incoherence"],"how":["Illustrators are the hand, head and eyebrow movements that stress words as we speak. Hughes' favourite thing to check is whether they match the tone and land on the words; when they don't, something is off, though that doesn't tell you what. He adds a second kind he calls body narration: someone sketching the thing they describe in the air, holding an invisible book or showing how tall a building was, so you can picture it with them.","Off-beat gestures are a reason to listen more closely, not a verdict."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Illustrators and body narration (Behavior Panel)”?","a":"Next time a friend tells you a story, watch whether their hands act it out. Narrating with the body is often a sign they're seeing the scene as they talk."},{"id":"k-bp-barriers-off","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Notice when the barriers come off","mv":"Barriers aren't only folded arms. Hughes counts clothing and objects too: tightening a tie, buttoning a jacket, zipping up, or putting a cup down between you and them.","tr":"In your next meeting or meal, notice whether anyone moves a cup, phone or bag out from between you as the talk warms up.","w":"Spot it when what they say and what their body shows don't quite match. Ask, don't assume.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel: barriers going on and coming off @0:14:42"],"rel":["k-bp-open-close"],"how":["Barriers aren't only folded arms. Hughes counts clothing and objects too: tightening a tie, buttoning a jacket, zipping up, or putting a cup down between you and them. Just as telling is the moment they go: the tie loosened, jacket undone, glass moved aside. That marks a point of comfort, where the person starts to open up, and he watches for several people in a group doing it together."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Notice when the barriers come off”?","a":"Barriers aren't only folded arms. Hughes counts clothing and objects too: tightening a tie, buttoning a jacket, zipping up, or putting a cup down between you and them."},{"id":"k-bp-lips-two","t":"ch","tp":"body","ti":"Pressed lips versus lips pulled in (Behavior Panel)","mv":"Hughes separates two mouth signals that look alike. Lips pressed together usually point to an opinion being held back.","tr":"When you see someone's lips pull in, try offering a little reassurance and see if their face relaxes. When they press, ask what they think.","w":"Each is a prompt to check in, not a label for the person.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel: lip signals @0:07:19"],"how":["Hughes separates two mouth signals that look alike. Lips pressed together usually point to an opinion being held back. Lips drawn back into the mouth point more to a need for reassurance. Biting, chewing and licking the lips sit with anxiety. He also notes that blink rate can stay steady overall yet bunch into little clusters at the moments someone feels threatened.","Each is a prompt to check in, not a label for the person."],"go":[{"tab":"cues","label":"See real body cues in Body"}]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “Pressed lips versus lips pulled in (Behavior Panel)”?","a":"Each is a prompt to check in, not a label for the person."},{"id":"k-bp-leading-questions","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"Ask without steering the answer","mv":"Hughes points out that the person asking can quietly steer the answers: nodding when they want a yes, shaking their head for a no, and phrasing questions so the answer is already inside them (\"you didn't know about that, right?\").","tr":"In your next real conversation, ask one open question with a still face and no built-in answer, and notice how different the reply is.","w":"Spot it when you catch yourself planning your reply instead of hearing theirs.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel: leading questions @1:00:21"],"rel":["k-m-incoherence"],"how":["Hughes points out that the person asking can quietly steer the answers: nodding when they want a yes, shaking their head for a no, and phrasing questions so the answer is already inside them (\"you didn't know about that, right?\"). When you read an interview, read the asker as well as the answerer.","The same applies to you: your nods and phrasing can shape what people tell you."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"Finish the line: “you didn't know about …”","a":"“you didn't know about that, right?”"},{"id":"k-bp-adding-to-self","t":"ch","tp":"warmth","ti":"Notice how much people add to themselves","mv":"Asked what he notices first on meeting anyone, Hughes says it's how much a person seems to feel they're not enough: how much extra they add to themselves, the flourishes and side stories that pad who they are.","tr":"When someone piles on achievements, try giving them a little sincere acknowledgement early, and see if the performance eases.","w":"Spot it when you genuinely feel it but haven't said it out loud yet.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel Q&A: what Hughes notices first @0:29:44"],"rel":["k-m-costly-honesty"],"how":["Asked what he notices first on meeting anyone, Hughes says it's how much a person seems to feel they're not enough: how much extra they add to themselves, the flourishes and side stories that pad who they are. It isn't a judgement; it shows him what they may need to feel at ease.","Use it as a cue for kindness. People who feel enough rarely need to perform."]},"wl":"Spot it","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Notice how much people add to themselves”?","a":"Asked what he notices first on meeting anyone, Hughes says it's how much a person seems to feel they're not enough: how much extra they add to themselves, the flourishes and side stories that pad who they are."},{"id":"k-yt-shorter-answers","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"When their answers get shorter, stop adding","mv":"Hughes treats the length of someone's replies as a gauge. If their answers start getting shorter, you are adding to them, and each extra push takes something out of them.","tr":"Next time someone's replies shrink, stop and count to nine in your head. Let them fill the space with their own words.","w":"The urge to try harder is the very signal to stop.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Helping Makes It Worse: sentence length @9:22","Practise counting nine seconds @17:02"],"rel":["k-yt-remove-reasons","k-yt-avoid-why","k-yt-translator-not-fix","k-yt-default-state"],"how":["Hughes treats the length of someone's replies as a gauge. If their answers start getting shorter, you are adding to them, and each extra push takes something out of them. The urge to try harder is the very signal to stop. In his crisis-call example, a few seconds of quiet did more than all the technique.","He also suggests making it free to say no: \"You don't have to answer that,\" or \"I'm not looking for an answer today.\" People often answer anyway, and at length, because a no-cost refusal is rare. Swapping \"why\" for \"how did that end up happening?\" tends to make replies longer too."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “When their answers get shorter, stop adding”?","a":"The urge to try harder is the very signal to stop."},{"id":"k-yt-reads-are-drafts","t":"ch","tp":"ideas","ti":"Every read on a person is a draft","mv":"Hughes warns that when you size someone up, you build your own internal model of them. Within the first ten minutes most of us decide what a person is, and from then on we predict them instead of actually looking.","tr":"After you've formed a view of someone, look for one thing they do that doesn't fit it, and update.","w":"Within the first ten minutes most of us decide what a person is, and from then on we predict them instead of actually looking.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – Not Possible: every read is a draft @30:35"],"rel":["k-yt-render-of-you","k-yt-memory-rewrite"],"how":["Hughes warns that when you size someone up, you build your own internal model of them. Within the first ten minutes most of us decide what a person is, and from then on we predict them instead of actually looking. Your model is also re-edited by whatever mood you were in each time you thought about them.","His rule is that any read on a human being stays a draft. Keep watching for behaviour that breaks your forecast rather than filing it away as noise."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Every read on a person is a draft”?","a":"After you've formed a view of someone, look for one thing they do that doesn't fit it, and update."},{"id":"k-yt-xba-room","t":"ch","tp":"presence","ti":"Owning a room: the XBA formula","mv":"Hughes says every room contains a silent negotiation over frame. His XBA idea runs in three parts.","tr":"Walk in slowly, greet first, say one unexpected but warm thing, and don't compete for airtime.","w":"Exceed their belief about you rather than fight it.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["Chase Hughes – OWN Any Room: The Social XBA Formula @1:40","Interruptions and disagreements @7:18"],"rel":["k-yt-default-leader"],"how":["Hughes says every room contains a silent negotiation over frame. His XBA idea runs in three parts. Interrupt people's autopilot expectation with something mildly unexpected, which snaps them into the present. Exceed their belief about you rather than fight it. Then act with calm, grounded behaviour as if your presence sets the tone.","He adds that you should validate others' frames rather than destroy them, and stay non-attached: you're glad you don't have to agree, and it's up to them. If someone interrupts, keep the same pace, volume and tone, leading without placing yourself above them. In a disagreement, try \"I can see how you'd see it that way. Would you be open to another angle?\" An unshakable frame is built by friction, real exposure, not by faking calm."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"In one breath: what’s the move in “Owning a room: the XBA formula”?","a":"Hughes says every room contains a silent negotiation over frame. His XBA idea runs in three parts."},{"id":"k-bp-big-answer-small-question","t":"ch","tp":"listening","ti":"A big answer to a small question","mv":"Hughes treats the size of an answer as information. When a calm, simple question gets a huge reply full of things nobody asked about, the person may be answering a suspicion they imagine rather than the question in front of them.","tr":"When a small question gets a big answer, ask a gentle follow-up about the part they seemed keenest to explain.","w":"When a calm, simple question gets a huge reply full of things nobody asked about, the person may be answering a suspicion they imagine rather than the question in front of them.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel: a big answer to a small question @20:36"],"rel":["k-yt-shorter-answers"],"how":["Hughes treats the size of an answer as information. When a calm, simple question gets a huge reply full of things nobody asked about, the person may be answering a suspicion they imagine rather than the question in front of them. In his example, a man asked during a routine welfare check rushed to show police proof of his story.","He files it as a reason to stay curious and ask more, not as proof of anything. Nervousness, culture and personality can all make people talk more, so compare with how they usually answer."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"What’s the trap with “A big answer to a small question”?","a":"When a calm, simple question gets a huge reply full of things nobody asked about, the person may be answering a suspicion they imagine rather than the question in front of them."},{"id":"k-bp-remorse-without-names","t":"ch","tp":"tough","ti":"Remorse without calling yourself names","mv":"Commenting on a public apology, Hughes calls it good advice for anyone that you can feel real remorse for a mistake without rewriting your identity around it. You don't have to call yourself names to be sorry.","tr":"Next time you apologise, name the specific thing you did and what you'll do differently, and leave out the insults to yourself.","w":"You don't have to call yourself names to be sorry.","ev":"Coach's view","d":{"src":["The Behavior Panel: remorse without changing who you are @39:24"],"rel":["k-yt-guilt-shame","k-yt-confidence-543"],"how":["Commenting on a public apology, Hughes calls it good advice for anyone that you can feel real remorse for a mistake without rewriting your identity around it. You don't have to call yourself names to be sorry.","He also contrasts two ways of closing the eyes while talking. With the head down and no eyebrow raise, it fits reflection. With raised brows, or a tilted-back head, it tends to come across as smug or self-satisfied. He still notes when someone stays vague about the specific thing they did."]},"wl":"Watch out","q":"You’ve got 5 seconds: how do you use “Remorse without calling yourself names”?","a":"Next time you apologise, name the specific thing you did and what you'll do differently, and leave out the insults to yourself."}]};
